window.DOCS_INDEX=[{"p":"Quick Start","h":"Quick Start","u":"quick-start.html#quick-start","x":"Shipready needs Unity 6000.0.85f1 or newer, the Unity UI package (it is in every new project) and TextMesh Pro's essential resources (Unity offers to import them the first time; say yes). It works in the Built-in, URP and HDRP render pipelines, with either input system."},{"p":"Quick Start","h":"1. Connect your game","u":"quick-start.html#1-connect-your-game","x":"Open Tools > Shipready > Launch Desk . On the Start tab, choose how Shipready joins your project: - New game : the open scene becomes your menu scene. The game starts on the title screen. - Existing game : the open scene is a level. The menus go in beside what is there and stay hidden until the pause key opens them. Your Event System, canvases, cameras and scripts are left alone. - Keep my own menus : only settings and saves are set up. You call them from your own screens. Read the plan. Every line is something Shipready will add, and nothing else changes. Press Connect . Scene changes are one"},{"p":"Quick Start","h":"2. Press Play","u":"quick-start.html#2-press-play","x":"The title screen and main menu work with mouse, keyboard and controller. Settings change the real game: resolution with a \"keep these settings?\" countdown, quality, volumes, key rebinding, accessibility. Escape (or Start on a controller) pauses. F5 saves and F9 loads while you test in the Editor. The save menu shows slots with pictures, dates and play time."},{"p":"Quick Start","h":"3. Choose what players get","u":"quick-start.html#3-choose-what-players-get","x":"On the Player Options tab, pick a starting point (Locked down, Standard, Full control), then set a whole category or one setting at a time: players change it, see it locked, or do not see it. See Player Options ."},{"p":"Quick Start","h":"4. Give it your look","u":"quick-start.html#4-give-it-your-look","x":"On the Look tab, press Create the game look , pick a starting scheme and change any colour. The menus, the save menu, the bug report form and the tutorial cards all follow. See Game Look ."},{"p":"Quick Start","h":"5. Check before you ship","u":"quick-start.html#5-check-before-you-ship","x":"On the Readiness tab, press Inspect . Fix blockers first, then warnings, then run the checks that are marked not tested. Export report saves the result as a page you can share. See Ship Readiness Checker ."},{"p":"Quick Start","h":"Where to go next","u":"quick-start.html#where-to-go-next","x":"I want to Read Save my own data (score, inventory, position) Save/Documentation/QuickStart.md Add or remove a setting Settings/Documentation/SettingsCatalog.md Change a screen's layout Settings/Documentation/Screens.md Add first-time hints Tutorials/Documentation/QuickStart.md Let testers send bug reports Playtest/Documentation/QuickStart.md Keep the save system I already have Working with other products"},{"p":"Quick Start","h":"If something does not work","u":"quick-start.html#if-something-does-not-work","x":"Open the question mark in the Launch Desk, press Copy system info , and send it with what you saw to the support address shown there. The Readiness tab also explains most setup problems and fixes many with one button."},{"p":"Launch Desk","h":"Launch Desk","u":"launch-desk.html#launch-desk","x":"Tools > Shipready > Launch Desk is the main window. It has four tabs and a question mark."},{"p":"Launch Desk","h":"Start","u":"launch-desk.html#start","x":"Connect my game sets Shipready up in the open project. Choose a path (New game, Existing game, Keep my own menus), read the plan, press Connect. The plan lists each step with a plus (will be done), a tick (already done) or a warning (cannot be done yet, with the reason, for example an unsaved scene). Look again reads the project again after you changed something. It changes nothing. Steps that touch the scene are one undo step. Steps that make assets put them in Assets/RareGeometryData/Shipready . Each part of Shipready adds its own steps. If you deleted the Tutorials or Playtest folder, their"},{"p":"Launch Desk","h":"Player Options","u":"launch-desk.html#player-options","x":"One asset decides what players see and may change. See Player Options ."},{"p":"Launch Desk","h":"Look","u":"launch-desk.html#look","x":"One asset gives every player screen the same colours, fonts and rounding. See Game Look ."},{"p":"Launch Desk","h":"Readiness","u":"launch-desk.html#readiness","x":"The Ship Readiness Checker. See Ship Readiness Checker ."},{"p":"Launch Desk","h":"The question mark","u":"launch-desk.html#the-question-mark","x":"Help: the quick start, the manual's folder, the support addresses as text to copy, and Copy system info ."},{"p":"Launch Desk","h":"The other windows","u":"launch-desk.html#the-other-windows","x":"Menu Window What it is for Tools > Shipready > Menus > Menu Builder Menu Builder Screens, layout, themes, the settings list, a check of the front end Tools > Shipready > Saves > Start Saves Status of the save setup and the demos Tools > Shipready > Saves > Setup Wizard Save Setup Slots, autosave, backups, encryption, the save menu Tools > Shipready > Saves > Menu Style Save Menu Style The save menu's look and layout Tools > Shipready > Saves > Save Inspector Save Inspector Read and edit save files Tools > Shipready > Saves > Diagnostics Save Diagnostics Problems in the save setup, with fixes T"},{"p":"Make it yours","h":"Make it yours","u":"make-it-yours.html#make-it-yours","x":"Everything players see can be changed, and so can what players are allowed to change. This page lists each thing and where it is. Your changes live in Assets/RareGeometryData/Shipready ; updates of Shipready never touch that folder."},{"p":"Make it yours","h":"The big switches","u":"make-it-yours.html#the-big-switches","x":"What Where Colours, fonts, text size and corner rounding of every screen at once Launch Desk > Look ( Game Look ) Which settings players see and may change, with limits and starting values Launch Desk > Player Options ( Player Options ) What players may do with saves; skipping tutorials; tester tools per build Launch Desk > Player Options Who a setting belongs to (device, each player, a save, this session) The setting's asset, Scope ( Per-player settings ) The colours your game uses to tell things apart Game Color settings ( Game colours )"},{"p":"Make it yours","h":"Menus and settings","u":"make-it-yours.html#menus-and-settings","x":"What players see Where you change it Which screens exist (title, main menu, pause, settings, loading, credits, extras) and their buttons Menu Builder > Setup The layout (centred, side column, bottom bar, full-screen tabs) Menu Builder > Setup, or Tools > Shipready > Menus > Layout The theme: fonts, sizes, colours, images, button shape, choice and switch style, tab line, scroll bar Menu Builder > Theme, or the theme asset. Three themes ship (Clean, Tactical, Storybook); copy one to start Backgrounds per screen (colour, image, video, blur of the game) and how see-through panels are The theme ass"},{"p":"Make it yours","h":"Saves","u":"make-it-yours.html#saves","x":"What players see Where you change it The save menu's look: six ready styles, every colour, fonts, sizes, rounding, spacing, your own images Tools > Shipready > Saves > Menu Style The save menu's layout: tabs, newest first, the status line, how many cards show Menu Style > Layout Every word of the save menu and its dialogs Menu Style > Words Slot pictures, place names, play time and date formats The save settings and the Save Menu component The saving indicator: where it sits, icon, text or both, how long \"Saved\" shows The Saving Indicator component, or the theme's messages Save notices (\"Autos"},{"p":"Make it yours","h":"Tutorials","u":"make-it-yours.html#tutorials","x":"What players see Where you change it Coach marks or in-game hints, per tutorial or per step The Tutorial Flow and its steps The look: dim, spotlight shape, outline, pointer, card colours and position, buttons, progress dots or counter The Tutorial Theme. Five presets ship The message of a step, as full text, short text or icon The step, or the theme's Display As Which device a step is for (keyboard and mouse, controller, touch) The step's Show On Skip, Back and Next buttons The theme and the step; Player Options decides if players may skip Sounds and animation The theme"},{"p":"Make it yours","h":"Bug reports (testers)","u":"make-it-yours.html#bug-reports-testers","x":"What testers see Where you change it The form's look: colours, font, text size, your own panel, button and field images, width The Bug Report Theme. Four presets ship Categories and severities, which parts a report includes, the privacy note Tools > Shipready > Playtest > Settings Where the overlay, the stats, the error badge and the report button sit The Bug Report Theme Every word The playtest text table The keys that open the form and the overlay Tools > Shipready > Playtest > Settings"},{"p":"Make it yours","h":"From code","u":"make-it-yours.html#from-code","x":"Every screen is also driven from code, and every list on this page has an API. Start with: PlayerOptions , GameLook , SettingsRuntime , SettingsPlayers , GameColors , MenuRoot , SaveKit , SaveMenu , TutorialFlow , BugReporter , GamePause , TextEntry , GameTrail ."},{"p":"Player Options","h":"Player Options","u":"player-options.html#player-options","x":"One asset where you decide what players get: which settings they see and may change, what they may do with saves, whether they can skip tutorials, and which builds carry the tester tools. Every Shipready screen asks this asset before it shows or allows something."},{"p":"Player Options","h":"Make it","u":"player-options.html#make-it","x":"Launch Desk > Player Options > Create Player Options . The asset is ShipreadyPlayerOptions in Assets/RareGeometryData/Shipready/Resources ; the game loads it by that name. With no asset, players get everything except the tester tools in release builds."},{"p":"Player Options","h":"Start from","u":"player-options.html#start-from","x":"Starting point For What it does Locked down A tightly directed game Sound, controls and accessibility only; graphics and display hidden; the game does the saving Standard Most games Every setting and the save tools; tester tools stay out of release builds Full control Early access, public tests Standard, plus turning autosave off and bug reports in every build"},{"p":"Player Options","h":"Settings players get","u":"player-options.html#settings-players-get","x":"The list shows every setting of your settings profile. For a whole category or for one setting choose: Choice Players Players change it see the setting and can change it Shown, locked see the setting, dimmed, and cannot change it. It runs at your starting value Hidden do not see it. It still runs at your starting value Open a setting's row for more: Starting value : the value it starts at and resets to. On or off, one of its options, or a number in its range. Limits : the lowest and highest value players may pick, or which options they are offered. A setting's own choice wins over its category"},{"p":"Player Options","h":"Saves, controls, tutorials","u":"player-options.html#saves-controls-tutorials","x":"Tick boxes for: save by hand, quicksave, name saves, copy saves, delete saves, restore a backup, turn autosave off, change keys and buttons, skip a tutorial, replay tutorials."},{"p":"Player Options","h":"Tester tools","u":"player-options.html#tester-tools","x":"Which builds have the bug report form and which have the debug console and options: none, development builds only, or every build. The Readiness tab tells you when these and the playtest settings disagree."},{"p":"Player Options","h":"From code","u":"player-options.html#from-code","x":"if (PlayerOptions.Current.Can(PlayerAction.SaveByHand)) ShowSaveButton(); // Ask and tell the player why not: raises PlayerOptions.Refused with a short reason. if (!PlayerOptions.Current.Check(PlayerAction.DeleteSaves)) return; PlayerOptions.Refused += (action, reason) => toast.Show(reason); // Another set of options from now on, for example per difficulty. PlayerOptions.Use(hardcoreOptions); PlayerOptions.Current is never null. AccessFor(settingId, categoryId) gives a setting's access, RuleFor its starting value and limits."},{"p":"Game Look","h":"Game Look","u":"game-look.html#game-look","x":"One look for every screen players see. The menus and settings, the save menu, the bug report form and the tutorial cards take their colours, fonts and corner rounding from one asset, so the whole game changes with one edit."},{"p":"Game Look","h":"Make it","u":"game-look.html#make-it","x":"Launch Desk > Look > Create the game look . The asset is ShipreadyLook in Assets/RareGeometryData/Shipready/Resources . With no asset, every screen keeps the look of its own theme."},{"p":"Game Look","h":"What you set","u":"game-look.html#what-you-set","x":"Value Used for Accent The selected control, the main button, highlights and pointers Text on accent Words and icons on top of the accent colour Panels Panels, dialogs and cards Controls Buttons, rows, sliders and text fields Shade behind screens Laid over the game behind an open screen; its alpha is how much of the game shows through Text, Quiet text Titles and body text; hints, dates and other quiet text Danger, Warning, Success Deleting and errors; things to look at; saved and done Menu font, Heading font TextMesh Pro fonts for the menus and the save menu Form and card font The font file for"},{"p":"Game Look","h":"Which screens wear it","u":"game-look.html#which-screens-wear-it","x":"Tick boxes for menus and settings, the save menu, the bug report form and tutorial cards. A screen left out keeps its own theme."},{"p":"Game Look","h":"What the look does not change","u":"game-look.html#what-the-look-does-not-change","x":"Each screen keeps its own theme for layout, sizes, images, sounds and animation. The look never writes into a theme asset: screens wear it on a copy made while the game runs. Remove the look asset and everything is as before. While a player has High contrast on, the menus use their high-contrast theme instead of the look."},{"p":"Game Look","h":"From code","u":"game-look.html#from-code","x":"// A look per chapter, or a colour scheme the player picked. GameLook.Use(winterLook); // every open screen restyles at once GameLook.Use(null); // back to the asset in Resources // A scheme made in code. GameLook look = GameLook.Create(GameLookPreset.Ember); look.Accent = teamColour; GameLook.Use(look); // Your own UI can read the same colours. GameLook current = GameLook.Current; // null when the game has no look if (current != null) myPanel.color = current.Panel; GameLook.Changed += Restyle; To start a theme of your own from a look, call the theme's Wear once: SettingsMenuTheme.Wear(myTheme"},{"p":"Per-player settings","h":"Per-player settings","u":"per-player-settings.html#per-player-settings","x":"Every setting belongs to someone. That decides where its value is kept and who may change it. Scope Belongs to Kept in Use for Device This computer or console The settings file Resolution, quality, volumes, language Player Each local player Player one: the settings file. Every other player: a file of their own Controls, key bindings, vibration, camera comfort Save Slot A playthrough The save Difficulty and other choices that belong to one playthrough Session This run of the game Nowhere Things that should start fresh every launch"},{"p":"Per-player settings","h":"Choosing a scope","u":"per-player-settings.html#choosing-a-scope","x":"Select a setting's asset and set Scope . Automatic uses the setting's usual scope: per player for look sensitivity, invert look, vibration, hold or toggle, field of view, camera shake, head bob and key bindings; per device for everything else. A setting that asks \"keep these settings?\" (the display mode) is always per device."},{"p":"Per-player settings","h":"Split screen and couch co-op","u":"per-player-settings.html#split-screen-and-couch-co-op","x":"Player one is the game-wide settings ( SettingsRuntime.Current ). When another player joins, give them their own: // For example from Unity's Player Input Manager, in its Player Joined event. void OnPlayerJoined(PlayerInput input) { SettingsController settings = SettingsPlayers.Join(input.playerIndex); // Read a value for this player. float sensitivity = SettingsPlayers.Holder(input.playerIndex, \"controls.gamepad-sensitivity\") .GetFloat(\"controls.gamepad-sensitivity\", 1f); } void OnPlayerLeft(PlayerInput input) => SettingsPlayers.Leave(input.playerIndex); A guest has only the per-player settin"},{"p":"Per-player settings","h":"Settings that travel with the save","u":"per-player-settings.html#settings-that-travel-with-the-save","x":"A Save Slot setting is stored inside the save, through the save contract every Rare Geometry product shares. Saving writes its value; loading a save puts it back and applies it. New Game in the front end sets these settings back to their starting values. From your own code: SettingsRuntime.Current.ResetScope(SettingScope.SaveSlot);"},{"p":"Per-player settings","h":"Session settings","u":"per-player-settings.html#session-settings","x":"A Session setting works for the rest of the run and starts from its default at the next launch."},{"p":"Game colours","h":"Game colours","u":"game-colours.html#game-colours","x":"Some things in a game are told apart by colour: enemies and allies, loot, danger zones, the path to follow. For players with colour blindness a filter over the whole picture rarely helps. What helps is letting them pick those colours themselves, from a palette whose colours stay apart for every kind of colour vision."},{"p":"Game colours","h":"Add one","u":"game-colours.html#add-one","x":"Create > Shipready > Menus > Accessibility > Game Color . Give it an id such as colors.enemy and a name players see (\"Enemies\"). Keep the starting palette (eight colours that stay apart with the common kinds of colour blindness) or edit it. Set Default Value to the colour your game uses today. Add the asset to your settings profile, in the Accessibility category. The settings menu shows the colour's name with a bar in that colour under it."},{"p":"Game colours","h":"Use it","u":"game-colours.html#use-it","x":"Without code: add a Game Color Target to the object (Add Component > Shipready > Menus > Game Color Target) and type the setting's id. It colours what it finds on the object: a sprite, a mesh or skinned renderer (through a property block, so materials are not copied), a line or trail, a light, a particle system, or a canvas image or text. It follows the player's choice at once. Option Meaning Player Which local player's choice to use (0 is player one) Keep Alpha Keep the object's own alpha and take only the colour Emission Also set the material's emission colour, multiplied by this Include Chi"},{"p":"Game colours","h":"The Steam \"Color Alternatives\" tag","u":"game-colours.html#the-steam-color-alternatives-tag","x":"The Readiness tab lists how many game colour settings players have. The tag is true once every important signal that used colour alone is one of them, or is backed by a shape, an icon or a word. Walk through the game and check."},{"p":"Ship Readiness Checker","h":"Ship Readiness Checker","u":"readiness.html#ship-readiness-checker","x":"Launch Desk > Readiness . It inspects the project and says what still needs attention before release, worst first. It is a pre-launch inspection: it finds real problems early. It does not certify a game for any store."},{"p":"Ship Readiness Checker","h":"Run it","u":"readiness.html#run-it","x":"Choose where the game is going (Windows, Mac and Linux; Steam Deck; mobile; WebGL; console-style) and press Inspect . Each place has its own checks."},{"p":"Ship Readiness Checker","h":"Reading a finding","u":"readiness.html#reading-a-finding","x":"Part Meaning Blocker Players will hit this. Fix before release Warning Likely to hurt players, or important and unproven Not tested A check nobody has run or recorded yet. It is never counted as passed Info Good to know. Nothing is broken Passed Checked and fine (shown with Show passed too ) Each finding also says how its result was established: Evidence Meaning Scanned Shipready read the project (settings, scenes, assets) Ran Shipready ran it, for example the save survival run that interrupts a save at four points Checked by hand You did the steps and recorded the result Needs the platform On"},{"p":"Ship Readiness Checker","h":"Checks you do by hand","u":"readiness.html#checks-you-do-by-hand","x":"Some things only a person can judge: the first launch from a fresh install, a full save, quit and reload, every screen with a controller only, text size on a small screen. Open the finding, follow the steps, and press Passed or Failed with a note. The result is kept per target in ProjectSettings/RareGeometry/ShipreadyReadiness.json together with the game version, so it travels with the project. Clear forgets it."},{"p":"Ship Readiness Checker","h":"What is checked","u":"readiness.html#what-is-checked","x":"The project: build scenes, game name and version, icon, development build, missing scripts, Event Systems, input handling, scripting backend. Menus: a front end is in the build and in the first scene, only one, its profile is sound, every screen has a place to start with a controller, every setting players can change reaches the game. Saves: the setup, and a survival run (save, restart, load, then four interrupted saves that must never report success or lose the last good save). Accessibility: the game look's contrast, and which Steam accessibility tags your settings cover, with the ones they "},{"p":"Ship Readiness Checker","h":"The report","u":"readiness.html#the-report","x":"Export report writes an HTML page to read or share and a JSON file for tools. Both say \"pre-launch inspection\", list every finding with its evidence, and never show an untested check as passed."},{"p":"Ship Readiness Checker","h":"Your own checks","u":"readiness.html#your-own-checks","x":"Write a class that derives from ReadinessRule anywhere in an editor assembly that references Shipready's editor assembly. The Checker finds it by itself. public sealed class CreditsRule : ReadinessRule { public override string Id => \"mygame.credits\"; public override string Title => \"Credits are filled in\"; public override ReadinessArea Area => ReadinessArea.Screens; public override void Check(ReadinessContext context, List<ReadinessFinding> into) { if (CreditsAreEmpty()) into.Add(Warning(\"The credits are empty\", \"Players and stores expect them.\", \"Fill in the Credits asset.\")); else into.Add(P"},{"p":"Working with other products","h":"Working with other products","u":"works-with.html#working-with-other-products","x":"Shipready needs no other product. This page is about what happens when there are others, and about games that keep part of what they already have."},{"p":"Working with other products","h":"Other Rare Geometry products","u":"works-with.html#other-rare-geometry-products","x":"When another Rare Geometry product is in the project, nothing has to be set up: One save. Whatever the other product saves (map discoveries, built pieces, object states) joins the player's save slot and comes back when that slot is loaded. A save the product made on its own before Shipready arrived still loads. One set of accessibility choices. The other product follows the player's settings: reduced motion, camera shake and flash strength, text size, high contrast, colour-blind mode, hold or toggle, subtitles. One readiness report. The other product's own diagnostics show on the Readiness tab"},{"p":"Working with other products","h":"Keeping your own save system","u":"works-with.html#keeping-your-own-save-system","x":"You can use Shipready's menus and settings with the save system you already have (your own, or another asset's). Add an Own Save Bridge to the object with the Menu Root (Add Component > Shipready > Menus > Own Save Bridge). Wire its four events to your save code: Event When Do On Continue Continue was pressed Load the newest save and enter the game On Open Load Load Game was pressed Open your load screen On Open Save Save Game was pressed in the pause menu Open your save screen, or save at once On Close Back was pressed while your screen was open Close it Tell it whether a save exists, so Cont"},{"p":"Working with other products","h":"Keeping your own menus","u":"works-with.html#keeping-your-own-menus","x":"Choose Keep my own menus on the Launch Desk's Start tab. Only settings and saves are set up. From your own screens: SettingsController settings = SettingsRuntime.Current; settings.SetPendingFloat(\"audio.master-volume\", 0.5f); settings.Apply(); SaveKit.Save(1); // slot 1 SaveKit.Load(1); Settings/Documentation/ExtendingAPI.md and Save/Documentation/Reference.md have the full lists."},{"p":"Working with other products","h":"Narration (text to speech)","u":"works-with.html#narration-text-to-speech","x":"While the player's Narration setting is on, the menus send every line a screen reader would speak (the focused button, a setting's name, value and help, each screen's title). Shipready does not speak by itself. Connect a voice: public sealed class MyVoice : INarrationVoice { public void Say(string text, bool interrupt) { /* your platform's text to speech */ } public void Stop() { } } MenuNarration.Voice = new MyVoice(); To check the wording first, add a Narration Caption to any object: it shows each line on screen."},{"p":"Shipready Settings","h":"Shipready Settings","u":"settings-index.html#shipready-settings","x":"A complete game front end for Unity: title screen, main menu, settings, credits, pause menu, loading screen, confirmation dialogs, subtitles and a controller-disconnected prompt. Everything works with keyboard, mouse and gamepad, comes in three themes, can be translated, and is set up from one editor window without code. Main menu, Clean theme"},{"p":"Shipready Settings","h":"Start here","u":"settings-index.html#start-here","x":"Quick Start : your first working menu in a few clicks. Samples : four scenes to try right away. Using it in an existing game : your input, cursor, pause, UI, saves, audio, post effects and scenes, and a five-minute check. Make it yours : presets, your own theme, switching parts off, per-element overrides, your own UI."},{"p":"Shipready Settings","h":"Features","u":"settings-index.html#features","x":"Screens : title, main menu, settings, credits, pause, loading, dialogs, controller prompt. Settings catalog : what ships, the Auto / Connect / Read tiers, adding and creating settings. Settings reference : every setting with its id, default and values. Display safety : the 15-second confirmation, crash recovery, the settings file. Audio : volumes with or without an Audio Mixer, UI sounds. Graphics : what each render pipeline gets. Controls and rebinding : key rebinding, controller prompts, sensitivity, vibration. Themes : Clean, Tactical, Storybook and your own. Accessibility : menu and text s"},{"p":"Shipready Settings","h":"For programmers","u":"settings-index.html#for-programmers","x":"Extending and API : reading settings, your own settings and appliers, events, code examples. Upgrading : what changes between versions."},{"p":"Shipready Settings","h":"Help","u":"settings-index.html#help","x":"Troubleshooting and FAQ Known issues Support Online manual: https://raregeometry.github.io/shipready/docs/"},{"p":"Quick Start","h":"Quick Start","u":"settings-quick-start.html#quick-start","x":"Your first working menu in a few clicks. You need Unity 6000.0 or newer. TextMesh Pro's Essential Resources must be in the project (Unity offers to import them the first time a text appears; you can also use Window > TextMeshPro > Import TMP Essential Resources)."},{"p":"Quick Start","h":"1. Open the menu builder","u":"settings-quick-start.html#1-open-the-menu-builder","x":"Tools > Shipready > Menus > Menu Builder. It opens on the Home tab. (A short welcome window also appears once after import; you can turn it off.)"},{"p":"Quick Start","h":"2. Follow the four Quick Start buttons","u":"settings-quick-start.html#2-follow-the-four-quick-start-buttons","x":"Each step turns into a green check mark when it is done. Copy profile. Copies Shipready's settings profile to Assets/RareGeometryData/Shipready , so your edits never touch Shipready's own files (updates stay safe). Create and assign. Creates a starter Audio Mixer (Master, Music, SFX, Voice, UI) and connects the volume sliders to it. Route your AudioSources to its groups. Master Volume works even without a mixer. Create front end. Adds the menu prefab to the open scene, plus an EventSystem if the scene has none. One Ctrl+Z undoes it. Add Menu Scene Mode. Marks this scene as your menu scene. In "},{"p":"Quick Start","h":"3. Press Play","u":"settings-quick-start.html#3-press-play","x":"Any key leaves the title screen. Open Settings , change something and press Apply . Changes are saved and come back the next time you play. Display changes ask \"Keep these display settings?\" and undo themselves after 15 seconds if you don't confirm. Settings, Display tab"},{"p":"Quick Start","h":"4. Connect your game","u":"settings-quick-start.html#4-connect-your-game","x":"New Game raises MenuRoot.onNewGame : in the Inspector of the front end, add your scene-loading method there (or set New Game Scene to load a scene with a loading screen). Continue and Load Game raise their own events; tell the menu whether a save exists with MenuRoot.Instance.SetHasSave(true) . Want a different look? Theme tab > Use on Tactical or Storybook."},{"p":"Quick Start","h":"The guided tour","u":"settings-quick-start.html#the-guided-tour","x":"On the Home tab, Next walks through the other tabs in six short steps: Setup, Theme, Settings, Check and About."},{"p":"Quick Start","h":"Next","u":"settings-quick-start.html#next","x":"Samples show Shipready in a puzzle, a first-person and a cozy adventure game. Settings catalog explains how settings reach your game."},{"p":"Samples","h":"Samples","u":"settings-samples.html#samples","x":"Open any scene in Samples/Scenes and press Play. Each one starts on its menu; New Game starts playing in the same scene, Esc / Start pauses. Every sample uses only what ships with Shipready and works with Built-in, URP and HDRP, and with either input system. Scene Theme What it shows TryIt Clean, Tactical, Storybook The smallest setup: the front end and a spinning cube. Pause freezes time. Menu music plays from the start, and the Background button in the Make it yours panel reaches a video. The Theme button (top right), T or the gamepad's View button cycles the three looks ( Make it yours ). P"},{"p":"Using it in an existing game","h":"Using it in an existing game","u":"settings-existing-game.html#using-it-in-an-existing-game","x":"The Quick Start is the fastest route in a new project. In a game you've already built, Shipready has to share the input, cursor, pause, UI, audio and post effects your game already owns. This page says what Shipready does with each one, what you may see, and how to fix it. Shipready never takes over one of your systems unless you ask it to. Before you start: commit or back up your project, then run the menu builder's Check tab after adding the front end. It lists extra EventSystems, settings that nothing listens to yet and a missing mixer, each with a Fix button."},{"p":"Using it in an existing game","h":"Input","u":"settings-existing-game.html#input","x":"You use a PlayerInput component (one player). Nothing to do: set the Key Bindings setting's Actions to the same Input Action Asset your PlayerInput uses. A single PlayerInput uses that asset itself, so rebinds reach your player straight away. You use a generated C# class ( controls = new MyControls() ). That class makes its own copy of your actions, so the menu's rebinds wouldn't reach it on their own. Register the copy once: using RareGeometry.Shipready.Settings.Rebinding; controls = new MyControls(); InputBindings.Track(controls.asset); // follows rebinds and look sensitivity from now on // "},{"p":"Using it in an existing game","h":"Mouse cursor (first-person and third-person games)","u":"settings-existing-game.html#mouse-cursor-first-person-and-third-person-games","x":"Release Cursor In Menus (front end > Menu Root, on by default): while any menu shows, the cursor is unlocked and visible; when the menus close, it goes back exactly as your game had it. If your controller locks the cursor every frame, Shipready still wins while a menu is open (it runs after your Update), but it's cleaner to stop locking while MenuRoot.Instance.InputLocked is true. Turn the switch off when your game has its own cursor manager."},{"p":"Using it in an existing game","h":"Pause and time scale","u":"settings-existing-game.html#pause-and-time-scale","x":"Freeze sets Time.timeScale to 0 and restores the value it had. If your game changes the time scale while paused (slow motion ending, hit-stop), your newer value is kept. Your own pause menu stays yours: set Pause Mode to Custom (only the onPaused / onResumed events) or turn off the Pause screen and keep Pause Without Screen on to use Shipready's time, audio and input lock with your UI. Menus, countdowns and transitions use unscaled time, so they run while the game is frozen. Menu sounds and music ignore AudioListener.pause ."},{"p":"Using it in an existing game","h":"Your UI and EventSystem","u":"settings-existing-game.html#your-ui-and-eventsystem","x":"EventSystem: if your scene has one, Shipready uses it and doesn't add another. It only creates one when there is none. The Check tab finds duplicates and wrong input modules. Draw order: the front end is a Screen Space Overlay canvas with Sort Order 100 . If your HUD or dialogs use a higher order, raise the front end's Canvas > Sort Order so menus draw above them. Keep your own menus: put Menu Button components on your own buttons to open Shipready's Settings or Credits, pause, resume or go back to the main menu ( MenuRoot.Instance.OpenScreen(\"...\") opens any screen by name), and turn off Ship"},{"p":"Using it in an existing game","h":"Saves","u":"settings-existing-game.html#saves","x":"Continue, Load Game and Save Game follow your save system once you set MenuRoot.Instance.SaveBridge (see Extending and API ). Without one, the buttons raise onNewGame , onContinue and onLoadGame and Continue shows after SetHasSave(true) . Settings are stored apart from your saves (or in your own storage through ISettingsStorage )."},{"p":"Using it in an existing game","h":"Audio","u":"settings-existing-game.html#audio","x":"No mixer: Master Volume works through AudioListener.volume ; the other volume sliders stay hidden until a mixer is assigned. Your own mixer: expose each group's volume and type its exact parameter name into the matching volume setting. A wrong name logs a warning that names the setting and the mixer. Your AudioSources must output to the right mixer groups for Music, Effects and Voice to change anything."},{"p":"Using it in an existing game","h":"Post effects and graphics (URP, HDRP, Post Processing v2)","u":"settings-existing-game.html#post-effects-and-graphics-urp-hdrp-post-processing-v2","x":"Your Volumes and profiles are never edited. Shipready adds its own global Volume (priority 1000, Default layer) with a runtime profile. When an effect is On , your own Volumes stay in charge; only Off adds an override. Brightness, while it isn't at 0, sets Post Exposure (and nothing else of your Color Adjustments). If your camera's Volume Mask leaves out the Default layer, Shipready's post settings can't reach that camera: add Default to the mask, or hide those settings. Your URP asset file is never changed; render scale, MSAA, HDR and shadows go to a runtime copy."},{"p":"Using it in an existing game","h":"Scenes","u":"settings-existing-game.html#scenes","x":"One persistent front end: it lives across scene loads (Don't Destroy On Load). A second front end in a later scene removes itself, so you can put one in every scene you test from. Bootstrap scene: put the front end in your bootstrap scene, then mark each scene with Menu Scene Mode (Front End, Gameplay or Hidden). Additive scenes: put Menu Scene Mode only in the scene that decides the mode, not in every additive scene. Your own scene loading (Addressables, a scene manager): leave New Game Scene empty and load from the onNewGame event, then call MenuRoot.Instance.EnterGameplay() . One-scene game"},{"p":"Using it in an existing game","h":"Multiplayer","u":"settings-existing-game.html#multiplayer","x":"Settings stay on each player's machine. Use Overlay Only pause so a networked game keeps running, and turn off Pause On Controller Disconnect for online games. The input lock is one switch for the whole machine; for split screen, decide per player in your own input code."},{"p":"Using it in an existing game","h":"Five-minute check","u":"settings-existing-game.html#five-minute-check","x":"Press Play in a level scene and press Esc / Start: the pause menu opens, the cursor is free, the game stops. Rebind Jump, Apply, resume: the new key jumps. Stop and start Play again: it's still rebound. Move Music Volume: the music changes. Turn Bloom off: it's gone; turn it on: your own look is back. Resume: the cursor locks again and your controls work. Something different? Kit window > About > Copy system info , and post it in Support ."},{"p":"Make it yours","h":"Make it yours","u":"settings-make-it-yours.html#make-it-yours","x":"Your players should see your game, not a menu kit. Everything the player sees, hears or feels in the front end can be changed or switched off, without code: the look (a theme ), where things go (a layout ), sizes and control styles, how messages and prompts show, the backgrounds and what plays behind the menu, which buttons and settings show, your title and logo. Start from a preset, then change only what you want. Every change shows live on the menu in the open scene, in edit mode and in Play mode. Before: Clean After: Storybook"},{"p":"Make it yours","h":"1. Pick a look and a layout (one click each)","u":"settings-make-it-yours.html#1-pick-a-look-and-a-layout-one-click-each","x":"Themes (the look): Clean, Tactical and Storybook, each with a high-contrast version. Layouts (where things go): Classic Center, Side Column, Bottom Bar and Fullscreen Tabs. Any layout works with any theme. In the editor: Tools > Shipready > Menus > Menu Builder > Theme tab: Use next to a theme, and Use next to a layout further down. Ctrl+Z undoes either. In the demo: open Samples/Scenes/TryIt , press Play, and click Theme and Layout in the top right corner (or press T and L; the gamepad's View button cycles themes). In your game: a Theme Cycler or Layout Cycler component on any Button lets pla"},{"p":"Make it yours","h":"2. Make your own theme and layout","u":"settings-make-it-yours.html#2-make-your-own-theme-and-layout","x":"Never edit Shipready's own assets (updates would replace them). Make a copy instead: Theme tab > Make my own next to any theme or layout, or Assets > Create > Rare Geometry > Settings Menu > Theme From Preset (or Layout From Preset ). Copies go to Assets/RareGeometryData/Shipready/Themes and .../Layouts , are used by the menu in the open scene, and are selected so you can edit them in the Inspector."},{"p":"Make it yours","h":"What a theme controls","u":"settings-make-it-yours.html#what-a-theme-controls","x":"Group Settings Font Font, Heading Font , title/heading/body/small sizes Colors background, scrim (behind dialogs), panel, text, muted text, accent, the five button states, slider track, focus frame. Alpha 0 hides a part Sprites panel, control, focus frame, track, handle, check mark (9-sliced when they have borders) Control styles Button Shape (Theme Sprite, Rectangle, Rounded, Pill, Outline, Underline, Text Only), Button Sprite , Choice Style (Arrows or Dropdown), Toggle Style (Checkbox or Switch), Show Tab Indicator , Show Scrollbar , Scrollbar Width Control sizes (0 keeps the prefab's) menu "},{"p":"Make it yours","h":"What a layout controls","u":"settings-make-it-yours.html#what-a-layout-controls","x":"Part Settings Main menu, pause, settings, credits, dialogs Horizontal (Left, Center, Right, Stretch), Vertical (Top, Middle, Bottom, Stretch), Width and Height (0 keeps the prefab's), Margin , Offset , Column (buttons in a column or a row), Content Alignment Title screen, loading screen where their texts sit (left, center, right; top, middle, bottom) and the margins Subtitles where the subtitle box sits Settings Settings Tabs On Side (a list at the side instead of tabs on top) and its width Screen scaling Reference Resolution and Match Width Or Height Other screen shapes. Menu Root (or menu bu"},{"p":"Make it yours","h":"3. Switch off what you don't need","u":"settings-make-it-yours.html#3-switch-off-what-you-dont-need","x":"Most of these are in one place: menu builder > Setup tab > Options for each screen . Each screen has its own foldout there, including its buttons, its words and the menu music. Part Where Switch Whole screens (title, main menu, settings, credits, pause, loading, controller prompt) front end (Menu Root) > Features buttons leading to a removed screen hide Any main or pause menu button the menu's Menu Button List (on its Buttons object) untick Visible \"Press any key\" prompt Title Screen Show Prompt , Prompt Animation (Blink, Pulse, None), Blink Seconds (0: still) Continue, Load Game, Quit / Main "},{"p":"Make it yours","h":"4. Order, rename and add menu buttons","u":"settings-make-it-yours.html#4-order-rename-and-add-menu-buttons","x":"Select the main menu's (or pause menu's) Buttons object: its Menu Button List shows the buttons in the order the player sees them. Drag entries to reorder, type a Label (your words, or a string-table key to translate it), give an Icon , untick Visible to hide. To add a button, add an entry without a Button and pick its Action (Open Settings, Open Credits, Quit, and so on): it looks like the others and is navigated like them."},{"p":"Make it yours","h":"5. Your title and logo","u":"settings-make-it-yours.html#5-your-title-and-logo","x":"On the front end ( Menu Root > Your Game ), or the menu builder's Theme tab: Setting What it does Game Title your title (or a string-table key, to translate it) Show Game Title off when your logo already has the name in it Logo your logo picture (empty: the title text only) Logo Placement Above Title , Replaces Title (the text comes back if you clear the logo) or Below Title Logo Height its height in canvas units Logo Max Width the widest it may be (0: no limit); a wide logo shrinks to fit, keeping its shape Logo Keep Aspect keep the logo's own shape (off: stretched to the height and widest wi"},{"p":"Make it yours","h":"6. Messages, prompts and holds: the style pickers","u":"settings-make-it-yours.html#6-messages-prompts-and-holds-the-style-pickers","x":"The first thing in the Menu Root inspector is Style (changes the menu at once) . Each picker's first choice is \"Theme (its value)\"; any other choice is this front end's own. Picker Choices Display As (notices, questions, warnings, the controller prompt) Full Text, Short Text, Icon Only, Icon In Color (the icon in its context color), Icon And Short Text, None Prompt Style (\"Press any key\", the button hint bar) Full, Short, Key Only, Icon Only, None Hold Fill (hold to skip the splash, hold to Reset All, Hold To Confirm) Ring, Key Fills, Bar, Radial, Panel, Sprite, None Message Position Bottom Ce"},{"p":"Make it yours","h":"7. Backgrounds and see-through","u":"settings-make-it-yours.html#7-backgrounds-and-see-through","x":"What fills the screen behind each menu. A theme has one background per screen type: Main Menu (title screen, main menu, credits, extras), Pause , Settings and Pop-up (questions and messages over another screen). The front end can override each one ( Menu Root > Backgrounds and see-through , or the menu builder's Theme tab). One screen can also have its own: tick Override Style on its Menu Background component. Mode What it shows Its settings None nothing: the game or scene as it is (clicks still stop at the menu) Solid Color the theme's background color, or your own Use Theme Color , Color Gra"},{"p":"Make it yours","h":"8. Live scenes behind the menu: the Menu Camera","u":"settings-make-it-yours.html#8-live-scenes-behind-the-menu-the-menu-camera","x":"Theme tab > Add Menu Camera adds a Menu Camera Director with three shots taken from where the Scene view looks. Move the Shot objects to frame your scene. Setting Choices Mode Scene Camera (one camera in your scene), Shots (points that cut or crossfade on a timer, in order or at random, each with its seconds, field of view and a slow drift), Orbit (circles a target, with a gentle dolly), Render Texture (your own camera draws into a texture) Active When Front End, Front End And Pause, Live Background Only, Manual (your code calls Activate / Deactivate) Backdrop Scene a scene loaded additively b"},{"p":"Make it yours","h":"9. Try it while playing","u":"settings-make-it-yours.html#9-try-it-while-playing","x":"Open Samples/Scenes/TryIt and press Play. A sample logo sits above the title and three camera shots crossfade behind the main menu. Make it yours (top right) opens a panel that switches, while playing: the theme and the layout; Display As, the prompt style, the hold fill and the message position; the background, the opacity and the title (text, logo and text, logo only); Hold To Confirm. It can also show a sample notice and ask a sample question. Add a Style Cycler to your own button to give players any of these choices."},{"p":"Make it yours","h":"10. Change one element only","u":"settings-make-it-yours.html#10-change-one-element-only","x":"Add a Theme Override component to any image or text in the front end: a different color , sprite , font or font size , or Hide it. Overrides always win over the theme; untick one and the theme's look comes back. Move or scale one element. Select it in the Hierarchy (a button, the settings footer, the tabs, anything inside the front end), then menu builder > Setup > Options for each screen > Move or scale the selected element . It gets an Element Placement with Move By and Scale . It stays put when lists rearrange, and removing the component puts it back. The title and logo have their own Title"},{"p":"Make it yours","h":"11. Move, resize or add anything: your own front end","u":"settings-make-it-yours.html#11-move-resize-or-add-anything-your-own-front-end","x":"Setup tab > Make my own front end (or Tools > Shipready > Menus > Make My Own Front End) makes a linked copy of the whole front end (a Prefab Variant) in Assets/RareGeometryData/Shipready/FrontEnd and uses it in the open scene. Open it and change anything: move or resize panels, add images, reorder, delete. When you update Shipready, your copy keeps every change you made and still gets Shipready's improvements for everything you didn't change."},{"p":"Make it yours","h":"12. Change the words","u":"settings-make-it-yours.html#12-change-the-words","x":"Every text goes through the string tables ( Content/Localization/English.asset , plus your own languages; see Languages )."},{"p":"Make it yours","h":"13. Bring your own UI","u":"settings-make-it-yours.html#13-bring-your-own-ui","x":"Use your own panels and keep Shipready's settings, saving and safety nets: Turn Shipready's screen off in Features (for example Main Menu). Put a Menu Button component on each of your buttons and pick its action. Quit and Main Menu ask for confirmation unless you untick Confirm . Your own pause screen: turn Pause off in Features, tick Pause Without Screen on the front end, and show your panel from its On Paused event (hide it on On Resumed ). Time, audio and the input lock still work. For code: MenuRoot.Instance has the same actions ( NewGame() , OpenSettings() , Pause() , SetTheme() , SetLayo"},{"p":"Screens","h":"Screens","u":"settings-screens.html#screens","x":"The front end is one prefab ( Content/Prefabs/FrontEnd.prefab ) with every screen. One instance survives scene loads, and there is always exactly one EventSystem. Turn screens you don't need off in the menu builder ( Setup tab > Screens ): they are removed when the game starts, and the buttons leading to them hide. Every screen's options are in the same tab, under Options for each screen . Screen What it does Title Your game's name and \"Press any key\", which can blink, pulse or stay still ( Prompt Animation ). Main menu Continue (only when a save exists), New Game, Load Game, Settings, Credits"},{"p":"Screens","h":"Startup screens (off until you switch them on)","u":"settings-screens.html#startup-screens-off-until-you-switch-them-on","x":"Kit window > Setup > Startup screens and extras (or the Startup Sequence component on the front end). They show before the title screen, in this order: Screen What it does Shows Splash logos ( SplashScreen > Logos) Your logos, faded in and out; any button skips. every launch Health / photosensitivity warning ( HealthWarningScreen ) A warning the player reads (Continue works after 3 seconds). Edit the text. every launch License / consent ( ConsentScreen ) Your license or privacy notice with Accept and Decline. Decline quits (or raises On Declined when Quit On Decline is off). until accepted Fir"},{"p":"Screens","h":"Extras, messages and settings pages","u":"settings-screens.html#extras-messages-and-settings-pages","x":"Extras (main menu button, off by default): ExtrasScreen > Entries, each a label, Unlocked and an On Chosen event (open your gallery, jukebox or level select). extras.SetUnlocked(\"Secret Level\", true) unlocks one. Messages: MenuRoot.Instance.ShowMessage(\"Saved\", \"Your game was saved.\") shows the MessageScreen (title, text, up to two buttons and callbacks). Settings pages: Calibrate Brightness (Graphics tab: a test picture and the Brightness slider; shown where Brightness works) and Screen Edges (Display tab, desktop and console: corner marks and a slider that moves the menus in from the edges o"},{"p":"Screens","h":"Saves: Continue, Load Game, Save Game","u":"settings-screens.html#saves-continue-load-game-save-game","x":"Set MenuRoot.Instance.SaveBridge to your save system ( ISaveGameBridge : HasSave, Continue, OpenLoad, OpenSave, IsOpen, HandleCancel). Continue and Load Game then follow its saves, and the pause menu gets a Save Game button. With the Save module in the project, the menu builder's Check tab offers Enable save system bridge (it adds the SHIPREADY_SETTINGS_SAVE define): Continue loads the newest manual, auto or quick save; Load Game and Save Game open the Save Menu above the front end (add its SaveMenu prefab to your scene). Without a bridge the menu raises its UnityEvents as before."},{"p":"Screens","h":"Settings screen options","u":"settings-screens.html#settings-screen-options","x":"On the Settings Screen component (FrontEnd > SettingsScreen): Show Search (on): a \"Search settings\" box above the tabs finds settings in every tab by name or help text, in the player's language. While searching, Reset Category resets the settings found. Show Reset Buttons (on): a small Reset button on each setting puts that one setting back to its default (mouse and touch; it's greyed out when there is nothing to reset). From code: SettingsRuntime.Current.ResetToDefault(\"audio.music-volume\") . Show Help Text : the help line (and the setting's Help Image, when it has one)."},{"p":"Screens","h":"Notices","u":"settings-screens.html#notices","x":"The front end shows short messages at the bottom of the screen ( Toast ): after Apply when a changed setting needs a restart, and whenever you call MenuRoot.Instance.ShowToast(\"Settings imported\") . Delete the Toast object to have none."},{"p":"Screens","h":"Scene modes","u":"settings-screens.html#scene-modes","x":"Put a Menu Scene Mode component in each scene: Front End (menu scenes): the menu shows on load, optionally with the title screen. Gameplay (levels): the menu is hidden; Esc / Start pauses. Hidden : the menu stays out of the way (cutscenes, loading levels)."},{"p":"Screens","h":"Navigation","u":"settings-screens.html#navigation","x":"Keyboard, mouse and gamepad work everywhere. Focus is always visible and comes back to the button you left when you go back. Back is Esc or the gamepad's east button."},{"p":"Screens","h":"Phones and tablets","u":"settings-screens.html#phones-and-tablets","x":"On touch devices the whole menu is 1.25 times bigger so every control is finger-sized (Menu Root > Touch Scale ), the title screen says \"Tap to start\" (Title Screen > Touch Prompt ), menus stay inside the safe area (notches, rounded corners), and nothing needs a mouse pointer hovering: a tap selects, and the help line follows the selection. Settings that only make sense on a desktop (Cursor Size, Mute In Background, Monitor, Screen Edges) are hidden there. Android builds are tested (IL2CPP ARM64 and Mono)."},{"p":"Screens","h":"Text","u":"settings-screens.html#text","x":"Every text can be translated ( Languages ). Texts shrink to fit long translations instead of being cut off; the game title may take two lines."},{"p":"Settings catalog","h":"Settings catalog","u":"settings-settings-catalog.html#settings-catalog","x":"A Settings Profile lists the tabs (categories) and settings your menu shows. Shipready ships a profile with the full launch catalog; add, remove, reorder or create settings in the menu builder's Settings tab."},{"p":"Settings catalog","h":"Tiers: how a setting reaches your game","u":"settings-settings-catalog.html#tiers-how-a-setting-reaches-your-game","x":"Tier Meaning Examples Auto Shipready applies it for you. Nothing to do. Resolution, VSync, quality, volumes, post effects, menu size, language Connect It drives something in your scene: a component (for example Camera Field Of View Target ), a Setting Event Binder with UnityEvents, or your callback. Field of view, your own custom settings Read Shipready stores it; your code reads it when it needs it. Difficulty, camera shake, vibration, hold or toggle The badge next to each setting in the window shows its tier."},{"p":"Settings catalog","h":"Tabs in the default profile","u":"settings-settings-catalog.html#tabs-in-the-default-profile","x":"Display, Graphics, Audio, Controls, Accessibility, Gameplay. Settings that need something the project doesn't have (a render pipeline, the Input System, a mixer, a second monitor) are hidden automatically, with the reason shown in the catalog. See the Settings reference for every setting."},{"p":"Settings catalog","h":"Add a setting from the catalog","u":"settings-settings-catalog.html#add-a-setting-from-the-catalog","x":"Settings tab > Catalog : search, then Add . Shipready copies the setting next to your profile (your copy, safe from updates). Remove takes it out again. Shipready's own profile can't be edited: make an editable copy first (Home tab, step 1)."},{"p":"Settings catalog","h":"Optional settings","u":"settings-settings-catalog.html#optional-settings","x":"Content/Settings/Optional/OptionalSettings holds settings many games want but not every game can use, so they are not in the default menu. Add the ones your game supports in the menu builder: Settings tab > Add optional settings (check boxes, then Add checked ). You can also find them in the Catalog , or add the whole profile to your profile's Includes . Setting Id Tier Settings Preset gameplay.settings-preset Read (picking one sets every value it lists: Comfort, Clean Image) Sound Captions accessibility.sound-captions Auto (with SubtitleDisplay.ShowCaption ) Reduce Flashing accessibility.redu"},{"p":"Settings catalog","h":"How your game uses each one","u":"settings-settings-catalog.html#how-your-game-uses-each-one","x":"In the menu builder: Settings tab > Add optional settings : check the ones your game supports, then Add checked . Without code: select a GameObject, Settings tab > Custom setting > Add event binder to selection , type the setting's id, and wire its UnityEvent (float, int, bool or string) to your script. It fires at startup and whenever the player applies settings. In C# (read when you need the value, or listen once): using RareGeometry.Shipready.Settings; var settings = SettingsRuntime.Current; // Settings Preset: nothing to do. Picking Comfort or Clean Image sets the values the preset lists; "},{"p":"Settings catalog","h":"Per-setting options","u":"settings-settings-catalog.html#per-setting-options","x":"Every setting asset has these fields (Inspector), all optional: Field What it does Hide In Menu Keeps the setting working but doesn't show it. Show When Id / Show When Values Shows it only while another setting has one of these values (comma-separated). Shipready uses it for Vibration Strength (Vibration on), the subtitle options (Subtitles on), Aim Assist Strength and Crosshair Color. Platforms Desktop, Mobile, Console, Web. Elsewhere the setting is hidden and not applied (Cursor Size and Mute In Background: desktop and web; Monitor: desktop). Requires Restart After Apply, a short notice says"},{"p":"Settings catalog","h":"Presets: many settings at once","u":"settings-settings-catalog.html#presets-many-settings-at-once","x":"A Settings Preset (Create > Rare Geometry > Settings Menu > Settings Preset) lists setting ids and values. Put presets in a Settings Preset Choice setting (the optional gameplay.settings-preset has Comfort and Clean Image) and players pick one in the menu; or call SettingsRuntime.Current.ApplyPreset(preset) and then Apply() . Ids your profile doesn't have are skipped."},{"p":"Settings catalog","h":"Create your own setting","u":"settings-settings-catalog.html#create-your-own-setting","x":"Settings tab > Custom setting (with UnityEvents) : choose Toggle, Slider, Whole number or Choice, give it an id (for example gameplay.hints ), a label and a tab, and click Create and add to profile . Then select a GameObject and click Add event binder to selection : its UnityEvents fire with the new value whenever the player applies settings."},{"p":"Settings catalog","h":"Needs connection","u":"settings-settings-catalog.html#needs-connection","x":"The Check tab lists Connect settings that nothing in the open scene listens to yet (for example Field of View without a camera target), each with a one-click fix."},{"p":"Settings reference","h":"Settings reference","u":"settings-settings-reference.html#settings-reference","x":"Every setting in the default profile, by tab. Tier : Auto = Shipready applies it; Connect = it drives something in your scene (a component or your callback); Read = Shipready stores it and your code reads it. Ids are what your code uses ( SettingsRuntime.Current.GetValue(\"id\") ) and what the settings file stores. Settings that need a package or a render pipeline are hidden where they can't work. This page is generated from the shipped settings."},{"p":"Settings reference","h":"Display","u":"settings-settings-reference.html#display","x":"Setting Id Tier Default Values What it does Resolution display.resolution Auto the game's launch value what the player's screen supports Screen resolution. Refresh Rate display.refresh-rate Auto the game's launch value what the player's screen supports Refresh rate for the chosen resolution (exclusive full screen). Window Mode display.window-mode Auto the game's launch value Fullscreen, Borderless, Windowed Full screen, borderless or windowed. Monitor display.monitor Auto the game's launch value what the player's screen supports Which monitor shows the game. Only shown when more than one is co"},{"p":"Settings reference","h":"Graphics","u":"settings-settings-reference.html#graphics","x":"Setting Id Tier Default Values What it does Quality Preset graphics.quality Auto the game's launch value your project's quality levels Overall graphics quality. The options below can override parts of it. Texture Quality graphics.texture-quality Auto Preset Preset, Full, Half, Quarter, Eighth Texture resolution. Lower values use less video memory. Anisotropic Filtering graphics.anisotropic Auto Preset Preset, Off, Per texture, Forced on Sharpens textures seen at steep angles. Shadow Distance graphics.shadow-distance Auto Preset Preset, 25 m, 50 m, 100 m, 150 m, 200 m How far away shadows are d"},{"p":"Settings reference","h":"Audio","u":"settings-settings-reference.html#audio","x":"Setting Id Tier Default Values What it does Master Volume audio.master-volume Auto 100% 0% to 100% Overall volume. Music audio.music-volume Auto 100% 0% to 100% Volume of music. Sound Effects audio.sfx-volume Auto 100% 0% to 100% Volume of sound effects. Voice audio.voice-volume Auto 100% 0% to 100% Volume of voice. Interface audio.ui-volume Auto 100% 0% to 100% Volume of interface. Ambience audio.ambient-volume Auto 100% 0% to 100% Volume of ambience. Mute In Background audio.mute-in-background Auto Off On, Off Silences the game while its window isn't in focus. Speaker Setup audio.speaker-mod"},{"p":"Settings reference","h":"Controls","u":"settings-settings-reference.html#controls","x":"Setting Id Tier Default Values What it does Mouse Sensitivity controls.mouse-sensitivity Auto 1.0 0.1 to 5.0 How fast the camera turns. Controller Sensitivity controls.gamepad-sensitivity Auto 1.0 0.1 to 5.0 How fast the camera turns. Invert Horizontal Look controls.invert-x Auto Off On, Off Reverses the look direction on this axis. Invert Vertical Look controls.invert-y Auto Off On, Off Reverses the look direction on this axis. Vibration controls.vibration Read On On, Off Controller vibration. Vibration Strength controls.vibration-strength Read 100% 0% to 100% How strong controller vibration "},{"p":"Settings reference","h":"Accessibility","u":"settings-settings-reference.html#accessibility","x":"Setting Id Tier Default Values What it does Menu Size accessibility.ui-scale Auto 100% 75% to 150% Makes the whole menu bigger or smaller. Text Size accessibility.text-size Auto 100% 80% to 160% Makes menu text bigger or smaller. High Contrast accessibility.high-contrast Auto Off On, Off Stronger colors and outlines for easier reading. Reduce Motion accessibility.reduced-motion Auto Off On, Off Turns off menu animations and camera shake. Focus Outline accessibility.focus-indicator Auto Normal Thin, Normal, Thick How thick the outline around the selected item is. Sprint accessibility.sprint-mod"},{"p":"Settings reference","h":"Gameplay","u":"settings-settings-reference.html#gameplay","x":"Setting Id Tier Default Values What it does Language gameplay.language Auto English English, Pseudo Language of the menus. Difficulty gameplay.difficulty Read Normal Easy, Normal, Hard How challenging the game is. Your game reads it (Read tier). Field of View gameplay.field-of-view Connect 70 50 to 110 Camera field of view in degrees. Camera Shake gameplay.camera-shake Read 100% 0% to 100% Strength of camera shake effects."},{"p":"Display safety","h":"Display safety","u":"settings-display-safety.html#display-safety","x":"Changing resolution, refresh rate, window mode or monitor can leave a player with a black screen. Shipready protects them: Confirmation: after a display change, \"Keep these display settings?\" counts down from 15 seconds. Without Keep , the previous mode comes back by itself. Crash recovery: Shipready notes the pending change in the settings file before applying it. If the game crashes or is closed during the countdown, the next start restores the previous mode. First launch: the defaults are the mode the game started in, so nothing changes until the player chooses. Display change confirmation"},{"p":"Display safety","h":"The settings file","u":"settings-display-safety.html#the-settings-file","x":"Saved as settings.json in Application.persistentDataPath (versioned JSON). Written safely: a temporary file is written first, and the previous file is kept as settings.json.bak . A damaged file is replaced by the backup or the defaults; the damaged copy is kept as settings.json.corrupt and one warning is logged. Start the game with -reset-settings to delete the saved settings (useful for support). Menu Root > Settings Storage chooses where settings are saved: Auto (the file; PlayerPrefs on WebGL), File or PlayerPrefs (the same JSON under one key, with a backup key). For a cloud save or a conso"},{"p":"Display safety","h":"Other details","u":"settings-display-safety.html#other-details","x":"Refresh rates are offered per resolution; a resolution change re-checks the refresh rate. Exclusive full screen is offered where the platform supports it. Monitor selection appears only on Windows with two or more monitors."},{"p":"Audio","h":"Audio","u":"settings-audio.html#audio","x":""},{"p":"Audio","h":"Volumes","u":"settings-audio.html#volumes","x":"Setting Needs a mixer? Master Volume No. Without a mixer it controls AudioListener.volume . Music, Sound Effects, Voice, Interface, Ambience Yes: each controls an exposed parameter of your Audio Mixer. Mute In Background No. Silences the game while its window isn't in focus (off by default). Speaker Setup (automatic, mono, stereo, quad, 5.1, 7.1) No. Changes Unity's speaker mode; changing it restarts audio, so sounds playing at that moment stop. Automatic keeps your project's setting. Night Mode (optional, Read) Your game lowers its dynamic range when audio.night-mode is on (for example a comp"},{"p":"Audio","h":"The starter mixer","u":"settings-audio.html#the-starter-mixer","x":"Home tab, step 2 (or Setup tab > Create starter mixer ) copies a mixer with the groups Master, Music, SFX, Voice, UI and Ambient and the exposed parameters MasterVolume , MusicVolume , SfxVolume , VoiceVolume , UiVolume and AmbientVolume , then assigns it. Route your AudioSources to the matching groups. To use your own mixer, expose each group's volume (right-click the Volume field > Expose) and type the parameter name into the volume setting. Sliders map linearly to decibels the way players expect (0% is silent)."},{"p":"Audio","h":"Menu sounds","u":"settings-audio.html#menu-sounds","x":"Each theme has move, submit, back, open, slider, toggle and error sounds (Kenney Interface Sounds, CC0). They play through the front end's UI Sound Player ; set its output to your UI mixer group so the Interface volume controls them. Menu sounds keep playing while the game is paused."},{"p":"Audio","h":"Menu music","u":"settings-audio.html#menu-music","x":"Kit window > Setup > Options for each screen > Menu music (the Menu Music component on the front end). Give the main menu, pause, settings, credits and loading screen a track each. Leave Settings or Credits empty and what's playing carries on; leave the others empty for silence there. Tracks crossfade ( Fade Seconds , 0 cuts), stop when no menu shows so your game's music takes over, and keep playing while the game is paused. Output is the mixer's Music group, so the Music Volume setting controls them. The demos' track is Samples/Art/DemoMenuMusic.wav ."},{"p":"Audio","h":"Pause","u":"settings-audio.html#pause","x":"In Freeze pause mode Shipready pauses AudioListener (game audio stops) and restores it exactly on resume. See Pause and multiplayer hooks ."},{"p":"Graphics","h":"Graphics","u":"settings-graphics.html#graphics","x":"Graphics settings follow the render pipeline your project uses. Settings a pipeline can't use are hidden there automatically. Setting Built-in URP HDRP Quality preset Yes Yes Yes Texture quality, anisotropic filtering, shadow distance, level of detail Yes Yes Yes VSync, frame rate limit Yes Yes Yes MSAA, shadow quality, shadow resolution Yes URP's own versions No Shadow cascades (1, 2, 4) Yes Yes No Lens flare (on/off) No Yes No Color blind filter (Accessibility tab) Yes Yes Yes Render scale, upscaling filter, HDR No Yes No Post anti-aliasing (FXAA, SMAA, TAA) No Yes No Bloom, motion blur, dep"},{"p":"Graphics","h":"Preset or override","u":"settings-graphics.html#preset-or-override","x":"Each detail (texture quality, shadows ...) is Preset by default: it follows the chosen quality preset. Picking a value overrides the preset for that detail, and the override stays when the player changes the preset."},{"p":"Graphics","h":"Your project's assets are never changed","u":"settings-graphics.html#your-projects-assets-are-never-changed","x":"URP: Shipready applies render scale, MSAA, HDR, upscaling and shadow resolution to a runtime copy of your active URP asset; your asset file is untouched (a test checks the files are byte-identical before and after). Post effects and brightness: a kit-owned global Volume (priority 1000) with a runtime profile. \"On\" leaves your own volumes in charge; only \"Off\" adds an override. In the Editor , every quality level and frame rate Shipready changed is put back when you leave Play mode. Graphics settings, Tactical theme"},{"p":"Controls and rebinding","h":"Controls and rebinding","u":"settings-controls-and-rebinding.html#controls-and-rebinding","x":""},{"p":"Controls and rebinding","h":"Without the Input System package","u":"settings-controls-and-rebinding.html#without-the-input-system-package","x":"The menus work with the old Input Manager (keyboard and gamepad navigation, Esc / Start to pause). Mouse and controller sensitivity, invert and vibration are stored settings (Read tier) your input code reads. Key rebinding and controller prompts need the Input System package."},{"p":"Controls and rebinding","h":"With the Input System package","u":"settings-controls-and-rebinding.html#with-the-input-system-package","x":"Rebinding, controller prompts and the look settings switch on by themselves. In the menu builder's Settings tab, click the Key Bindings setting of your profile copy (or add it from the catalog) to select it. Set Actions to your Input Action Asset, and Action Maps to the maps players may rebind (for example Player ). Optional: Include Actions to show only some actions. Protected Actions are never offered and never swapped away, so players can't lose menu controls (Submit, Cancel, Navigate, Pause by default). Look Actions : your look action(s). Mouse / Controller Sensitivity and Invert X / Y are"},{"p":"Controls and rebinding","h":"When your game uses a copy of the actions","u":"settings-controls-and-rebinding.html#when-your-game-uses-a-copy-of-the-actions","x":"The menu rebinds the asset set in Actions . A single PlayerInput uses that asset itself, so nothing else is needed. Two cases use a copy, and the copy must be registered so it follows the rebinds and look settings: A generated C# input class: after controls = new MyControls(); call InputBindings.Track(controls.asset); (namespace RareGeometry.Shipready.Settings.Rebinding ). Local multiplayer with PlayerInputManager: add Player Input Bindings Sync next to each PlayerInput. Symptom when this is missing: the new key shows in the menu, but the character still uses the old one. More in Using it in a"},{"p":"Controls and rebinding","h":"Controller prompts","u":"settings-controls-and-rebinding.html#controller-prompts","x":"Bindings show button pictures for keyboard and mouse, Xbox, PlayStation and Switch controllers (Kenney Input Prompts, CC0), and switch to the device the player used last. Generic controllers show the stick pictures and text for face buttons. The Glyph Map asset ( InputSystem/Content/DefaultGlyphMap ) maps control names to sprites: replace any sprite with your own. The Button Prompts setting (Controls tab) lets players pick one set for every controller (Xbox, PlayStation, Switch or generic) instead of following the device; read DisplayExtras.PromptStyle for your own HUD prompts. Vertical Sensit"},{"p":"Controls and rebinding","h":"Vibration","u":"settings-controls-and-rebinding.html#vibration","x":"ControllerRumble.Play(low, high, seconds, this) vibrates the current gamepad, scaled by the Vibration and Vibration Strength settings."},{"p":"Controls and rebinding","h":"Pause action","u":"settings-controls-and-rebinding.html#pause-action","x":"The Input System front end uses Esc / gamepad Start for pause, or your own action: Setup tab > 3. Pause action ."},{"p":"Themes","h":"Themes","u":"settings-themes.html#themes","x":"One Theme asset styles the whole front end: colors, font, text sizes and style (uppercase, letter spacing), sizes and spacing, sprites (panels, controls, focus frame, slider, toggle), screen transition (fade, slide up, scale), focus animation and UI sounds. It also sets how messages, prompts and hold fills show (Display As, prompt style, hold fill, message position, the message icons), the background of each screen type (main menu, pause, settings, pop-ups) and how see-through panels, the dim and buttons are. See Make it yours for every option. Theme Look For Clean Neutral dark, rounded, blue "},{"p":"Themes","h":"Switch themes","u":"settings-themes.html#switch-themes","x":"Tools > Shipready > Menus > Menu Builder > Theme : click Use on a theme. The front end in the open scene restyles immediately (undoable). Tick Preview high contrast to see the variant. At runtime: MenuRoot.Instance.SetTheme(theme) ."},{"p":"Themes","h":"Make your own","u":"settings-themes.html#make-your-own","x":"Duplicate a theme asset and change it, or create one with Create > Rare Geometry > Settings Menu > Theme. Sprites are white and tinted by the theme colors; 9-slice borders keep corners crisp at any size. To support High Contrast, duplicate your theme as its variant, set strong colors (text at least 4.5:1 against panels and controls) and assign it to High Contrast Variant ."},{"p":"Accessibility","h":"Accessibility","u":"settings-accessibility.html#accessibility","x":"The default profile has an Accessibility tab. Every option works in the menu with no code; the ones your game needs to react to are available from code too. Setting What the menu does What your game can do Menu Size (75% to 150%) Scales the whole menu canvas. Read AccessibilityState.UiScale for your own HUD. Text Size (80% to 160%) Scales every menu text. Read AccessibilityState.TextScale . High Contrast Switches to the theme's high-contrast variant (text contrast at least 4.5:1, checked by a test). Read AccessibilityState.HighContrast . Reduce Motion No screen fades, slides or focus zoom; ins"},{"p":"Accessibility","h":"Subtitles","u":"settings-accessibility.html#subtitles","x":"Drag Content/Prefabs/Subtitles.prefab into your first scene (or a scene that stays loaded). From your dialogue code: SubtitleDisplay.Show(\"Where is everyone?\", 3f, \"Mara\"); Lines queue and play in order, in unscaled time (they keep going while paused). The player's Subtitles, Subtitle Size, Subtitle Background, Subtitle Color, Speaker Name Color and Readable Font settings apply automatically. Sound captions: SubtitleDisplay.ShowCaption(\"thunder rumbles\"); (shown only when the player turned Sound Captions on)."},{"p":"Accessibility","h":"Color blind filter","u":"settings-accessibility.html#color-blind-filter","x":"The filter corrects the whole picture (\"daltonization\"): it works out which color differences a player with that kind of color blindness loses, and moves them into colors they can see. Strength blends from no change (0%) to the full correction (100%). Built-in pipeline: a command buffer after the image effects of every game camera, added only while the filter is on. Nothing is added to your scenes. URP: a Channel Mixer on Shipready's own global Volume (priority 1000). Your volumes and URP assets are not changed. HDRP: the same, through Shipready's HDRP integration (it compiles only when HDRP i"},{"p":"Accessibility","h":"Keyboard, controller and mouse","u":"settings-accessibility.html#keyboard-controller-and-mouse","x":"Every screen is fully usable with a keyboard or a controller; focus is always visible and is restored when you go back. Rebinding protects the menu controls, so a player can't lock themselves out."},{"p":"Accessibility","h":"Menu narration (a hook for text-to-speech)","u":"settings-accessibility.html#menu-narration-a-hook-for-text-to-speech","x":"The optional Menu Narration setting ( accessibility.narration ) turns on MenuNarration : the menu sends each screen's title and what the player focuses (a button's label; a setting's name, value and help) to MenuNarration.Spoken . Connect your platform's text-to-speech there: MenuNarration.Spoken += text => MyTextToSpeech.Speak(text); Add the Narration Log component to see the texts in the Console while you set it up."},{"p":"Accessibility","h":"Not supported: screen readers","u":"settings-accessibility.html#not-supported-screen-readers","x":"The menus are not read aloud by system screen readers (Narrator, NVDA, VoiceOver, TalkBack) on their own; use the narration hook above with a text-to-speech of your choice. Unity's uGUI has no built-in screen reader support, and Shipready does not add one. If your game needs a screen reader, plan for a dedicated solution."},{"p":"Languages","h":"Languages","u":"settings-localization.html#languages","x":"Every string the menu shows has a key ( ui.… , setting.<id>.name , setting.<id>.help , setting.<id>.option.<value> , category.<id> , input.action.<name> ). Shipready ships an English String Table ( Content/Localization/English.asset )."},{"p":"Languages","h":"Add a language","u":"settings-localization.html#add-a-language","x":"Duplicate English.asset , rename it (e.g. German.asset ), and set Language Code ( de ) and Native Name ( Deutsch ). Translate the values (keep the keys and any {0} placeholders or <b> tags). Add the table to the Language setting (in your profile copy). Missing keys fall back to English. The default language is the player's system language when a table matches it, otherwise English."},{"p":"Languages","h":"Pseudo-localization (testing)","u":"settings-localization.html#pseudo-localization-testing","x":"In the Editor and in development builds the Language setting also offers Pseudo : every text gets accented letters, 40% more length and brackets ( [Ñéŵ Ĝáɱé~~~~] ). Use it to find text that is cut off or not translated. Menu texts shrink to fit long translations; an automated test checks every screen at 4:3, 16:10, 16:9, 21:9 and 32:9, at 75%, 100% and 150% menu size, in every theme, with pseudo-localization on."},{"p":"Languages","h":"Fonts for other scripts (Chinese, Japanese, Korean, …)","u":"settings-localization.html#fonts-for-other-scripts-chinese-japanese-korean-","x":"The included Inter font covers Latin scripts (Western European languages). For Chinese, Japanese, Korean, Cyrillic, Greek, Arabic or other scripts, create a TextMesh Pro font asset from a font that covers them (for example Noto Sans CJK, SIL OFL) and add it to the Fallback Font Assets list of the theme's font asset (or set it as the theme's font). Dynamic atlas population keeps the build small. Right-to-left languages need extra work in TextMesh Pro and are not handled by Shipready."},{"p":"Languages","h":"Unity Localization package (optional)","u":"settings-localization.html#unity-localization-package-optional","x":"When com.unity.localization is installed, the menu also asks a Unity Localization string table named SettingsMenuKit (same keys), and follows the package's selected locale. The bridge is compiled only with the package (1.4 or newer). A text missing from the selected locale's table comes from the English Unity table, then Shipready's English table, then the text in the prefab. Change the table name with UnityLocalizationStringSource.TableName , or stop following the selected locale with UnityLocalizationStringSource.FollowSelectedLocale = false . Credits and loading tips come from your own text"},{"p":"Languages","h":"Translators: CSV","u":"settings-localization.html#translators-csv","x":"Select one or more String Tables in the Project window, right-click > Rare Geometry > Export String Tables to CSV : one row per key, one column per language. Spreadsheets open it directly. Import CSV into String Tables reads it back into the selected tables (Undo works); a column for a new language creates its table next to them (add it to the Language setting's Tables)."},{"p":"Pause and multiplayer hooks","h":"Pause and multiplayer hooks","u":"settings-pause-and-multiplayer.html#pause-and-multiplayer-hooks","x":"Choose the Pause Mode on the front end (the MenuRoot component): Mode What happens Use it for Freeze Time.timeScale = 0 and (optionally) AudioListener.pause , both restored exactly on resume; the input lock is raised. Single-player games Overlay Only The pause menu shows over the running game; time doesn't stop; the input lock is raised. Multiplayer and online games, where time can't stop Custom Only the onPaused / onResumed events; your game decides everything. Your own pause logic In Freeze mode, if your game changes Time.timeScale while paused (slow motion ending, hit-stop), Resume keeps yo"},{"p":"Pause and multiplayer hooks","h":"Mouse cursor","u":"settings-pause-and-multiplayer.html#mouse-cursor","x":"Release Cursor In Menus (on by default): while any menu shows, the cursor is unlocked and visible, and when the menus close it goes back exactly as your game had it (locked and hidden in a first-person game). Turn it off when your game manages the cursor itself; MenuRoot.Instance.CursorReleased says whether Shipready has released it."},{"p":"Pause and multiplayer hooks","h":"Input lock","u":"settings-pause-and-multiplayer.html#input-lock","x":"While a menu should block gameplay input, MenuRoot.InputLocked is true and MenuRoot.InputLockChanged (static event) and onInputLockChanged (UnityEvent) fire. Disable your player input there: void OnEnable() => MenuRoot.InputLockChanged += OnLock; void OnDisable() => MenuRoot.InputLockChanged -= OnLock; void OnLock(bool locked) => playerInput.enabled = !locked;"},{"p":"Pause and multiplayer hooks","h":"Events","u":"settings-pause-and-multiplayer.html#events","x":"onNewGame , onContinue , onLoadGame , onMainMenuRequested , onQuitRequested , onPaused , onResumed , onLoadCompleted , onInputLockChanged : all UnityEvents on the front end."},{"p":"Pause and multiplayer hooks","h":"Multiplayer notes","u":"settings-pause-and-multiplayer.html#multiplayer-notes","x":"Settings are local to each player's machine (one settings file per user); nothing goes over the network. Use Overlay Only (or Custom) so pausing never stops a networked simulation. Pause On Controller Disconnect can be turned off for online games."},{"p":"Extending and API","h":"Extending and API","u":"settings-extending-api.html#extending-and-api","x":"Everything is in the RareGeometry.Shipready.Settings namespaces. Shipready's public API won't break in 1.x updates (an automated test checks every public type, member, serialized field, script GUID, menu item and setting id against the released version)."},{"p":"Extending and API","h":"Read a setting","u":"settings-extending-api.html#read-a-setting","x":"using RareGeometry.Shipready.Settings; var settings = SettingsRuntime.Current; // created by the front end or a Settings Bootstrap float shake = settings.GetFloat(\"gameplay.camera-shake\", 1f); bool hints = settings.GetBool(\"gameplay.hints\"); string difficulty = settings.GetValue(\"gameplay.difficulty\"); Ids are listed in the Settings reference ."},{"p":"Extending and API","h":"React when settings change","u":"settings-extending-api.html#react-when-settings-change","x":"// Connect tier: called now and after every Apply that changes it. SettingsRuntime.Current.Connect(\"gameplay.field-of-view\", v => cam.fieldOfView = float.Parse(v, System.Globalization.CultureInfo.InvariantCulture)); // Any tier: an event per applied setting. SettingsRuntime.Current.ValueApplied += d => Debug.Log($\"{d.Id} is now {SettingsRuntime.Current.GetValue(d.Id)}\"); No code: add a Setting Event Binder component (UnityEvents for float, int, bool and string values)."},{"p":"Extending and API","h":"A connector component","u":"settings-extending-api.html#a-connector-component","x":"public sealed class LightIntensityTarget : SettingConnector { [SerializeField] Light target; protected override void OnValue(string value) => target.intensity = float.Parse(value, System.Globalization.CultureInfo.InvariantCulture); }"},{"p":"Extending and API","h":"Your own Auto setting","u":"settings-extending-api.html#your-own-auto-setting","x":"Derive a definition (for example from FloatSettingDefinition ) and an applier, and register the applier once: public sealed class GammaApplier : SettingApplier { public override bool Handles(SettingDefinition d) => d is GammaSettingDefinition; public override void Apply(SettingsController c, IReadOnlyList<SettingDefinition> changed) { foreach (var d in changed) MyPostStack.SetGamma(c.GetFloat(d.Id, 1f)); } } [RuntimeInitializeOnLoadMethod(RuntimeInitializeLoadType.AfterAssembliesLoaded)] static void Register() => SettingsAppliers.Register(() => new GammaApplier());"},{"p":"Extending and API","h":"Change settings from code","u":"settings-extending-api.html#change-settings-from-code","x":"var c = SettingsRuntime.Current; c.SetPendingFloat(\"audio.music-volume\", 0.5f); c.Apply(); // or c.Cancel()"},{"p":"Extending and API","h":"Where settings are saved","u":"settings-extending-api.html#where-settings-are-saved","x":"Implement ISettingsStorage (Load, Save, Delete) to keep settings in a cloud save, a console save system or your own save file. Use it before the menu starts: public sealed class CloudSettingsStorage : ISettingsStorage { public SettingsLoadResult Load() { var json = MyCloud.Read(\"settings\"); return json != null && SettingsStore.TryFromJson(json, out var data, out _) ? new SettingsLoadResult(SettingsLoadStatus.Loaded, data, false, \"\") : new SettingsLoadResult(SettingsLoadStatus.NotFound, new SettingsData(), false, \"\"); } public bool Save(SettingsData data) => MyCloud.Write(\"settings\", SettingsSt"},{"p":"Extending and API","h":"Export, import, presets, reset one","u":"settings-extending-api.html#export-import-presets-reset-one","x":"var c = SettingsRuntime.Current; string json = c.ExportJson(); // the applied values (settings file format) int count = c.ImportJson(json, out var error); // pending values; -1 when the text isn't settings JSON c.ApplyPreset(myPreset); // a SettingsPreset asset c.ResetToDefault(\"audio.music-volume\"); // one setting back to its default c.Apply(); c.RestartRequired += changed => MenuRoot.Instance.ShowToast(\"Restart to finish\");"},{"p":"Extending and API","h":"Settings from your code ([GameSetting])","u":"settings-extending-api.html#settings-from-your-code-gamesetting","x":"public static class MyGameSettings { [GameSetting(\"gameplay.hints\", \"Tutorial Hints\", Help = \"Shows tips about the controls.\")] public static bool Hints = true; [GameSetting(\"gameplay.hud-scale\", \"HUD Size\", Min = 0.5f, Max = 2f)] public static float HudScale = 1f; [GameSetting(\"gameplay.crosshair\", Category = \"gameplay\")] public static CrosshairStyle Crosshair = CrosshairStyle.Dot; // an enum becomes a choice } Kit window > Settings > Custom setting > Create settings from [GameSetting] fields creates a setting for each one (bool: toggle, float: slider, int: whole number, enum: choice). While "},{"p":"Extending and API","h":"Visual Scripting","u":"settings-extending-api.html#visual-scripting","x":"With Unity's Visual Scripting package installed, the node library gets Get Setting , Set Setting (optionally applying) and the event On Setting Applied (category Rare Geometry). Regenerate the nodes once (Project Settings > Visual Scripting > Regenerate Nodes) after importing Shipready."},{"p":"Extending and API","h":"Your save system","u":"settings-extending-api.html#your-save-system","x":"public sealed class MySaves : ISaveGameBridge { public bool HasSave() => MySaveSystem.Any(); public void Continue() { MySaveSystem.LoadNewest(); MenuRoot.Instance.EnterGameplay(); } public void OpenLoad() => myLoadWindow.Open(); public void OpenSave() => mySaveWindow.Open(); public bool IsOpen => myLoadWindow.IsOpen || mySaveWindow.IsOpen; public bool HandleCancel() { myLoadWindow.Close(); mySaveWindow.Close(); return true; } } MenuRoot.Started += menu => menu.SaveBridge = new MySaves(); // or set it once the front end exists"},{"p":"Extending and API","h":"The menu","u":"settings-extending-api.html#the-menu","x":"var menu = MenuRoot.Instance; menu.SetHasSave(true); // shows Continue menu.EnterGameplay(); // hide menus; Esc / Start pauses menu.ShowFrontEnd(); // back to the main menu menu.Pause(); menu.Resume(); menu.SetTheme(myTheme); menu.ShowToast(\"Settings imported\"); // a short message at the bottom menu.ShowMessage(\"Saved\", \"Your game was saved.\"); menu.OpenScreen(\"BrightnessScreen\"); // any screen by name menu.LoadScene(\"Level1\", MenuMode.Gameplay); // with the loading screen"},{"p":"Extending and API","h":"Styles, backgrounds, logo and the Menu Camera","u":"settings-extending-api.html#styles-backgrounds-logo-and-the-menu-camera","x":"var menu = MenuRoot.Instance; menu.SetDisplayAs(DisplayAs.IconAndShortText); // null: back to the theme's menu.SetPromptDisplay(PromptDisplay.KeyOnly); menu.SetFillStyle(FillStyle.Radial); menu.SetMessagePosition(MessagePosition.TopCenter); menu.HoldToConfirm = true; menu.ShowToast(\"Saved\", \"Saved\", MessageKind.Saving, 2f); menu.SetBackgroundMode(BackgroundSlot.Pause, BackgroundMode.BlurredGame); // null: the theme's menu.SetBackground(BackgroundSlot.MainMenu, new MenuBackgroundStyle { mode = BackgroundMode.Image, image = mySprite, panAndZoom = true }); menu.SetOpacity(0.85f, null, 0.85f); // "},{"p":"Extending and API","h":"Accessibility, subtitles, languages","u":"settings-extending-api.html#accessibility-subtitles-languages","x":"SubtitleDisplay.Show(\"Over here!\", 3f, \"Mara\"); float shake = AccessibilityState.CameraShakeScale; // 0 with Reduce Motion bool reduced = AccessibilityState.ReducedMotion; string label = MenuLocalization.Get(\"mygame.hud.ammo\", \"Ammo\");"},{"p":"Extending and API","h":"Optional integrations","u":"settings-extending-api.html#optional-integrations","x":"URP, the Input System, Post Processing v2, Cinemachine and Unity's Localization package are detected with assembly versionDefines : their code compiles only when the package is installed, and nothing is required."},{"p":"Upgrading","h":"Upgrading","u":"settings-upgrading.html#upgrading","x":""},{"p":"Upgrading","h":"1.0.0","u":"settings-upgrading.html#100","x":"The first release. Nothing to do."},{"p":"Upgrading","h":"How updates work","u":"settings-upgrading.html#how-updates-work","x":"Your profile, settings, mixer and themes copies live in Assets/RareGeometryData/Shipready (or wherever you saved them), outside Shipready's folder: an update never overwrites them. Back up your project (or commit it) before importing an update, then import the new package over the old one. Public API, serialized fields, script GUIDs, menu paths and setting ids are kept across 1.x versions (an automated test checks them against the released version), so your scenes, prefabs and code keep working. A .unitypackage update never deletes files: files Shipready no longer needs are left as harmless st"},{"p":"Troubleshooting and FAQ","h":"Troubleshooting and FAQ","u":"settings-troubleshooting.html#troubleshooting-and-faq","x":""},{"p":"Troubleshooting and FAQ","h":"The menu doesn't react to the keyboard or gamepad","u":"settings-troubleshooting.html#the-menu-doesnt-react-to-the-keyboard-or-gamepad","x":"The scene needs one EventSystem. The front end creates one when the scene has none. The menu builder's Check tab finds extra EventSystems and wrong input modules, with a Fix button. Player Settings > Active Input Handling: Shipready works with Input Manager (Old) , Input System Package (New) or Both . With \"New\" you need the Input System package installed."},{"p":"Troubleshooting and FAQ","h":"Text is invisible or shows squares","u":"settings-troubleshooting.html#text-is-invisible-or-shows-squares","x":"Import TMP Essential Resources (Window > TextMeshPro > Import TMP Essential Resources). For other alphabets, see Languages ."},{"p":"Troubleshooting and FAQ","h":"Music, Sound Effects, Voice or Interface volume doesn't show in Settings","u":"settings-troubleshooting.html#music-sound-effects-voice-or-interface-volume-doesnt-show-in-settings","x":"These need an Audio Mixer: menu builder > Home , step 2 (or Setup > Create starter mixer, then Assign). Master Volume works without one."},{"p":"Troubleshooting and FAQ","h":"A graphics setting is missing","u":"settings-troubleshooting.html#a-graphics-setting-is-missing","x":"It doesn't apply to your render pipeline or project (for example URP settings in a Built-in project, or Monitor with one screen). The Settings tab's catalog shows why each hidden setting is hidden."},{"p":"Troubleshooting and FAQ","h":"Key Bindings doesn't show","u":"settings-troubleshooting.html#key-bindings-doesnt-show","x":"It needs the Input System package and your Input Action Asset in the Key Bindings setting ( Controls and rebinding )."},{"p":"Troubleshooting and FAQ","h":"The display change reverted by itself","u":"settings-troubleshooting.html#the-display-change-reverted-by-itself","x":"That's the 15-second safety countdown: choose Keep to keep a new display mode ( Display safety )."},{"p":"Troubleshooting and FAQ","h":"Settings were reset","u":"settings-troubleshooting.html#settings-were-reset","x":"A damaged settings file is replaced by its backup or the defaults (the damaged copy is kept as settings.json.corrupt ). Starting the game with -reset-settings deletes the saved settings on purpose."},{"p":"Troubleshooting and FAQ","h":"My quality settings changed after Play mode","u":"settings-troubleshooting.html#my-quality-settings-changed-after-play-mode","x":"They shouldn't: Shipready puts every quality value back when Play mode ends. If you see a change, please report it with your system info."},{"p":"Troubleshooting and FAQ","h":"Still stuck?","u":"settings-troubleshooting.html#still-stuck","x":"Kit window > About > Copy system info , then post it with your question: Discord: https://discord.gg/AmpBDxRAKu Bug tracker: https://github.com/raregeometry/Support Website: https://raregeometry.github.io"},{"p":"Known issues and limits","h":"Known issues and limits","u":"settings-known-issues.html#known-issues-and-limits","x":"Screen readers (Narrator, NVDA, VoiceOver, TalkBack) are not supported: Unity's uGUI has no screen reader support. HDRP: the menus and all engine-wide settings work; HDRP-specific graphics settings are not included in 1.0. Right-to-left languages (Arabic, Hebrew) are not handled; Chinese, Japanese and Korean need a font asset that covers them ( Languages ). Monitor selection is available on Windows only (Unity's API). Mobile and consoles: the menus are safe-area aware and controller-friendly, but 1.0 was tested on Windows (Mono and IL2CPP players), with an Android build (IL2CPP, ARM64) and a W"},{"p":"Support","h":"Support","u":"settings-support.html#support","x":"Need help, found a bug, or have an idea? Here is where to go: Discord (questions, help, feature requests): https://discord.gg/AmpBDxRAKu Post in #help or #feature-requests and pick the Shipready Settings tag. Bug tracker (confirmed bugs): https://github.com/raregeometry/Support Use the bug report form and choose Shipready. Website: https://raregeometry.github.io Online manual: https://raregeometry.github.io/shipready/docs/ Email: the address in Tools > Shipready > Menus > Help"},{"p":"Support","h":"What to include in a report","u":"settings-support.html#what-to-include-in-a-report","x":"Open the menu builder's About tab and click Copy system info , then paste it into your report. It contains: Kit version (e.g. 1.0.0) Unity version (e.g. 6000.3.24f1) Render pipeline: Built-in, URP or HDRP Input backend and the platform you build for Please add what you did, what you expected, what happened, and any Console errors. Replies usually come within a few days."},{"p":"Shipready: Quick Start","h":"Shipready: Quick Start","u":"save-quick-start.html#shipready-quick-start","x":"Version 1.0 · Rare Geometry Five minutes from import to an object that remembers its state, without writing code. Each step ends with a Checkpoint that says what you should see. The Guide has the details behind every step. You need Unity 2022.3 LTS or newer on Windows 10 or 11 ( Unity versions and platforms ), and a scene with something that changes during play: a door, a chest, a pickup, or just a cube."},{"p":"Shipready: Quick Start","h":"1. Import Shipready","u":"save-quick-start.html#1-import-shipready","x":"Open Window → Package Manager , choose My Assets , find Shipready , then click Download and Import . Leave everything ticked and click Import . When Unity has finished compiling, the Start window opens by itself. To open it again, choose Tools → Shipready → Saves → Start . If the menu and the Start window do not appear, Unity UI or TextMesh Pro is missing from the project: the Console says which, and Tools → Shipready → Saves → Required Packages shows how to install it ( If Shipready's menus are missing after import ). Checkpoint: the Start window is open and says \"Before your first save\"."},{"p":"Shipready: Quick Start","h":"2. Fix what the status strip shows","u":"save-quick-start.html#2-fix-what-the-status-strip-shows","x":"Open the scene you want to test in. The strip at the top of the Start window checks five things; click the button under each one that is not green yet: Check Click What it does No settings yet Create Makes Shipready's settings in Assets/RareGeometryData/Shipready , outside Shipready's folder, with the recommended defaults Scene not saved Save scene A load can only re-open a scene that is saved to a file Scene not in Build Settings Add A build only loads scenes on that list No hotkeys Add Puts the save menu and the F5 / F9 test hotkeys in the scene. The keys work in the Editor only. No menu tex"},{"p":"Shipready: Quick Start","h":"3. Make an object remember its state","u":"save-quick-start.html#3-make-an-object-remember-its-state","x":"In the Start window, click Make objects remember their state . Select your object in the Hierarchy (hold Ctrl to pick several), then click Make the selected objects remember their state . Select the object. The top of its Saveable says in one sentence what it saves, such as \"Saves position, active state and whether it was destroyed.\" To save a value from one of your scripts, such as Is Open or Gold , tick it under Values from your scripts . Save the scene (Ctrl+S). Set starting values in the Inspector, in the field's declaration or in Awake , never in Start : Shipready restores saved values be"},{"p":"Shipready: Quick Start","h":"4. Try it","u":"save-quick-start.html#4-try-it","x":"Press Play . Press F5 and click Save on the Slot 1 card. Press F5 again to close the menu. Change something: move the object in the Scene view, or change a ticked value in the Inspector. Press F9 , click Load on Slot 1 , then Load again to confirm. The save menu in Save mode, with slot cards showing thumbnails, dates and playtime F5 opens the Save menu. Each used slot shows a picture, the date and the playtime. Checkpoint: the object is back as it was when you saved, and a note at the top of the Game view says what happened, such as \"Loaded Slot 1: 1 restored\". If something was skipped, the no"},{"p":"Shipready: Quick Start","h":"5. Make it yours","u":"save-quick-start.html#5-make-it-yours","x":"Click Make it yours in the Start window (or Tools → Shipready → Saves → Menu Style ). Click Use on one of six styles (Dark, Light, Fantasy, Sci-fi, Cozy or Minimal), then change any colour, switch the list to a grid, hide details you do not need, or rewrite any word. A live preview shows each change, and Edit → Undo takes it back. Your changes go into your own copy in Assets/RareGeometryData/Shipready/Menu , never into Shipready ( Make it yours ). Checkpoint: press Play and F9: the Load menu has your style."},{"p":"Shipready: Quick Start","h":"If something did not come back","u":"save-quick-start.html#if-something-did-not-come-back","x":"What you see What to do F5 and F9 do nothing The status strip shows No hotkeys or Hotkeys off : click its button. The menu says \"Saving is not available yet.\" There is no settings asset: click Create in the Start window. ( SK005 ) The note says \"1 skipped ... not found (SK130)\" The saved object is no longer in the scene, or has a new id. Save again. ( SK130 ) An SK134 or SK133 warning on load The scene is not saved, or not in Build Settings: use the strip's Save scene and Add . ( SK134 , SK133 ) A value comes back, then resets A script sets it in Start . Move that line to Awake . Every warning"},{"p":"Shipready: Quick Start","h":"Next steps","u":"save-quick-start.html#next-steps","x":"Save values from your scripts. Choose Save values from my scripts in the Start window, or add using RareGeometry.Shipready.Save; and: csharp SaveKit.Data.Set(\"gold\", gold); // the next save writes it gold = SaveKit.Data.Get(\"gold\", 0); // after a load; 0 when nothing is saved yet See Saving plain values and Events . - See what was saved. Tools → Shipready → Saves → Save Inspector opens any save as a tree, and during Play shows what a save would write right now ( Save Inspector ). - Try the demos from Tools > Shipready > Saves > Open Demo (start with World (start here) ), or in Assets/RareGeome"},{"p":"Shipready: Quick Start","h":"Read more","u":"save-quick-start.html#read-more","x":"Walkthroughs : illustrated step-by-step pages: first save, world objects, the menu, game updates, backups Guide : how to do each task, step by step Reference : how each part works, every setting and tool GameUpdates.md : keeping old saves loading after you change your game Coop.md and SteamCloud.md : host-only co-op, and Steam Auto-Cloud LimitsAndFAQ.md , Troubleshooting.md and ApiReference.md Every document in Documentation also comes as an offline HTML page with the same name, such as Guide.html , to read in a browser. Get help: Discord https://discord.gg/AmpBDxRAKu · bug tracker https://git"},{"p":"Shipready: Guide","h":"Shipready: Guide","u":"save-guide.html#shipready-guide","x":"Version 1.0 · Rare Geometry This Guide shows how to do things with Shipready, one task at a time: getting started, saving values from your scripts, slots and autosaves, making objects remember their state, the save menu, co-op and Steam Cloud. For how each part works in detail, every setting and every editor tool, see the Reference . New to Shipready? Start with the Quick Start . Settings in bold, such as Save Version , are fields on the settings asset ShipreadySaveSettings . To change one, select the asset in the Project window; most are on its Basics tab, the rest under Advanced ."},{"p":"Shipready: Guide","h":"Contents","u":"save-guide.html#contents","x":"Introduction - What Shipready does - What Shipready does not do - Unity versions and platforms - Package contents and folders Getting started - Required Unity packages - The Start window and the Setup Wizard - Starting Shipready in your game - Your first save - Using Shipready from your own assemblies Saving plain values - Set, Get, Has and Remove Saving and loading - Slots: save and load - Partial saves and loads - The global file - Autosaves - The quicksave - Starting a new game - Continue, copy, rename and reset progress - No code: Save Actions, Save Events and Save Point - Events - Results"},{"p":"Shipready: Guide","h":"Introduction","u":"save-guide.html#introduction","x":""},{"p":"Shipready: Guide","h":"What Shipready does","u":"save-guide.html#what-shipready-does","x":"Shipready is a complete, crash-safe save system for Unity, with a finished save and load menu included. A beginner can set it up in minutes. When a save breaks, Shipready shows the developer why and gets the player back into their game. What you get: Saving with one line of code , or by ticking the values you want saved in the Inspector. Slots, autosaves and a quicksave , plus one global file for player settings that loading a slot never overwrites. World persistence. Objects in your scenes remember their position, active state, destruction, physics and the fields you tick. Objects spawned at "},{"p":"Shipready: Guide","h":"What Shipready does not do","u":"save-guide.html#what-shipready-does-not-do","x":"It does not save any object automatically. It saves plain values and the Unity state described in this Guide and the Reference . Anything else needs a short script. It is not anti-cheat. Encryption stops casual save editing, not a determined player. It has no cloud sync in 1.0. A Steam Cloud setup guide is included instead. It does not load a save in the middle of a co-op session. The host loads before friends join. Some engine state is not captured: particle systems, physics joints, running coroutines, and the exact path of a NavMesh agent (it walks to its saved destination again). The Animat"},{"p":"Shipready: Guide","h":"Unity versions and platforms","u":"save-guide.html#unity-versions-and-platforms","x":"Supported in 1.0 Unity Supported from 2022.3 LTS, the minimum version. Tested on 6.3 LTS and the newest Unity 6 release. Other versions, including 2022.3 and 6.0 LTS, are supported but not tested separately. Platforms Windows 10 and 11, 64-bit. macOS, iOS, Android, Linux, WebGL and consoles are not supported in 1.0. Scripting backends Mono and IL2CPP Render pipelines Built-in, URP and HDRP. The core has no rendering code. Demos are checked in Built-in and URP; HDRP gets automated tests only. Input The Input Manager, the Input System, or both Dependencies Only Unity's own packages: uGUI and Tex"},{"p":"Shipready: Guide","h":"Package contents and folders","u":"save-guide.html#package-contents-and-folders","x":"Everything Shipready ships sits in one folder, Assets/RareGeometry/Shipready/Save : Folder What is in it Runtime/ The RareGeometry.Shipready.Save assembly: SaveKit , the save tree, files, backups, compression, encryption, world persistence and the co-op hooks UI/ The RareGeometry.Shipready.Save.UI assembly: the save and load menu. UI/Prefabs holds SaveMenu , SlotCard , ConfirmDialog , GameMenu (title screen and pause menu) and SavingIndicator ; UI/Settings holds DefaultMenuTheme and DefaultMenuText . Editor/ The RareGeometry.Shipready.Save.Editor assembly: the Saveable inspector with its Field"},{"p":"Shipready: Guide","h":"Getting started","u":"save-guide.html#getting-started","x":"QuickStart.md takes you from import to a working save and load in about ten minutes, with screenshots. This section is the short version."},{"p":"Shipready: Guide","h":"Required Unity packages","u":"save-guide.html#required-unity-packages","x":"The save menu, the editor tools' windows (the Start window, the Setup Wizard, the Save Inspector and Diagnostics) and the demos are built on Unity UI ( com.unity.ugui ) and TextMesh Pro , which is part of Unity UI from Unity 2023.2 and the separate com.unity.textmeshpro package in Unity 2022.3. Projects made from Unity's templates already have both. The Asset Store installs them together with Shipready when the publisher declares them as dependencies; otherwise install them in the Package Manager, as below. If a project lacks one of them, the import still finishes without compile errors. The r"},{"p":"Shipready: Guide","h":"The Start window and the Setup Wizard","u":"save-guide.html#the-start-window-and-the-setup-wizard","x":"The Start window opens by itself the first time Shipready is imported into a project that has no settings asset, and at any time from Tools → Shipready → Saves → Start . It offers four choices: Make objects remember their state: select objects in the Hierarchy and click one button; each gets a Saveable, with the fields that look like state already ticked. Add a save/load menu: choose where the F5 / F9 test hotkeys work and add the menu to the open scene. Save values from my scripts: two lines of code to copy. Make it yours: opens the Menu Style window, to give the menu your game's look, layout"},{"p":"Shipready: Guide","h":"Starting Shipready in your game","u":"save-guide.html#starting-shipready-in-your-game","x":"Shipready starts by itself. Initialise Automatically is on by default; on the settings asset's Basics tab, and in the wizard under Slots and autosave , it is Start Shipready by itself . With it on, Shipready starts when the game runs, just before the first scene loads, using the settings asset ShipreadySaveSettings from its Resources folder. This happens in the Editor and in builds, in every Play session, also with domain reload switched off. You need no code: the save menu, the Saveable components and your own scripts can save and load from the first frame, and SaveKit.IsInitialised is alread"},{"p":"Shipready: Guide","h":"Your first save","u":"save-guide.html#your-first-save","x":"Open Tools → Shipready → Saves → Start . Click every fix the status strip offers: Create (the settings), Save scene , Add (to Build Settings) and Add (the save menu and the F5 / F9 test hotkeys). A load opens the saved scene again from its file, so a scene that was never saved cannot come back. Choose Make objects remember their state . Select an object that should remember its state, such as a chest or a door, and click Make the selected objects remember their state . Select the object. The top of its Saveable says in one sentence what it saves. To save another script value such as isOpen , t"},{"p":"Shipready: Guide","h":"Using Shipready from your own assemblies","u":"save-guide.html#using-shipready-from-your-own-assemblies","x":"The RareGeometry.Shipready.Save and RareGeometry.Shipready.Save.UI assemblies are auto-referenced, so scripts in Unity's default assemblies can use them straight away. If your scripts sit in an assembly definition of your own, add a reference to RareGeometry.Shipready.Save , and to RareGeometry.Shipready.Save.UI if they use the menu. An editor assembly of your own that calls the registry upkeep ( RegistryUpkeep , in RareGeometry.Shipready.Save.Editor.Registries ) references RareGeometry.Shipready.Save.Editor.Core ; the editor tools' windows are in RareGeometry.Shipready.Save.Editor . Most game"},{"p":"Shipready: Guide","h":"Saving plain values","u":"save-guide.html#saving-plain-values","x":"SaveKit.Data holds the plain values your game saves: gold, the current day, a volume setting, the list of unlocked levels. Values live in memory until a save writes them, and a load replaces them."},{"p":"Shipready: Guide","h":"Set, Get, Has and Remove","u":"save-guide.html#set-get-has-and-remove","x":"using RareGeometry.Shipready.Save; using UnityEngine; public class Wallet : MonoBehaviour { int gold; public void Earn(int amount) { gold += amount; SaveKit.Data.Set(\"gold\", gold, \"Progress\"); } public void ReadBack() { gold = SaveKit.Data.Get(\"gold\", 0, \"Progress\"); // 0 when nothing is saved yet } } Set(key, value, category) stores a value. Leave the category out to use the Default Category . Get(key, fallback, category) reads it. The fallback is returned when there is no value, so a value added in a game update simply starts at its default in old saves. Has(key, category) says whether a val"},{"p":"Shipready: Guide","h":"Saving and loading","u":"save-guide.html#saving-and-loading","x":""},{"p":"Shipready: Guide","h":"Slots: save and load","u":"save-guide.html#slots-save-and-load","x":"Every save and load comes in a background form that you await and a blocking form that finishes before the next line runs. Slot saves and loads also come with a callback, for code that does not use await . Call Form Use it for SaveKit.SaveAsync(slot) Background Saving during play SaveKit.LoadAsync(slot) Background Loading during play. In the default load mode it loads the saved scenes first. SaveKit.SaveInBackground(slot, onComplete) Background, with a callback The same as SaveAsync , calling your method with the result (see Callbacks ) SaveKit.LoadInBackground(slot, onComplete) Background, wi"},{"p":"Shipready: Guide","h":"Partial saves and loads","u":"save-guide.html#partial-saves-and-loads","x":"Every save and load takes an optional list of categories. A checkpoint can save only Progress and World and leave Inventory and Quests in the file exactly as they were: SaveResult checkpoint = await SaveKit.SaveAsync(1, new[] { \"Progress\", \"World\" }); LoadResult progressOnly = SaveKit.Load(1, new[] { \"Progress\" }); Leaving the list out, or passing null , means every category. Every save is a merge: a category that holds no values in memory also keeps what the file holds. A save that has to keep categories from the file fails, and leaves the file untouched, when that file cannot be read, comes "},{"p":"Shipready: Guide","h":"The global file","u":"save-guide.html#the-global-file","x":"Shipready loads the global file when it starts. Save it on its own when the player changes a setting: SaveKit.Data.Set(\"musicVolume\", 0.8f, \"Settings\"); SaveResult saved = SaveKit.SaveGlobal(); // or await SaveKit.SaveGlobalAsync() SaveKit.LoadGlobal() and LoadGlobalAsync() take the settings back to what was last saved. A game with no global file yet gets FileNotFound ( SK100 ). The global file always falls back to its newest good backup when it is damaged, whatever Automatic Fallback says, because there is no slot card to restore it from. In co-op, every player keeps their own global file. Th"},{"p":"Shipready: Guide","h":"Autosaves","u":"save-guide.html#autosaves","x":"Trigger Setting Default When it fires Quit Autosave On Quit on When the game closes, and on mobile platforms when the game is paused or suspended. On a desktop, switching to another window does not autosave. Timer Autosave On Timer , Autosave Interval Seconds off, 300 Every interval, counted in unscaled time, so setting Time.timeScale to 0 does not stop it Scene change Autosave On Scene Change off Each time the active scene changes The quit autosave uses the blocking path, so the operating system cannot cut it short. Timed and scene-change autosaves run in the background. Each autosave overwri"},{"p":"Shipready: Guide","h":"The quicksave","u":"save-guide.html#the-quicksave","x":"Switch on Quicksave Enabled (the wizard's Quicksave slot ), then: SaveResult quick = SaveKit.Quicksave(); // InvalidArgument (SK002) while the quicksave is switched off LoadResult back = SaveKit.LoadQuicksave(); Both are blocking calls. await SaveKit.QuicksaveAsync() and await SaveKit.LoadQuicksaveAsync() do the same in the background, like SaveAsync and LoadAsync . The menu lists the quicksave in Load mode once one exists."},{"p":"Shipready: Guide","h":"Starting a new game","u":"save-guide.html#starting-a-new-game","x":"Call SaveKit.NewGame() when the player starts a new game. Shipready resets its own data; your game resets its own objects. NewGame works in memory only, and nothing on disk changes: every Slot category becomes empty, so the next save replaces those categories in the file instead of keeping an old slot's; scene memory, destroyed lists, spawned objects and per-player data are forgotten; the slot name, location, summary lines, playtime and the autosave timer start again; Global categories, such as the player's settings, are kept. NewGame does not touch the objects in the scenes that are open: the"},{"p":"Shipready: Guide","h":"Continue, copy, rename and reset progress","u":"save-guide.html#continue-copy-rename-and-reset-progress","x":"A title screen, an options screen and a pause menu need a few more calls. Each one returns a result like any save or load. Call What it does SaveKit.LoadMostRecent() and LoadMostRecentAsync() Loads the newest save of every kind: a slot, an autosave or the quicksave, by its save time. Saves that cannot be loaded (damaged with no valid backup, or from a newer version of the game) are passed over. This is what a Continue button does. SaveKit.HasAnySave Whether there is anything to continue from. Grey Continue out while it is false. SaveKit.GetMostRecentSaveInfo() The slot info of that newest save"},{"p":"Shipready: Guide","h":"No code: Save Actions, Save Events and Save Point","u":"save-guide.html#no-code-save-actions-save-events-and-save-point","x":"Three components put saving into the Inspector, for UI buttons, triggers and sounds. Save Actions (Add Component > Shipready > Save Actions): pick one of its methods in a Button's On Click list, or any UnityEvent: Save To Slot , Load Slot , Quicksave , Quickload , Autosave , Continue , New Game , Delete Slot or Delete All Saves . The slot number goes in the box beside the method. Each runs in the background and then raises On Succeeded or On Failed (with the reason). Set New Game Scene to load your first level, and Continue Scene when your title screen is in another scene than the game. Nothin"},{"p":"Shipready: Guide","h":"Events","u":"save-guide.html#events","x":"SaveEvents has five events. Each handler receives a SaveEventInfo with the operation, the slot number (0 for the global file, an autosave or the quicksave), the file name, the result code, the report and, for a load, which copy of the file was read. Event When Saving A save is starting, before anything is gathered. Values you put into SaveKit.Data here are part of the save. Saved A save has finished, successfully or not Loading A load is starting, before the file is read Loaded A load has finished, successfully or not. In the default load mode it fires once every saved scene is loaded and rest"},{"p":"Shipready: Guide","h":"Results","u":"save-guide.html#results","x":"Every call returns a result instead of throwing. Saves, deletes and restores return a SaveResult ; loads return a LoadResult . Member What it tells you Success Whether you got what you asked for. A load rescued from a backup counts as a success. Code A SaveErrorCode , such as FileDamaged or HostOnly . Each has a stable SK number. Message One plain-English sentence, with details such as the file name in brackets DocumentationLink The entry in Troubleshooting.md, for example RareGeometry/Shipready/Save/Documentation/Troubleshooting.md#sk101 Report Counts, timings, bytes and warnings (see Logging"},{"p":"Shipready: Guide","h":"Blocking or background: the one rule","u":"save-guide.html#blocking-or-background-the-one-rule","x":"Every call that ends in Async finishes back on the main thread, so the code after an await can touch Unity objects. That also means the main thread must stay free to get there. Never call .Wait() or read .Result on one of these tasks from the main thread. The task is waiting for the main thread, so the game would freeze. When a save must be finished before the next line runs, such as when the game quits, use the blocking SaveKit.Save or SaveKit.Load . They are built for exactly that. In a coroutine, wait for a task without blocking: Task<SaveResult> saving = SaveKit.SaveAsync(1); yield return "},{"p":"Shipready: Guide","h":"Callbacks instead of await","u":"save-guide.html#callbacks-instead-of-await","x":"SaveKit.SaveInBackground and SaveKit.LoadInBackground do the same work as SaveAsync and LoadAsync , and call a method of yours with the result when they have finished: using RareGeometry.Shipready.Save; using UnityEngine; public class SaveButtonsWithCallbacks : MonoBehaviour { public void SaveSlot(int slot) { SaveKit.SaveInBackground(slot, result => { if (!result.Success) Debug.LogWarning(result.ToString()); }); } public void LoadSlot(int slot) { SaveKit.LoadInBackground(slot, OnLoaded); } void OnLoaded(LoadResult result) { if (!result.Success) Debug.LogWarning(result.ToString()); } } The call"},{"p":"Shipready: Guide","h":"Slots and slot info","u":"save-guide.html#slots-and-slot-info","x":""},{"p":"Shipready: Guide","h":"Listing slots","u":"save-guide.html#listing-slots","x":"using System.Collections.Generic; using RareGeometry.Shipready.Save; using UnityEngine; public class SlotList : MonoBehaviour { readonly List<SlotInfo> slots = new List<SlotInfo>(); public void Show() { SaveKit.GetSlotInfos(slots); // one entry per slot, empty slots included foreach (SlotInfo info in slots) { if (!info.Exists) { Debug.Log(\"Slot \" + info.SlotNumber + \": empty\"); continue; } Debug.Log(info.Name + \", \" + info.Location + \", saved \" + info.SavedUtc.ToLocalTime() + \", played \" + PlaytimeTracker.Format(info.PlaytimeSeconds) + (info.IsDamaged ? \" (damaged)\" : string.Empty)); } } } Get"},{"p":"Shipready: Guide","h":"Thumbnails","u":"save-guide.html#thumbnails","x":"The save menu takes a thumbnail by itself, just before the Save menu opens, from its Camera for the picture (empty: main camera) ( ThumbnailCamera ) or from Camera.main . Without a camera there is simply no thumbnail. To set one from code, call SaveKit.SetNextThumbnail(texture) before you save. The picture is scaled down to 320×180 and copied straight away, so you can destroy your texture at once. The next successful slot save, autosave or quicksave stores it beside its file as <save file>.thumb.png , after the save itself is safely in place, and then forgets it. A picture that cannot be used "},{"p":"Shipready: Guide","h":"Deleting and restoring backups","u":"save-guide.html#deleting-and-restoring-backups","x":"SaveKit.Delete(slot) deletes the save, its temporary file, its backups and its thumbnail. Damaged copies kept for support ( .corrupt-<time> ) stay. SaveKit.RestoreBackup(slot) , or RestoreBackupAsync(slot) , puts the newest valid backup of a damaged slot back in place through the crash-safe write. The damaged file is kept as .corrupt-<time> and the backups stay where they are. It returns RecoveredFromBackup ( SK102 ) on success, which counts as a success, and FileDamaged ( SK101 ) when no backup is valid, with nothing changed. A main file that reads correctly is never replaced. The menu offers"},{"p":"Shipready: Guide","h":"World persistence","u":"save-guide.html#world-persistence","x":""},{"p":"Shipready: Guide","h":"The Saveable component","u":"save-guide.html#the-saveable-component","x":"Add Add Component → Shipready → Saveable to any GameObject that should remember its state. Each Saveable is saved under a permanent id: Object Its id Placed in a scene A GUID given in the Editor when the Saveable is added and saved with the scene. A duplicated object gets a new id automatically; the original keeps its own. A prefab asset None. The prefab carries a prefab id instead: its asset GUID. Spawned at runtime A new GUID when it wakes up, saved with its prefab id A Saveable on a child of a spawned prefab The root's id plus an id that is fixed inside the prefab; saved with the root If an"},{"p":"Shipready: Guide","h":"The built-in savers","u":"save-guide.html#the-built-in-savers","x":"Saver Default What it saves and restores Position on Local position, rotation and scale, and the parent when the parent is a saved object or the scene root (see Parents ) Active on Whether the GameObject is switched on ( activeSelf ) Destroyed on A scene object destroyed during play stays destroyed after a load and when its scene comes back. When it comes back decides how: Destroy (destroy it again) or Disable (keep it switched off, so links to it stay valid). Physics on when the object has a Rigidbody or Rigidbody 2D as you add the Saveable Velocity, angular velocity, kinematic flag and sleep"},{"p":"Shipready: Guide","h":"The Field Picker","u":"save-guide.html#the-field-picker","x":"The Field Picker sits at the bottom of the Saveable inspector. It lists, per component on the same GameObject, every field Unity itself saves: public fields and fields marked [SerializeField] , including those declared in base classes. Tick a field and its value is saved and restored. Fields that cannot be saved are greyed out, with the reason shown. Field type Can be ticked Numbers, string , char , bool Yes Enums Yes, saved by name Vector2 to Vector4 , Vector2Int , Vector3Int , Quaternion , Color , Color32 , Rect , RectInt , Bounds , BoundsInt Yes A class or struct marked [Serializable] Yes, "},{"p":"Shipready: Guide","h":"Custom scripts: ISaveable","u":"save-guide.html#custom-scripts-isaveable","x":"For anything the savers and the Field Picker do not cover, such as dictionaries, properties, polymorphic data or a value that has to be worked out, implement ISaveable on a script on the same GameObject as the Saveable: // ItemDefinition.cs using UnityEngine; [CreateAssetMenu(menuName = \"My Game/Item Definition\")] public class ItemDefinition : ScriptableObject { public string displayName; } // Chest.cs using System.Collections.Generic; using RareGeometry.Shipready.Save; using UnityEngine; public class Chest : MonoBehaviour, ISaveable { [SerializeField] bool isOpen; [SerializeField] List<ItemDe"},{"p":"Shipready: Guide","h":"Objects spawned at runtime","u":"save-guide.html#objects-spawned-at-runtime","x":"Put a Saveable on the root of a prefab. The Editor stamps the prefab id and adds the prefab to the Prefab Registry automatically whenever the prefab is imported or saved. From then on, a normal Instantiate is tracked: the copy gets a runtime id as it wakes up and is saved, with its prefab id, in the scene it is in. On load, Shipready re-creates each saved runtime object from its prefab, gives it its saved id, applies its state, and then sets its parent and position in a second pass. When a load restores into a scene that is already open, the runtime objects already there are removed first and "},{"p":"Shipready: Guide","h":"References between objects and to assets","u":"save-guide.html#references-between-objects-and-to-assets","x":"A ticked field can link to: another saved object: a field typed as Saveable , GameObject , Transform or any component, pointing at an object with a Saveable. A link to a GameObject is saved as { \"obj\": \"<id>\" } . A link to a component also stores the component's full type name, as { \"obj\": \"<id>\", \"t\": \"<component type>\" } , plus \"c\" , its position among the components of exactly that type, when it is not the first. A link without \"t\" loads too: the component is then found by the field's type; an asset: a ScriptableObject, a prefab or another asset listed in the Asset Registry or the Prefab Re"},{"p":"Shipready: Guide","h":"Scene memory","u":"save-guide.html#scene-memory","x":"Scene memory keeps the state of scenes the player has left, and restores it when they come back. Leaving a scene. SaveKit.LoadScene and SaveKit.UnloadScene capture a scene's state before it unloads. Coming back. When a scene loads during play, its remembered state is applied automatically, after Awake and before Start . This works however the scene was loaded. Saving. A save includes the remembered state of every scene, loaded or not, so the player can save in scene B and still find scene A as they left it after a load. using RareGeometry.Shipready.Save; using UnityEngine.SceneManagement; publ"},{"p":"Shipready: Guide","h":"Multiple and additive scenes","u":"save-guide.html#multiple-and-additive-scenes","x":"Each scene has its own section in the save, keyed by the scene's id from the Scene Registry (its asset GUID), so renaming or moving a scene never breaks a save. The world category also records which scenes were loaded, in load order, and which one was active. When LoadAsync loads a save in the default load mode, it loads the first saved scene on its own, adds the others one after another, restores each as it loads, and then makes the saved active scene active. A saved scene that cannot be loaded, because it is not in Build Settings or not in the Scene Registry, is a warning; the other scenes s"},{"p":"Shipready: Guide","h":"Load order: Awake, restore, Start","u":"save-guide.html#load-order-awake-restore-start","x":"Unity wakes a scene's objects ( Awake , OnEnable ) and starts them a frame later ( Start ). Shipready restores saved state in between, so your Start code already sees saved values. Objects re-created by a load are restored in the frame they are made, also before their Start . The rule: set defaults in Awake or in field initialisers, never in Start . A default set in Start would overwrite the value Shipready just restored. A full load runs in this order: Read, verify, unpack and migrate, in the background for LoadAsync . Put the categories into SaveKit.Data , and restore the DontDestroyOnLoad o"},{"p":"Shipready: Guide","h":"The save and load menu","u":"save-guide.html#the-save-and-load-menu","x":"Shipready ships a finished menu: one prefab with two modes, Save and Load."},{"p":"Shipready: Guide","h":"Adding it","u":"save-guide.html#adding-it","x":"With the Start window: Add a save/load menu adds the SaveMenu prefab to the open scene, an EventSystem with the right input module, and the F5 / F9 test hotkeys (in the Editor only, unless you choose otherwise). The Setup Wizard's Menu and hotkeys step does the same. By hand: drag Assets/RareGeometry/Shipready/Save/UI/Prefabs/SaveMenu.prefab into your scene. It stays hidden until something opens it. The menu's text uses TextMesh Pro. If the text does not show, import TMP's essential resources: the Import TMP Essentials button in the Start window or the wizard, or Window → TextMeshPro → Import "},{"p":"Shipready: Guide","h":"Make it yours","u":"save-guide.html#make-it-yours","x":"Tools → Shipready → Saves → Menu Style (also Make it yours in the Start window) changes how the menu looks and what it says, with no Project window, prefab or code. It works on the save menu in the open scene; if there is none, its first button adds one. A live preview shows the menu with made-up saves as you change things. The preview is drawn away from your scene, so it never changes your scene or marks it as changed. Tab What it changes Messages How the saving indicator, the save notes, the status line and the badges show ( Display as ), where the indicator and the notes sit, how long they "},{"p":"Shipready: Guide","h":"Messages: Display as and places","u":"save-guide.html#messages-display-as-and-places","x":"Every message Shipready shows the player can show in six ways. Pick one in the Menu Style window's Messages tab for the whole game, or on a part's own Inspector for that part alone: Display as Shows Example Full text the whole sentence \"Game saved\", \"From a newer version\" Short text a word or two \"Saved\", \"Newer\" Icon only the icon, in the text colour (on a badge, the muted text colour) a tick Icon in its colour the icon, in its state's colour a green tick, a red cracked page Icon and short text both a green tick and \"Saved\" None nothing Which parts: the saving indicator (default: icon and sho"},{"p":"Shipready: Guide","h":"Tabs, newest first, names and copies","u":"save-guide.html#tabs-newest-first-names-and-copies","x":"Four more choices, each off by default so the menu stays as simple as it ships: Tabs (Menu Style > Layout > The list): the Load menu gets All , Saves and Autosaves tabs, so a long list with autosaves stays easy to read. They show only when the Load menu lists autosaves or the quicksave (both are on by default). Up from the top card reaches the tabs; left and right move along them. Newest first (Menu Style > Layout > The list): the saves are listed by save time, newest at the top, in both menus; empty slots follow. Players can name their saves ( PlayerNamedSaves , on the Save Menu's Inspector, "},{"p":"Shipready: Guide","h":"The Game Menu: title screen and pause menu","u":"save-guide.html#the-game-menu-title-screen-and-pause-menu","x":"GameMenu.prefab (in UI/Prefabs ) is a title screen or a pause menu with no code: Continue , New Game , Save Game , Load Game , Delete All Saves and Quit . Drop it into a scene and drag your save menu into its Save Menu field. Continue loads the newest save of every kind, and is greyed out while there is none. Set Continue Scene when the title screen is not the game's scene. New Game starts a new game ( SaveKit.NewGame() ) and loads New Game Scene when you set one. Save Game and Load Game open the save menu. Save Game is off by default, as a title screen has nothing to save. Delete All Saves (o"},{"p":"Shipready: Guide","h":"The saving indicator","u":"save-guide.html#the-saving-indicator","x":"SavingIndicator.prefab shows a small \"Saving...\" with a turning ring at the edge of the screen while a save runs, then \"Saved\" with a tick (or \"Save failed\") for a moment, then fades out. Players expect to see that an autosave happened. Drop it into your first scene; it stays across scene loads. The demos have one; a new game has none until you add it. Everything about it is yours: its colours, icons and font come from the Menu Theme, its words from the Menu Text (Saving indicator, and Short words). Display as and Position sit at the top of its Inspector, with a live preview (see Messages: Dis"},{"p":"Shipready: Guide","h":"The save notes","u":"save-guide.html#the-save-notes","x":"SaveNotes.prefab shows a short note when a load finished (\"Loaded\"), when a save came back from its backup (\"Restored\"), or when a save or a load did not work, for a moment at the top centre of the screen, then fades out. Saves that worked are off by default, because the indicator shows them. Drop it into your first scene beside the indicator; it stays across scene loads. Display as , Position , which news it notes, how long a note stays and the fade are on its Inspector. Show(MessageKind.Loaded) shows a note for your own news."},{"p":"Shipready: Guide","h":"Co-op clients","u":"save-guide.html#co-op-clients","x":"On a co-op client, SaveKit.CanSave and SaveKit.CanLoad are false. The menu then shows the cards without their Save, Load, Delete and Restore buttons, and a line saying \"Only the host can save or load.\""},{"p":"Shipready: Guide","h":"Visual Scripting and Localization","u":"save-guide.html#visual-scripting-and-localization","x":"Two optional parts compile only when their Unity package is in your project, so Shipready never needs either package. Add the package and they appear; remove it and they go away with nothing broken."},{"p":"Shipready: Guide","h":"Visual Scripting nodes","u":"save-guide.html#visual-scripting-nodes","x":"With Visual Scripting (com.unity.visualscripting) installed, the fuzzy finder has an Shipready group: Save To Slot , Load Slot , Delete Slot , Autosave , Quicksave , Quickload , Continue (Load Most Recent) , New Game and Delete All Saves , each with Success and Message outputs to branch on; Has Save and save system State (has any save, has unsaved progress, seconds since the last save); Get Saved and Set Saved for whole numbers, numbers, true or false, text and Vector3, which are SaveKit.Data with a key and a category; and the events On Game Saved and On Game Loaded (with success, slot and whe"},{"p":"Shipready: Guide","h":"Unity Localization","u":"save-guide.html#unity-localization","x":"With Localization (com.unity.localization) installed, add Localized Menu Text (Add Component > Shipready) beside your save menu and pick a String Table. Each entry's key is the name of a word, as the field is called in Menu Text: saveTitle , loadTitle , deleteQuestion , indicatorSaving and so on. LocalizedMenuText.Keys lists them all. When the table loads, and whenever the player picks another locale, every Save Menu, Game Menu and Saving Indicator under it (or in its lists) takes the words. A key the table does not have keeps the word from Fallback , or Shipready's English. Dates follow the t"},{"p":"Shipready: Guide","h":"Co-op","u":"save-guide.html#co-op","x":"Shipready supports host-only co-op saving. It has no networking code of its own; three small interfaces in RareGeometry.Shipready.Save.Coop connect it to any network library. The host owns the world. Set SaveKit.Authority to an ISaveAuthority that says whether this game instance may save. On a client, every slot, autosave and quicksave save, load, delete and restore returns HostOnly ( SK140 ) without touching storage, and SaveKit.CanSave and CanLoad are false, so the menu hides those buttons. Every player keeps their own settings. The global file stays available to clients. Per-player data liv"},{"p":"Shipready: Guide","h":"Steam Cloud","u":"save-guide.html#steam-cloud","x":"Shipready works with Steam's code-free Auto-Cloud. Its file names are chosen so that one pattern uploads only the real saves: Root: WinAppDataLocalLow Subdirectory: <Company Name>/<Product Name>/Saves Pattern: *.save Backups, temporary files, damaged copies, thumbnails and the slot cache do not match *.save , so they stay on the player's machine as a local safety net. Two Steam users on one PC. In Steamworks, end the Subdirectory with /{64BitSteamID} , which Steam fills in. In Shipready, set Profile Folder to {profile} and set SaveKit.ProfileId to the same 64-bit Steam ID before Shipready star"},{"p":"Shipready: Guide","h":"Samples","u":"save-guide.html#samples","x":"Shipready includes six demo scenes in Samples/Demos/Scenes , plus the World demo's cellar. Each shows its instructions on screen. Move with WASD, the arrow keys or the left stick, interact with E or the gamepad's South button, and press F5 and F9 for the Save and Load menus. Demo What it shows Quick Start A settings panel (volume, subtitles) saved in the global file, and progress values (gold, day) saved in the slots, with the save and load menu World Chests that open and stay open, pickups that stay gone, a spawner that drops physics crates, a switch linked to a door (a restored reference), a"},{"p":"Shipready: Guide","h":"Troubleshooting and support","u":"save-guide.html#troubleshooting-and-support","x":"Every result and every Console line carries a code such as SK101 . Troubleshooting.md has one entry per code: what it means, the likely cause and the fix. The link in each result goes straight to its entry, for example Troubleshooting.md#sk101 . When something does not save or load the way you expect: Check result.Success and read result.ToString() . Read the warnings in the report, in the Console or in the Save Inspector's Recent saves and loads . Run Diagnostics. Open the save in the Save Inspector to see exactly what it holds. Set Log Level to Verbose and try again. LimitsAndFAQ.md answers "},{"p":"Shipready: Guide","h":"Support","u":"save-guide.html#support","x":"Support covers bugs in Shipready and questions about setting it up. It does not cover reviewing or debugging your own game code. The end of Troubleshooting.md lists what to send for a quick answer. Report a bug with the bug report form , ask a question in Discussions , email the address in Tools > Shipready > Saves > Help or visit https://raregeometry.github.io/ ."},{"p":"Walkthroughs","h":"Walkthroughs","u":"save-walkthroughs.html#walkthroughs","x":"Illustrated, step-by-step pages for the things most games need. Each takes a few minutes and uses the demos in Samples/Demos/Scenes , so you can follow along. Your first save : the Start window, one object that remembers its state, F5 / F9 and the note in the Game view. World objects : doors and chests, pickups that stay collected, crates spawned during play, a switch wired to a gate, and scenes that remember. The save and load menu : adding it, opening it from your own pause menu, what the cards show, and making it yours with the Menu Style window. Game updates and migrations : keeping old sa"},{"p":"Walkthrough: your first save","h":"Walkthrough: your first save","u":"save-walkthroughs-first-save.html#walkthrough-your-first-save","x":"Make one object remember its state and see it come back, without code, in about five minutes. The Quick Start is the short version."},{"p":"Walkthrough: your first save","h":"1. Open the Start window","u":"save-walkthroughs-first-save.html#1-open-the-start-window","x":"After Shipready is imported, the Start window opens by itself. Any time later, choose Tools → Shipready → Saves → Start . The Start window: the \"Before your first save\" checks with a fix button for each, and the three choices Open the scene you want to test in. The strip at the top checks five things a first save needs. Click the button under each one that is not green yet: Create makes Shipready's settings in Assets/RareGeometryData/Shipready , outside Shipready's folder, so Shipready updates never overwrite them. Save scene saves the open scene. A load re-opens the scene the save was made in"},{"p":"Walkthrough: your first save","h":"2. Pick what should be remembered","u":"save-walkthroughs-first-save.html#2-pick-what-should-be-remembered","x":"Click Make objects remember their state . Select a door, a chest or a crate in the Hierarchy (hold Ctrl for several) and click Make the selected objects remember their state . Each object gets a Saveable component. Select one: the first line of its Inspector says what it saves, for example \"Saves position, active state and whether it was destroyed.\" A chest's Saveable: \"Saves 1 field: Is Open\", with Is Open ticked under the chest's script To save a value from one of your scripts, such as whether a chest is open, tick it under Values from your scripts . Only fields Unity itself saves are offere"},{"p":"Walkthrough: your first save","h":"3. Save, change, load","u":"save-walkthroughs-first-save.html#3-save-change-load","x":"Press Play . Press F5 and click Save on Slot 1 . Change something: open the chest, or drag the crate somewhere else in the Scene view. Press F9 , click Load on Slot 1 and confirm. A note appears at the top of the Game view after each save and load: The Play Mode note after a save: Saved Slot 1: 11 objects, 50 values The Play Mode note after a load: Loaded Slot 1: 11 restored If something was skipped, the note says which object and why, with a code such as SK130. Click the note to open the full report in the Save Inspector."},{"p":"Walkthrough: your first save","h":"4. Look inside the save","u":"save-walkthroughs-first-save.html#4-look-inside-the-save","x":"Tools → Shipready → Saves → Save Inspector lists every save file. Click one to open it as a tree: file, category, scene, object, value. Your object is under World , by name."},{"p":"Walkthrough: your first save","h":"When something does not come back","u":"save-walkthroughs-first-save.html#when-something-does-not-come-back","x":"F5 and F9 do nothing: the Start window's strip shows No hotkeys or Hotkeys off ; click its button. \"Saving is not available yet.\" in the menu: there are no settings yet; click Create . A value resets right after a load: a script sets it in Start ; move that line to Awake . Every message from Shipready starts with [Shipready] and has a code with its own entry in Troubleshooting . Next: World objects shows pickups, spawned objects and links between objects."},{"p":"Walkthrough: the save and load menu","h":"Walkthrough: the save and load menu","u":"save-walkthroughs-save-menu.html#walkthrough-the-save-and-load-menu","x":"A finished menu for your players: slots with pictures, dates and playtime, autosaves, confirmations, and mouse, keyboard and gamepad control. This page adds it, opens it from your own UI and restyles it."},{"p":"Walkthrough: the save and load menu","h":"1. Add it","u":"save-walkthroughs-save-menu.html#1-add-it","x":"In the Start window, choose Add a save/load menu , pick where the F5 / F9 test hotkeys work ( Editor only is the safe choice) and click Add the save menu . The Setup Wizard's Menu and hotkeys step does the same. To do it by hand, drag UI/Prefabs/SaveMenu.prefab into the scene; it stays hidden until something opens it. The menu's text uses TextMesh Pro. If the text does not show, click Import TMP Essentials in the Start window."},{"p":"Walkthrough: the save and load menu","h":"2. Try it","u":"save-walkthroughs-save-menu.html#2-try-it","x":"Press Play, then F5 for the Save menu: The Save menu: empty slots, each with Save here to start a new file An empty slot saves at once; a used one asks \"Overwrite?\" first. A picture of the game is taken just before the menu shows. Press F9 for the Load menu: The Load menu with one saved slot showing its date, playtime, location and summary It lists the autosaves first, newest first, then the quicksave if you use one, then your slots. Loading asks first whether unsaved progress may be lost. A damaged save shows a Damaged badge; with the default settings, loading it uses its newest good backup ("},{"p":"Walkthrough: the save and load menu","h":"3. Open it from your own menu","u":"save-walkthroughs-save-menu.html#3-open-it-from-your-own-menu","x":"The F5 / F9 hotkeys are for testing. In your game, open the menu from a pause menu button: using RareGeometry.Shipready.Save.UI; using UnityEngine; public class PauseMenu : MonoBehaviour { [SerializeField] SaveMenu saveMenu; public void OnSaveButton() { saveMenu.Open(SaveMenuMode.Save); } public void OnLoadButton() { saveMenu.Open(SaveMenuMode.Load); } } When the menu closes, the selection goes back to whatever was selected before, such as your Save button, so gamepad players are never lost. If your pause menu also listens for Escape, see Controls so one key press does not close both."},{"p":"Walkthrough: the save and load menu","h":"4. Choose what each card says","u":"save-walkthroughs-save-menu.html#4-choose-what-each-card-says","x":"Each card shows a location and summary lines, such as \"Gold 100 · Ada\". Set them before saving, for example whenever they change: SaveKit.Location = \"Courtyard\"; SaveKit.SetSummary(\"Gems 3 / 8\", \"Gold 120, crates 2\"); The World demo does exactly this ( Slots and slot info )."},{"p":"Walkthrough: the save and load menu","h":"5. Make it yours","u":"save-walkthroughs-save-menu.html#5-make-it-yours","x":"Open Tools → Shipready → Saves → Menu Style , or click Make it yours in the Start window. It finds the menu in the open scene and shows a live preview beside its tabs: Style: click Use on one of six looks: Dark, Light, Fantasy, Sci-fi, Cozy or Minimal. Colours and Text and shape: change any colour, the font, the sizes and the corners. Layout: switch the list to a grid of 2 to 4 tiles, hide the details your game does not use, move the panel to the left or right, and set its width. Words: rewrite any line, then click Save as my text . Share: export the whole style to a .json file to use in anoth"},{"p":"Walkthrough: world objects","h":"Walkthrough: world objects","u":"save-walkthroughs-world-objects.html#walkthrough-world-objects","x":"Doors that stay open, pickups that stay collected, crates spawned during play, and switches wired to gates. Everything here is in the World demo: open Samples/Demos/Scenes/WorldDemo , press Play and follow along. The World demo's courtyard: chests, gems, a crate dropper, a switch and the stairs to the cellar"},{"p":"Walkthrough: world objects","h":"Doors and chests: a value from your script","u":"save-walkthroughs-world-objects.html#doors-and-chests-a-value-from-your-script","x":"A chest's script has a field isOpen . To keep it: Select the chest and give it a Saveable (the Start window's Make objects remember their state , or Add Component → Shipready → Saveable ). Under Values from your scripts , tick Is Open . The Saveable's summary now reads \"Saves position, active state and 1 field: Is Open.\" After a load, isOpen comes back. If the chest's lid is set up from isOpen in Start , also do it when a load finishes, because objects that are already running do not run Start again: in RestoreState if the script implements ISaveable , or in a SaveEvents.Loaded handler ( Load "},{"p":"Walkthrough: world objects","h":"Pickups: stay collected","u":"save-walkthroughs-world-objects.html#pickups-stay-collected","x":"A gem the player collects is destroyed. Keep Destroyed ticked on its Saveable (it is on by default): after a load, a collected gem is destroyed again instead of coming back. When it comes back chooses whether it is destroyed or only switched off."},{"p":"Walkthrough: world objects","h":"Spawned objects: crates from a dropper","u":"save-walkthroughs-world-objects.html#spawned-objects-crates-from-a-dropper","x":"Objects made during play come back too, if they are made from a prefab: Put a Saveable on the root of the prefab, here the crate. Make copies with an ordinary Instantiate . The Editor adds the prefab to Shipready's Prefab Registry by itself when the prefab is saved. Each copy gets its own id as it is made and is saved with its position. Tick Movement to keep a Rigidbody's speed and spin, so a crate that was falling keeps falling after a load. On load, Shipready re-creates each saved copy from the prefab and removes copies that were not in the save. A copy of something that is not a registered "},{"p":"Walkthrough: world objects","h":"Links between objects: a switch wired to a gate","u":"save-walkthroughs-world-objects.html#links-between-objects-a-switch-wired-to-a-gate","x":"The switch's script has a field that points at the gate it opens. Tick that field on the switch's Saveable. The link is saved as the gate's id, so after a load the switch points at the same gate again, even if the wiring box rewired it during play. The gate needs a Saveable too; the Field Picker greys out a link to an object without one and says why. Links to assets, such as an item's ScriptableObject, work the same way once the asset is in the Asset Registry : select it and choose Tools → Shipready → Saves → Registries → Register Selected Assets ."},{"p":"Walkthrough: world objects","h":"Scenes you leave and come back to","u":"save-walkthroughs-world-objects.html#scenes-you-leave-and-come-back-to","x":"Take the stairs to the cellar and back. Each area remembers what you did there, because Shipready keeps the state of scenes the player has left (scene memory) and puts it back when the scene loads again. The cellar, a second scene with its own remembered state Load scenes through SaveKit.LoadScene , or call SaveKit.World.CaptureScene before unloading a scene yourself, so its changes are kept. Shipready warns when a scene with Saveables unloads without being captured. The crate prefab's Saveable: position, active state, destroyed state and Movement ticked, with its one-sentence summary at the t"},{"p":"Walkthrough: backups and recovery","h":"Walkthrough: backups and recovery","u":"save-walkthroughs-backups-and-recovery.html#walkthrough-backups-and-recovery","x":"Power cuts, crashes during a save and full disks happen. This page shows what Shipready does about them, using the Recovery demo ( Samples/Demos/Scenes/RecoveryDemo ), which damages a save on purpose."},{"p":"Walkthrough: backups and recovery","h":"What you get without doing anything","u":"save-walkthroughs-backups-and-recovery.html#what-you-get-without-doing-anything","x":"Writes are crash-safe. A save is written to a temporary file first and swapped in only when it is complete, so a crash never leaves a half-written main file. Every save keeps older copies. With the default of 2, each save leaves slot_01.save.bak1 (the previous save) and .bak2 (the one before) beside the main file. Every file has a checksum, so damage is detected rather than loaded. A damaged save loads from its newest good copy by itself , and the damaged file is kept aside as a .corrupt copy, never deleted."},{"p":"Walkthrough: backups and recovery","h":"Try it","u":"save-walkthroughs-backups-and-recovery.html#try-it","x":"Open the Recovery demo and press Play. Press Save slot 1 , add a coin with +1 coin , and press Save slot 1 again. The first save is now the backup. Press Damage slot 1 . It scrambles a few bytes in the main file, as a failing disk might. The file list marks it DAMAGED : The Recovery demo after Damage slot 1: the main file is marked DAMAGED, and backup 1 is beside it Press Load slot 1 , or load it from the Load menu (F9), where it shows a Damaged badge. After loading: backup 1 was used, the result is SK102 Restored from backup, and the damaged file was kept as a .corrupt copy The load succeeds "},{"p":"Walkthrough: backups and recovery","h":"In your game","u":"save-walkthroughs-backups-and-recovery.html#in-your-game","x":"The player sees \"Restored from backup (date)\" in the save menu. A slot whose details come from a backup shows a Restored badge. Your code can listen for SaveEvents.RecoveredFromBackup to tell the player, or to log it. The Save Inspector shows each save's status (OK, Damaged, Wrong key, ...), lets you open any backup or the damaged copy as a tree, and has Restore backup to put the newest good copy back in place."},{"p":"Walkthrough: backups and recovery","h":"Choosing the settings","u":"save-walkthroughs-backups-and-recovery.html#choosing-the-settings","x":"On the settings asset's Advanced tab, Packing, encryption and backups : Older copies kept (0 to 5, default 2): more copies cost disk space, fewer give less to fall back on. Use a good copy by itself (on by default): off makes a damaged save fail its load and the menu offer Restore backup instead, so the player chooses. A save edited by hand: a readable save that no longer matches its checksum is treated as damaged (the default) or loaded with a warning. In the Editor, edited saves always load, so you can test by editing them."},{"p":"Walkthrough: backups and recovery","h":"When the disk is full","u":"save-walkthroughs-backups-and-recovery.html#when-the-disk-is-full","x":"A save that cannot be written fails with DiskFull (SK110) or AccessDenied (SK111), and every existing save stays exactly as it was. Nothing is ever deleted to make room. More: Reliability and recovery in the Reference."},{"p":"Walkthrough: game updates and migrations","h":"Walkthrough: game updates and migrations","u":"save-walkthroughs-game-updates.html#walkthrough-game-updates-and-migrations","x":"You shipped your game, players have saves, and now an update renames a value. This page shows how old saves keep loading. The Game Updates demo ( Samples/Demos/Scenes/GameUpdatesDemo ) plays it through; the Game Updates guide has every rule."},{"p":"Walkthrough: game updates and migrations","h":"The situation","u":"save-walkthroughs-game-updates.html#the-situation","x":"Version 1 of a game saved the player's money as cash . Version 2 calls it money and adds gems . A version 1 save still says cash : The Game Updates demo before the update: a save from version 1 with cash 1,250 Without help, version 2 would read money from that save, not find it, and start the player at 0."},{"p":"Walkthrough: game updates and migrations","h":"Do you need a migration at all?","u":"save-walkthroughs-game-updates.html#do-you-need-a-migration-at-all","x":"Most changes need none. Adding a value, adding an object, deleting an object, reordering enum values, renaming a scene, or switching compression or encryption on all just work: old saves load and new values keep their defaults. You need one when an update renames something saved (a value, a ticked field, a category, a script that saves itself), changes what a value means (0 to 100 becomes 0 to 1), or moves a value to another category."},{"p":"Walkthrough: game updates and migrations","h":"1. Raise the save version","u":"save-walkthroughs-game-updates.html#1-raise-the-save-version","x":"Select the settings asset, open the Advanced tab, Game updates , and raise Save version by one: from 1 to 2. Every save records the version it was written with."},{"p":"Walkthrough: game updates and migrations","h":"2. Write one step","u":"save-walkthroughs-game-updates.html#2-write-one-step","x":"A migration step is a static method that upgrades a save from one version to the next: using RareGeometry.Shipready.Save; public static class SaveMigrations { [SaveMigration(fromVersion: 1)] static void RenameCashToMoney(SaveTree save) { save.RenameKeyEverywhere(\"cash\", \"money\"); save.AddDefaultBeside(\"money\", \"gems\", SaveNode.FromNumberText(\"0\")); } } Shipready finds it by itself ( Find migration steps by itself is on by default). When a version 1 save loads, the step runs first, then the save loads as if version 2 had written it: After the update: money 1,250 (was cash), gems 0 (new), and th"},{"p":"Walkthrough: game updates and migrations","h":"3. Keep every step","u":"save-walkthroughs-game-updates.html#3-keep-every-step","x":"Each update that needs one adds one step: fromVersion: 2 , then fromVersion: 3 , and so on. A save from version 1 passes through every step on its way to the current version, so never delete an old step and never lower Save version after a release. A save written by a newer version of your game is refused with NewerVersion (SK120) and left untouched, so a player who goes back to an older build does not lose it."},{"p":"Walkthrough: game updates and migrations","h":"4. Test with real old saves","u":"save-walkthroughs-game-updates.html#4-test-with-real-old-saves","x":"Keep a few save files from each version you released, and load them in the new build before you ship it. The Save Inspector opens any save file as a tree and shows its save version in the list; Import... copies a file into the save folder to try it."},{"p":"Walkthrough: game updates and migrations","h":"Things that help","u":"save-walkthroughs-game-updates.html#things-that-help","x":"Enums are saved by name, so reordering them is safe; renaming one needs a step. If you also use IL2CPP with high code stripping, Shipready keeps your migration methods from being stripped by itself. GameUpdates.md lists every helper on SaveTree , such as renaming keys, moving values between categories and changing a value's units. Next: Backups and recovery ."},{"p":"Walkthrough: use your own menu","h":"Walkthrough: use your own menu","u":"save-walkthroughs-own-menu.html#walkthrough-use-your-own-menu","x":"Shipready's save menu can be restyled a long way (see Make it yours ). If your game already has its own menu art, you can instead build the menu yourself on Shipready's public API and keep everything else: slots, autosaves, backups, pictures and world saving. This page walks through the Own Menu demo, which does exactly that in one short script."},{"p":"Walkthrough: use your own menu","h":"1. Try it","u":"save-walkthroughs-own-menu.html#1-try-it","x":"Open Samples/Demos/Scenes/OwnMenuDemo.unity and press Play. A plain list shows five slots. Click +1 coin a few times, then Save on a slot: the row shows the date, \"3 coins\" and a picture of the scene. Click +1 coin again, then Load : the coin count goes back to what was saved. Delete empties the slot. Like every demo, it runs on its own settings and saves into its own folder ( SaveKitDemos/OwnMenu ), so it never touches your game's saves."},{"p":"Walkthrough: use your own menu","h":"2. Read the script","u":"save-walkthroughs-own-menu.html#2-read-the-script","x":"Samples/Demos/Scripts/OwnMenu/OwnMenuDemo.cs builds its uGUI list in code, so the whole menu is in one file. The parts that use Shipready are marked 1 to 5: List the slots. SaveKit.GetSlotInfos(list) fills a list with one SlotInfo per slot: Exists , SavedUtc , PlaytimeSeconds , Location , Summary and more. Draw one row per entry. Save. Set what the row will say, take a picture, then save: csharp SaveKit.SetSummary(coins + \" coins\"); Texture2D picture = ThumbnailCapture.Capture(Camera.main); SaveKit.SetNextThumbnail(picture); SaveResult result = SaveKit.Save(slot); Thumbnails.DestroyTexture(pic"},{"p":"Walkthrough: use your own menu","h":"3. Make it your own","u":"save-walkthroughs-own-menu.html#3-make-it-your-own","x":"Copy the script into your project, rename it, and swap the plain boxes for your own prefabs. Things Shipready's menu does for you that you may want too: Ask before overwriting, loading or deleting: check info.Exists and show your own confirmation first. Autosaves and the quicksave: SaveKit.GetAutosaveInfos(list) and SaveKit.GetQuicksaveInfo() ; load them with SaveKit.Load(info) . Damaged saves: info.IsDamaged and info.HasValidBackup ; SaveKit.RestoreBackup(slot) puts the newest good backup back. Saves from a newer version of the game: info.IsFromNewerVersion ; do not offer Load for them. Large"},{"p":"Walkthrough: your own menu in UI Toolkit","h":"Walkthrough: your own menu in UI Toolkit","u":"save-walkthroughs-own-menu-ui-toolkit.html#walkthrough-your-own-menu-in-ui-toolkit","x":"Shipready's own menus are built with uGUI. If your game's UI is built with UI Toolkit (UXML layouts and USS style sheets), keep it that way: build the save menu in UI Toolkit on Shipready's public API, and keep everything else (slots, autosaves, backups, pictures and world saving). The Own Menu (UI Toolkit) demo does exactly that with one layout, one style sheet and one short script. UI Toolkit is part of Unity, so no package is needed."},{"p":"Walkthrough: your own menu in UI Toolkit","h":"1. Try it","u":"save-walkthroughs-own-menu-ui-toolkit.html#1-try-it","x":"Open Samples/Demos/Scenes/OwnMenuUIToolkitDemo.unity and press Play. A list shows five slots, each with a picture frame and Save , Load and Delete ; a used slot also shows its name, date and playtime. Click +1 coin three times, then Save on a slot: the row shows the date, the playtime, \"3 coins\" and a picture of the scene. Continue loads the newest save; it is greyed out until there is one. Delete empties a slot. It shares the Own Menu demo's settings and save folder ( SaveKitDemos/OwnMenu ), so the uGUI and UI Toolkit versions show the same saves, and neither touches your game's saves."},{"p":"Walkthrough: your own menu in UI Toolkit","h":"2. The three files","u":"save-walkthroughs-own-menu-ui-toolkit.html#2-the-three-files","x":"File What it is Samples/Demos/UIToolkit/OwnMenuUIToolkit.uxml The layout: a panel with a title, the coin count, +1 coin , Continue , a scroll view for the slots and a status line. Open it in UI Builder to move things around. Samples/Demos/UIToolkit/OwnMenuUIToolkit.uss The look: every colour, size and corner. Change a class here and every row follows. Samples/Demos/Scripts/OwnMenu/OwnMenuToolkitDemo.cs The script on the scene's UIDocument : it finds the parts by name, adds one row per slot, and calls Shipready. OwnMenuUIToolkitPanelSettings.asset and OwnMenuUIToolkitTheme.tss beside them are t"},{"p":"Walkthrough: your own menu in UI Toolkit","h":"3. Read the script","u":"save-walkthroughs-own-menu-ui-toolkit.html#3-read-the-script","x":"The parts that use Shipready are marked 1 to 5, as in the uGUI version: List the slots. SaveKit.GetSlotInfos(list) ; one row ( VisualElement with the slot-row class) per entry. The picture is an Image filled from Thumbnails.Read(SaveKit.Storage, info.FileName) ; destroy the texture with Thumbnails.DestroyTexture when the rows are rebuilt. Save. SaveKit.SetSummary(...) , a picture from ThumbnailCapture.Capture(Camera.main) with SaveKit.SetNextThumbnail , then SaveKit.Save(slot) . Load. SaveKit.Load(slot) . Delete. SaveKit.Delete(slot) . Continue. SaveKit.LoadMostRecent() loads the newest slot, "},{"p":"Walkthrough: your own menu in UI Toolkit","h":"4. Make it your own","u":"save-walkthroughs-own-menu-ui-toolkit.html#4-make-it-your-own","x":"Copy the three files into your project and rename them (the script's class too). Then: Point the layout at your style sheet: in UI Builder, StyleSheets > Add Existing USS, or change the <Style src=\"...\"> line in the UXML. A layout still naming the old file shows unstyled. Give the UIDocument a Panel Settings: reuse OwnMenuUIToolkitPanelSettings , or make one with Create > UI Toolkit > Panel Settings Asset. A UIDocument without one shows nothing. Keep five names: the script finds coins , add-coin , continue , slots and status . Rename one in UI Builder and the script says in the Console which i"},{"p":"Game Updates","h":"Game Updates","u":"save-game-updates.html#game-updates","x":"This guide is for programmers. It explains how to change your game after release without breaking the saves your players already have. Shipready plans for updates in three ways: Every save records which version of your save data it holds. Small migration steps that you write upgrade old saves one version at a time. Loading is tolerant, so most changes need no migration at all."},{"p":"Game Updates","h":"The rules in short","u":"save-game-updates.html#the-rules-in-short","x":"Ship your first release with Save Version 1. It is in the settings asset ShipreadySaveSettings , under Game updates. When an update changes saved data in a way that old saves cannot handle by themselves, raise Save Version by one. Then write one migration step from the previous version. Keep every step you have ever written. A save from version 1 passes through every step on its way to the current version. Never lower Save Version after a release. Test every release with real save files from the versions you shipped before. Settings in bold, such as Save Version , are fields on the settings as"},{"p":"Game Updates","h":"What the save version is","u":"save-game-updates.html#what-the-save-version-is","x":"Every save file starts with a header like this one: \"header\": { \"saveVersion\": 3, \"gameVersion\": \"1.2.0\", \"kitVersion\": \"1.0.0\", \"savedUtc\": \"2026-11-02T18:41:07Z\", \"checksum\": \"crc32:5F1A9C02\", \"packing\": \"readable\" } saveVersion is the Save Version of the build that wrote the file. This is the number Shipready acts on. gameVersion is Version from Project Settings → Player ( Application.version ). It is recorded for your information. Shipready never decides anything from it. kitVersion is the version of Shipready that wrote the file. packing says how the data was stored: readable , gzip , aes"},{"p":"Game Updates","h":"When to raise the save version","u":"save-game-updates.html#when-to-raise-the-save-version","x":"Raise it only when old saves need help. Every version in the chain needs a step, even an empty one, so an unneeded raise just means an unneeded step. What your update changes Raise Save Version ? Adds a new value, a new ISaveable value or a newly ticked field No. Old saves do not hold it, so it keeps its default. Adds a new object with a Saveable to a scene No. It keeps its scene defaults. Deletes a saved object from a scene No. Its saved state is skipped and reported. Reorders enum values or adds new ones No. Enums are saved by name. Renames or moves a scene No. The Scene Registry knows scene"},{"p":"Game Updates","h":"Writing a migration step","u":"save-game-updates.html#writing-a-migration-step","x":"A migration step is a static method marked [SaveMigration(fromVersion: N)] . It upgrades a save from version N to version N + 1. It receives the whole save as a SaveTree and edits it, so it never needs your old classes. using RareGeometry.Shipready.Save; public static class SaveMigrations { // Turns a version 1 save into a version 2 save. [SaveMigration(fromVersion: 1, Description = \"cash renamed to money\")] static void RenameCashToMoney(SaveTree save) { save.RenameKeyEverywhere(\"cash\", \"money\"); } } A step must follow these rules. When Shipready starts, it warns in the Console about any step "},{"p":"Game Updates","h":"Adding steps by hand","u":"save-game-updates.html#adding-steps-by-hand","x":"You can register a step in code instead of marking a method, with SaveKit.Migrations.Add . Add it before Shipready starts: Shipready loads the global file while it starts, and that file may need the step too. Shipready starts by itself just before the first scene loads, so add your steps in a static method that Unity runs earlier, marked AfterAssembliesLoaded : using RareGeometry.Shipready.Save; using UnityEngine; public static class HandAddedMigrations { // Runs in every Play session and every build, before Shipready starts by itself. [RuntimeInitializeOnLoadMethod(RuntimeInitializeLoadType.A"},{"p":"Game Updates","h":"The SaveTree helpers","u":"save-game-updates.html#the-savetree-helpers","x":"A step gets the whole save: save.Data holds one member per category, such as Progress or World . save.Info holds the slot info that the menu shows. save.Header holds the header. Shipready raises its SaveVersion after each step, so leave it alone. Most changes need only these helpers. Each one does nothing when the key or category is missing, so it is safe on every kind of save file. Helper What it does Returns RenameKeyEverywhere(from, to) Renames every value called from , at any depth, in every category How many it renamed RemoveKeyEverywhere(name) Removes every value called name , at any dep"},{"p":"Game Updates","h":"Where your values are in the tree","u":"save-game-updates.html#where-your-values-are-in-the-tree","x":"The easiest way to find a path is to open a readable save in a text editor. In the Editor and in development builds the file is indented, so it is easy to read. Saved with Path from the data root SaveKit.Data.Set(\"gold\", gold, \"Progress\") Progress/gold A field ticked in the Field Picker <category>/scenes/<scene id>/objects/<object id>/fields/<Component>.<field> , for example .../fields/Chest.isOpen An ISaveable component <category>/scenes/<scene id>/objects/<object id>/custom/<Component>/<name> An object marked DontDestroyOnLoad <category>/persistent/objects/<object id>/... An object spawned a"},{"p":"Game Updates","h":"Per-player data and RenameCategory","u":"save-game-updates.html#per-player-data-and-renamecategory","x":"RenameCategory renames the category at the top of the data only. In a co-op save, each player's values sit under players/<player id>/<category> , so rename those as well: using RareGeometry.Shipready.Save; using RareGeometry.Shipready.Save.Data; public static class CoopMigrations { [SaveMigration(fromVersion: 5)] static void QuestsBecomeJournal(SaveTree save) { save.RenameCategory(\"Quests\", \"Journal\"); SaveNode players = save.FindPath(\"players\"); if (players == null || !players.IsObject) return; for (int i = 0; i < players.MemberCount; i++) { SaveNode player = players.GetMemberAt(i); if (playe"},{"p":"Game Updates","h":"Renaming an enum value","u":"save-game-updates.html#renaming-an-enum-value","x":"Enums are saved by name, so reordering an enum or adding values never breaks a save. Renaming a value does: the old name no longer matches, so the default comes back. Rewrite the old name in a step: using RareGeometry.Shipready.Save; using RareGeometry.Shipready.Save.Data; public static class EnumMigrations { // Difficulty.Hard was renamed to Difficulty.Expert. [SaveMigration(fromVersion: 6)] static void HardBecomesExpert(SaveTree save) { save.VisitAll((name, node) => { if (!node.IsObject) return; SaveNode difficulty = node.GetMember(\"difficulty\"); if (difficulty != null && difficulty.Kind == "},{"p":"Game Updates","h":"Order and gaps","u":"save-game-updates.html#order-and-gaps","x":"Steps run in order. They start from the file's version and run one version at a time. A version 1 save in a version 4 build runs steps 1, 2 and 3, and each step sees the save as the step before left it. The whole chain is checked first. If any step is missing, nothing runs. The load returns MigrationMissing and names the missing version, and the file is left untouched (see SK121 ). The player's save loads as soon as a build with the missing step ships. Steps run wherever an old file is read: slots, autosaves, the quicksave and the global file. A save into an old file runs them too, so one file"},{"p":"Game Updates","h":"Duplicates","u":"save-game-updates.html#duplicates","x":"Each version should have exactly one step. If two [SaveMigration] methods are marked for the same version, Shipready uses only the first one. It picks it in a fixed order: assembly name, then full class name, then method name, compared character by character. The Console warns and names both methods. A step added with SaveKit.Migrations.Add wins over every [SaveMigration] step for that version."},{"p":"Game Updates","h":"When a step fails","u":"save-game-updates.html#when-a-step-fails","x":"Nothing is half-changed. Steps work on a copy of the save. If a step throws an exception, the load returns MigrationFailed , naming the step and the exception (see SK122 ). The file and the game's live data stay as they were. Write tolerant steps. Check FindPath and GetCategory results for null before you use them. Prefer the helpers, which do nothing when a key is missing. Steps run more than once. The same step runs on every load of an old file until that file is saved again, and on every slot. Keep it deterministic: the same input must always give the same output."},{"p":"Game Updates","h":"Steps run on a background thread","u":"save-game-updates.html#steps-run-on-a-background-thread","x":"Steps run on a background thread, together with the rest of the file work for that file. That is true for LoadAsync and SaveAsync , for autosaves, and for the blocking Load and Save too: those wait for the background work to finish, but the step itself still runs there. So a step must only edit the SaveTree . Inside a step, never touch: GameObjects, components or ScriptableObjects Resources , PlayerPrefs , Time or Application any other Unity API If a step needs a value such as a price table, write that value into the step's code as a constant."},{"p":"Game Updates","h":"Saves from a newer version are refused","u":"save-game-updates.html#saves-from-a-newer-version-are-refused","x":"If a file's saveVersion is higher than the build's Save Version , the load returns NewerVersion and leaves the file untouched (see SK120 ). This happens when a player goes back to an older build, for example through a beta branch or a rollback. SlotInfo.IsFromNewerVersion is true for such a slot. The save menu shows a \"From a newer version\" badge and does not load it. A save of only some categories into that slot is refused. A save of every category replaces the newer file. The newer file is kept as the newest backup while Backup Count is above 0. This is why Save Version must never go down af"},{"p":"Game Updates","h":"Tolerant loading: what needs no migration","u":"save-game-updates.html#tolerant-loading-what-needs-no-migration","x":"Missing values use their defaults. SaveKit.Data.Get(\"key\", fallback) and SaveReader.Read(name, fallback) return the fallback when the save has no such value. A ticked field the save does not hold keeps its current value. A [Serializable] class is filled in place, so fields added to it keep their defaults. A slot category the file does not hold starts empty. Objects deleted from a scene are skipped and reported. The load still succeeds. Each skipped object is listed in Report.SkippedObjects and in a warning (see SK130 ). New objects keep their scene defaults. An object the save knows nothing ab"},{"p":"Game Updates","h":"Changing compression or encryption in an update","u":"save-game-updates.html#changing-compression-or-encryption-in-an-update","x":"Each file records how it was packed ( packing in the header), and the loader follows the file, not the settings. That is why you can change these in an update without stranding old saves: File Options → Compress , Encrypt , Encrypt Slot Info and Pretty Print Backup Count , Checksum Policy and Automatic Fallback Use Slot Options For Global File and the packing options in Global File Options Giving the global file its own extension, such as .local , or taking it away again. Shipready then loads whichever of the old and the new global file was written last, so players keep their settings, and the"},{"p":"Game Updates","h":"IL2CPP and code stripping","u":"save-game-updates.html#il2cpp-and-code-stripping","x":"Nothing in your game calls a [SaveMigration] method directly. Shipready finds the methods by their attribute, which IL2CPP code stripping cannot see, so stripping could remove the very methods old saves need. To prevent this, Shipready adds a link file to every player build. It lists each class that holds a [SaveMigration] method and tells the linker to keep those classes. The file is written to Temp/RareGeometry.Shipready.Save/MigrationLink.xml in your project and passed straight to the linker. Unity clears the Temp folder when the Editor closes, so nothing is added to your Assets folder. The"},{"p":"Game Updates","h":"The pre-migration file stays as a backup","u":"save-game-updates.html#the-pre-migration-file-stays-as-a-backup","x":"A load never writes to the file. Until the player saves that slot again, the file on disk stays at its old version, and every load migrates it again. The first save in the new version writes the upgraded file. The old file becomes slot_01.save.bak1 , provided Backup Count is 1 or more. It then moves down one place with each later save, and it is deleted once more than Backup Count saves have followed. A step with a bug can therefore be caught while the original data still exists, but only for a few saves. Test before you release."},{"p":"Game Updates","h":"Testing with old save files","u":"save-game-updates.html#testing-with-old-save-files","x":"1. Keep real saves from every version you release. Before you raise Save Version , copy a few saves from the save folder into your project's repository: an early-game slot, a late-game slot, an autosave and global.save . A folder next to Assets , such as SaveFixtures/v1/ , keeps Unity from importing them. Commit them to version control. 2. Try them by hand. Copy a fixture into the save folder and load it in Play Mode. Check the values, and check that loaded.Report.MigrationsRun lists the steps you expect. The Save Inspector ( Tools → Shipready → Saves → Save Inspector ) can import a file and o"},{"p":"Game Updates","h":"Worked example: version 1 to version 3","u":"save-game-updates.html#worked-example-version-1-to-version-3","x":""},{"p":"Game Updates","h":"Version 1 (shipped with Save Version 1)","u":"save-game-updates.html#version-1-shipped-with-save-version-1","x":"using System; using RareGeometry.Shipready.Save; [Serializable] public class PlayerStats { public int health = 100; } public static class Version1Saving { public static void Store(int cash, PlayerStats stats, int lastHint) { SaveKit.Data.Set(\"cash\", cash, \"Progress\"); SaveKit.Data.Set(\"stats\", stats, \"Progress\"); SaveKit.Data.Set(\"lastHint\", lastHint, \"Quests\"); } } The data in a version 1 save: \"data\": { \"Progress\": { \"cash\": 4300, \"stats\": { \"health\": 80 } }, \"Quests\": { \"active\": [\"find_the_dog\"], \"lastHint\": 3 } }"},{"p":"Game Updates","h":"Version 2 (Save Version 2)","u":"save-game-updates.html#version-2-save-version-2","x":"cash is renamed to money . A new value, tutorialDone , is false in a new game. Players who already have a save have finished the tutorial, so their saves should get true ."},{"p":"Game Updates","h":"Version 3 (Save Version 3)","u":"save-game-updates.html#version-3-save-version-3","x":"The Quests category is renamed to Journal, in the settings asset under Categories too. The hint system is removed, so lastHint goes. PlayerStats.health becomes a float from 0 to 1 instead of a whole number from 0 to 100."},{"p":"Game Updates","h":"The steps in the version 3 build","u":"save-game-updates.html#the-steps-in-the-version-3-build","x":"using RareGeometry.Shipready.Save; using RareGeometry.Shipready.Save.Data; public static class StarFarmMigrations { [SaveMigration(fromVersion: 1, Description = \"money and tutorial\")] static void UpgradeTo2(SaveTree save) { save.RenameKeyEverywhere(\"cash\", \"money\"); save.AddDefaultBeside(\"money\", \"tutorialDone\", ValueConverters.From(true)); } [SaveMigration(fromVersion: 2, Description = \"journal, no hints, health as a fraction\")] static void UpgradeTo3(SaveTree save) { save.RenameCategory(\"Quests\", \"Journal\"); save.RemoveKeyEverywhere(\"lastHint\"); // The global file has no Progress category, s"},{"p":"Game Updates","h":"What happens","u":"save-game-updates.html#what-happens","x":"A version 1 save loads in the version 3 build. Steps 1 and 2 run, and loaded.Report.MigrationsRun is [1, 2] . A version 2 save runs step 2 only. A version 3 save runs nothing. The global file runs both steps too. They change nothing in it, because it holds neither cash , Quests nor Progress . The data after loading the version 1 save: \"data\": { \"Progress\": { \"money\": 4300, \"stats\": { \"health\": 0.8 }, \"tutorialDone\": true }, \"Journal\": { \"active\": [\"find_the_dog\"] } } A float is written with the fewest digits that read back as exactly the same float, so 0.8 shows as 0.8 . A few values need more"},{"p":"Co-op","h":"Co-op","u":"save-coop.html#co-op","x":"This guide is for programmers. It explains how Shipready saves a co-op game: who may save, how each player's own data is kept, how saved objects come back as networked objects, and what your network library still has to do. It ends with a walkthrough of the Netcode for GameObjects (NGO) sample. Shipready has no networking code of its own. Its core works with any networking library through three small interfaces in the namespace RareGeometry.Shipready.Save.Coop . The 1.0 sample uses NGO 2.x in Unity 6, and NGO is the only library shown and tested. Settings in bold, such as World Load Mode , are"},{"p":"Co-op","h":"The rules in short","u":"save-coop.html#the-rules-in-short","x":"The host owns the world. Only the host, or a solo player, saves and loads world data. A client that tries gets HostOnly ( SK140 ), and no file is touched. The host loads before friends join. There is no loading during a session in 1.0. The host can save at any time. Clients never see the save buttons. Each player's own data lives in the host's save , under a stable player ID that your game supplies, such as a Steam ID. Shipready restores state on the host only. Your network library shows that state to the clients. Every value a client must see has to be network-synced by your game. Every playe"},{"p":"Co-op","h":"The host-only model","u":"save-coop.html#the-host-only-model","x":""},{"p":"Co-op","h":"Who may save: ISaveAuthority","u":"save-coop.html#who-may-save-isaveauthority","x":"public interface ISaveAuthority { bool HasSaveAuthority { get; } } Set it with SaveKit.Authority . Left at null, the default, the game counts as a solo game and saving is always allowed. A game with no co-op never sets it. When HasSaveAuthority returns false, Shipready refuses these calls with HostOnly before it touches storage: SaveKit.Save , SaveAsync , Autosave , AutosaveAsync and Quicksave SaveKit.Load , LoadAsync , LoadNewestAutosave and LoadQuicksave , including the versions that take a SlotInfo SaveKit.Delete , RestoreBackup and RestoreBackupAsync The global file stays available, so a c"},{"p":"Co-op","h":"Hiding the save buttons","u":"save-coop.html#hiding-the-save-buttons","x":"SaveKit.CanSave and SaveKit.CanLoad are true when Shipready has started and there is no authority, or the authority says yes. Both are false on a connected client. Shipready's save menu checks them for you. A client sees the slot cards without their Save, Load, Delete and Restore backup buttons, and a line saying \"Only the host can save or load.\" Change that line in the menu's text asset ( HostOnlyLine ). For buttons of your own, check the same two properties. The sample's menu goes one step further. It shows Save only while hosting, and Load only before a session starts: // Hide what this pla"},{"p":"Co-op","h":"Clients keep their own settings","u":"save-coop.html#clients-keep-their-own-settings","x":"Every copy of the game keeps its own global file, client or host. Shipready loads it when it starts, and a load of the host's world never touches it. The sample remembers the name each player types this way: SaveKit.Data.Set(PlayerNameKey, playerName, SettingsCategory); SaveKit.SaveGlobalAsync();"},{"p":"Co-op","h":"The host loads before friends join","u":"save-coop.html#the-host-loads-before-friends-join","x":"A co-op session runs like this: The host picks a save, or a new game, from the main menu. The game loads the world on the host before the session starts. The host starts the session and friends join. The network library shows them the world, and the game gives each player their own data back by player ID. The host can save at any time. Clients do not see the save buttons. When a friend leaves, their data stays in the host's save and comes back the next time they join. Loading a different save during a session is not part of 1.0. To switch saves, end the session, load, and host again."},{"p":"Co-op","h":"Saving during a session","u":"save-coop.html#saving-during-a-session","x":"The host can save whenever it likes, by hand or through the autosave timer. The one exception is while SaveKit.LoadAsync is still loading the scenes of its save (see SK154 ), and that does not happen mid-session because the host loads first. SaveEvents.Saving is raised before a save gathers anything, for autosaves too. Handle it to write every connected player's latest values into SaveKit.Players , so they are part of that save. The sample's bridge does exactly that."},{"p":"Co-op","h":"Player IDs and per-player data","u":"save-coop.html#player-ids-and-per-player-data","x":""},{"p":"Co-op","h":"Stable player IDs: IPlayerIdentity","u":"save-coop.html#stable-player-ids-iplayeridentity","x":"public interface IPlayerIdentity { bool TryGetPlayerId(ulong connectionId, out string playerId); } connectionId is the network library's number for a connection. It changes every time a player connects, so it can never be the player ID. If your library uses int numbers, convert them to ulong . playerId must stay the same for one player across sessions. A Steam ID is a good choice, as is a platform account ID or an ID from your own login. Return false while a player's ID is not known yet, for example before their Steam ID arrives. Do not throw. If TryGetPlayerId throws, Shipready catches the ex"},{"p":"Co-op","h":"Per-player data: SaveKit.Players","u":"save-coop.html#per-player-data-savekitplayers","x":"SaveKit.Players holds each player's own data on the host: position, inventory, anything that belongs to one player rather than to the world. It is never null. Member What it does For(playerId) The player's SaveData , created empty the first time you ask. Null for a null or empty ID. You can keep the object: a load refills it in place. ForConnection(connectionId) The same, found through SaveKit.Identity . Null when there is no identity or it does not name the player, with an SK141 warning in the Console once per connection. TryGetPlayerId(connectionId, out playerId) Asks SaveKit.Identity quietl"},{"p":"Co-op","h":"The spawn hook: ISpawnHandler","u":"save-coop.html#the-spawn-hook-ispawnhandler","x":"public interface ISpawnHandler { GameObject Spawn(GameObject prefab, Vector3 position, Quaternion rotation, Transform parent); void Despawn(GameObject instance); } Set it with SaveKit.SpawnHandler . Left at null, Shipready uses Instantiate and Destroy . When Shipready calls it: Spawn : during a load, once for each saved runtime object, before its state is restored. A runtime object is an instance of a prefab that was created during play and saved with its prefab ID. The prefab comes from the Prefab Registry. For an object at the root of the scene whose Saveable saves its position, position and"},{"p":"Co-op","h":"Networked spawning on the host","u":"save-coop.html#networked-spawning-on-the-host","x":"On the host, the hook should spawn through your network library, so that every client gets the object. The sample's NgoSpawnHandler handles three cases: NetworkObject prefabObject = prefab.GetComponent<NetworkObject>(); if (prefabObject == null) return Object.Instantiate(prefab, position, rotation, parent); NetworkManager manager = _manager; bool listening = manager != null && manager.IsListening && !manager.ShutdownInProgress; if (listening && manager.IsServer) return SpawnOnServer(manager, prefab, prefabObject, position, rotation, parent); GameObject instance = Object.Instantiate(prefab, pos"},{"p":"Co-op","h":"Pools","u":"save-coop.html#pools","x":"A hook backed by a pool takes an object from the pool in Spawn and returns it to the pool in Despawn . Shipready gives the object its saved ID after Spawn returns, so the pool does not have to. Keep calling SaveKit.World.Despawned(obj) when your pool takes an object back during play, and SaveKit.World.Spawned(obj) when it hands one out, so an object waiting in the pool is never saved (see the pooled objects row in LimitsAndFAQ.md )."},{"p":"Co-op","h":"What your network library must sync","u":"save-coop.html#what-your-network-library-must-sync","x":"Shipready restores state on the host only. It never sends anything over the network. Getting the restored state to the clients is the network library's job, so every value a client must see has to be network-synced by your game, for example with a NetworkVariable . What is saved What the host has after a load How clients see it in the sample Runtime-spawned networked objects Re-created through the spawn hook as networked objects NGO sends spawned NetworkObjects to every client Position and rotation Set on the host's transform A NetworkTransform with server authority Fields of your scripts (Fie"},{"p":"Co-op","h":"Other networking libraries","u":"save-coop.html#other-networking-libraries","x":"The core is not tied to NGO. Any library that has a host, or a server, connects to Shipready through the same three interfaces and two habits. Your library's own documentation tells you which of its calls answer each question below. ISaveAuthority : may this copy of the game save? Return true when it is not in a session, which is what lets the host load before hosting, and when it is the host or a dedicated server. Return false for a connected client. Set it with SaveKit.Authority . IPlayerIdentity : who is this connection? Map the library's number for a connection to the stable ID the player "},{"p":"Co-op","h":"The NGO sample","u":"save-coop.html#the-ngo-sample","x":"The sample is a small host-and-join game. The host saves a world of spawned cubes, friends join and see it, and a friend who leaves and joins again gets their own position and inventory back. A short version of this walkthrough ships with it as Samples/CoopNGO/README.md ."},{"p":"Co-op","h":"What you need","u":"save-coop.html#what-you-need","x":"Unity 6. The sample was tested in Unity 6000.6. Netcode for GameObjects 2.x , from the Package Manager. It installs Unity Transport. The sample was tested with NGO 2.13.3; its assembly accepts any 2.x version. TextMesh Pro's essential resources , for the menu's text. Import them with Window → TextMeshPro → Import TMP Essential Resources if the generator warns that they are missing. Either input system works: the Input System package or the old Input Manager. The sample makes its materials for the active render pipeline at runtime. It was checked in URP; HDRP gets automated tests only. Shipread"},{"p":"Co-op","h":"Building the sample","u":"save-coop.html#building-the-sample","x":"The sample ships as scripts only. A menu command builds its scene and prefabs, because the Asset Store limits packages nested inside packages. Install Netcode for GameObjects 2.x with the Package Manager. Choose Tools → Shipready → Saves → Samples → Build Co-op NGO Sample . The menu item appears once NGO 2.x is installed. Press Build . The scene that is open now is closed first, with the usual offer to save it. When asked, press Add to put the sample scene in the build's scene list. Two builds need it. The command builds everything in Assets/RareGeometryData/Shipready/Samples/CoopNGO/ , outsid"},{"p":"Co-op","h":"Trying it","u":"save-coop.html#trying-it","x":"With two builds: Check that CoopNGO.unity is in the build's scene list ( File → Build Profiles , Scene List). Build for Windows and start the build twice. One build plus Play in the Editor also works. In the first window, type a name and press Host new game . Windows may ask whether to let the game through the firewall. Allow it on private networks if a second computer is going to join. In the second window, type the host's address and a different name, then press Join . On the same computer the address is 127.0.0.1 . From another computer on your network, use the host computer's address. The "},{"p":"Co-op","h":"What each script does","u":"save-coop.html#what-each-script-does","x":"All of them are in Samples/CoopNGO/Scripts , in the namespace RareGeometry.Shipready.Save.Samples.NGO . CoopSaveBridge connects Shipready to a Netcode session. Two calls, once Shipready has started, are all a host needs: CoopSaveBridge bridge = CoopSaveBridge.Install(NetworkManager.Singleton); bridge.StartHost(); // or bridge.StartServer(); a client calls NetworkManager.StartClient() Install it on every peer , host and clients alike. It turns connection approval on, and connection approval is part of Netcode's configuration check, so a client whose setting differs from the host's cannot connec"},{"p":"Co-op","h":"How the host loads before hosting","u":"save-coop.html#how-the-host-loads-before-hosting","x":"The host loads while it is not yet in a session, so the network cannot spawn anything at that moment. The sample handles this in five steps: Start. Shipready runs with World Load Mode set to Restore Into Current Scenes, so a load restores into the scene that is already open. The bridge is installed, and the NetworkManager is not listening. Load. The host presses Load. NgoSaveAuthority says yes, because the manager is not listening. SaveKit.LoadAsync brings back SaveKit.Players and re-creates the saved cubes through NgoSpawnHandler.Spawn . With no server running, each cube is instantiated and k"},{"p":"Co-op","h":"Starting a new game","u":"save-coop.html#starting-a-new-game","x":"A co-op host starts a new game in the scene that is already open, so the old world has to go before hosting. The sample's Host new game does it with three calls: void StartNewGame() { if (_bridge != null) _bridge.SpawnHandler.DiscardPending(); SaveKit.World.ClearAll(); SaveKit.Players.Clear(); } DiscardPending() throws away any items a load made that are still waiting to be hosted. SaveKit.World.ClearAll() forgets scene memory, destroyed lists and spawned objects, and SaveKit.Players.Clear() forgets every player's data. They leave SaveKit.Data , the playtime and the slot name alone. The sample"},{"p":"Co-op","h":"Adapting it to your game","u":"save-coop.html#adapting-it-to-your-game","x":"Copy what you need. Copy the scripts out of Samples/CoopNGO/Scripts into your own folder and namespace, because a Shipready update replaces the sample folder. CoopSaveBridge , NgoSaveAuthority , NgoPlayerIdentity , NgoSpawnHandler and CoopConnectionPayload are the reusable parts. CoopPlayer , CoopItem and the menu are examples. Install after Shipready starts, on every peer. Call CoopSaveBridge.Install(NetworkManager.Singleton) once Shipready has started, which with Initialise Automatically on is already the case in your first scene's Awake , and before the host or client starts. Install it on "},{"p":"Co-op","h":"Limits","u":"save-coop.html#limits","x":"There is no loading during a session. The host loads before friends join. Only Netcode for GameObjects 2.x is shown and tested , with NGO 2.13.3. Other libraries can use the three interfaces, but 1.0 has no samples for them. A dropped connection lingers. A player whose connection dropped stays in the game until Unity Transport's disconnect timeout, 30 seconds by default. Rejoining under the same ID is turned away until then. Switched-off items stay local. An item saved switched off is re-created switched off and stays a local object on the host, still saved, until it is switched on and SpawnPe"},{"p":"Co-op","h":"Contact","u":"save-coop.html#contact","x":"Report a bug with the bug report form , ask a question in Discussions , email the address in Tools > Shipready > Saves > Help or visit https://raregeometry.github.io/ ."},{"p":"Steam Cloud","h":"Steam Cloud","u":"save-steam-cloud.html#steam-cloud","x":"Steam Auto-Cloud copies the save files you choose to Steam's servers when the player quits. When the game starts again, on any PC, Steam copies them back. You set it up on the Steamworks partner site. It needs no game code, and Shipready works with it as it is. This guide gives the Auto-Cloud settings that match Shipready's folders and file names. Steam's own documentation is at partner.steamgames.com/doc/features/cloud . Scope. Shipready 1.0 is tested on Windows, so this guide covers Windows builds. Shipready has no built-in cloud sync and never talks to Steam. Steam does all the copying."},{"p":"Steam Cloud","h":"Before you start","u":"save-steam-cloud.html#before-you-start","x":"Choose these names before your first release. Changing any of them later makes Shipready look in a different folder or for different files, so existing saves seem to disappear (see SK100 ): Company Name and Product Name in Project Settings → Player . Save Folder Name , Profile Folder , the file name patterns, File Options → File Extension and, if you use it, Global File Options → File Extension in the settings asset ShipreadySaveSettings . Settings in bold, such as Save Folder Name , are fields on that settings asset unless the text says otherwise. Select the asset in the Project window to cha"},{"p":"Steam Cloud","h":"Step 1: Set the byte quota and the file count","u":"save-steam-cloud.html#step-1-set-the-byte-quota-and-the-file-count","x":"Steam keeps Auto-Cloud locked until both limits are set. Open your app's Steam Cloud Settings page on the Steamworks partner site. Fill in Byte quota per user and Number of files allowed per user . Click Save at the bottom of the page, then publish the change. Choosing the file count. Each save is one file. Add up: Slot Count (10 by default) Autosave File Count (3 by default) 1 for the global file, which holds the Settings category, unless you keep it on the PC (Step 4) 1 for the quicksave, if Quicksave Enabled is on With the defaults that is 14 files. Leave room to grow, for example 20. The m"},{"p":"Steam Cloud","h":"Step 2: Add the Root Path","u":"save-steam-cloud.html#step-2-add-the-root-path","x":"On Windows, Unity keeps each game's files in this folder, which is Application.persistentDataPath : %USERPROFILE%\\AppData\\LocalLow\\<Company Name>\\<Product Name> Shipready saves into the Save Folder Name folder inside it, which is Saves by default. In the Auto-Cloud section, add one Root Path: Field Value Root WinAppDataLocalLow Subdirectory <Company Name>/<Product Name>/Saves Pattern *.save OS [Cross-Platform] , the default Recursive Off Subdirectory: copy Company Name and Product Name exactly as they appear in Project Settings → Player . For example, the company MyStudio and the product Star "},{"p":"Steam Cloud","h":"Step 3: Give each Steam user their own folder","u":"save-steam-cloud.html#step-3-give-each-steam-user-their-own-folder","x":"Two people can share one PC with two Steam accounts. If both accounts use the same save folder, they share one set of saves, and Steam uploads the same files into both accounts. Two placeholders, one on each side, keep them apart: On Steam's side , the Subdirectory may contain {64BitSteamID} . Steam replaces it with the signed-in user's 64-bit Steam ID, such as 76561198000000000 . On Shipready's side , Profile Folder set to {profile} puts the saves into a folder inside the save folder, named after SaveKit.ProfileId . Your game sets SaveKit.ProfileId to the same 64-bit Steam ID, so both sides n"},{"p":"Steam Cloud","h":"Step 4: Keep device-specific settings local","u":"save-steam-cloud.html#step-4-keep-device-specific-settings-local","x":"Steam advises against syncing machine-specific settings such as video quality. A laptop and a gaming PC need different values. In Shipready, the categories marked Global (Settings by default) are kept in the global file, which is global.save by default. That name matches *.save , so it syncs. That suits preferences that should follow the player, such as subtitles, key bindings and volume. It does not suit screen resolution, graphics quality or the chosen monitor. Keep the global file on the PC by giving it its own extension. The global file can have file options of its own, including its own e"},{"p":"Steam Cloud","h":"Step 5: What stays local","u":"save-steam-cloud.html#step-5-what-stays-local","x":"Every file Shipready writes for slot 1 is listed below. Only files that end in .save match the pattern *.save . File What it is Synced by *.save slot_01.save The save Yes autosave_1.save to autosave_3.save The rotating autosaves Yes quicksave.save The quicksave, when it is switched on Yes global.save The Global categories, such as Settings, while the global file uses the slots' extension Yes global.local The same file, when Global File Options → File Extension is .local (Step 4) No slot_01.save.tmp A save that was still being written No slot_01.save.bak0 The file being replaced, for a moment d"},{"p":"Steam Cloud","h":"Step 6: Test","u":"save-steam-cloud.html#step-6-test","x":"Steam's test steps: Sign in to Steam with an account that owns the game. If the game is already released, you can tick Enable cloud support for developers only while you test. Then only accounts with a developer licence for the game see the change. Open the Steam console by entering steam://open/console in your web browser. In the console, enter testappcloudpaths <AppId> , for example testappcloudpaths 480 . Enter set_spew_level 4 4 for detailed cloud logging. Start the game from Steam, save into a slot and quit. Steam uploads when the game exits. Watch the console. On a second PC, signed in t"},{"p":"Steam Cloud","h":"If nothing syncs","u":"save-steam-cloud.html#if-nothing-syncs","x":"Check that the Steamworks changes were published. Then wait up to 10 minutes, or restart Steam. Read cloud_log.txt in the logs folder of the Steam installation, for example C:\\Program Files (x86)\\Steam\\logs\\cloud_log.txt . It is Steam's own record of each sync. Compare the folder Steam uses with the folder from the Console line in Step 2. The usual causes are: - Company Name or Product Name spelled differently, including spaces and capital letters. - Save Folder Name changed in the settings asset but not in Steamworks. - Profile Folder set to {64BitSteamID} , which Shipready does not know, ins"},{"p":"Steam Cloud","h":"Settings at a glance","u":"save-steam-cloud.html#settings-at-a-glance","x":"Where Setting Value Steamworks Byte quota per user Largest save × file count, doubled Steamworks Number of files allowed per user Slots + autosaves + 1 global + 1 quicksave, plus room Steamworks Root WinAppDataLocalLow Steamworks Subdirectory <Company Name>/<Product Name>/Saves , or .../Saves/{64BitSteamID} Steamworks Pattern *.save Steamworks OS [Cross-Platform] Steamworks Recursive Off Settings asset Save Folder Name Saves , or the same name as in the Subdirectory Settings asset Profile Folder {profile} , with the Subdirectory ending in /{64BitSteamID} ; empty without it Settings asset Initi"},{"p":"Shipready: Reference","h":"Shipready: Reference","u":"save-reference.html#shipready-reference","x":"Version 1.0 · Rare Geometry This Reference explains how each part of Shipready works: the save tree and files, what can be saved, world persistence in depth, reliability and recovery, file options, the save menu's options, the editor tools, performance and logging. For step-by-step tasks, see the Guide ; for the C# API, the API Reference . Settings in bold, such as Save Version , are fields on the settings asset ShipreadySaveSettings . To change one, select the asset in the Project window; most are on its Basics tab, the rest under Advanced ."},{"p":"Shipready: Reference","h":"Contents","u":"save-reference.html#contents","x":"Core concepts - The save tree - Files: slots, the global file, autosaves and the quicksave - File names and the save folder - Profile folders: several players on one PC - Categories - Save versions - The settings asset Saving plain values - What can be saved - Dictionaries Saving and loading - Threads and the file queue - Save Actions, Save Events and Save Point Slots and slot info - What a slot records World persistence - Parents - Load modes - Physics - Component savers - DontDestroyOnLoad objects - World data in the file - The registries Reliability and recovery - Crash-safe writes - Checks"},{"p":"Shipready: Reference","h":"Core concepts","u":"save-reference.html#core-concepts","x":""},{"p":"Shipready: Reference","h":"The save tree","u":"save-reference.html#the-save-tree","x":"Everything Shipready saves goes into one structure, the save tree. A save file is a small envelope around it: a header : the save format, your game's save version, the game version, Shipready version, the time of the save in UTC, a checksum and how the file is packed; the slot info the menu shows: name, playtime, location, game version and summary lines; the data : one section per category, plus a players section in co-op games. In memory, the data part is SaveKit.Data . Your plain values go there, and the Saveable objects in your scenes write their state there when a save gathers it. A save c"},{"p":"Shipready: Reference","h":"Files: slots, the global file, autosaves and the quicksave","u":"save-reference.html#files-slots-the-global-file-autosaves-and-the-quicksave","x":"File Default name What it holds Manual slots slot_01.save to slot_10.save One playthrough each. 10 slots by default, adjustable from 1 to 99. The global file global.save The Global categories, such as Settings. One file for the whole game. Autosaves autosave_1.save to autosave_3.save Each autosave overwrites the oldest of these files, so an autosave never replaces the newest one. 3 by default, adjustable from 1 to 10. The quicksave quicksave.save One extra slot for a quick-save key. Off by default. Slot files, autosaves and the quicksave hold the Slot categories. The global file holds the Glob"},{"p":"Shipready: Reference","h":"File names and the save folder","u":"save-reference.html#file-names-and-the-save-folder","x":"Saves are kept in a folder inside Application.persistentDataPath . On Windows that is: %USERPROFILE%\\AppData\\LocalLow\\<Company Name>\\<Product Name>\\Saves Company Name and Product Name come from Project Settings → Player . The Editor and a Windows build use the same folder. Saves is Save Folder Name . If Profile Folder is set, saves go into that folder inside it (see Profile folders ). Once Shipready has started, SaveKit.Storage.DescribeLocation() returns the folder. The Save Inspector's Copy folder path button shows it in Explorer. You can change the names in the settings asset: Setting Defaul"},{"p":"Shipready: Reference","h":"Profile folders: several players on one PC","u":"save-reference.html#profile-folders-several-players-on-one-pc","x":"Profile Folder puts the saves into a folder inside the save folder, so that two people who share a PC each keep their own saves. A fixed name , such as Player1 , is used exactly as written. {profile} is replaced, when Shipready starts, by SaveKit.ProfileId , which your game sets at runtime, for example to the player's Steam ID. A Profile Folder of {profile} puts the saves of the Steam user 76561198000000000 into Saves/76561198000000000 . The placeholder can also sit inside a longer name, such as Profile_{profile} . Shipready never works out the id itself: it only knows what your game gives it."},{"p":"Shipready: Reference","h":"Categories","u":"save-reference.html#categories","x":"Every saved value belongs to one category. A category decides two things: which file the value goes into, and which saves include it. Slot categories are stored inside each save slot. Progress, inventory, quests and world state belong here. Global categories are stored in the one global file. Player settings belong here. A new settings asset starts with five categories: Category Scope Role Settings Global Player settings Progress Slot The Default Category : values saved without a category go here Inventory Slot Quests Slot World Slot The World Category : Saveable objects are saved here unless "},{"p":"Shipready: Reference","h":"Save versions","u":"save-reference.html#save-versions","x":"Every file records the save version it was written with. Save Version is a whole number in the settings asset, 1 by default. Raise it when your saved data changes in a way old saves need help with, and write a small migration step that upgrades version N to N + 1. Migration steps run in order when an old file loads. Saves from a newer version of your game are refused with NewerVersion ( SK120 ) and left untouched. GameUpdates.md explains when to raise the version, how to write steps, the tree editing helpers, and how to test with old save files."},{"p":"Shipready: Reference","h":"The settings asset","u":"save-reference.html#the-settings-asset","x":"Every setting, with its default. Select the asset to edit them: its Inspector has a Basics tab for what most games touch and an Advanced tab whose sections start collapsed. The Inspector uses plainer labels than the field names below, which are the names used in code; the In the Inspector column gives both. The Setup Wizard edits the Basics. Group Setting Default In the Inspector What it does Start-up Initialise Automatically on Basics: Start Shipready by itself Starts Shipready by itself before the first scene loads. Off: call SaveKit.Initialise() yourself. See Starting Shipready . Files Save"},{"p":"Shipready: Reference","h":"Saving plain values","u":"save-reference.html#saving-plain-values","x":"More on this topic is in the Guide ."},{"p":"Shipready: Reference","h":"What can be saved","u":"save-reference.html#what-can-be-saved","x":"Kind Types Numbers int , long , float , double , decimal , short , byte , sbyte , ushort , uint , ulong Text and flags string , char , bool Time and ids DateTime (stored as UTC), TimeSpan , Guid , byte[] Enums Any enum, stored by name, so reordering or inserting values never breaks a save Unity values Vector2 , Vector3 , Vector4 , Vector2Int , Vector3Int , Quaternion , Color , Color32 , Rect , RectInt , Bounds , BoundsInt Collections List<T> and T[] of any of the above, or of your own classes Your own classes and structs Marked [Serializable] , saved under Unity's own rules through JsonUtility"},{"p":"Shipready: Reference","h":"Dictionaries","u":"save-reference.html#dictionaries","x":"A Dictionary<string, T> or a Dictionary<int, T> is saved like any other value, as long as T is a type Shipready can save: var kills = new Dictionary<string, int> { { \"slime\", 12 }, { \"bat\", 3 } }; SaveKit.Data.Set(\"kills\", kills, \"Progress\"); Dictionary<string, int> loaded = SaveKit.Data.Get(\"kills\", new Dictionary<string, int>(), \"Progress\"); Get builds a new dictionary from the save each time. When nothing is saved under the key, it returns your fallback. In the file, a dictionary is a JSON object. int keys are written as text, such as \"12\" . A dictionary with other keys, such as an enum, or"},{"p":"Shipready: Reference","h":"Saving and loading","u":"save-reference.html#saving-and-loading","x":"More on this topic is in the Guide ."},{"p":"Shipready: Reference","h":"Save Actions, Save Events and Save Point","u":"save-reference.html#save-actions-save-events-and-save-point","x":"Component Settings Notes Save Actions Continue Scene , New Game Scene , Delete All Includes Settings , On Succeeded , On Failed (with the reason) Methods for UnityEvents: SaveToSlot(int) , LoadSlot(int) , Quicksave() , Quickload() , Autosave() , Continue() , NewGame() , DeleteSlot(int) , DeleteAllSaves() . Saves and loads run in the background; deletes are immediate. Running counts the actions still working Save Events ( SaveEventListener ) Slots , Autosaves , Quicksave (on), Global File (off); seven UnityEvents Listens while enabled. A file of no known kind counts as a slot when the event nam"},{"p":"Shipready: Reference","h":"Threads and the file queue","u":"save-reference.html#threads-and-the-file-queue","x":"Capturing and restoring run on the main thread, because Unity's APIs require it. Reading the old file, migrating, merging, serialising, compressing, encrypting and writing run on a background thread for the Async calls and for SaveInBackground and LoadInBackground . The blocking calls do the same work on the calling thread. Profiler markers show which thread each stage ran on. Work for one file runs one item at a time, in the order it was asked for. Two saves fired in a row both finish, and the file holds the second one."},{"p":"Shipready: Reference","h":"Slots and slot info","u":"save-reference.html#slots-and-slot-info","x":"More on this topic is in the Guide ."},{"p":"Shipready: Reference","h":"What a slot records","u":"save-reference.html#what-a-slot-records","x":"Each save records slot info that the menu reads without unpacking the game data: SlotInfo member What it is SlotNumber The slot, counting from 1; 0 for an autosave or the quicksave FileName , Exists The file, and whether there is a save in it Name SaveKit.SlotName at the time of the save, or Slot N when the game set none SavedUtc When it was saved, in UTC (use ToLocalTime() to show it) PlaytimeSeconds Playtime at the time of the save Location SaveKit.Location at the time of the save Summary Up to four short lines, such as \"Day 12\" and \"$4,300\". The menu shows the first two. GameVersion , SaveV"},{"p":"Shipready: Reference","h":"World persistence","u":"save-reference.html#world-persistence","x":"More on this topic is in the Guide ."},{"p":"Shipready: Reference","h":"Parents","u":"save-reference.html#parents","x":"The Position saver records the parent when the parent is a saved object or the scene root, and restores it in the second pass, once every object exists. A scene object under a parent that is not saved keeps the parent it has in the scene. A runtime object under a parent that is not saved, such as a plain \"Enemies\" container, records that parent's path in the scene and its world pose, so it comes back in the same place. The path assumes sibling names are unique and contain no / . If the parent cannot be found, the object goes to the scene root at the same world position."},{"p":"Shipready: Reference","h":"Load modes","u":"save-reference.html#load-modes","x":"World Load Mode in the settings decides how a load treats scenes. SaveKit.World.LoadMode changes it at runtime. Mode LoadAsync Load (blocking) Load Saved Scenes (default) Loads the scenes the save was made in, restores each as it loads, sets the active scene, then fires Loaded Restores into the scenes that are loaded now Restore Into Current Scenes Restores into the scenes that are loaded now Restores into the scenes that are loaded now When restoring into the scenes that are loaded now, the state of any other saved scene stays in memory and is applied when that scene loads. A file that holds "},{"p":"Shipready: Reference","h":"Component savers","u":"save-reference.html#component-savers","x":"The Animator, Audio Source, Enabled and NavMesh Agent savers are components beside a Saveable, saved in its entry under their own name like any ISaveable (see World data in the file ). Each compiles only when its Unity module is switched on in the Package Manager (Animation, Audio, AI); the Enabled saver always does. Component Stored members Notes Animator Saver parameters (one member per float, int or bool parameter), stateHashes , stateTimes , layerWeights Save Parameters and Save States switch the two halves. A state that no longer exists after a game update is left to the controller. The s"},{"p":"Shipready: Reference","h":"Physics","u":"save-reference.html#physics","x":"Every body whose position a restore puts back is held kinematic for at least one simulated physics step, then released with its saved velocities and sleep state (or with its own settings when the Physics saver is off). Objects that are restored touching each other, such as stacked crates, therefore start from rest instead of bursting apart. The release happens in FixedUpdate . If Physics.simulationMode is set to Update or Script, the release may not line up with your simulation step."},{"p":"Shipready: Reference","h":"DontDestroyOnLoad objects","u":"save-reference.html#dontdestroyonload-objects","x":"Saveables under DontDestroyOnLoad , such as a game manager, are saved in a separate persistent section of their category, not with any scene. A load restores them first, before the scenes. Their destruction is not recorded by the Destroyed saver."},{"p":"Shipready: Reference","h":"World data in the file","u":"save-reference.html#world-data-in-the-file","x":"A readable save, shortened, with ids cut short for space: { \"aiosk\": 1, \"header\": { \"saveVersion\": 1, \"gameVersion\": \"0.9.2\", \"kitVersion\": \"1.0.0\", \"savedUtc\": \"2026-11-02T18:41:07Z\", \"checksum\": \"crc32:5F1A9C02\", \"packing\": \"readable\" }, \"info\": { \"name\": \"Slot 1\", \"playtimeSeconds\": 8123, \"location\": \"Downtown\", \"gameVersion\": \"0.9.2\", \"summary\": [\"Day 12\", \"$4,300\"] }, \"data\": { \"Progress\": { \"gold\": 4300, \"day\": 12 }, \"World\": { \"scenes\": { \"4f2a9c…\": { \"label\": \"Assets/Levels/Town.unity\", \"objects\": { \"8c1f…\": { \"name\": \"Chest_03\", \"Position\": { \"p\": [12, 0, -4], \"r\": [0, 0, 0, 1], \"s\": "},{"p":"Shipready: Reference","h":"The registries","u":"save-reference.html#the-registries","x":"Three registry assets, created by the wizard and linked from the settings asset, let Shipready find things again: Registry Lists Kept up to date Prefab Registry Prefabs with a Saveable on the root, by asset GUID Automatically: prefabs are checked whenever Unity imports them, and deleted ones leave the registry Scene Registry Scenes, by asset GUID, with their path Automatically: every scene in Build Settings is listed, and moving or renaming a listed scene updates its path Asset Registry Assets a save may link to, such as item definitions By you: Diagnostics registers assets linked from ticked "},{"p":"Shipready: Reference","h":"Reliability and recovery","u":"save-reference.html#reliability-and-recovery","x":"The promise: at every moment during a save, at least one complete, valid copy of it is on disk, and nothing Shipready does throws into your game."},{"p":"Shipready: Reference","h":"Crash-safe writes","u":"save-reference.html#crash-safe-writes","x":"Every save follows the same steps: Capture the values on the main thread. Serialise, compress and encrypt (when switched on). Write slot_01.save.tmp , flush it to the disk, read it back and check it. Swap it into place with the operating system's atomic replace, then rotate the backups. Write the thumbnail and the slot cache, then report. The old save stays intact until the new one is proven good. Anything that goes wrong before the swap leaves every file as it was. A problem tidying the backups after the swap is a warning on a successful save, never a failed one."},{"p":"Shipready: Reference","h":"Checksums","u":"save-reference.html#checksums","x":"Every file carries a CRC-32 checksum of its data, checked on every load. A readable file whose data no longer matches its checksum counts as damaged (see Edited saves for the exceptions). An encrypted file also carries a tamper check (see Encryption )."},{"p":"Shipready: Reference","h":"Rotating backups","u":"save-reference.html#rotating-backups","x":"Each save keeps File Options → Backup Count older copies, 2 by default, adjustable from 0 to 5: .bak1 is the newest, .bak2 the one before. A load tries the main file first, then a complete .tmp , then .bak1 , .bak2 and so on, and the first valid copy wins. Only damage makes a load move on; a locked file or a missing drive reports itself instead."},{"p":"Shipready: Reference","h":"Automatic fallback","u":"save-reference.html#automatic-fallback","x":"File Options → Automatic Fallback is on by default. On: a damaged slot, autosave or quicksave loads from its newest valid backup. The result is a success with the code RecoveredFromBackup ( SK102 ), LoadedFrom and SavedUtc say which copy was used, and the RecoveredFromBackup event fires. Off: loading a damaged save fails with its damage code and moves nothing. The slot info reports IsDamaged and HasValidBackup , and the menu offers Restore backup, which calls SaveKit.RestoreBackup . The global file always falls back , whatever this setting says. Saving over a damaged slot works either way. The"},{"p":"Shipready: Reference","h":"Damaged files are kept","u":"save-reference.html#damaged-files-are-kept","x":"When a load falls back, the damaged main file is renamed .corrupt-<time> and kept for support, never deleted. A file with no valid copy at all gives a clean FileDamaged result ( SK101 ); the game keeps running and nothing is deleted."},{"p":"Shipready: Reference","h":"Disk full and access denied","u":"save-reference.html#disk-full-and-access-denied","x":"If the disk is full or the folder cannot be written, the save fails with DiskFull ( SK110 ) or AccessDenied ( SK111 ), and every existing save stays byte-for-byte as it was. A save tree nested deeper than 128 levels is refused before anything is written, because it could never be read back."},{"p":"Shipready: Reference","h":"What the player sees","u":"save-reference.html#what-the-player-sees","x":"In the save menu, a damaged slot shows a Damaged badge. Loading it with fallback on shows \"Restored from backup (date)\". With fallback off, the card shows a Restore backup button. A slot whose details come from a backup copy shows a Restored badge. See The save and load menu ."},{"p":"Shipready: Reference","h":"File options","u":"save-reference.html#file-options","x":"File Options in the settings asset decides how the slots, the autosaves and the quicksave are written. The global file is written the same way, unless you give it options of its own (see The global file's own options ). Setting Default What it does Pretty Print Automatic Indents readable files. Automatic: indented in the Editor and in development builds, compact in release builds. Always or Never force it. Compress off GZip-compresses the data Encrypt off Encrypts the data, with a tamper check Encrypt Slot Info off Encrypts the slot info too. Off, the menu reads names, dates and summaries with"},{"p":"Shipready: Reference","h":"Readable JSON and pretty printing","u":"save-reference.html#readable-json-and-pretty-printing","x":"Saves are readable JSON by default: any text editor opens them, and they are easy to support. With Pretty Print on Automatic, files are indented while you develop and compact in a release build, which is the compact mode for shipping. Whitespace never changes the checksum."},{"p":"Shipready: Reference","h":"Compression","u":"save-reference.html#compression","x":"GZip makes large saves several times smaller. It is off by default because typical saves are small. Compressed files can no longer be read in a text editor; the Save Inspector still opens them."},{"p":"Shipready: Reference","h":"Encryption","u":"save-reference.html#encryption","x":"Encryption stops players editing saves in a text editor, and its tamper check notices edits. How it works: AES-256 in CBC mode with a random IV for every write, and an HMAC-SHA256 over the header and the encrypted data (encrypt-then-MAC). The keys are derived once, when Shipready starts, from Passphrase , Key Salt and Key Iterations with PBKDF2 (SHA-256). A changed byte fails the tamper check ( TamperDetected , SK105 ) and the backup loads. A file written with another passphrase gives EncryptionKeyWrong ( SK106 ). Its limits: the key ships inside your game, so a determined player can dig it ou"},{"p":"Shipready: Reference","h":"How the options combine","u":"save-reference.html#how-the-options-combine","x":"Data is compressed first, then encrypted, because encrypted data cannot be compressed. A compressed or encrypted file uses a binary envelope: a short marker, a readable header, then the packed data. The loader tells readable and packed files apart from their first bytes, and the header records which options were used, so any mix loads correctly."},{"p":"Shipready: Reference","h":"Changing options in an update","u":"save-reference.html#changing-options-in-an-update","x":"Each file records how it was written, and the loader follows the file, not the settings. You can switch compression or encryption on or off in a game update and old files still load; they are written the new way the next time they are saved. The same goes for giving the global file its own options, or taking them away again (see below). The one thing you must not change after release is the passphrase, salt or iteration count. See GameUpdates.md ."},{"p":"Shipready: Reference","h":"Edited saves: the checksum policy","u":"save-reference.html#edited-saves-the-checksum-policy","x":"A readable save edited by hand no longer matches its checksum. Treat Edits As Damaged (default): in a build, the file counts as damaged and the newest backup loads instead ( ChecksumMismatch , SK104 ). This also catches files that were cut short. Accept With Warning: the file loads, with a warning in the report. Use this for moddable games. The Editor always accepts edited files , with a warning, so you can tweak saves while you test."},{"p":"Shipready: Reference","h":"The global file's own options","u":"save-reference.html#the-global-files-own-options","x":"The global file holds the player's settings. By default it is written with File Options , like the slots. To give it options of its own, tick Write it differently under Shared file on the settings asset's Advanced tab (in code, untick Use Slot Options For Global File ) and fill in Global File Options , or tick Different options for the settings file in the wizard's File options step. Global File Options has the same fields as File Options . The global file then uses its own Pretty Print , Compress , Encrypt , Encrypt Slot Info , Backup Count , Checksum Policy and File Extension . Passphrase , "},{"p":"Shipready: Reference","h":"The save and load menu","u":"save-reference.html#the-save-and-load-menu","x":"More on this topic is in the Guide ."},{"p":"Shipready: Reference","h":"Modes and cards","u":"save-reference.html#modes-and-cards","x":"Save mode lists the manual slots. Load mode lists the autosaves (newest first), the quicksave when it is switched on and exists, then the manual slots that hold a save, then the empty ones. Empty slots are dimmed, show \"Empty\" and cannot be loaded. Each card shows the thumbnail (or \"No preview\"), the name, the local date and time, the playtime, the location, the first two summary lines, and badges: Badge Means Autosave / Quicksave The card is an autosave or the quicksave Damaged The main file is damaged Restored The card's details come from a backup copy From a newer version Saved by a newer v"},{"p":"Shipready: Reference","h":"Actions and confirmations","u":"save-reference.html#actions-and-confirmations","x":"Action What happens Save to an empty slot Saves straight away Save over a used slot Asks \"Overwrite …?\" first. A damaged or newer-version slot gets its own warning. Load Asks \"Load …? Unsaved progress will be lost.\" first, unless Ask before loading is off Load a damaged slot (fallback on) Loads the newest good backup; the status line says \"Restored from backup (date)\" and the menu stays open so the player can read it Restore backup (fallback off) Puts the newest good backup back; asks first only when Ask before restoring a backup is on Delete Asks \"Delete …? This cannot be undone.\" first Save,"},{"p":"Shipready: Reference","h":"Menu settings","u":"save-reference.html#menu-settings","x":"On the SaveMenu component: Setting Default What it does Theme , Words the default assets The look and the words Ask before loading ( ConfirmLoad ) on Ask before loading Ask before restoring a backup ( ConfirmRestore ) off Ask before Restore backup List autosaves in the Load menu ( ShowAutosavesInLoadMode ) on The player can load an autosave from the Load menu List the quicksave in the Load menu ( ShowQuicksaveInLoadMode ) on The player can load the quicksave from the Load menu Take a picture for each save ( CaptureThumbnail ) on Take a thumbnail just before the Save menu shows Camera for the p"},{"p":"Shipready: Reference","h":"The menu's look and layout","u":"save-reference.html#the-menus-look-and-layout","x":"The look lives on a Menu Theme asset: every colour, the TextMesh Pro font (empty uses TMP's default), seven font sizes, Corner Radius (0 to 48), Selection Outline (1 to 8) and Card Spacing (0 to 64). Its Layout block ( MenuTheme.Layout , a MenuLayout ) arranges the menu without any prefab edit: Setting Default What it does Columns 1 1 is the list; 2 to 4 is a grid of tiles with the picture on top and the buttons at the bottom Card Height 184 A list card's height, 140 to 320. Grid tiles size themselves Show Thumbnail , Show Date , Show Playtime , Show Location , Show Summary , Show Badges on Wh"},{"p":"Shipready: Reference","h":"Controls","u":"save-reference.html#controls","x":"Mouse and touch work through uGUI pointer events. Buttons are at least 64 pixels tall at the 1920×1080 reference resolution. Keyboard and gamepad work through uGUI navigation, with the old Input Manager, the Input System, or both: arrow keys or the d-pad move between cards and their buttons, Submit presses, Cancel (Escape, or East/B on a gamepad) closes the open confirmation, or else the menu. Something is always selected while the menu is open, and the list scrolls to keep the selected card in view, also with 99 slots. Closing the menu gives the selection back to whatever was selected before "},{"p":"Shipready: Reference","h":"The Game Menu","u":"save-reference.html#the-game-menu","x":"GameMenu.prefab in UI/Prefabs . Its own canvas sits under the save menu's (sort order 90 to the menu's 100), so Save Game and Load Game open over it. Setting Default What it does Theme , Text the default assets The same look and words as the save menu Where the buttons sit ( Placement ) Centre The panel in the centre, or 96 pixels in from the left or right edge Show Continue , Show New Game , Show Load Game , Show Quit on Which buttons show Show Save Game , Show Reset Progress off Save Game opens the save menu to save; Delete All Saves deletes every save after asking Save Menu empty The save m"},{"p":"Shipready: Reference","h":"The saving indicator","u":"save-reference.html#the-saving-indicator","x":"SavingIndicator.prefab in UI/Prefabs : its own canvas at sort order 200, which never takes a click. Setting Default What it does Theme , Text the default assets Box colour (Dialog), words colour (Dialog Text), each state's icon and colour, font and corner roundness; the full words are in Menu Text, Saving indicator, the short ones in Short words Display as the theme's (icon and short text) Full text, short text, icon, icon in its colour, icon and short text, or none Position the theme's (bottom right, 40 in) Six places on the screen's edges or Custom (anchor plus offset), with a margin; it gli"},{"p":"Shipready: Reference","h":"Every screen and the safe area","u":"save-reference.html#every-screen-and-the-safe-area","x":"The menu prefabs scale with the Canvas Scaler's Expand mode at a 1920×1080 reference: the whole design always fits, on 720p to 4K, 21:9 ultrawide, 4:3 and phones held upright, with more room around it on wider or taller screens. Each panel (the save menu's, the Game Menu's, the indicator's and the notes' boxes) sits under a Safe Area Panel object, which shrinks to Screen.safeArea : clear of a phone's notch, rounded corners and home bar, and of a TV's overscan. The dim backdrops still cover the whole screen. Phones held upright. Each kit canvas also has an Orientation Scaler , which picks the r"},{"p":"Shipready: Reference","h":"The save notes","u":"save-reference.html#the-save-notes","x":"SaveNotes.prefab in UI/Prefabs : its own canvas at sort order 199, which never takes a click. Setting Default What it does Theme , Text the default assets As the indicator; the sentences are in Menu Text, Save notes Display as the theme's (icon and short text) As the indicator Position the theme's (top centre, 40 in) As the indicator Note loads that worked ( Loads ), Note saves that came back from a backup ( Restores ), Note saves and loads that failed ( Failures ) on Which news gets a note Note saves that worked ( Saves ) off A save that worked (the indicator already shows it) Show each note "},{"p":"Shipready: Reference","h":"Editor tools","u":"save-reference.html#editor-tools","x":"Every tool is under Tools → Shipready ."},{"p":"Shipready: Reference","h":"The Start window","u":"save-reference.html#the-start-window","x":"Tools → Shipready → Saves → Start. It opens by itself once per project, the first time Shipready loads in a project without a settings asset (never in batch mode or in Play Mode). With Show this window when the project opens ticked on its Help and support page, it also opens once each time the project opens. The status strip checks what a first save needs, each with a one-click fix that Edit → Undo takes back: Check Fix Settings found Create makes the settings asset and the three registries with the recommended defaults Scene saved Save scene saves the open scene, asking where when it is still"},{"p":"Shipready: Reference","h":"Menu Style","u":"save-reference.html#menu-style","x":"Tools → Shipready → Saves → Menu Style , also Make it yours in the Start window. It edits the save menu in the open scene; with none, Add the save menu to this scene adds the prefab (one Undo step). With several, a list picks one. Tab What it does Style Use applies one of the six styles: colours, sizes and shape. The menu's font, layout, words and message choices are kept Messages Display as for the indicator, the notes, the status line and the badges; where the indicator and the notes sit, and the glide time; the state colours; each kind's icon and own display. The preview switches to Message"},{"p":"Shipready: Reference","h":"Setup Wizard","u":"save-reference.html#setup-wizard","x":"Tools → Shipready → Saves → Setup Wizard. Running it again edits the settings that are there. Each step has Back , Skip and Apply and continue ; each Apply is one Undo step. What kind of game is it? Step 1 starts with presets. Picking one lists exactly what it would change, before anything changes; Use these settings puts the values into the step, and Apply and continue applies them as one Undo step. The settings asset's Basics tab has the same row, which applies at once as one Undo step. Preset Sets Story / RPG 20 slots, quicksave on, autosave on quit and when a scene loads, timer off, 3 auto"},{"p":"Shipready: Reference","h":"The settings asset's Inspector","u":"save-reference.html#the-settings-assets-inspector","x":"Select ShipreadySaveSettings to edit it. Basics holds the game presets , where saves go, slots, autosave, start-up and Console messages. Advanced holds, each in a section that starts collapsed: File names and the profile folder : Profile folder (see Profile folders ), Shared file name , Autosave file names , Quicksave file name . Categories : rename, add or remove categories, choose In each slot or One shared file for each, and pick where values from code and Saveable objects go (those two cannot be removed). Apply the categories applies them as one Undo step; Undo my edits goes back to what t"},{"p":"Shipready: Reference","h":"The Play Mode toast","u":"save-reference.html#the-play-mode-toast","x":"In the Editor, a short note at the top of the Game view says what each save or load did, for example \"Saved Slot 1: 12 objects, 3 values\" or \"Loaded Slot 1: 11 restored, 1 skipped, Chest_02 not found (SK130). Click for details\". A load of plain values only says how many values came back. A failed call names its code. Clicking the note opens the Save Inspector on that report. It is on by default; Tools → Shipready → Saves → Play Mode Toasts switches it off and on, per user. It is never added in batch mode and does not exist in builds."},{"p":"Shipready: Reference","h":"Save Inspector","u":"save-reference.html#save-inspector","x":"Tools → Shipready → Saves → Save Inspector shows what is inside any save. The list shows every save in the game's save folder, including a global file with its own extension, with a status badge (OK, Damaged, Wrong key, Missing, Unreadable, Newer version, Older version), Encrypted and Compressed badges, and a line with its backups, size, save version and local time. A damaged save with a good backup says so. Choose folder... looks at another folder, such as a copy of a player's save folder or one player's profile folder; Game's folder goes back. Refresh lists again. With {profile} in Profile F"},{"p":"Shipready: Reference","h":"Diagnostics","u":"save-reference.html#diagnostics","x":"Tools → Shipready → Saves → Diagnostics finds what would stop saves working. Choose what to check in the toolbar ( Open scenes , Build Settings scenes , Prefabs ) and press Run . Build Settings scenes are read one at a time without opening them. Shipready's own folders are left out. Scenes and prefabs are checked outside Play Mode only. Check Finds One-click fix DuplicateId Two Saveables sharing an id, in one scene or across the scenes checked Gives the copy a new id; the original keeps its own MissingId A scene Saveable without an id Assigns an id PrefabNotRegistered A prefab with a Saveable "},{"p":"Shipready: Reference","h":"The Saveable inspector","u":"save-reference.html#the-saveable-inspector","x":"The Saveable's inspector has these parts: The summary: one sentence at the top that says what it saves, such as \"Saves position, active state and 2 fields: Is Open, Gold.\" What to save: the built-in savers Position , Active state , Destroyed with When it comes back , and Movement (physics), each with a one-line explanation. Values from your scripts (the Field Picker): one group per component, with a ticked count, a Ticked only filter, greyed fields with their reasons, and a list of broken ticks with Untick buttons. Select a single object to use it. The Start warning: when the script's own Star"},{"p":"Shipready: Reference","h":"Other menu commands","u":"save-reference.html#other-menu-commands","x":"Every command Shipready adds under Tools → Shipready : Command What it does Tools → Shipready → Saves → Start Opens the Start window (see The Start window ) Tools → Shipready → Saves → Setup Wizard Opens the Setup Wizard (see Setup Wizard ) Tools → Shipready → Saves → Play Mode Toasts Switches the Play Mode toast on and off (see The Play Mode toast ) Tools → Shipready → Saves → Report a Bug Copies a bug report to the clipboard (kit, Unity and render pipeline versions, platform, the last three saves and loads, and Diagnostics' findings, with no file paths beyond the save folder's name and no sa"},{"p":"Shipready: Reference","h":"Performance","u":"save-reference.html#performance","x":""},{"p":"Shipready: Reference","h":"Background work","u":"save-reference.html#background-work","x":"On the main thread, a save only captures: it reads the Saveables and copies the values. Reading the old file, migrating, merging, serialising, compressing, encrypting, writing and verifying, and writing the thumbnail and the slot cache, run on a background thread for every Async call and for SaveInBackground . A load reads, verifies, unpacks and migrates on a background thread, and only puts the values into the game on the main thread. This holds for every Async load and for LoadInBackground . The blocking calls ( Save , Load , SaveGlobal , LoadGlobal , Autosave , Quicksave , LoadQuicksave , L"},{"p":"Shipready: Reference","h":"Profiler markers","u":"save-reference.html#profiler-markers","x":"Each stage of a save and a load shows up in Unity's Profiler under its own marker, on the thread it ran on. Open Window → Analysis → Profiler , record while the game saves or loads, and find the markers in the CPU Usage module's Timeline view. Marker Stage Shipready.Save.Capture Gathering the values and the world before a save, on the main thread Shipready.Save.Serialize Turning the save tree into JSON text Shipready.Save.Compress GZip compression of the data Shipready.Save.Encrypt Encrypting the data and signing the file Shipready.Save.Write Every disk write: the temporary file, the swap into"},{"p":"Shipready: Reference","h":"Where the time goes","u":"save-reference.html#where-the-time-goes","x":"Main-thread time grows with the number of Saveables and ticked fields. Objects whose pose has not changed since the last save cost less, because Shipready reuses their saved position. Every report records how long each stage took ( CaptureMilliseconds , SerializeMilliseconds , CompressMilliseconds , EncryptMilliseconds , StorageMilliseconds , RestoreMilliseconds and TotalMilliseconds ), with the file size and object counts. The Console's one-line report gives the total for every save and load, and at the Verbose log level the time of each stage too. The Save Inspector's report list shows them "},{"p":"Shipready: Reference","h":"Compact files for release","u":"save-reference.html#compact-files-for-release","x":"With Pretty Print on Automatic, release builds write compact JSON, without indentation. For large worlds, switch on Compress : JSON usually shrinks several times."},{"p":"Shipready: Reference","h":"Measured benchmarks","u":"save-reference.html#measured-benchmarks","x":"Shipready is built to these targets, measured in a release Windows build: Test Target P1: save 1,000 objects with 5 fields each Main-thread capture 8 ms or less; the whole save 100 ms or less P2: save 10,000 objects Main-thread capture 60 ms or less; compression makes the file at least 3 times smaller P3: restore 1,000 objects into a loaded scene 50 ms or less on the main thread P4: a background save No disk work on the main thread, confirmed by the Profiler markers Results for 1.0.0. The machine: AMD Ryzen 7 5700X3D (8 cores, 16 threads), 32 GB RAM, AMD Radeon RX 9060 XT, Windows 11 (10.0.262"},{"p":"Shipready: Reference","h":"Logging and reports","u":"save-reference.html#logging-and-reports","x":""},{"p":"Shipready: Reference","h":"Log levels","u":"save-reference.html#log-levels","x":"Every line Shipready writes to the Console starts with [Shipready] , followed by the SK code when there is one. Level What reaches the Console Off Nothing Errors Failures only Warnings (default) Failures and warnings, including the warnings in each save and load report, and a one-line report for every save and load while Report To Console is on Verbose Everything: the one-line report for every save and load, with the time of each stage, and information lines such as where the saves are kept Set Log Level in the settings asset; it applies when Shipready starts. To change it while the game runs,"},{"p":"Shipready: Reference","h":"The one-line report","u":"save-reference.html#the-one-line-report","x":"With Report To Console on (under Diagnostics in the settings asset; off by default, so the Console stays quiet), every save and load prints one line at the Warnings and Verbose levels: [Shipready] Save slot_01.save: 12.4 ms, 5 categories, 214 values, 40 objects (3 spawned), 5321 bytes, 0 warnings [Shipready] Load slot_01.save: 9.8 ms, 5 categories, 214 values, 40 objects restored, 1 skipped, 5321 bytes, read from Backup1, 2 warnings A save or load that failed says so after the file name, for example Load slot_01.save failed with SK101 FileDamaged: ... . \"read from Backup1\" means the load fell "},{"p":"Shipready: Reference","h":"The report","u":"save-reference.html#the-report","x":"Every save and load produces a SaveReport , available as result.Report and in the Saved and Loaded events: Member What it holds Operation , FileName What was done, to which file CaptureMilliseconds , SerializeMilliseconds , CompressMilliseconds , EncryptMilliseconds , StorageMilliseconds , RestoreMilliseconds , TotalMilliseconds Timings FileBytes , UnpackedBytes Size on disk, and size of the data once unpacked CategoryCount , ValueCount Categories and values ObjectCount , SpawnedCount , RestoredCount , DestroyedCount World objects saved or restored, runtime objects written or re-created, destr"},{"p":"Shipready: API Reference","h":"Shipready: API Reference","u":"save-api-reference.html#shipready-api-reference","x":"Version 1.0.0 · Rare Geometry · Generated 2026-10-30 Every public type and member of Shipready, generated from the documentation comments in its source code. 191 types and 2091 members in 25 namespaces. For explanations, walkthroughs and examples, start with the Guide ; for how each part works, the Reference . Every error code has an entry in Troubleshooting . Assembly What it holds Runs in RareGeometry.Shipready.Save Saving and loading, slots, files, backups, compression and encryption, world persistence, migrations and the co-op hooks Game and Editor RareGeometry.Shipready.Save.UI The save a"},{"p":"Shipready: API Reference","h":"Contents","u":"save-api-reference.html#contents","x":"RareGeometry.Shipready.Save AnimatorSaver · class. The Animator saver: an Animator's parameters (floats, ints and bools), and for each layer the state it is in, how far through it is, and the layer's weight. AudioSourceSaver · class. The Audio Source saver: whether an AudioSource is playing, how far into its clip it is, and its volume and pitch. AutosaveScheduler · class. Decides when an autosave is due. AutosaveTrigger · enum. Why an autosave is being asked for. DestroyedHandling · enum. What happens to a scene object that was destroyed during play when its scene comes back. EnabledSaver · cl"},{"p":"Shipready: API Reference","h":"Namespace RareGeometry.Shipready.Save","u":"save-api-reference.html#namespace-raregeometryshipreadysave","x":""},{"p":"Shipready: API Reference","h":"AnimatorSaver","u":"save-api-reference.html#animatorsaver","x":"class in RareGeometry.Shipready.Save · assembly RareGeometry.Shipready.Save public sealed class AnimatorSaver : MonoBehaviour, ISaveable The Animator saver: an Animator's parameters (floats, ints and bools), and for each layer the state it is in, how far through it is, and the layer's weight. Add it beside a Saveable ; no code. A door saved half open comes back half open, a character saved crouching comes back crouching. Triggers are not saved: they last one moment and would fire again on load. A layer in the middle of a blend is saved as the state it is blending into. The state comes back onc"},{"p":"Shipready: API Reference","h":"AudioSourceSaver","u":"save-api-reference.html#audiosourcesaver","x":"class in RareGeometry.Shipready.Save · assembly RareGeometry.Shipready.Save public sealed class AudioSourceSaver : MonoBehaviour, ISaveable The Audio Source saver: whether an AudioSource is playing, how far into its clip it is, and its volume and pitch. Add it beside a Saveable ; no code. Music saved halfway through comes back halfway through, a radio switched off stays off. The clip itself is not changed: the AudioSource keeps the clip it has in the scene. The state comes back as soon as the AudioSource can play, so an object loaded inactive takes it when it is switched on. Add Component menu"},{"p":"Shipready: API Reference","h":"AutosaveScheduler","u":"save-api-reference.html#autosavescheduler","x":"class in RareGeometry.Shipready.Save · assembly RareGeometry.Shipready.Save public sealed class AutosaveScheduler Decides when an autosave is due. Each trigger is optional, and the Setup Wizard asks which ones a game wants. The timer counts in unscaled time, so pausing the game by setting the time scale to zero does not stop autosaves, and it restarts after every autosave rather than drifting. Constructors AutosaveScheduler(SaveKitSettings) public AutosaveScheduler(SaveKitSettings settings); Builds a scheduler for a set of settings. Parameter Description settings The settings holding the trigg"},{"p":"Shipready: API Reference","h":"AutosaveTrigger","u":"save-api-reference.html#autosavetrigger","x":"enum in RareGeometry.Shipready.Save · assembly RareGeometry.Shipready.Save public enum AutosaveTrigger Why an autosave is being asked for. Values Name Value Description None 0 Not triggered. Timer 1 The timer ran out. SceneChange 2 The game changed scene. Quit 3 The game is quitting or being suspended. Manual 4 The game asked for one directly."},{"p":"Shipready: API Reference","h":"DestroyedHandling","u":"save-api-reference.html#destroyedhandling","x":"enum in RareGeometry.Shipready.Save · assembly RareGeometry.Shipready.Save public enum DestroyedHandling What happens to a scene object that was destroyed during play when its scene comes back. Values Name Value Description Destroy 0 It is destroyed again, as soon as its scene is restored. Disable 1 It is switched off instead, so links to it stay valid."},{"p":"Shipready: API Reference","h":"EnabledSaver","u":"save-api-reference.html#enabledsaver","x":"class in RareGeometry.Shipready.Save · assembly RareGeometry.Shipready.Save public sealed class EnabledSaver : MonoBehaviour, ISaveable The Enabled saver: the enabled tick box of the components you choose, such as a light, a renderer, a collider or one of your scripts. Add it beside a Saveable and drag the components into its list; no code. A lamp switched off stays off, a trap disarmed by switching off its collider stays disarmed. Any component with an enabled tick box works: every Behaviour (scripts, lights, audio sources, UI), every Renderer and every Collider. When it is added, the list st"},{"p":"Shipready: API Reference","h":"ISaveable","u":"save-api-reference.html#isaveable","x":"interface in RareGeometry.Shipready.Save · assembly RareGeometry.Shipready.Save public interface ISaveable Implemented by a component that writes and reads its own state. This is the way out for anything the built-in savers and the Field Picker cannot cover: dictionaries, polymorphic data, properties, or a value that has to be worked out rather than copied. Shipready calls CaptureState(SaveWriter) on the main thread while a save is being gathered, and RestoreState(SaveReader) after Unity has woken the scene and before Start runs, so code in Start already sees the saved values. Set defaults in "},{"p":"Shipready: API Reference","h":"LoadResult","u":"save-api-reference.html#loadresult","x":"readonly struct in RareGeometry.Shipready.Save · assembly RareGeometry.Shipready.Save public readonly struct LoadResult What a load call did. It adds the two things only loading can report: which copy of the file the data came from, and which migration steps ran on the way in. Constructors LoadResult(SaveErrorCode, string, SaveReport, SaveFileCopy) public LoadResult(SaveErrorCode code, string detail, SaveReport report, SaveFileCopy loadedFrom); Builds a result. Games read results rather than build them; Shipready and its tests use this constructor. Parameter Description code The code that clas"},{"p":"Shipready: API Reference","h":"NavMeshAgentSaver","u":"save-api-reference.html#navmeshagentsaver","x":"class in RareGeometry.Shipready.Save · assembly RareGeometry.Shipready.Save public sealed class NavMeshAgentSaver : MonoBehaviour, ISaveable The NavMesh Agent saver: where a NavMeshAgent is, where it was walking to, its speed and whether it was stopped. Add it beside a Saveable ; no code. A guard saved on patrol carries on to the same point after a load. The agent is moved with Warp , which a NavMeshAgent needs: moving its transform alone would be undone by the agent. That happens on the agent's first frame after the load, after the Saveable has put the transform back, so leave the Saveable's "},{"p":"Shipready: API Reference","h":"PlaytimeTracker","u":"save-api-reference.html#playtimetracker","x":"class in RareGeometry.Shipready.Save · assembly RareGeometry.Shipready.Save public sealed class PlaytimeTracker Counts how long the player has played, so a slot card can show it. Shipready adds to it each frame once the game is running, and a load sets it to whatever the save recorded, so playtime carries on from where the save left off rather than from when the application launched. Properties IsPaused public bool IsPaused { get; set; } Whether counting is paused. A game pauses it for cutscenes or menus if it wants playtime to mean time spent playing. Seconds public double Seconds { get; } Ho"},{"p":"Shipready: API Reference","h":"Saveable","u":"save-api-reference.html#saveable","x":"class in RareGeometry.Shipready.Save · assembly RareGeometry.Shipready.Save public sealed class Saveable : MonoBehaviour, ISerializationCallbackReceiver Makes a GameObject part of the saved world. Add it to an object and the object's position, active state and the fields ticked in its Field Picker come back on load, matched by a permanent id. Scripts on the same object that implement ISaveable are saved with it. The built-in savers: Position (local position, rotation, scale and parent), Active , Destroyed (an object destroyed during play stays destroyed, or disabled) and Physics (velocities an"},{"p":"Shipready: API Reference","h":"SaveActions","u":"save-api-reference.html#saveactions","x":"class in RareGeometry.Shipready.Save · assembly RareGeometry.Shipready.Save public sealed class SaveActions : MonoBehaviour Saving and loading without code: drop this on any object, then pick one of its methods in a UI Button's On Click list (or any UnityEvent): Save To Slot, Load Slot, Quicksave, Quickload, Autosave, Continue, New Game, Delete Slot or Delete All Saves. The slot number goes in the box beside the method. Each call runs in the background, so the game does not stop while a file is written, and then raises OnSucceeded or OnFailed , for a sound or a message. A call made while anoth"},{"p":"Shipready: API Reference","h":"SaveActions.StringEvent","u":"save-api-reference.html#saveactionsstringevent","x":"class in RareGeometry.Shipready.Save · assembly RareGeometry.Shipready.Save public sealed class StringEvent : UnityEvent<string> A UnityEvent that carries a line of text."},{"p":"Shipready: API Reference","h":"SaveData","u":"save-api-reference.html#savedata","x":"class in RareGeometry.Shipready.Save · assembly RareGeometry.Shipready.Save public sealed class SaveData The plain values a game saves: gold, the current day, a volume setting, a list of unlocked levels. One line to store a value and one to read it back. Values live in memory until a save writes them, and a load replaces them. Every value belongs to a category, which decides whether it ends up inside a slot or in the one global file that a slot load never touches. Example SaveKit.Data.Set(\"gold\", gold, \"Progress\"); gold = SaveKit.Data.Get(\"gold\", 0); Properties CategoryCount public int Categor"},{"p":"Shipready: API Reference","h":"SavedField","u":"save-api-reference.html#savedfield","x":"class in RareGeometry.Shipready.Save · assembly RareGeometry.Shipready.Save public sealed class SavedField One field ticked in a Saveable's Field Picker: a component on the same GameObject and the name of one of its Inspector fields. The value is saved under <ComponentType>.<field> in the object's fields section. Constructors SavedField(Component, string) public SavedField(Component component, string fieldName); Creates a ticked field. Parameter Description component A component on the same GameObject as the Saveable. fieldName The name of a top-level field Unity saves: public or [SerializeFie"},{"p":"Shipready: API Reference","h":"SaveErrorCode","u":"save-api-reference.html#saveerrorcode","x":"enum in RareGeometry.Shipready.Save · assembly RareGeometry.Shipready.Save public enum SaveErrorCode Every outcome Shipready can report. Each value has a stable SK identifier and one entry in Documentation/Troubleshooting.md , so a message in the Console always leads to a fix. Values are grouped by hundreds and are never renumbered, because saved support reports quote them. Values Name Value Description None 0 The call succeeded and there is nothing to report. SK000. Unknown 1 Something failed that Shipready could not classify. SK001. InvalidArgument 2 A value handed to Shipready was missing, "},{"p":"Shipready: API Reference","h":"SaveErrorInfo","u":"save-api-reference.html#saveerrorinfo","x":"static class in RareGeometry.Shipready.Save · assembly RareGeometry.Shipready.Save public static class SaveErrorInfo Turns a SaveErrorCode into the three things a developer needs: a stable SK identifier, a plain-English message and the place in the bundled documentation that explains it. Every lookup is allocation-free apart from the strings it returns, which are constants. Constants TroubleshootingPath public const string TroubleshootingPath = \"RareGeometry/Shipready/Save/Documentation/Troubleshooting.md\"; The documentation file every error code links into, as a path inside the package. Metho"},{"p":"Shipready: API Reference","h":"SaveEventInfo","u":"save-api-reference.html#saveeventinfo","x":"struct in RareGeometry.Shipready.Save · assembly RareGeometry.Shipready.Save public struct SaveEventInfo What an event is telling the game about. Properties Success public bool Success { get; } Whether the operation did what it was asked to do. Fields Code public SaveErrorCode Code; The code the operation reported. FileName public string FileName; The storage-relative file name. LoadedFrom public SaveFileCopy LoadedFrom; Which copy of the file was read. Only meaningful for a load. Operation public SaveOperation Operation; Which operation this is about. Report public SaveReport Report; Counts, "},{"p":"Shipready: API Reference","h":"SaveEventListener","u":"save-api-reference.html#saveeventlistener","x":"class in RareGeometry.Shipready.Save · assembly RareGeometry.Shipready.Save public sealed class SaveEventListener : MonoBehaviour Shipready's save and load events as UnityEvents, so a sound, an animation or a message can follow a save without code: drag an object into a list and pick what it does. Shown in the Add Component menu as Save Events . The tick boxes pick which saves count: slot saves, autosaves, the quicksave and the global file (player settings), so a \"Game saved\" sound need not play every time a setting is stored. Listening starts when the component is enabled and stops when it is"},{"p":"Shipready: API Reference","h":"SaveEvents","u":"save-api-reference.html#saveevents","x":"static class in RareGeometry.Shipready.Save · assembly RareGeometry.Shipready.Save public static class SaveEvents The points a game can hook into: a save or load starting, finishing, or finishing by falling back to a backup. A handler runs on the main thread, so it is safe to touch Unity objects, show a saving icon or pause the game from one. The one thing a handler must not do is start another save of the same file, which would queue behind the one that is finishing. Handlers are cleared when static state resets, so turning off domain reload cannot leave a handler from a previous play session"},{"p":"Shipready: API Reference","h":"SaveFileKind","u":"save-api-reference.html#savefilekind","x":"enum in RareGeometry.Shipready.Save · assembly RareGeometry.Shipready.Save public enum SaveFileKind What a save file is to the game. See SaveKit.KindOf(string) . Values Name Value Description Other 0 Not one of Shipready's files, or Shipready has not started. Slot 1 A numbered slot the player saves into. Autosave 2 One of the autosaves. Quicksave 3 The quicksave. Global 4 The global file, where player settings live."},{"p":"Shipready: API Reference","h":"SaveKit","u":"save-api-reference.html#savekit","x":"static class in RareGeometry.Shipready.Save · assembly RareGeometry.Shipready.Save public static class SaveKit The front door. Everything a game needs for saving is reachable from here: the plain values, the slots, the global file, the autosaves and the migrations. Nothing here throws. Every call hands back a result with a code, a plain-English message and a link into the Troubleshooting guide. Every call that ends in Async finishes back on the main thread , so the code after an await can touch Unity objects freely. That also means the main thread must be left free to get there: never call .Wa"},{"p":"Shipready: API Reference","h":"SaveKitAutoStart","u":"save-api-reference.html#savekitautostart","x":"static class in RareGeometry.Shipready.Save · assembly RareGeometry.Shipready.Save public static class SaveKitAutoStart Starts Shipready by itself when the game runs, just before the first scene loads, so a game set up with the Setup Wizard can save and load without a line of code. It uses the settings asset in a Resources folder and only acts when that asset has SaveKitSettings.InitialiseAutomatically on and Shipready has not been started yet. A failure is written to the Console; it never throws. Code that calls SaveKit.Initialise() itself keeps working: starting again is allowed. Methods Sho"},{"p":"Shipready: API Reference","h":"SaveKitEnvironment","u":"save-api-reference.html#savekitenvironment","x":"static class in RareGeometry.Shipready.Save · assembly RareGeometry.Shipready.Save public static class SaveKitEnvironment The handful of Unity values Shipready needs while it is working on a background thread. Unity only allows most of its API on the main thread, and the whole point of saving in the background is that the packing and the disk work happen off it. So the values are read once, on the main thread, and the background work reads the copies. They are captured again whenever static state resets, so turning off domain reload cannot leave the previous play session values behind. Propert"},{"p":"Shipready: API Reference","h":"SaveKitRunner","u":"save-api-reference.html#savekitrunner","x":"class in RareGeometry.Shipready.Save · assembly RareGeometry.Shipready.Save public sealed class SaveKitRunner : MonoBehaviour The one hidden object Shipready keeps in a running game. It counts playtime, watches the autosave timer, makes sure a save still happens when the game is closing, applies scene memory as scenes load, and lets go of restored rigidbodies on the next physics step. It is created whenever Shipready starts in a running game, whichever autosave triggers are switched on, because playtime and scene memory are handled here too. It is marked so that it survives a scene change and "},{"p":"Shipready: API Reference","h":"SaveKitVersion","u":"save-api-reference.html#savekitversion","x":"static class in RareGeometry.Shipready.Save · assembly RareGeometry.Shipready.Save public static class SaveKitVersion Version numbers Shipready stamps into every save file and reports in the Editor. Constants Format public const int Format = 1; The save envelope format number, written as the aiosk field of every file. It changes only when the envelope itself changes, never when a game's data changes. Kit public const string Kit = \"1.0.0\"; Shipready's own release version, as shown on the Asset Store. It is the same in Shipready's package.json and the newest entry of its CHANGELOG.md , which a t"},{"p":"Shipready: API Reference","h":"SaveMigrationAttribute","u":"save-api-reference.html#savemigrationattribute","x":"class in RareGeometry.Shipready.Save · assembly RareGeometry.Shipready.Save public sealed class SaveMigrationAttribute : Attribute Marks a static method as the step that upgrades a save from one version to the next. The method takes a SaveTree and nothing else. That is deliberate: a migration edits the saved data rather than the game classes, so a step written today still works after those classes have been renamed, split or deleted. Migrations run on a background thread, together with the rest of the file work, so a step must only edit the tree and must not touch Unity objects. [SaveMigration"},{"p":"Shipready: API Reference","h":"SavePoint","u":"save-api-reference.html#savepoint","x":"class in RareGeometry.Shipready.Save · assembly RareGeometry.Shipready.Save public sealed class SavePoint : MonoBehaviour A checkpoint: saves when the player walks into its trigger, or when a UnityEvent calls SaveNow() , with a cooldown so walking back and forth does not save every step, and an \"only once\" option. Put it on an object with a trigger collider (3D or 2D) and tag the player Player ; no code. It writes the next autosave by default, or a chosen slot or the quicksave. The location name, when set, is what the slot card shows (\"Old Mill\"). The save runs in the background. Whether it ha"},{"p":"Shipready: API Reference","h":"SavePointTarget","u":"save-api-reference.html#savepointtarget","x":"enum in RareGeometry.Shipready.Save · assembly RareGeometry.Shipready.Save public enum SavePointTarget What a SavePoint writes. Values Name Value Description Autosave 0 The next autosave, the usual checkpoint. Slot 1 A numbered slot. Quicksave 2 The quicksave."},{"p":"Shipready: API Reference","h":"SaveReader","u":"save-api-reference.html#savereader","x":"class in RareGeometry.Shipready.Save · assembly RareGeometry.Shipready.Save public sealed class SaveReader What a game reads its state out of. Every read takes the value to use when the save does not hold that name, which is what makes loading tolerant: a value added in a game update simply keeps its default, and an old save still loads. Properties Count public int Count { get; } How many values this group holds. IsEmpty public bool IsEmpty { get; } Whether this reader has anything in it at all. Node public SaveNode Node { get; } The tree node this reader reads from. Methods For(SaveNode) publ"},{"p":"Shipready: API Reference","h":"SaveResult","u":"save-api-reference.html#saveresult","x":"readonly struct in RareGeometry.Shipready.Save · assembly RareGeometry.Shipready.Save public readonly struct SaveResult What a save, delete or verify call did. Shipready never throws out of its public API, so every one of those calls hands back one of these instead. Constructors SaveResult(SaveErrorCode, string, SaveReport) public SaveResult(SaveErrorCode code, string detail, SaveReport report); Builds a result. Games read results rather than build them; Shipready and its tests use this constructor. Parameter Description code The code that classifies the outcome. detail Extra detail to append "},{"p":"Shipready: API Reference","h":"SaveTree","u":"save-api-reference.html#savetree","x":"class in RareGeometry.Shipready.Save · assembly RareGeometry.Shipready.Save public sealed class SaveTree One whole save, in memory: its header, the slot info the menu shows, and the game data split into categories. Every tool in Shipready works on this one structure, which is why the menu, the Save Inspector, Diagnostics and recovery all fit into a compact asset. A migration is handed the tree rather than the game classes, so a migration written today still works after those classes change. Constructors SaveTree(SaveHeader, SaveNode, SaveNode) public SaveTree(SaveHeader header, SaveNode info, "},{"p":"Shipready: API Reference","h":"SaveWorld","u":"save-api-reference.html#saveworld","x":"class in RareGeometry.Shipready.Save · assembly RareGeometry.Shipready.Save public sealed class SaveWorld The saved world: scene memory, the scene loader, and the hooks a pool or a custom scene loader needs. Reach it through SaveKit.World . Scene memory keeps the state of scenes the player has left. A scene that loads during play gets its remembered state back automatically, before any Start runs. Leaving a scene through SaveKit.LoadScene(string, LoadSceneMode) or SaveKit.UnloadScene(Scene) captures it on the way out; a game with its own scene loading calls CaptureScene(Scene) first. Example S"},{"p":"Shipready: API Reference","h":"SaveWriter","u":"save-api-reference.html#savewriter","x":"class in RareGeometry.Shipready.Save · assembly RareGeometry.Shipready.Save public sealed class SaveWriter What a game writes its state into. One writer wraps one object in the save tree, so a component that implements ISaveable gets a small, private place to put its values and never has to know where in the file it ends up. Writing the same name twice replaces the earlier value and keeps its position, so the order of a readable save stays stable between runs. Properties Count public int Count { get; } How many values have been written. Node public SaveNode Node { get; } The tree node this wri"},{"p":"Shipready: API Reference","h":"SlotIndex","u":"save-api-reference.html#slotindex","x":"class in RareGeometry.Shipready.Save · assembly RareGeometry.Shipready.Save public sealed class SlotIndex A small cache of what each slot holds, so a menu with ninety-nine slots does not have to open ninety-nine files to draw itself. It is only ever a cache. Each entry records the size and the time of the file it describes, and an entry is used only while both still match, so a save written by anything other than Shipready, a file restored from a cloud backup or a hand edit all make Shipready read the real file again. A cache that can go stale without noticing would show a player the wrong sav"},{"p":"Shipready: API Reference","h":"SlotInfo","u":"save-api-reference.html#slotinfo","x":"class in RareGeometry.Shipready.Save · assembly RareGeometry.Shipready.Save public sealed class SlotInfo What the save menu shows about one slot, and what Shipready records about it. It is kept in the readable part of every save file, so the menu can list slots without unpacking or decrypting anything. Properties BackupCount public int BackupCount { get; set; } How many backups of this save exist. Exists public bool Exists { get; set; } Whether there is a save in this slot at all. FileBytes public long FileBytes { get; set; } How many bytes the save file holds. FileName public string FileName "},{"p":"Shipready: API Reference","h":"ThumbnailCapture","u":"save-api-reference.html#thumbnailcapture","x":"static class in RareGeometry.Shipready.Save · assembly RareGeometry.Shipready.Save public static class ThumbnailCapture Takes the picture a slot card shows: a camera renders the game once into a small temporary texture, which is read back. Screen-space overlay UI, such as the HUD and the save menu itself, is not drawn by cameras, so it never appears in a thumbnail. The save menu uses it before a save, and Shipready before every slot save, autosave and quicksave while SaveKitSettings.CaptureAutosaveThumbnails is on. It renders the camera the way the active render pipeline renders it, with no co"},{"p":"Shipready: API Reference","h":"Thumbnails","u":"save-api-reference.html#thumbnails","x":"static class in RareGeometry.Shipready.Save · assembly RareGeometry.Shipready.Save public static class Thumbnails The small picture a slot card shows. It is stored beside the save as a PNG rather than inside it, so the menu can show a wall of thumbnails without reading a single save file, and so a thumbnail never bloats a save that a player syncs to the cloud. The file extension is deliberately not the save extension, which keeps thumbnails out of a Steam Auto-Cloud pattern of *.save . Constants Height public const int Height = 180; How tall a stored thumbnail is. Suffix public const string Su"},{"p":"Shipready: API Reference","h":"WorldLoadMode","u":"save-api-reference.html#worldloadmode","x":"enum in RareGeometry.Shipready.Save · assembly RareGeometry.Shipready.Save public enum WorldLoadMode How a load treats scenes. Values Name Value Description LoadSavedScenes 0 SaveKit.LoadAsync loads the scenes the save was made in, makes the saved active scene active, and restores each scene as it loads. The default. RestoreIntoCurrentScenes 1 The game manages scenes itself: a load restores into the scenes that are loaded now, and every other scene's state is applied when the game loads it."},{"p":"Shipready: API Reference","h":"Namespace RareGeometry.Shipready.Save.Codecs","u":"save-api-reference.html#namespace-raregeometryshipreadysavecodecs","x":""},{"p":"Shipready: API Reference","h":"AesHmacCodec","u":"save-api-reference.html#aeshmaccodec","x":"static class in RareGeometry.Shipready.Save.Codecs · assembly RareGeometry.Shipready.Save public static class AesHmacCodec Optional encryption for the data section of a save: AES-256 in CBC mode with a fresh random starting block for every write, and an HMAC-SHA256 signature over the header and the encrypted bytes. Encrypt first, then sign, which is the order that makes the signature trustworthy. The payload is laid out as the 16-byte starting block, then the encrypted bytes, then the 32-byte signature. A single changed byte anywhere in it fails the signature check. Encryption here stops a pla"},{"p":"Shipready: API Reference","h":"GzipCodec","u":"save-api-reference.html#gzipcodec","x":"static class in RareGeometry.Shipready.Save.Codecs · assembly RareGeometry.Shipready.Save public static class GzipCodec GZip compression for the data section of a save. Compression is off by default because a typical save is already small, but JSON compresses several times over, so a large world is worth packing. The output is deterministic: the same input always produces the same bytes, which keeps the acceptance tests able to compare files directly. Methods Compress(byte[]) public static byte[] Compress(byte[] data); Compresses a byte array. Parameter Description data The bytes to compress. "},{"p":"Shipready: API Reference","h":"KeyDerivation","u":"save-api-reference.html#keyderivation","x":"static class in RareGeometry.Shipready.Save.Codecs · assembly RareGeometry.Shipready.Save public static class KeyDerivation Turns a developer passphrase into encryption keys, once per run. Stretching the passphrase with PBKDF2 takes a noticeable fraction of a second on purpose, so the result is cached: the first encrypted save pays for it and the rest do not. The cache is cleared when static state resets, so turning off domain reload cannot leave a stale key behind after the passphrase is changed in the Inspector. This is not anti-cheat. The passphrase ships inside the game, so a determined pl"},{"p":"Shipready: API Reference","h":"SaveKeys","u":"save-api-reference.html#savekeys","x":"class in RareGeometry.Shipready.Save.Codecs · assembly RareGeometry.Shipready.Save public sealed class SaveKeys The two keys an encrypted save needs: one to encrypt with and a separate one to sign with. Using separate keys is what makes the tamper check meaningful. Properties EncryptionKey public byte[] EncryptionKey { get; } The 32-byte key used for AES-256. SigningKey public byte[] SigningKey { get; } The 32-byte key used for HMAC-SHA256."},{"p":"Shipready: API Reference","h":"Namespace RareGeometry.Shipready.Save.Coop","u":"save-api-reference.html#namespace-raregeometryshipreadysavecoop","x":""},{"p":"Shipready: API Reference","h":"IPlayerIdentity","u":"save-api-reference.html#iplayeridentity","x":"interface in RareGeometry.Shipready.Save.Coop · assembly RareGeometry.Shipready.Save public interface IPlayerIdentity Maps a connected player to a stable id string that survives the player leaving and coming back, such as a Steam ID. The host keeps each player's data in SaveKit.Players under that id. Methods TryGetPlayerId(ulong, string) bool TryGetPlayerId(ulong connectionId, out string playerId); The stable id of a connected player. Parameter Description connectionId The network library's id for the connection. playerId The stable id, or null when the player is not known yet. Returns: true w"},{"p":"Shipready: API Reference","h":"ISaveAuthority","u":"save-api-reference.html#isaveauthority","x":"interface in RareGeometry.Shipready.Save.Coop · assembly RareGeometry.Shipready.Save public interface ISaveAuthority Decides who may save and load world data. In co-op only the host may; a solo game needs no authority at all, because SaveKit.Authority left at null always allows it. When the authority says no, every slot, autosave and quicksave save, load and delete returns SaveErrorCode.HostOnly without touching storage. Saving and loading the global file stays allowed, so a client keeps its own settings. Example sealed class NgoAuthority : ISaveAuthority { public bool HasSaveAuthority { get {"},{"p":"Shipready: API Reference","h":"ISpawnHandler","u":"save-api-reference.html#ispawnhandler","x":"interface in RareGeometry.Shipready.Save.Coop · assembly RareGeometry.Shipready.Save public interface ISpawnHandler Creates and removes runtime objects for Shipready. A load re-creates the objects that were spawned at runtime through this hook, so a pool, a network library or Addressables can make them. Left at null, Shipready uses Instantiate and Destroy . Shipready gives the new object its saved id itself, straight after Spawn(GameObject, Vector3, Quaternion, Transform) returns, so the hook only has to make the object. Methods Despawn(GameObject) void Despawn(GameObject instance); Removes an"},{"p":"Shipready: API Reference","h":"PlayerData","u":"save-api-reference.html#playerdata","x":"class in RareGeometry.Shipready.Save.Coop · assembly RareGeometry.Shipready.Save public sealed class PlayerData Per-player data in a co-op game: position, inventory, anything that belongs to one player rather than to the world. The host keeps it, under the stable id the game supplies (see IPlayerIdentity ), and it is saved inside the host's slot beside the categories, as players.<player id>.<category> . A save writes each player's values for every category it saves; a load brings them back, so a player who rejoins gets their data again and a player the save does not know starts from defaults. "},{"p":"Shipready: API Reference","h":"Namespace RareGeometry.Shipready.Save.Data","u":"save-api-reference.html#namespace-raregeometryshipreadysavedata","x":""},{"p":"Shipready: API Reference","h":"SaveNode","u":"save-api-reference.html#savenode","x":"class in RareGeometry.Shipready.Save.Data · assembly RareGeometry.Shipready.Save public sealed class SaveNode One node of the save tree, which is Shipready data model that everything else reads: the JSON writer and reader, the menu, the Save Inspector, Diagnostics and migrations. Numbers are held as the exact text they were written with rather than as a Double . That is what makes a Single come back bit-exact, keeps a Int64 past 2^53 intact, and makes a readable save look the way a developer expects instead of showing 0.100000001490116 where the game wrote 0.1. Object members keep their insert"},{"p":"Shipready: API Reference","h":"SaveNodeKind","u":"save-api-reference.html#savenodekind","x":"enum in RareGeometry.Shipready.Save.Data · assembly RareGeometry.Shipready.Save public enum SaveNodeKind What one node in a save tree holds. Values Name Value Description Null 0 No value. Written as JSON null . Bool 1 True or false. Number 2 A number, kept in the exact text it was written with. String 3 Text. Object 4 Named members, in the order they were added. Array 5 Numbered items, in order."},{"p":"Shipready: API Reference","h":"ValueConverters","u":"save-api-reference.html#valueconverters","x":"static class in RareGeometry.Shipready.Save.Data · assembly RareGeometry.Shipready.Save public static class ValueConverters Turns the supported value types into save tree nodes and back. Every number, date and identifier goes through here, which is how Shipready keeps one promise that quietly breaks a lot of save systems: the file is byte-identical whatever language the machine is set to. A float is written with the fewest significant digits, seven, eight or nine, that read back as exactly the same float, and a double with fifteen, sixteen or seventeen; so a readable save shows 0.8 where the g"},{"p":"Shipready: API Reference","h":"Namespace RareGeometry.Shipready.Save.Diagnostics","u":"save-api-reference.html#namespace-raregeometryshipreadysavediagnostics","x":""},{"p":"Shipready: API Reference","h":"PlayModeToast","u":"save-api-reference.html#playmodetoast","x":"class in RareGeometry.Shipready.Save.Diagnostics · assembly RareGeometry.Shipready.Save public sealed class PlayModeToast : MonoBehaviour The Editor's Play Mode toast: a short note at the top of the Game view after each save or load, such as \"Saved Slot 1: 12 objects, 3 values\". Clicking it raises Clicked , which Shipready's editor tools answer by opening the Save Inspector on that report. Shipready's editor code adds it at the start of Play Mode when the toasts are switched on (Tools → Shipready → Saves → Play Mode Toasts). Editor only: this class does not exist in builds, so nothing of it ca"},{"p":"Shipready: API Reference","h":"SaveFileCopy","u":"save-api-reference.html#savefilecopy","x":"enum in RareGeometry.Shipready.Save.Diagnostics · assembly RareGeometry.Shipready.Save public enum SaveFileCopy Which copy of a save file was read. Values Name Value Description None 0 No file was read. Main 1 The main file, which is the normal case. Temporary 2 A complete temporary file left behind by a save that was cut short. Backup1 3 The newest backup. Backup2 4 The second newest backup. OlderBackup 5 A backup further back than the second."},{"p":"Shipready: API Reference","h":"SaveLog","u":"save-api-reference.html#savelog","x":"static class in RareGeometry.Shipready.Save.Diagnostics · assembly RareGeometry.Shipready.Save public static class SaveLog The one place Shipready writes to the Console. Every line carries the product prefix and, where there is one, the SK identifier, so a developer can search for it. Properties Level public static SaveLogLevel Level { get; set; } How much is written to the Console. Defaults to SaveLogLevel.Warnings . RecentReports public static IReadOnlyList<SaveReport> RecentReports { get; } The reports of the most recent saves and loads of this play session, oldest first, at most RecentRepo"},{"p":"Shipready: API Reference","h":"SaveLogLevel","u":"save-api-reference.html#saveloglevel","x":"enum in RareGeometry.Shipready.Save.Diagnostics · assembly RareGeometry.Shipready.Save public enum SaveLogLevel How much Shipready writes to the Console. Values Name Value Description Off 0 Nothing at all. Errors 1 Only failures. Warnings 2 Failures and warnings, plus a one-line report of every save and load while SaveLog.ReportToConsole is on. This is the default. Verbose 3 Everything, including a report with stage timings for every save and load."},{"p":"Shipready: API Reference","h":"SaveOperation","u":"save-api-reference.html#saveoperation","x":"enum in RareGeometry.Shipready.Save.Diagnostics · assembly RareGeometry.Shipready.Save public enum SaveOperation Which operation a report describes. Values Name Value Description None 0 No operation. Save 1 Writing a save file. Load 2 Reading a save file. Delete 3 Deleting a save file and its backups. ReadInfo 4 Reading only the slot info of one or more files. Verify 5 Checking a file without using its contents. RestoreBackup 6 Putting the newest valid backup of a damaged save back in place."},{"p":"Shipready: API Reference","h":"SavePacking","u":"save-api-reference.html#savepacking","x":"enum in RareGeometry.Shipready.Save.Diagnostics · assembly RareGeometry.Shipready.Save public enum SavePacking How the data section of a file is packed. Values Name Value Description Readable 0 Plain JSON that any text editor can open. Compressed 1 GZip compressed. Encrypted 2 AES encrypted with a tamper check. CompressedAndEncrypted 3 Compressed first, then encrypted."},{"p":"Shipready: API Reference","h":"SaveProfilerMarkers","u":"save-api-reference.html#saveprofilermarkers","x":"static class in RareGeometry.Shipready.Save.Diagnostics · assembly RareGeometry.Shipready.Save public static class SaveProfilerMarkers The names of the Profiler markers Shipready wraps around each stage of a save and a load, so every stage shows up in Unity's Profiler, on the thread it ran on. Capture and Restore run on the main thread; the others run on the background thread a save or load uses. A save: Capture (gathering the values), Serialize (the tree to JSON), Compress , Encrypt , Write (every disk write, swap, backup move and delete) and Verify (reading the temporary file back and checki"},{"p":"Shipready: API Reference","h":"SaveReport","u":"save-api-reference.html#savereport","x":"class in RareGeometry.Shipready.Save.Diagnostics · assembly RareGeometry.Shipready.Save public sealed class SaveReport What one save or load actually did: how long each stage took, how much was written and anything worth a warning. Every public call returns one, and the Save Inspector and the Console both print it. Properties CaptureMilliseconds public double CaptureMilliseconds { get; } Milliseconds spent capturing game state on the main thread. CategoryCount public int CategoryCount { get; } How many categories took part. CompressMilliseconds public double CompressMilliseconds { get; } Milli"},{"p":"Shipready: API Reference","h":"SaveToastText","u":"save-api-reference.html#savetoasttext","x":"static class in RareGeometry.Shipready.Save.Diagnostics · assembly RareGeometry.Shipready.Save public static class SaveToastText The one-line text of the Editor's Play Mode toast after a save or a load, for example \"Saved Slot 1: 12 objects, 3 values\" or \"Loaded Slot 1: 11 restored, 1 skipped, Chest_02 not found (SK130). Click for details\". Editor only: it does not exist in builds. Methods Format(SaveEventInfo) public static string Format(SaveEventInfo info); Builds the toast text for a finished save or load. Parameter Description info The event Shipready raised when the operation finished. Re"},{"p":"Shipready: API Reference","h":"SaveWarning","u":"save-api-reference.html#savewarning","x":"struct in RareGeometry.Shipready.Save.Diagnostics · assembly RareGeometry.Shipready.Save public struct SaveWarning One thing a save or load wants to flag without failing the call. Constructors SaveWarning(SaveErrorCode, string) public SaveWarning(SaveErrorCode code, string detail); Creates a warning. Parameter Description code The code that classifies it. detail What specifically happened. Fields Code public readonly SaveErrorCode Code; The code that classifies this warning. Detail public readonly string Detail; What specifically happened, such as the name of a missing object. Methods ToString"},{"p":"Shipready: API Reference","h":"Namespace RareGeometry.Shipready.Save.Files","u":"save-api-reference.html#namespace-raregeometryshipreadysavefiles","x":""},{"p":"Shipready: API Reference","h":"AtomicFileWriter","u":"save-api-reference.html#atomicfilewriter","x":"static class in RareGeometry.Shipready.Save.Files · assembly RareGeometry.Shipready.Save public static class AtomicFileWriter Writes a save file so that a crash can never leave a player without one. The promise is that at every moment during a save, at least one complete, valid copy exists on disk. The new bytes go to a temporary file first and are read back and checked against what Shipready meant to write. Only then is the temporary file swapped into place in a single step, with the file it replaces parked under a staging name. The backups are shuffled down afterwards, never before, so a sav"},{"p":"Shipready: API Reference","h":"AtomicWriteOutcome","u":"save-api-reference.html#atomicwriteoutcome","x":"struct in RareGeometry.Shipready.Save.Files · assembly RareGeometry.Shipready.Save public struct AtomicWriteOutcome What writing a save file did. Fields BackupCount public int BackupCount; How many backups exist afterwards. Code public SaveErrorCode Code; Why it failed, or SaveErrorCode.None . Error public string Error; A plain-English reason, or null on success. FileBytes public long FileBytes; How many bytes the finished file holds. Step public AtomicWriteStep Step; Which step of the write it reached, for the report and for support. Success public bool Success; Whether the new save is now in"},{"p":"Shipready: API Reference","h":"AtomicWriteStep","u":"save-api-reference.html#atomicwritestep","x":"enum in RareGeometry.Shipready.Save.Files · assembly RareGeometry.Shipready.Save public enum AtomicWriteStep The steps of a crash-safe write, in the order they run. Values Name Value Description NotStarted 0 Nothing has happened yet. WriteTemporary 1 Writing the temporary file. VerifyTemporary 2 Reading the temporary file back and checking it. Swap 3 Swapping the temporary file into place and parking the file it replaced. Everything a player could lose is still intact up to the moment this step succeeds. RotateBackups 4 Moving the existing backups down a number. The new save is already in plac"},{"p":"Shipready: API Reference","h":"BackupRotator","u":"save-api-reference.html#backuprotator","x":"static class in RareGeometry.Shipready.Save.Files · assembly RareGeometry.Shipready.Save public static class BackupRotator Keeps the rotating backups for one save file. Two are kept by default, which is one more copy than almost any player needs and still costs nothing worth measuring. Rotation happens just before a new save is swapped into place: the oldest backup is dropped, the rest shuffle down a number, and the file being replaced becomes the newest backup. At no point is more than one copy in motion, so a crash during rotation still leaves a complete save on disk. Constants MaximumBackup"},{"p":"Shipready: API Reference","h":"Checksum","u":"save-api-reference.html#checksum","x":"static class in RareGeometry.Shipready.Save.Files · assembly RareGeometry.Shipready.Save public static class Checksum The checksum written into every save header and verified on every load. CRC-32 is enough to spot a truncated, half-written or hand-edited file, which is the job here; encrypted files get a cryptographic tamper check on top of it. Constants Crc32Prefix public const string Crc32Prefix = \"crc32:\"; The prefix every checksum string carries, so other kinds can be added later. Methods Crc32(byte[]) public static uint Crc32(byte[] data); The CRC-32 of a whole byte array. Parameter Desc"},{"p":"Shipready: API Reference","h":"EnvelopeReadOutcome","u":"save-api-reference.html#envelopereadoutcome","x":"struct in RareGeometry.Shipready.Save.Files · assembly RareGeometry.Shipready.Save public struct EnvelopeReadOutcome What reading an envelope produced. Fields ChecksumMismatchAccepted public bool ChecksumMismatchAccepted; Whether the checksum did not match but the file was accepted anyway, because the settings allow hand-edited saves or because this is the Editor. Code public SaveErrorCode Code; Why it failed, or SaveErrorCode.None . CompressMilliseconds public double CompressMilliseconds; Milliseconds spent decompressing. EncryptMilliseconds public double EncryptMilliseconds; Milliseconds spe"},{"p":"Shipready: API Reference","h":"EnvelopeWriteOutcome","u":"save-api-reference.html#envelopewriteoutcome","x":"struct in RareGeometry.Shipready.Save.Files · assembly RareGeometry.Shipready.Save public struct EnvelopeWriteOutcome What writing an envelope produced. Fields Bytes public byte[] Bytes; The finished file contents. Code public SaveErrorCode Code; Why it failed, or SaveErrorCode.None . CompressMilliseconds public double CompressMilliseconds; Milliseconds spent compressing. EncryptMilliseconds public double EncryptMilliseconds; Milliseconds spent encrypting. Error public string Error; A plain-English reason, or null on success. Packing public SavePacking Packing; How the data section ended up pa"},{"p":"Shipready: API Reference","h":"RecoveryAttempt","u":"save-api-reference.html#recoveryattempt","x":"struct in RareGeometry.Shipready.Save.Files · assembly RareGeometry.Shipready.Save public struct RecoveryAttempt One copy of a save that the recovery loader tried, and how it went. Constructors RecoveryAttempt(string, SaveFileCopy, SaveErrorCode, string) public RecoveryAttempt(string fileName, SaveFileCopy copy, SaveErrorCode code, string error); Records one attempt. Parameter Description fileName The file name that was tried. copy Which copy of the save it was. code What was wrong with it. error A plain-English reason. Properties Succeeded public bool Succeeded { get; } Whether this attempt i"},{"p":"Shipready: API Reference","h":"RecoveryLoader","u":"save-api-reference.html#recoveryloader","x":"static class in RareGeometry.Shipready.Save.Files · assembly RareGeometry.Shipready.Save public static class RecoveryLoader Reads a save, falling back through every copy Shipready keeps until one works. The order is the main file, then a complete temporary file left behind by a save that was cut short, then each backup from newest to oldest. The first valid copy wins, and the result says which one it was, so the menu can show \"Restored from backup\" and the game can tell the player what happened. A damaged main file is moved aside rather than deleted, because a player who has lost progress dese"},{"p":"Shipready: API Reference","h":"RecoveryOutcome","u":"save-api-reference.html#recoveryoutcome","x":"struct in RareGeometry.Shipready.Save.Files · assembly RareGeometry.Shipready.Save public struct RecoveryOutcome What reading a save, with recovery, produced. Fields Attempts public List<RecoveryAttempt> Attempts; One entry per copy that was tried and what was wrong with it, which is what a support email needs and what the Save Inspector shows. ChecksumMismatchAccepted public bool ChecksumMismatchAccepted; Whether the checksum did not match but the file was accepted anyway. Code public SaveErrorCode Code; Why it failed, or the code that describes how it succeeded. CompressMilliseconds public d"},{"p":"Shipready: API Reference","h":"SaveEnvelope","u":"save-api-reference.html#saveenvelope","x":"static class in RareGeometry.Shipready.Save.Files · assembly RareGeometry.Shipready.Save public static class SaveEnvelope The file format. A readable save is plain JSON that any text editor opens. A compressed or encrypted save is a short binary marker, then a readable header, then the packed data, so the menu can still read slot info without unpacking anything. The loader tells the two apart from the first bytes, which is what lets a game turn compression or encryption on in an update without stranding the saves players already have. Constants and fields Marker public static readonly byte[] M"},{"p":"Shipready: API Reference","h":"SaveFileNames","u":"save-api-reference.html#savefilenames","x":"static class in RareGeometry.Shipready.Save.Files · assembly RareGeometry.Shipready.Save public static class SaveFileNames The naming rules for everything Shipready writes. They exist so that one Steam Auto-Cloud pattern of *.save uploads the real saves and nothing else: backups, temporary files and quarantined copies all end in something different, so they stay on the machine where they are useful and out of a player cloud quota where they are not. Constants BackupSuffix public const string BackupSuffix = \".bak\"; The suffix backups carry, followed by the backup number. CorruptSuffix public co"},{"p":"Shipready: API Reference","h":"SaveFileReadResult","u":"save-api-reference.html#savefilereadresult","x":"struct in RareGeometry.Shipready.Save.Files · assembly RareGeometry.Shipready.Save public struct SaveFileReadResult What reading one save file produced. Fields HasValidBackup public bool HasValidBackup; Filled by SaveFileStore.ReadInfo(string) when the main file is damaged or missing: whether the temporary file or a backup reads as a valid save, which is what Restore backup would put back. Header public SaveHeader Header; The header of the copy that was read, when one was readable. Info public SaveNode Info; The slot info of the copy that was read, when one was readable. MainFileDamaged public"},{"p":"Shipready: API Reference","h":"SaveFileStatus","u":"save-api-reference.html#savefilestatus","x":"class in RareGeometry.Shipready.Save.Files · assembly RareGeometry.Shipready.Save public sealed class SaveFileStatus What one save file looks like on disk, for the Save Inspector: whether it reads, how it is packed, which save version wrote it and how many backups it has. Worked out by SaveFileStore.DescribeFile(string, int) , which only reads: nothing is moved, renamed, quarantined or written. Properties BackupCount public int BackupCount { get; } How many rotating backups ( .bak1 to .bak5 ) exist. Bytes public long Bytes { get; } The size of the main file in bytes, or 0 when it is missing. C"},{"p":"Shipready: API Reference","h":"SaveFileStore","u":"save-api-reference.html#savefilestore","x":"class in RareGeometry.Shipready.Save.Files · assembly RareGeometry.Shipready.Save public sealed class SaveFileStore One save file, read and written safely. This is the layer the slots, the menu and the Save Inspector all sit on: it packs and unpacks the envelope, keeps the backups, falls back when a file is damaged, and makes sure two saves of the same file never overlap. Work that does not need Unity runs on a background thread. Requests for the same file are queued and run in the order they arrived, so a quicksave fired twice in a second leaves the second one on disk, not the first. Construc"},{"p":"Shipready: API Reference","h":"SaveHeader","u":"save-api-reference.html#saveheader","x":"class in RareGeometry.Shipready.Save.Files · assembly RareGeometry.Shipready.Save public sealed class SaveHeader The bookkeeping at the front of every save file: which envelope format it uses, which save version the game was on, when it was written, how it is packed, and the checksum that proves it is intact. The header is always readable, even when the data is encrypted, so the menu can list slots and the Save Inspector can report on a file without having to unpack it. Properties Checksum public string Checksum { get; set; } The checksum of the data section, in the form crc32:XXXXXXXX . Forma"},{"p":"Shipready: API Reference","h":"SaveMergeJob","u":"save-api-reference.html#savemergejob","x":"abstract class in RareGeometry.Shipready.Save.Files · assembly RareGeometry.Shipready.Save public abstract class SaveMergeJob Builds the save to write from the copy already on disk, for SaveFileStore.WriteMerged(string, SaveMergeJob) and SaveFileStore.WriteMergedAsync(string, SaveMergeJob) . Both methods run on a background thread, inside the queue for the file, so nothing else can read or write the file between reading what is there and writing what replaces it. Neither may touch Unity objects. Everything a job needs from the game has to be gathered on the main thread before the job is handed"},{"p":"Shipready: API Reference","h":"Namespace RareGeometry.Shipready.Save.Json","u":"save-api-reference.html#namespace-raregeometryshipreadysavejson","x":""},{"p":"Shipready: API Reference","h":"JsonTextReader","u":"save-api-reference.html#jsontextreader","x":"static class in RareGeometry.Shipready.Save.Json · assembly RareGeometry.Shipready.Save public static class JsonTextReader Reads JSON back into a save tree. It never throws: a file that is not JSON comes back as a failure with the position and reason, which the recovery loader turns into a damaged file result and moves on to the next copy. Numbers keep the exact text they were written with, so a float comes back bit-exact and a long past 2^53 keeps every digit. Constants MaxDepth public const int MaxDepth = 128; How deeply a save may nest. The writer refuses anything deeper, so a file this rea"},{"p":"Shipready: API Reference","h":"JsonTextWriter","u":"save-api-reference.html#jsontextwriter","x":"static class in RareGeometry.Shipready.Save.Json · assembly RareGeometry.Shipready.Save public static class JsonTextWriter Writes a save tree as JSON. Shipready has its own writer rather than using JsonUtility for the whole file, because the tree has to come back exactly as it went in: member order preserved, numbers in the text they were written with, and nothing that depends on the machine language settings. Compact output is deterministic: the same tree always produces the same bytes. The checksum in the save header is measured over compact output, so turning pretty-printing on or off never"},{"p":"Shipready: API Reference","h":"JsonUtilityBridge","u":"save-api-reference.html#jsonutilitybridge","x":"static class in RareGeometry.Shipready.Save.Json · assembly RareGeometry.Shipready.Save public static class JsonUtilityBridge Saves a developer own [Serializable] class or struct by handing it to Unity and then folding the result into the save tree. The rule this gives a developer is simple: if the Inspector shows the value, Shipready can save it. The result is folded into the tree rather than stored as a blob of text, so a custom class is still searchable in the Save Inspector and still reachable by a migration. Methods ToNode(object) public static SaveNode ToNode(object value); Turns a value"},{"p":"Shipready: API Reference","h":"Namespace RareGeometry.Shipready.Save.Migration","u":"save-api-reference.html#namespace-raregeometryshipreadysavemigration","x":""},{"p":"Shipready: API Reference","h":"MigrationOutcome","u":"save-api-reference.html#migrationoutcome","x":"struct in RareGeometry.Shipready.Save.Migration · assembly RareGeometry.Shipready.Save public struct MigrationOutcome What running the migration steps did. Fields Code public SaveErrorCode Code; Why it failed, or SaveErrorCode.None . Error public string Error; A plain-English reason, or null on success. MissingVersion public int MissingVersion; The version that has no step, when the run stopped because of a gap. Zero otherwise. StepsRun public int[] StepsRun; Which steps ran, lowest version first. Empty when none were needed. Success public bool Success; Whether the save reached the version th"},{"p":"Shipready: API Reference","h":"MigrationRunner","u":"save-api-reference.html#migrationrunner","x":"class in RareGeometry.Shipready.Save.Migration · assembly RareGeometry.Shipready.Save public sealed class MigrationRunner Runs the migration steps that bring an old save up to the version the game is on. Steps run in order, one version at a time, so a save from three versions ago passes through every step in between rather than needing its own special case. A gap in that chain is reported rather than guessed at: loading a save the game only half understands is how progress gets quietly mangled. A step can arrive two ways: added by hand with Add(int, Action<SaveTree>, string) , or found through"},{"p":"Shipready: API Reference","h":"Namespace RareGeometry.Shipready.Save.Settings","u":"save-api-reference.html#namespace-raregeometryshipreadysavesettings","x":""},{"p":"Shipready: API Reference","h":"ChecksumPolicy","u":"save-api-reference.html#checksumpolicy","x":"enum in RareGeometry.Shipready.Save.Settings · assembly RareGeometry.Shipready.Save public enum ChecksumPolicy What Shipready does when a readable save no longer matches its own checksum. Values Name Value Description TreatEditsAsDamaged 0 Treat the file as damaged and fall back to a backup. This is the default, because a mismatch almost always means a truncated file rather than a deliberate edit. AcceptWithWarning 1 Load the file anyway and add a warning to the report. For games that want players to be able to edit their saves."},{"p":"Shipready: API Reference","h":"PrettyPrintMode","u":"save-api-reference.html#prettyprintmode","x":"enum in RareGeometry.Shipready.Save.Settings · assembly RareGeometry.Shipready.Save public enum PrettyPrintMode When Shipready indents a readable save file. Values Name Value Description Automatic 0 Indented in the Editor and in development builds, compact in a release build. This is the default: readable while a game is being made, smaller once it ships. Always 1 Always indented, even in a release build. Never 2 Never indented."},{"p":"Shipready: API Reference","h":"SaveCategory","u":"save-api-reference.html#savecategory","x":"class in RareGeometry.Shipready.Save.Settings · assembly RareGeometry.Shipready.Save public sealed class SaveCategory One named group of saved values. Every value Shipready stores belongs to exactly one category, which is what makes a partial save possible: a checkpoint can write Progress and World and leave everything else in the file as it was. Constructors SaveCategory(string, SaveCategoryScope) public SaveCategory(string name, SaveCategoryScope scope); Builds a category. Parameter Description name The category name, as the game refers to it in code. scope Whether it lives in each slot or i"},{"p":"Shipready: API Reference","h":"SaveCategoryScope","u":"save-api-reference.html#savecategoryscope","x":"enum in RareGeometry.Shipready.Save.Settings · assembly RareGeometry.Shipready.Save public enum SaveCategoryScope Where the values in a category are kept. Values Name Value Description Slot 0 Inside each save slot, so every slot has its own copy. This is the default, and what progress, inventory, quests and world state all want. Global 1 In one file for the whole game, outside the slots. Player settings go here, so that loading a save never takes somebody audio and controls back to an older state."},{"p":"Shipready: API Reference","h":"SaveFileOptions","u":"save-api-reference.html#savefileoptions","x":"class in RareGeometry.Shipready.Save.Settings · assembly RareGeometry.Shipready.Save public sealed class SaveFileOptions How save files are written: readable or packed, how many backups to keep, the extension, and what to do about a file that has been edited. The settings asset holds two sets: SaveKitSettings.FileOptions for the slots, the autosaves and the quicksave, and SaveKitSettings.GlobalFileOptions , which the global settings file uses instead when SaveKitSettings.UseSlotOptionsForGlobalFile is off. A SaveFileStore applies one set to every file unless it is given a set for a particular "},{"p":"Shipready: API Reference","h":"SaveKitSettings","u":"save-api-reference.html#savekitsettings","x":"class in RareGeometry.Shipready.Save.Settings · assembly RareGeometry.Shipready.Save public sealed class SaveKitSettings : ScriptableObject Every choice a game makes about saving, in one asset. The Setup Wizard creates it and fills it in; Shipready reads it and never writes to it at runtime. The asset lives in a Resources folder in the game own project, not inside the Shipready folder, so that updating Shipready never overwrites a developer settings. Properties AssetRegistry public AssetRegistry AssetRegistry { get; set; } The assets a save may link to, such as item definitions. The Editor kee"},{"p":"Shipready: API Reference","h":"Namespace RareGeometry.Shipready.Save.Storage","u":"save-api-reference.html#namespace-raregeometryshipreadysavestorage","x":""},{"p":"Shipready: API Reference","h":"FileSaveStorage","u":"save-api-reference.html#filesavestorage","x":"class in RareGeometry.Shipready.Save.Storage · assembly RareGeometry.Shipready.Save public sealed class FileSaveStorage : ISaveStorage Save files on the local disk, under a folder inside Application.persistentDataPath . This is what a shipped game uses. Writes are flushed all the way to the disk rather than left in the operating system cache, because the next step in a save verifies what was written and the step after that swaps it into place. A write that is still in a cache would make both of those lies. Constructors FileSaveStorage(string) public FileSaveStorage(string folderName); Storage "},{"p":"Shipready: API Reference","h":"ISaveStorage","u":"save-api-reference.html#isavestorage","x":"interface in RareGeometry.Shipready.Save.Storage · assembly RareGeometry.Shipready.Save public interface ISaveStorage Where save files live. Shipready ships a file storage for the real game and a memory storage for tests; a platform that needs something else, such as browser storage or a cloud service, can plug in here without the rest of Shipready changing. Names handed to these calls are plain file names inside the storage, never full paths, so the same save code works whatever the storage is. Nothing here throws. Every call reports what happened, because a save system that throws when a dis"},{"p":"Shipready: API Reference","h":"MemorySaveStorage","u":"save-api-reference.html#memorysavestorage","x":"class in RareGeometry.Shipready.Save.Storage · assembly RareGeometry.Shipready.Save public sealed class MemorySaveStorage : ISaveStorage Save files held in memory. Shipready tests run against this, and a game can use it to test its own save and load code without writing anything to disk. It can also be told to fail. That is what the reliability tests use to reproduce a full disk, a denied folder and a crash part way through a save: set FailAfterOperations to the step to fail at, and every file is left exactly as it was at that moment. Properties CountReads public bool CountReads { get; set; } "},{"p":"Shipready: API Reference","h":"StorageOutcome","u":"save-api-reference.html#storageoutcome","x":"struct in RareGeometry.Shipready.Save.Storage · assembly RareGeometry.Shipready.Save public struct StorageOutcome What one storage call did. Fields Code public SaveErrorCode Code; Why it failed, or SaveErrorCode.None . Error public string Error; A plain-English reason, or null on success. Success public bool Success; Whether the call did what it was asked to do. Methods Fail(SaveErrorCode, string) public static StorageOutcome Fail(SaveErrorCode code, string error); A failed outcome. Parameter Description code Why it failed. error A plain-English reason. Returns: An outcome whose Success is fal"},{"p":"Shipready: API Reference","h":"Namespace RareGeometry.Shipready.Save.World","u":"save-api-reference.html#namespace-raregeometryshipreadysaveworld","x":""},{"p":"Shipready: API Reference","h":"FieldSaver","u":"save-api-reference.html#fieldsaver","x":"static class in RareGeometry.Shipready.Save.World · assembly RareGeometry.Shipready.Save public static class FieldSaver The Field Picker saver: saves the Inspector fields ticked on a Saveable. It only accepts fields Unity itself saves (public or [SerializeField] , not [NonSerialized] , not static, not read-only) of a type Shipready supports: numbers, text, true/false, enums and the Unity vector, rotation, colour, rectangle and bounds types; classes and structs marked [Serializable] , saved under Unity's own rules; links to saved objects (a Saveable, its GameObject or one of its components) and"},{"p":"Shipready: API Reference","h":"ISceneLoader","u":"save-api-reference.html#isceneloader","x":"interface in RareGeometry.Shipready.Save.World · assembly RareGeometry.Shipready.Save public interface ISceneLoader Loads scenes for Shipready, for a game that starts its scene loads its own way, such as scenes that are not in Build Settings or a load that also shows a loading screen. Set it on SaveWorld.SceneLoader . Left at null, Shipready loads scenes that are in Build Settings with SceneManager.LoadSceneAsync . Return the AsyncOperation of a SceneManager load, and leave allowSceneActivation on: Shipready waits for the operation to finish, up to SaveWorld.SceneLoadTimeoutSeconds . A game th"},{"p":"Shipready: API Reference","h":"Namespace RareGeometry.Shipready.Save.World.Registries","u":"save-api-reference.html#namespace-raregeometryshipreadysaveworldregistries","x":""},{"p":"Shipready: API Reference","h":"AssetReferences","u":"save-api-reference.html#assetreferences","x":"static class in RareGeometry.Shipready.Save.World.Registries · assembly RareGeometry.Shipready.Save public static class AssetReferences Where Shipready looks up assets while saving and loading. The Asset Registry sets itself here when Shipready starts, and the save writer and reader use whatever is set. Nothing is registered until a game has an Asset Registry, so a reference written before then is reported rather than written as something that cannot be read back. Properties IsAvailable public static bool IsAvailable { get; } Whether a registry is available. Source public static IAssetReferenc"},{"p":"Shipready: API Reference","h":"AssetRegistry","u":"save-api-reference.html#assetregistry_1","x":"class in RareGeometry.Shipready.Save.World.Registries · assembly RareGeometry.Shipready.Save public sealed class AssetRegistry : ScriptableObject, IAssetReferenceSource Every asset a save may point at, such as ScriptableObject item definitions, under a permanent id: the asset's Unity GUID. A link to an asset is saved as that id, so a developer can rename or move the asset without breaking a single save. Shipready asks this registry first and then the Prefab Registry whenever it writes or reads an asset link, through AssetReferences . Point the settings asset at it with SaveKitSettings.AssetReg"},{"p":"Shipready: API Reference","h":"IAssetReferenceSource","u":"save-api-reference.html#iassetreferencesource","x":"interface in RareGeometry.Shipready.Save.World.Registries · assembly RareGeometry.Shipready.Save public interface IAssetReferenceSource Turns an asset into an identifier that survives a save, and back again. The Asset Registry implements it; the save writer and reader only ever talk to this. Methods TryGetAsset(string, Type, UnityEngine.Object) bool TryGetAsset(string id, Type type, out UnityEngine.Object asset); The asset an identifier refers to. Parameter Description id The stored identifier. type The type the caller is expecting. asset The asset, or null when the identifier is not registere"},{"p":"Shipready: API Reference","h":"PrefabRegistry","u":"save-api-reference.html#prefabregistry_1","x":"class in RareGeometry.Shipready.Save.World.Registries · assembly RareGeometry.Shipready.Save public sealed class PrefabRegistry : ScriptableObject Every prefab Shipready can re-create on load, under a permanent id: the prefab asset's Unity GUID. Objects spawned at runtime are saved with their prefab id, and a load finds the prefab here again. The Editor keeps this list up to date on its own whenever a prefab with a Saveable is imported, so a developer never edits it by hand. Point the settings asset at it with SaveKitSettings.PrefabRegistry . Properties Count public int Count { get; } How many"},{"p":"Shipready: API Reference","h":"SceneRegistry","u":"save-api-reference.html#sceneregistry_1","x":"class in RareGeometry.Shipready.Save.World.Registries · assembly RareGeometry.Shipready.Save public sealed class SceneRegistry : ScriptableObject Every scene Shipready saves, under a permanent id: the scene asset's Unity GUID, with its path as a label. Each scene's section in a save is keyed by that id, so renaming or moving a scene never breaks a save: the Editor updates the path here and the id stays. A loaded scene is found by its path, or by its name when it has no path (a scene created at runtime). Point the settings asset at it with SaveKitSettings.SceneRegistry . Properties Count public"},{"p":"Shipready: API Reference","h":"Namespace RareGeometry.Shipready.Save.UI","u":"save-api-reference.html#namespace-raregeometryshipreadysaveui","x":""},{"p":"Shipready: API Reference","h":"ConfirmDialog","u":"save-api-reference.html#confirmdialog","x":"class in RareGeometry.Shipready.Save.UI · assembly RareGeometry.Shipready.Save.UI public sealed class ConfirmDialog : MonoBehaviour The yes-or-no question the menu asks before anything that cannot be undone: overwriting a save, loading over unsaved progress, deleting. It covers the menu while it is open, so the mouse cannot reach anything else, and keyboard and gamepad navigation stays between its two buttons. Cancel (Escape, or East on a gamepad) backs out. The Cancel button is selected when it opens, so pressing Submit twice by accident never confirms something destructive. Add Component men"},{"p":"Shipready: API Reference","h":"DisplayAs","u":"save-api-reference.html#displayas","x":"enum in RareGeometry.Shipready.Save.UI · assembly RareGeometry.Shipready.Save.UI public enum DisplayAs How a message or state shows to the player: in words, as an icon, both, or not at all. The same choice is offered for every message Shipready shows: the saving indicator, the save notes, the menu's status line and co-op line, and the slot card badges. Values Name Value Description FullText 0 The whole sentence, such as \"Saved to Slot 1.\" or \"From a newer version\". ShortText 1 A word or two, such as \"Saved\" or \"Newer\". Icon 2 The icon alone, in the text colour. ColouredIcon 3 The icon alone, i"},{"p":"Shipready: API Reference","h":"GameMenu","u":"save-api-reference.html#gamemenu","x":"class in RareGeometry.Shipready.Save.UI · assembly RareGeometry.Shipready.Save.UI public sealed class GameMenu : MonoBehaviour A title screen or pause menu that needs no code: Continue (the newest save of any kind, greyed out when there is none), New Game, Save Game and Load Game (they open your SaveMenu ), Delete All Saves and Quit. Drop GameMenu.prefab into a scene, drag your save menu into its Save Menu field, and tick the buttons you want. It asks before anything that loses play: Quit and New Game ask \"without saving?\" when there is unsaved progress ( SaveKit.HasUnsavedProgress ), and Dele"},{"p":"Shipready: API Reference","h":"HotkeysActiveIn","u":"save-api-reference.html#hotkeysactivein","x":"enum in RareGeometry.Shipready.Save.UI · assembly RareGeometry.Shipready.Save.UI public enum HotkeysActiveIn Where the test hotkeys of SaveMenuHotkeys work. Values Name Value Description EditorOnly 0 Only in the Editor's Play Mode. A built game ignores the keys. The default. EditorAndBuilds 1 In the Editor and in built games. Off 2 Nowhere: the keys do nothing."},{"p":"Shipready: API Reference","h":"IndicatorCorner","u":"save-api-reference.html#indicatorcorner","x":"enum in RareGeometry.Shipready.Save.UI · assembly RareGeometry.Shipready.Save.UI public enum IndicatorCorner Which corner of the screen the SavingIndicator sits in. Values Name Value Description BottomRight 0 Bottom right, where most games put it. The default. BottomLeft 1 Bottom left. TopRight 2 Top right. TopLeft 3 Top left."},{"p":"Shipready: API Reference","h":"ISaveMenuConfirmation","u":"save-api-reference.html#isavemenuconfirmation","x":"interface in RareGeometry.Shipready.Save.UI · assembly RareGeometry.Shipready.Save.UI public interface ISaveMenuConfirmation Your own confirmation for the save menu's questions (\"Overwrite Slot 2?\", \"Load Slot 1?\", \"Delete Slot 3?\"), in place of Shipready's ConfirmDialog . Put it on a component and drag that component into the menu's Confirmation field, or set SaveMenu.Confirmation in code. Show the question your way, then call exactly one of the two callbacks when the player answers. The menu waits until then; it does not time out. Properties IsOpen bool IsOpen { get; } Whether the question i"},{"p":"Shipready: API Reference","h":"ISaveMenuNamePrompt","u":"save-api-reference.html#isavemenunameprompt","x":"interface in RareGeometry.Shipready.Save.UI · assembly RareGeometry.Shipready.Save.UI public interface ISaveMenuNamePrompt Your own name box for the save menu's player-named saves and Rename, beside your own ISaveMenuConfirmation . Implement it on the same component: when the menu's Confirmation has it, the menu asks for names through it; when it does not, the menu uses its own dialog's name box for names. Methods AskForName(string, string, string, string, string, bool, Action<string>, Action) void AskForName(string question, string currentName, string placeholder, string confirmLabel, string "},{"p":"Shipready: API Reference","h":"MenuCancelRelay","u":"save-api-reference.html#menucancelrelay","x":"class in RareGeometry.Shipready.Save.UI · assembly RareGeometry.Shipready.Save.UI public sealed class MenuCancelRelay : MonoBehaviour, ICancelHandler Passes Cancel (Escape, or East on a gamepad) from the EventSystem to the menu. An input module sends Cancel only to the selected object, whichever input system drives it, so every selectable in the menu carries one of these. The menu then closes the open confirmation, or else itself, at most once per frame. The generated prefabs already have it on every button; the menu also adds it at runtime to any selectable that lacks it, such as a button add"},{"p":"Shipready: API Reference","h":"MenuInputBridge","u":"save-api-reference.html#menuinputbridge","x":"class in RareGeometry.Shipready.Save.UI · assembly RareGeometry.Shipready.Save.UI public sealed class MenuInputBridge Reads the few keys the menu needs outside the EventSystem, from whichever input system the project uses: the old Input Manager, the Input System package, or both. The menu's buttons are driven by the EventSystem's input module; this covers Cancel when nothing is selected and the optional hotkeys of SaveMenuHotkeys . The Input System is read only when the package is installed and is an active input handler (Player Settings, Active Input Handling); the Input Manager only when it "},{"p":"Shipready: API Reference","h":"MenuLayout","u":"save-api-reference.html#menulayout","x":"class in RareGeometry.Shipready.Save.UI · assembly RareGeometry.Shipready.Save.UI public sealed class MenuLayout How the save menu is laid out: a list or a grid of cards, how tall a card is, which details a card shows, where the panel sits and how wide it is, and whether the title and the dimmed backdrop show. It lives on the MenuTheme ( MenuTheme.Layout ), so one theme asset carries the whole look, and Tools > Shipready > Menu Style edits it without opening a prefab. The defaults are the menu's original look: one column of 184-pixel cards with every detail showing, a 1440-pixel panel in the m"},{"p":"Shipready: API Reference","h":"MenuPlacement","u":"save-api-reference.html#menuplacement","x":"enum in RareGeometry.Shipready.Save.UI · assembly RareGeometry.Shipready.Save.UI public enum MenuPlacement Where the save menu's panel sits on the screen. Values Name Value Description Centre 0 In the middle of the screen. The default. Left 1 Against the left edge. Right 2 Against the right edge."},{"p":"Shipready: API Reference","h":"MenuText","u":"save-api-reference.html#menutext","x":"class in RareGeometry.Shipready.Save.UI · assembly RareGeometry.Shipready.Save.UI public sealed class MenuText : ScriptableObject Every word the save menu shows, in one asset, so translating the menu needs no extra package: duplicate DefaultMenuText , translate the copy and assign it to the menu ( SaveMenu.Text ), or swap assets when the player changes language. Lines with {0} are formatted with string.Format : {0} is the slot's name in the questions, the date in RestoredFromBackupStatus , and so on, as each field says. Dates use DateFormat in the culture DateCultureName names (invariant by de"},{"p":"Shipready: API Reference","h":"MenuTheme","u":"save-api-reference.html#menutheme","x":"class in RareGeometry.Shipready.Save.UI · assembly RareGeometry.Shipready.Save.UI public sealed class MenuTheme : ScriptableObject How the save menu looks: every colour, the font, the font sizes, the corner radius and the spacing, in one asset. Change a value here and every panel, card, button and badge follows, because each of them carries a ThemedElement that reads it. The easy way to restyle is Tools > Shipready > Menu Style: pick one of six ready-made styles, change any colour, size or the layout ( Layout ), and see it at once. It works on a copy in your data folder, never on Shipready's o"},{"p":"Shipready: API Reference","h":"MenuThemeRole","u":"save-api-reference.html#menuthemerole","x":"enum in RareGeometry.Shipready.Save.UI · assembly RareGeometry.Shipready.Save.UI public enum MenuThemeRole What a piece of the menu is, which decides the colours, font size and shape the theme gives it. Values Name Value Description None 0 Left alone by the theme. Backdrop 1 The full-screen dimming behind the menu. Panel 2 The menu panel, with the full corner radius. Divider 3 A thin separating line. AccentBar 4 The small accent bar beside the title. Card 5 A slot card's background. CardOutline 6 The selection ring around a card. ThumbnailFrame 7 The frame behind a thumbnail. Dialog 8 The conf"},{"p":"Shipready: API Reference","h":"MessageIcons","u":"save-api-reference.html#messageicons","x":"static class in RareGeometry.Shipready.Save.UI · assembly RareGeometry.Shipready.Save.UI public static class MessageIcons Shipready's built-in icons, one for each MessageKind : a spinner for saving and loading, a tick for saved, a document with a crack for damaged, a padlock, two people for co-op, and so on. They are drawn from exact shapes when first asked for, so Shipready ships no image files: every line has the same weight and smooth edges, the shape sits exactly in the middle of its square, and the big texture with its smaller copies stays crisp from a small badge up to a 4K screen. Each "},{"p":"Shipready: API Reference","h":"MessageKind","u":"save-api-reference.html#messagekind","x":"enum in RareGeometry.Shipready.Save.UI · assembly RareGeometry.Shipready.Save.UI public enum MessageKind The kinds of message Shipready shows the player. Each has its own colour and icon in the theme ( MenuTheme.ContextColour(MessageKind) , MenuTheme.IconFor(MessageKind) ) and its own short words in the menu text ( MenuText.ShortWords(MessageKind) ). Values Name Value Description Saving 0 A save is running. Saved 1 A save worked. SaveFailed 2 A save did not work. Loading 3 A load is running. Loaded 4 A load worked. LoadFailed 5 A load did not work. Restored 6 A save came back from its backup. "},{"p":"Shipready: API Reference","h":"MessageKindLook","u":"save-api-reference.html#messagekindlook","x":"class in RareGeometry.Shipready.Save.UI · assembly RareGeometry.Shipready.Save.UI public sealed class MessageKindLook One kind of message's own settings in a theme: its icon (empty uses Shipready's built-in icon) and, when ticked, a display of its own that every part of Shipready uses for it. Properties Display public DisplayAs Display { get; set; } How this kind of message shows when OwnDisplay is on. Icon public Sprite Icon { get; set; } The icon. Null uses Shipready's built-in icon. OwnDisplay public bool OwnDisplay { get; set; } Whether this kind of message uses Display everywhere."},{"p":"Shipready: API Reference","h":"MessageLook","u":"save-api-reference.html#messagelook","x":"class in RareGeometry.Shipready.Save.UI · assembly RareGeometry.Shipready.Save.UI public sealed class MessageLook How Shipready's messages look, on the MenuTheme ( MenuTheme.Messages ): how each part shows its messages ( DisplayAs ), where the saving indicator and the save notes sit, the colour of each state and each kind's icon. Set it once in the theme; the indicator, the notes and the menu can each choose their own on top. The colours of the Autosave, Damaged, Restored and From a newer version states are the theme's badge colours, so a badge and its icon always match. Properties Badges publ"},{"p":"Shipready: API Reference","h":"MessageSurface","u":"save-api-reference.html#messagesurface","x":"enum in RareGeometry.Shipready.Save.UI · assembly RareGeometry.Shipready.Save.UI public enum MessageSurface What a MessageView sits on. Values Name Value Description Pill 0 A pill in the state's colour, as a slot card's badges are. An icon alone shows without the pill. Panel 1 A box of its own that the theme colours, as the saving indicator and the save notes are. Plain 2 Nothing behind it, as the menu's status line is."},{"p":"Shipready: API Reference","h":"MessageView","u":"save-api-reference.html#messageview","x":"class in RareGeometry.Shipready.Save.UI · assembly RareGeometry.Shipready.Save.UI public sealed class MessageView : MonoBehaviour, ILayoutElement Shows one message the way the theme asks ( DisplayAs ): its full or short words, its icon (plain or in the state's colour), both, or nothing. The saving indicator, the save notes, the menu's status line and the slot card badges are each one of these. It lays its icon and words out itself, so they are always exactly centred: the block is in the middle of the box, the icon's middle sits level with the middle of the capital letters, and a turning icon t"},{"p":"Shipready: API Reference","h":"OrientationScaler","u":"save-api-reference.html#orientationscaler","x":"class in RareGeometry.Shipready.Save.UI · assembly RareGeometry.Shipready.Save.UI public sealed class OrientationScaler : MonoBehaviour Sits beside a kit canvas's Canvas Scaler and picks its reference size for the screen: the 1920×1080 design on a wide screen, and on a screen held upright (with the layout's MenuLayout.Portrait on Auto or Custom) a narrow one, so a phone gets words and buttons it can read and press. The menus listen to Changed and lay themselves out again. It checks the canvas's size every frame and acts only when it changes, as when a phone turns; it allocates nothing. Add Com"},{"p":"Shipready: API Reference","h":"PortraitLayout","u":"save-api-reference.html#portraitlayout","x":"enum in RareGeometry.Shipready.Save.UI · assembly RareGeometry.Shipready.Save.UI public enum PortraitLayout How Shipready's menus lay out on a screen held upright (taller than wide), such as a phone. Values Name Value Description Auto 0 A phone layout: the menus are drawn for a 760-unit-wide screen, so words and buttons are as big as a phone needs (body text about 31 pixels and buttons about 90 on a 1080-wide phone), the save menu fills the width with one column of tiles, and the Game Menu takes most of it. The default. Landscape 1 The same layout as a wide screen, scaled down to fit the width"},{"p":"Shipready: API Reference","h":"RoundedSprites","u":"save-api-reference.html#roundedsprites","x":"static class in RareGeometry.Shipready.Save.UI · assembly RareGeometry.Shipready.Save.UI public static class RoundedSprites Makes the rounded shapes the menu is drawn with, so Shipready ships no sprite assets: a filled rounded rectangle and a rounded outline for any radius, as small 9-sliced sprites with anti-aliased corners. Each shape is made once and cached for the rest of the run. The cache is cleared when a play session starts, so it is correct with domain reload switched off. Constants MaximumRadius public const int MaximumRadius = 64; The largest radius a sprite is made for. Larger requ"},{"p":"Shipready: API Reference","h":"SafeAreaPanel","u":"save-api-reference.html#safeareapanel","x":"class in RareGeometry.Shipready.Save.UI · assembly RareGeometry.Shipready.Save.UI public sealed class SafeAreaPanel : MonoBehaviour Keeps a panel inside the screen's safe area: clear of a phone's notch, rounded corners and the home bar, and of a TV's overscan. It shrinks its own rectangle to Screen.safeArea , so everything under it (the save menu, the Game Menu, the saving indicator, the save notes) stays where the player can see and reach it. On a screen with no unsafe edges it fills the whole screen, so nothing changes. It checks again when the screen or its safe area changes, and allocates "},{"p":"Shipready: API Reference","h":"SaveMenu","u":"save-api-reference.html#savemenu_1","x":"class in RareGeometry.Shipready.Save.UI · assembly RareGeometry.Shipready.Save.UI public sealed class SaveMenu : MonoBehaviour The player's save and load menu: one prefab, two modes. Drop SaveMenu.prefab into a scene and call Open(SaveMenuMode) from a pause menu or a main menu. Save mode lists the manual slots. An empty slot saves at once; a used one asks \"Overwrite?\" first. A thumbnail is taken just before the menu shows. Load mode lists the autosaves (newest first), the quicksave, then the manual slots that hold a save, then the empty ones, dimmed. Loading asks first whether unsaved progress"},{"p":"Shipready: API Reference","h":"SaveMenuHotkeys","u":"save-api-reference.html#savemenuhotkeys","x":"class in RareGeometry.Shipready.Save.UI · assembly RareGeometry.Shipready.Save.UI public sealed class SaveMenuHotkeys : MonoBehaviour Test hotkeys for the save menu: F5 opens it in Save mode and F9 in Load mode, and the same key again closes it. The keys can be changed in the Inspector. Works with the old Input Manager and with the Input System package. Meant for trying saves while you build the game, so by default the keys work only in the Editor ( ActiveIn ); choose Editor and builds to keep them in the finished game. The Start window and the Setup Wizard add it next to the menu. Add Compone"},{"p":"Shipready: API Reference","h":"SaveMenuMode","u":"save-api-reference.html#savemenumode","x":"enum in RareGeometry.Shipready.Save.UI · assembly RareGeometry.Shipready.Save.UI public enum SaveMenuMode Which job the save menu is doing: the same prefab saves and loads. Values Name Value Description Save 0 Lists the manual slots to save into: an empty slot saves at once, a used one asks first. Load 1 Lists the autosaves, the quicksave and the manual slots to load from."},{"p":"Shipready: API Reference","h":"SaveMenuTab","u":"save-api-reference.html#savemenutab","x":"enum in RareGeometry.Shipready.Save.UI · assembly RareGeometry.Shipready.Save.UI public enum SaveMenuTab Which saves the Load menu's tabs show. See SaveMenu.Tab . Values Name Value Description All 0 Every save: the autosaves, the quicksave and the player's own slots. Saves 1 Only the player's own slots. Autosaves 2 Only the autosaves and the quicksave."},{"p":"Shipready: API Reference","h":"SaveNotes","u":"save-api-reference.html#savenotes","x":"class in RareGeometry.Shipready.Save.UI · assembly RareGeometry.Shipready.Save.UI public sealed class SaveNotes : MonoBehaviour A short note to the player when a load finished (\"Game loaded\"), when a save came back from its backup, or when a save or a load did not work, shown for a moment at the edge of the screen. The saving indicator already shows saves, so saves that worked are off by default. Drop SaveNotes.prefab into your first scene; it stays across scene loads. No code. Like the indicator, it takes its colours, icons and font from the MenuTheme and its words from the MenuText . The the"},{"p":"Shipready: API Reference","h":"SavingIndicator","u":"save-api-reference.html#savingindicator","x":"class in RareGeometry.Shipready.Save.UI · assembly RareGeometry.Shipready.Save.UI public sealed class SavingIndicator : MonoBehaviour A small \"Saving...\" with a turning icon at the edge of the screen while a save runs, then \"Saved\" (or \"Save failed\") for a moment before it fades out. Players expect to see that an autosave happened. Drop SavingIndicator.prefab into your first scene; it stays across scene loads. No code. Everything about it is yours: the colours, icons and font come from the same MenuTheme as the save menu, the words from its MenuText . The theme says how it shows ( DisplayAs : "},{"p":"Shipready: API Reference","h":"SavingIndicator.State","u":"save-api-reference.html#savingindicatorstate","x":"enum in RareGeometry.Shipready.Save.UI · assembly RareGeometry.Shipready.Save.UI public enum State What the indicator is showing. Values Name Value Description Hidden 0 Nothing: it is hidden. Saving 1 A save is running. Saved 2 The save worked: \"Saved\" for a moment. Failed 3 The save failed: \"Save failed\" for a moment."},{"p":"Shipready: API Reference","h":"ScreenPlacement","u":"save-api-reference.html#screenplacement","x":"class in RareGeometry.Shipready.Save.UI · assembly RareGeometry.Shipready.Save.UI public sealed class ScreenPlacement Where a message box sits: one of six places on the screen's edges, or any point of it, and how far in from the edge. Sizes are in pixels at the 1920×1080 reference resolution. Constructors ScreenPlacement() public ScreenPlacement(); Makes a placement in the bottom right corner, 40 pixels in. ScreenPlacement(ScreenPosition, float) public ScreenPlacement(ScreenPosition position, float margin); Makes a placement at one of the six places. Parameter Description position The place. m"},{"p":"Shipready: API Reference","h":"ScreenPosition","u":"save-api-reference.html#screenposition","x":"enum in RareGeometry.Shipready.Save.UI · assembly RareGeometry.Shipready.Save.UI public enum ScreenPosition Where on the screen a message box sits. Values Name Value Description TopLeft 0 The top left corner. TopCentre 1 The middle of the top edge. TopRight 2 The top right corner. BottomLeft 3 The bottom left corner. BottomCentre 4 The middle of the bottom edge. BottomRight 5 The bottom right corner. Custom 6 Any point of the screen: ScreenPlacement.CustomAnchor plus ScreenPlacement.Offset ."},{"p":"Shipready: API Reference","h":"SlotCard","u":"save-api-reference.html#slotcard","x":"class in RareGeometry.Shipready.Save.UI · assembly RareGeometry.Shipready.Save.UI public sealed class SlotCard : MonoBehaviour One save in the menu's list: its thumbnail on the left; its name, badges, date, playtime, location and first two summary lines in the middle; its buttons on the right. SaveMenu makes the cards from the SlotCard prefab and fills them in; a game reads them, it does not need to drive them. The card itself is selectable ( MainSelectable ): Submit on it, or a click or tap on it, does the card's main action, Save or Load. Its buttons sit to its right on the keyboard and game"},{"p":"Shipready: API Reference","h":"SlotListLayout","u":"save-api-reference.html#slotlistlayout","x":"class in RareGeometry.Shipready.Save.UI · assembly RareGeometry.Shipready.Save.UI public sealed class SlotListLayout : LayoutGroup Lays the save menu's cards out as a list (one column) or as a grid of tiles (two to four columns). SaveMenu sets the columns and the spacing from the theme's MenuTheme.Layout , so switching between list and grid needs no prefab edit. With one column it lays the cards out exactly as a VerticalLayoutGroup that controls the children's size and stretches them to the full width: each card is as tall as its preferred height. In a grid every tile in a row gets the same wi"},{"p":"Shipready: API Reference","h":"SlotListScroller","u":"save-api-reference.html#slotlistscroller","x":"class in RareGeometry.Shipready.Save.UI · assembly RareGeometry.Shipready.Save.UI public sealed class SlotListScroller : MonoBehaviour Keeps the selected entry of a scrolling list in view. When keyboard or gamepad navigation moves the selection to an entry that is partly or wholly outside the ScrollRect 's viewport, the list glides just far enough to show all of it. This is what makes every one of 99 slots reachable with a gamepad. Put it on the object with the ScrollRect . It only reacts to a change of selection, so it never fights the mouse wheel or a drag. It runs on unscaled time, so it al"},{"p":"Shipready: API Reference","h":"ThemedElement","u":"save-api-reference.html#themedelement","x":"class in RareGeometry.Shipready.Save.UI · assembly RareGeometry.Shipready.Save.UI public sealed class ThemedElement : MonoBehaviour, ISelectHandler, IDeselectHandler Gives one piece of the menu its look from a MenuTheme : the colour and rounded shape of an image, the colours of a button and its label, or the font, size and colour of a label. The generated prefabs carry one on every piece, and SaveMenu applies the theme when it opens and whenever SaveMenu.Theme changes. Because the theme is applied at runtime, a colour changed by hand on an element that has this component is replaced by the the"},{"p":"Shipready: API Reference","h":"Namespace RareGeometry.Shipready.Save.Editor","u":"save-api-reference.html#namespace-raregeometryshipreadysaveeditor","x":""},{"p":"Shipready: API Reference","h":"KitLinks","u":"save-api-reference.html#kitlinks","x":"static class in RareGeometry.Shipready.Save.Editor · assembly RareGeometry.Shipready.Save.Editor.Core public static class KitLinks Every web address and the e-mail address Shipready shows, in one place: the editor tools' help buttons read them from here, and tools/fill-placeholders.ps1 reads this file to write them into the documents, in place of the WEBSITE, SUPPORT_REPO, ISSUE_FORM, DISCUSSIONS, DISCORD and SUPPORT_EMAIL placeholders and of the values it wrote last time. To change one, edit it here and run that script. Keep each value a plain string constant on one line, so the script can re"},{"p":"Shipready: API Reference","h":"Namespace RareGeometry.Shipready.Save.Editor.Build","u":"save-api-reference.html#namespace-raregeometryshipreadysaveeditorbuild","x":""},{"p":"Shipready: API Reference","h":"MigrationLinkXmlBuilder","u":"save-api-reference.html#migrationlinkxmlbuilder","x":"class in RareGeometry.Shipready.Save.Editor.Build · assembly RareGeometry.Shipready.Save.Editor.Core public sealed class MigrationLinkXmlBuilder : IUnityLinkerProcessor Keeps migration steps alive through code stripping. Nothing in a game calls a migration directly: Shipready finds them by looking for the [SaveMigration] attribute. Code stripping cannot see that, so in an IL2CPP build with stripping turned up it would remove the very methods that let an old save load. This adds a link file during the build naming every type that holds one, which is the supported way to tell the stripper to lea"},{"p":"Shipready: API Reference","h":"Namespace RareGeometry.Shipready.Save.Editor.Common","u":"save-api-reference.html#namespace-raregeometryshipreadysaveeditorcommon","x":""},{"p":"Shipready: API Reference","h":"SceneLoadingHooksInstaller","u":"save-api-reference.html#sceneloadinghooksinstaller","x":"static class in RareGeometry.Shipready.Save.Editor.Common · assembly RareGeometry.Shipready.Save.Editor.Core public static class SceneLoadingHooksInstaller Lets Shipready's default scene loader open, in the Editor, a saved scene that is not in Build Settings, so a scene being tested before it is added there still loads and restores with SaveKit.LoadAsync . A build cannot load such a scene, and Shipready warns once per scene when this happens. Unity installs it whenever scripts load. Methods FindSceneAsset(string, string, string, bool) public static string FindSceneAsset(string sceneId, string "},{"p":"Shipready: API Reference","h":"Namespace RareGeometry.Shipready.Save.Editor.Registries","u":"save-api-reference.html#namespace-raregeometryshipreadysaveeditorregistries","x":""},{"p":"Shipready: API Reference","h":"RegistryUpkeep","u":"save-api-reference.html#registryupkeep","x":"static class in RareGeometry.Shipready.Save.Editor.Registries · assembly RareGeometry.Shipready.Save.Editor.Core public static class RegistryUpkeep Keeps the Prefab, Asset and Scene Registries in step with the project, and keeps the Saveable ids on prefab assets right. Prefabs. A Saveable on the root of a prefab asset carries the prefab's asset GUID as its prefab id, and the prefab is listed in the Prefab Registry under that GUID. A Saveable on a child carries a prefab-local id that is unique inside the prefab and never changes once given. No Saveable on a prefab asset carries an object id: th"},{"p":"Shipready: API Reference","h":"RegistryUpkeepResult","u":"save-api-reference.html#registryupkeepresult","x":"class in RareGeometry.Shipready.Save.Editor.Registries · assembly RareGeometry.Shipready.Save.Editor.Core public sealed class RegistryUpkeepResult What one run of RegistryUpkeep changed: prefab assets whose Saveable ids were put right, and registry entries added, updated or removed. Properties Added public int Added { get; } Registry entries that were added. Failed public int Failed { get; } Prefab assets that needed fixing but could not be saved. The Console says why. HasChanges public bool HasChanges { get; } Whether anything was changed. PrefabsUpdated public int PrefabsUpdated { get; } Pre"},{"p":"Shipready: API Reference","h":"SaveableDuplicateScan","u":"save-api-reference.html#saveableduplicatescan","x":"static class in RareGeometry.Shipready.Save.Editor.Registries · assembly RareGeometry.Shipready.Save.Editor.Core public static class SaveableDuplicateScan Keeps every Saveable in the open scenes on its own id while you edit. A Saveable fixes its own id when it wakes, but an inactive object never wakes, so a copy of an inactive object would keep the original's id. This scan runs after any change to the Hierarchy, at most once per Editor update and never in Play Mode. It looks at every Saveable in every loaded scene, inactive ones included, and gives a new id to any that has none or shares one w"},{"p":"Shipready: API Reference","h":"SaveableEditorHooksInstaller","u":"save-api-reference.html#saveableeditorhooksinstaller","x":"static class in RareGeometry.Shipready.Save.Editor.Registries · assembly RareGeometry.Shipready.Save.Editor.Core public static class SaveableEditorHooksInstaller Connects the Saveable component to the Editor. The runtime code cannot use Editor API, yet a Saveable has to know two Editor-only things when it gives itself an id in Edit Mode: whether it is part of a prefab asset or a prefab being edited (those never carry an object id), and how to tell Unity that its scene changed so the new id is saved. This class answers both, and Unity installs it whenever scripts load. Marking dirty is deferred"},{"p":"Shipready: API Reference","h":"Namespace RareGeometry.Shipready.Save.Editor.Diagnostics","u":"save-api-reference.html#namespace-raregeometryshipreadysaveeditordiagnostics","x":""},{"p":"Shipready: API Reference","h":"DiagnosticCheck","u":"save-api-reference.html#diagnosticcheck","x":"enum in RareGeometry.Shipready.Save.Editor.Diagnostics · assembly RareGeometry.Shipready.Save.Editor public enum DiagnosticCheck The checks Diagnostics runs. Each issue names the check that found it. Values Name Value Description DuplicateId 0 Two Saveables share an id, in one scene or across the scenes checked. MissingId 1 A Saveable in a scene has no id, so its state cannot be saved. PrefabNotRegistered 2 A prefab with a Saveable on its root is not in the Prefab Registry, or carries the wrong prefab id. AssetNotRegistered 3 A ticked field links to an asset that neither the Asset Registry nor"},{"p":"Shipready: API Reference","h":"DiagnosticIssue","u":"save-api-reference.html#diagnosticissue","x":"class in RareGeometry.Shipready.Save.Editor.Diagnostics · assembly RareGeometry.Shipready.Save.Editor public sealed class DiagnosticIssue One problem Diagnostics found: which check found it, how serious it is, a plain-English message naming the object, the object or asset it is about, the matching error code, and a one-click fix where one is safe. Properties CanFix public bool CanFix { get; } Whether there is a safe one-click fix. CheckId public DiagnosticCheck CheckId { get; } The check that found the problem. Context public Object Context { get; } What the problem is about: a GameObject or c"},{"p":"Shipready: API Reference","h":"DiagnosticSeverity","u":"save-api-reference.html#diagnosticseverity","x":"enum in RareGeometry.Shipready.Save.Editor.Diagnostics · assembly RareGeometry.Shipready.Save.Editor public enum DiagnosticSeverity How serious a Diagnostics issue is. Values Name Value Description Info 0 Worth knowing; nothing breaks. Warning 1 Something will not be saved or loaded the way you expect. Error 2 Saving or loading cannot work as set up."},{"p":"Shipready: API Reference","h":"DiagnosticsResult","u":"save-api-reference.html#diagnosticsresult","x":"class in RareGeometry.Shipready.Save.Editor.Diagnostics · assembly RareGeometry.Shipready.Save.Editor public sealed class DiagnosticsResult What one Diagnostics run found: every issue, most serious first, and how much was checked. Properties ErrorCount public int ErrorCount { get; } How many issues are errors. FinishedUtc public DateTime FinishedUtc { get; } When the run finished, in UTC. FixableCount public int FixableCount { get; } How many issues have a one-click fix that has not run yet. InfoCount public int InfoCount { get; } How many issues are information only. Issues public IReadOnlyLi"},{"p":"Shipready: API Reference","h":"DiagnosticsRunner","u":"save-api-reference.html#diagnosticsrunner","x":"static class in RareGeometry.Shipready.Save.Editor.Diagnostics · assembly RareGeometry.Shipready.Save.Editor public static class DiagnosticsRunner Runs every Diagnostics check over a DiagnosticsScope and hands back the issues found, each with a plain-English message and, where it is safe, a one-click fix that is recorded for Undo. Scenes that are not open are read one at a time into a preview scene (on Unity 2022.3, opened beside the open scenes for a moment and closed again unsaved), with the Editor's automatic id repair paused, so two copies of an id are never loaded together and nothing qui"},{"p":"Shipready: API Reference","h":"DiagnosticsScope","u":"save-api-reference.html#diagnosticsscope","x":"class in RareGeometry.Shipready.Save.Editor.Diagnostics · assembly RareGeometry.Shipready.Save.Editor public sealed class DiagnosticsScope What Diagnostics looks at. The default is the open scenes plus the project's prefabs, with the project's own settings asset and the active build target. Shipready's own folders ( Assets/RareGeometry/Shipready/Save and its test and build folders) are left out of the prefab search and of the Build Settings check, unless a scene path or an asset folder in the scope names them. Constructors DiagnosticsScope() public DiagnosticsScope(); Builds the default scope:"},{"p":"Shipready: API Reference","h":"DiagnosticsWindow","u":"save-api-reference.html#diagnosticswindow","x":"class in RareGeometry.Shipready.Save.Editor.Diagnostics · assembly RareGeometry.Shipready.Save.Editor public sealed class DiagnosticsWindow : EditorWindow The Diagnostics window: runs every check over the chosen scope and lists what it finds, most serious first, each with a plain-English message, the object it is about and a one-click fix where one is safe. Every fix can be undone with Edit > Undo. Constants MenuPath public const string MenuPath = \"Tools/Shipready/Diagnostics\"; The menu command that opens Diagnostics. Methods Open() public static void Open(); Opens Diagnostics and runs it."},{"p":"Shipready: API Reference","h":"Namespace RareGeometry.Shipready.Save.Editor.Drawers","u":"save-api-reference.html#namespace-raregeometryshipreadysaveeditordrawers","x":""},{"p":"Shipready: API Reference","h":"FieldPickerModel","u":"save-api-reference.html#fieldpickermodel","x":"class in RareGeometry.Shipready.Save.Editor.Drawers · assembly RareGeometry.Shipready.Save.Editor public sealed class FieldPickerModel The Field Picker of one Saveable , without any drawing: one row per field Unity serializes on each component of the GameObject, whether it can be ticked, and why not when it cannot. The Saveable's custom Inspector draws it; tests drive it directly. Rows list the fields the Inspector shows: public or [SerializeField] , not [NonSerialized] , not static, not read-only, not [HideInInspector] , found by reflection on the component's class and its base classes. The T"},{"p":"Shipready: API Reference","h":"FieldPickerRow","u":"save-api-reference.html#fieldpickerrow","x":"class in RareGeometry.Shipready.Save.Editor.Drawers · assembly RareGeometry.Shipready.Save.Editor public sealed class FieldPickerRow One field in the Field Picker: a field Unity serializes on one component of the Saveable's GameObject, whether Shipready can save it, why not when it cannot, and whether it is ticked. Properties Component public Component Component { get; } The component the field belongs to. DisplayName public string DisplayName { get; } The name the Inspector shows, for example Is Open . Field public FieldInfo Field { get; } The field itself. FieldName public string FieldName {"},{"p":"Shipready: API Reference","h":"SaveableEditor","u":"save-api-reference.html#saveableeditor","x":"class in RareGeometry.Shipready.Save.Editor.Drawers · assembly RareGeometry.Shipready.Save.Editor public sealed class SaveableEditor : UnityEditor.Editor The Inspector of a Saveable : a one-sentence summary of what it saves, the built-in savers with a one-line explanation each, and the Field Picker, where the fields of the other components on the object are ticked to be saved. Fields Shipready cannot save are greyed out, with the reason, and a ticked field that the script's Start may set gets a dismissible warning. The id and the category sit in a collapsed Advanced section. Methods OnInspecto"},{"p":"Shipready: API Reference","h":"SaveableSummary","u":"save-api-reference.html#saveablesummary","x":"static class in RareGeometry.Shipready.Save.Editor.Drawers · assembly RareGeometry.Shipready.Save.Editor public static class SaveableSummary The one sentence at the top of a Saveable's Inspector that says what it saves, for example \"Saves position, active state and 2 fields: Is Open, Gold\". Constants MaxNamedFields public const int MaxNamedFields = 4; How many field names the sentence lists before it says \"and N more\". Methods Describe(bool, bool, bool, bool, IList<string>) public static string Describe(bool position, bool active, bool destroyed, bool physics, IList<string> fieldNames); Builds"},{"p":"Shipready: API Reference","h":"SaveKitSettingsEditor","u":"save-api-reference.html#savekitsettingseditor","x":"class in RareGeometry.Shipready.Save.Editor.Drawers · assembly RareGeometry.Shipready.Save.Editor public sealed class SaveKitSettingsEditor : UnityEditor.Editor The Inspector of Shipready's settings asset, in two tabs. Basics holds what most games touch: start-up, where saves go, slots, autosave and Console messages, in plain words. Advanced holds everything else, each in a section that starts collapsed: file names, categories, packing, encryption and backups, game updates, world objects and the scene scan. Categories and file options are edited through the same checks as the Setup Wizard and "},{"p":"Shipready: API Reference","h":"StartAssignmentScan","u":"save-api-reference.html#startassignmentscan","x":"static class in RareGeometry.Shipready.Save.Editor.Drawers · assembly RareGeometry.Shipready.Save.Editor public static class StartAssignmentScan A quick look through a script's source for fields that its Start method sets. Shipready restores saved values after Awake and before Start , so a value set in Start overwrites the one just loaded. This is a plain text scan, not a compiler: it finds simple assignments such as isOpen = false; or this.gold += 5; in the script's own Start , and can miss or wrongly flag unusual code, so the Inspector words its warning as \"may\". Methods Describe(string, ILi"},{"p":"Shipready: API Reference","h":"Namespace RareGeometry.Shipready.Save.Editor.FirstRun","u":"save-api-reference.html#namespace-raregeometryshipreadysaveeditorfirstrun","x":""},{"p":"Shipready: API Reference","h":"BugReport","u":"save-api-reference.html#bugreport","x":"static class in RareGeometry.Shipready.Save.Editor.FirstRun · assembly RareGeometry.Shipready.Save.Editor public static class BugReport Report a Bug: copies a ready-to-paste report to the clipboard (kit, Unity and pipeline versions, platform, the last three saves and loads, and Diagnostics' issues), and the Start window's Help and support page shows the bug report form's address, with the fields it can fill already filled, to copy. Nothing is opened in a browser. The report holds no file paths beyond the save folder's name and no saved values. From the Start window and Tools → Shipready → Save"},{"p":"Shipready: API Reference","h":"BugReportFacts","u":"save-api-reference.html#bugreportfacts","x":"class in RareGeometry.Shipready.Save.Editor.FirstRun · assembly RareGeometry.Shipready.Save.Editor public sealed class BugReportFacts What a bug report says about the project, gathered by BugReport.Gather(bool) . Fields Issues public List<string> Issues; Diagnostics' issues, already described by BugReport.Describe(DiagnosticIssue) . Null when Diagnostics did not run. KitVersion public string KitVersion; Shipready's version, such as 1.0.0. Platform public string Platform; The operating system and the active build target. RenderPipeline public string RenderPipeline; Built-in, URP, HDRP or the na"},{"p":"Shipready: API Reference","h":"DemoTextCheck","u":"save-api-reference.html#demotextcheck","x":"static class in RareGeometry.Shipready.Save.Editor.FirstRun · assembly RareGeometry.Shipready.Save.Editor public static class DemoTextCheck The demos' text uses TextMesh Pro. When a demo scene is opened in a project without TextMesh Pro's essential resources, its panels and buttons would be blank, so Shipready asks once per editor session whether to import them. Never in batch mode or Play Mode. Constants DemoScenesFolder public const string DemoScenesFolder = \"Assets/RareGeometry/Shipready/Save/Samples/Demos/Scenes/\"; The folder of Shipready's demo scenes. Methods IsDemoScene(string) public s"},{"p":"Shipready: API Reference","h":"FirstRunCheck","u":"save-api-reference.html#firstruncheck","x":"enum in RareGeometry.Shipready.Save.Editor.FirstRun · assembly RareGeometry.Shipready.Save.Editor public enum FirstRunCheck The things the Start window checks before a first save can work. Values Name Value Description Settings 0 The project has Shipready's settings asset. SceneSaved 1 The open scene is saved to a file, so a load can open it again. SceneInBuildSettings 2 The open scene is in Build Settings, so a build can load it. Hotkeys 3 The open scene has F5 / F9 test hotkeys that work in the Editor. MenuText 4 TextMesh Pro's essential resources are in the project, so the save menu and the"},{"p":"Shipready: API Reference","h":"FirstRunStatus","u":"save-api-reference.html#firstrunstatus","x":"static class in RareGeometry.Shipready.Save.Editor.FirstRun · assembly RareGeometry.Shipready.Save.Editor public static class FirstRunStatus Works out the Start window's status strip from what the Editor reports: settings found, scene saved, scene in Build Settings, hotkeys on and the menu's text resources. Pure logic, so it can be tested without a scene. Methods CountOk(IList<FirstRunStatusItem>) public static int CountOk(IList<FirstRunStatusItem> items); How many of the items pass. Parameter Description items The items from Evaluate(bool, bool, bool, bool, bool, bool) . Returns: The number o"},{"p":"Shipready: API Reference","h":"FirstRunStatusItem","u":"save-api-reference.html#firstrunstatusitem","x":"class in RareGeometry.Shipready.Save.Editor.FirstRun · assembly RareGeometry.Shipready.Save.Editor public sealed class FirstRunStatusItem One check of the Start window's status strip, with its fix. Properties CanFix public bool CanFix { get; } Whether the fix can run now. A scene that was never saved cannot be added to Build Settings yet. Check public FirstRunCheck Check { get; } Which check this is. Detail public string Detail { get; } One sentence on why it matters and what the fix does. Shown as a tooltip. FixLabel public string FixLabel { get; } The text of the one-click fix button, such a"},{"p":"Shipready: API Reference","h":"PlayModeToasts","u":"save-api-reference.html#playmodetoasts","x":"static class in RareGeometry.Shipready.Save.Editor.FirstRun · assembly RareGeometry.Shipready.Save.Editor public static class PlayModeToasts Switches the Play Mode toast on and off, and adds it to the Game view at the start of Play Mode. The toast says what each save or load did; clicking it opens the Save Inspector on that report, or Diagnostics when the operation failed without one. It is on by default, never runs in batch mode (builds, tests, command-line runs) and never exists in a build. The choice is kept per user in EditorPrefs. Properties Enabled public static bool Enabled { get; set; "},{"p":"Shipready: API Reference","h":"StartWindow","u":"save-api-reference.html#startwindow","x":"class in RareGeometry.Shipready.Save.Editor.FirstRun · assembly RareGeometry.Shipready.Save.Editor public sealed class StartWindow : EditorWindow The Start window: the first thing a new buyer sees. A status strip checks the five things a first save needs (settings, a saved scene, the scene in Build Settings, F5 / F9 hotkeys and the menu's text resources), each with a one-click fix, and three big choices lead through short guided flows: make objects remember their state, add a save and load menu, or save values from scripts. It opens by itself once after Shipready is imported (see StartWindowAu"},{"p":"Shipready: API Reference","h":"StartWindow.Flow","u":"save-api-reference.html#startwindowflow","x":"enum in RareGeometry.Shipready.Save.Editor.FirstRun · assembly RareGeometry.Shipready.Save.Editor public enum Flow The guided flows the Start window offers. Values Name Value Description Home 0 The three choices. RememberObjects 1 Make objects remember their state. AddMenu 2 Add a save and load menu. SaveFromScripts 3 Save values from scripts. Help 4 Help and support: the version, the documents, where to get help, system info and Report a Bug."},{"p":"Shipready: API Reference","h":"StartWindowAutoOpen","u":"save-api-reference.html#startwindowautoopen","x":"static class in RareGeometry.Shipready.Save.Editor.FirstRun · assembly RareGeometry.Shipready.Save.Editor public static class StartWindowAutoOpen Opens the Start window once, the first time the Editor loads Shipready in a project. It never opens in batch mode or while the game plays, and not when the project already has a settings asset. The \"already shown\" flag is kept per project in EditorPrefs. With ShowAtStartup on (off by default), it also opens once each time the project opens. Properties HasBeenShown public static bool HasBeenShown { get; } Whether the Start window has already opened by"},{"p":"Shipready: API Reference","h":"Namespace RareGeometry.Shipready.Save.Editor.Generators","u":"save-api-reference.html#namespace-raregeometryshipreadysaveeditorgenerators","x":""},{"p":"Shipready: API Reference","h":"DemoSceneGenerator","u":"save-api-reference.html#demoscenegenerator","x":"static class in RareGeometry.Shipready.Save.Editor.Generators · assembly RareGeometry.Shipready.Save.Editor public static class DemoSceneGenerator Builds the four demo scenes and everything they use, in Samples/Demos : the scenes ( QuickStartDemo , WorldDemo , WorldDemo_Cellar , GameUpdatesDemo , RecoveryDemo ), the crate prefab and the four on-screen panel prefabs, one settings asset per demo with the demo Prefab, Asset and Scene Registries, and the version 1 example save, written through Shipready itself. Scenes, prefabs and assets are only ever generated, never hand-written YAML. Safe to ru"},{"p":"Shipready: API Reference","h":"MenuPrefabGenerator","u":"save-api-reference.html#menuprefabgenerator","x":"static class in RareGeometry.Shipready.Save.Editor.Generators · assembly RareGeometry.Shipready.Save.Editor public static class MenuPrefabGenerator Builds the player menu's prefabs and its default theme and text assets: SaveMenu , SlotCard and ConfirmDialog in UI/Prefabs , DefaultMenuTheme and DefaultMenuText in UI/Settings . Prefabs are only ever generated, never hand-written YAML. Safe to run again: the three prefabs are overwritten in place, so their GUIDs, and every reference to them, stay the same. The theme and text assets are created only when they are missing and are never overwritten."},{"p":"Shipready: API Reference","h":"MenuStyleGenerator","u":"save-api-reference.html#menustylegenerator","x":"static class in RareGeometry.Shipready.Save.Editor.Generators · assembly RareGeometry.Shipready.Save.Editor public static class MenuStyleGenerator Writes the six ready-made menu styles ( MenuStylePresets ) as theme assets in UI/Settings/Styles . Theme assets are only ever generated, never hand-written YAML. Safe to run again: each asset is updated in place, so its GUID stays the same, and set to exactly its style's values. Throws on any failure, which makes a batch-mode run exit with a non-zero code. Run it with -executeMethod RareGeometry.Shipready.Save.Editor.Generators.MenuStyleGenerator.Ge"},{"p":"Shipready: API Reference","h":"Namespace RareGeometry.Shipready.Save.Editor.Inspector","u":"save-api-reference.html#namespace-raregeometryshipreadysaveeditorinspector","x":""},{"p":"Shipready: API Reference","h":"SaveInspectorFile","u":"save-api-reference.html#saveinspectorfile","x":"class in RareGeometry.Shipready.Save.Editor.Inspector · assembly RareGeometry.Shipready.Save.Editor public sealed class SaveInspectorFile One save in the Save Inspector's list: the main file name, its status, and the other copies that belong to it (backups, a half-written temporary file, damaged copies kept for support). Properties Copies public IReadOnlyList<string> Copies { get; } The other files that belong to this save, newest backup first: .tmp , .bak1 to .bak5 , .corrupt-* . FileName public string FileName { get; } The main file name, for example slot_01.save . Status public SaveFileStat"},{"p":"Shipready: API Reference","h":"SaveInspectorModel","u":"save-api-reference.html#saveinspectormodel","x":"class in RareGeometry.Shipready.Save.Editor.Inspector · assembly RareGeometry.Shipready.Save.Editor public sealed class SaveInspectorModel Everything the Save Inspector does, without drawing anything: it lists the saves in a folder with their status, opens one (or one of its backups) as a searchable tree, highlights the live object a tree row belongs to, shows what a save would write right now in Play Mode, and imports, exports, restores and deletes saves. Reading never moves, quarantines or writes a file. Nothing here shows a dialog; the window asks first. Constructors SaveInspectorModel() pu"},{"p":"Shipready: API Reference","h":"SaveInspectorWindow","u":"save-api-reference.html#saveinspectorwindow","x":"class in RareGeometry.Shipready.Save.Editor.Inspector · assembly RareGeometry.Shipready.Save.Editor public sealed class SaveInspectorWindow : EditorWindow The Save Inspector: every save in the save folder with its status, any save (or backup) opened as a searchable tree, the live view of what a save would write in Play Mode, the recent save and load reports, and import, export, restore, delete and open-folder actions. A thin view over SaveInspectorModel . Constants MenuPath public const string MenuPath = \"Tools/Shipready/Save Inspector\"; The menu command that opens the Save Inspector. Methods "},{"p":"Shipready: API Reference","h":"SaveTreeNode","u":"save-api-reference.html#savetreenode","x":"class in RareGeometry.Shipready.Save.Editor.Inspector · assembly RareGeometry.Shipready.Save.Editor public sealed class SaveTreeNode One row of the Save Inspector tree: file, category, scene, object or value. The tree is kept flat, in the order the rows are shown, with each row knowing its depth and parent. Properties ChildCount public int ChildCount { get; } How many rows sit directly below this one. Depth public int Depth { get; } How deep the row sits: 0 for the file, 1 for a category, and so on. Expanded public bool Expanded { get; } Whether the row's children are shown. HasChildren public"},{"p":"Shipready: API Reference","h":"SaveTreeNodeKind","u":"save-api-reference.html#savetreenodekind","x":"enum in RareGeometry.Shipready.Save.Editor.Inspector · assembly RareGeometry.Shipready.Save.Editor public enum SaveTreeNodeKind What a row of the Save Inspector tree stands for. Values Name Value Description File 0 The file itself, at the top. Section 1 The file's header or its slot info. Category 2 A category, such as Progress or World. Scene 3 A scene inside a category. Object 4 A saved object, with its id. Group 5 A group of values: an object or a list in the data. Value 6 A single value."},{"p":"Shipready: API Reference","h":"SaveTreeView","u":"save-api-reference.html#savetreeview","x":"class in RareGeometry.Shipready.Save.Editor.Inspector · assembly RareGeometry.Shipready.Save.Editor public sealed class SaveTreeView An opened save as a flat, searchable tree: file, then header and slot info, then each category, scene, object and value. Nodes holds every row; Rows holds the rows to show, which follows the expanded state and the search. Scenes are shown with the path the Scene Registry (or the save itself) knows them by, and objects with the name they were saved with, while their ids stay one click away. Properties MatchCount public int MatchCount { get; } How many rows match t"},{"p":"Shipready: API Reference","h":"Namespace RareGeometry.Shipready.Save.Editor.MenuStyle","u":"save-api-reference.html#namespace-raregeometryshipreadysaveeditormenustyle","x":""},{"p":"Shipready: API Reference","h":"MenuPreview","u":"save-api-reference.html#menupreview","x":"class in RareGeometry.Shipready.Save.Editor.MenuStyle · assembly RareGeometry.Shipready.Save.Editor public sealed class MenuPreview : IDisposable Draws the save menu with made-up saves into a picture, for the Menu Style window. A copy of the menu is made in a preview scene of its own, which no camera of the game sees and which is never saved, so the open scene is never changed or marked as changed. The copy follows the theme and wording it is given, so edits show at once. Properties SampleSaves public IReadOnlyList<SlotInfo> SampleSaves { get; } The made-up saves the preview shows: an autosave"},{"p":"Shipready: API Reference","h":"MenuStyleModel","u":"save-api-reference.html#menustylemodel","x":"static class in RareGeometry.Shipready.Save.Editor.MenuStyle · assembly RareGeometry.Shipready.Save.Editor public static class MenuStyleModel What the Menu Style window does, without the window: finds the save menu in a scene or adds one, makes the game's own copy of the menu's theme and wording in the data folder, applies a ready-made style, saves the wording, and exports and imports a whole style as a .json file. Shipready's own assets are never changed: every edit goes to a copy in OwnFolder . Each action is one undo step. Properties LastError public static string LastError { get; } Why the"},{"p":"Shipready: API Reference","h":"MenuStylePreset","u":"save-api-reference.html#menustylepreset","x":"class in RareGeometry.Shipready.Save.Editor.MenuStyle · assembly RareGeometry.Shipready.Save.Editor public sealed class MenuStylePreset One ready-made look for the save menu: every colour, the sizes and the shape. The MenuStyleGenerator writes each one as a theme asset in UI/Settings/Styles , and the Menu Style window applies it to a copy in the data folder. Properties AssetPath public string AssetPath { get; } The project path of the style's theme asset. Description public string Description { get; } One line on what the style looks like. Id public string Id { get; } The short name the asset "},{"p":"Shipready: API Reference","h":"MenuStylePresets","u":"save-api-reference.html#menustylepresets","x":"static class in RareGeometry.Shipready.Save.Editor.MenuStyle · assembly RareGeometry.Shipready.Save.Editor public static class MenuStylePresets The six ready-made looks of the save menu: Dark (the default), Light, Fantasy, Sci-fi, Cozy and Minimal. Each uses TextMesh Pro's default font, so no font asset ships with Shipready, and each keeps readable text: Shipready's tests check the contrast of every pair of text and background colours. Properties All public static IReadOnlyList<MenuStylePreset> All { get; } Every style, Dark first. Methods Contrast(Color, Color) public static float Contrast(Co"},{"p":"Shipready: API Reference","h":"MenuStyleWindow","u":"save-api-reference.html#menustylewindow","x":"class in RareGeometry.Shipready.Save.Editor.MenuStyle · assembly RareGeometry.Shipready.Save.Editor public sealed class MenuStyleWindow : EditorWindow Tools > Shipready > Menu Style: makes the save menu in the open scene look and read the way the game wants, without the Project window, a prefab or code. Pick one of six ready-made styles, change any colour, the font, sizes and shape, the layout (list or grid, which details show, where the panel sits) and every word, and see it at once in the preview. A style can be exported to a .json file and imported in another project. With no save menu in t"},{"p":"Shipready: API Reference","h":"MenuTextField","u":"save-api-reference.html#menutextfield","x":"class in RareGeometry.Shipready.Save.Editor.MenuStyle · assembly RareGeometry.Shipready.Save.Editor public sealed class MenuTextField One line of the menu's wording, as the Words tab shows it. Properties Field public string Field { get; } The serialized field of MenuText , such as saveTitle . Group public string Group { get; } The group the line is shown in, such as Buttons . HasPlaceholder public bool HasPlaceholder { get; } Whether the menu fills {0} in for this line. Label public string Label { get; } The label the window shows. Placeholder public string Placeholder { get; } What {0} become"},{"p":"Shipready: API Reference","h":"MenuTextFields","u":"save-api-reference.html#menutextfields","x":"static class in RareGeometry.Shipready.Save.Editor.MenuStyle · assembly RareGeometry.Shipready.Save.Editor public static class MenuTextFields Every line of the menu's wording ( MenuText ) in the groups the Words tab shows, with what {0} means where the menu fills it in, and a check that says when a line would show wrongly. Shipready's tests make sure no line is missing. Properties All public static IReadOnlyList<MenuTextField> All { get; } Every line, in the order the Words tab shows them. Methods Check(MenuTextField, string, bool) public static string Check(MenuTextField line, string text, ou"},{"p":"Shipready: API Reference","h":"MessageNames","u":"save-api-reference.html#messagenames","x":"static class in RareGeometry.Shipready.Save.Editor.MenuStyle · assembly RareGeometry.Shipready.Save.Editor public static class MessageNames The plain names the editor's pickers show for the message choices. Methods Displays() public static string[] Displays(); The names of DisplayAs , in order. DisplaysWithTheme() public static string[] DisplaysWithTheme(); The names of DisplayAs , in order, after a first choice that keeps the theme's. Kind(MessageKind) public static string Kind(MessageKind kind); A kind of message's name, such as \"Save failed\". Parameter Description kind The kind. Returns: It"},{"p":"Shipready: API Reference","h":"Namespace RareGeometry.Shipready.Save.Editor.Wizard","u":"save-api-reference.html#namespace-raregeometryshipreadysaveeditorwizard","x":""},{"p":"Shipready: API Reference","h":"GamePreset","u":"save-api-reference.html#gamepreset","x":"enum in RareGeometry.Shipready.Save.Editor.Wizard · assembly RareGeometry.Shipready.Save.Editor public enum GamePreset A kind of game the settings can be set up for in one click. Values Name Value Description Custom 0 Leave the settings as they are. StoryRpg 1 A story game or an RPG: many slots, a quicksave, autosave on scene changes and on quit, pictures on. Roguelike 2 A roguelike: one run kept in one slot, autosaved on quit; the player does not pick slots. ArcadeMobile 3 An arcade or mobile game: autosave only, into one slot, with small packed files."},{"p":"Shipready: API Reference","h":"GamePresetChange","u":"save-api-reference.html#gamepresetchange","x":"class in RareGeometry.Shipready.Save.Editor.Wizard · assembly RareGeometry.Shipready.Save.Editor public sealed class GamePresetChange One setting a game preset changes, with its value before and after, in plain words. Properties From public string From { get; } Its value now. Setting public string Setting { get; } The setting, as the settings Inspector names it. To public string To { get; } Its value after the preset. Warning public string Warning { get; } What the player could notice, such as saves that stop showing in the menu, or null."},{"p":"Shipready: API Reference","h":"GamePresets","u":"save-api-reference.html#gamepresets","x":"static class in RareGeometry.Shipready.Save.Editor.Wizard · assembly RareGeometry.Shipready.Save.Editor public static class GamePresets Settings for common kinds of game, applied in one click from the Setup Wizard's first page or the settings' Basics tab. Plan(SetupWizardModel, GamePreset) lists exactly what a preset would change before anything changes; ApplyTo(SetupWizardModel, GamePreset) writes it into a SetupWizardModel , whose Apply is one undo step. Presets only touch slots, autosave, pictures and how compact files are. They never change the encryption passphrase or key, the save folder"},{"p":"Shipready: API Reference","h":"SaveableSuggestion","u":"save-api-reference.html#saveablesuggestion","x":"class in RareGeometry.Shipready.Save.Editor.Wizard · assembly RareGeometry.Shipready.Save.Editor public sealed class SaveableSuggestion One object the Setup Wizard's scene scan thinks should remember its state, with the reason in plain English. Nothing happens to it unless it is ticked ( Selected ) and passed to SetupWizardModel.AddSaveables(IList<SaveableSuggestion>) . Properties Reason public string Reason { get; } Why the object looks like it changes during play, for example \"Has a Rigidbody\". Selected public bool Selected { get; set; } Whether to add a Saveable. Every suggestion starts unt"},{"p":"Shipready: API Reference","h":"SetupWizardModel","u":"save-api-reference.html#setupwizardmodel","x":"class in RareGeometry.Shipready.Save.Editor.Wizard · assembly RareGeometry.Shipready.Save.Editor public sealed class SetupWizardModel Everything the Setup Wizard does, without drawing anything: the choices of each step, and one Apply per step that writes them into the project. The Setup Wizard window is a thin view over this class, and tests drive it directly. The first Apply creates the settings asset and its three registries in DataFolder , which lies outside Shipready's own folder so that a Shipready update never overwrites them, and fills the registries. Every Apply is one undo group, so E"},{"p":"Shipready: API Reference","h":"SetupWizardWindow","u":"save-api-reference.html#setupwizardwindow","x":"class in RareGeometry.Shipready.Save.Editor.Wizard · assembly RareGeometry.Shipready.Save.Editor public sealed class SetupWizardWindow : EditorWindow The Setup Wizard: two short steps, where saves go and the menu with its test hotkeys, then a list of everything it created or changed. Categories, file options, game updates and the scene scan live in the Advanced tab of the settings asset's Inspector. A thin view over SetupWizardModel ; each \"Apply and continue\" is one undo step. Constants MenuPath public const string MenuPath = \"Tools/Shipready/Setup Wizard\"; The menu command that opens the wiz"},{"p":"Shipready: API Reference","h":"TmpEssentials","u":"save-api-reference.html#tmpessentials","x":"static class in RareGeometry.Shipready.Save.Editor.Wizard · assembly RareGeometry.Shipready.Save.Editor public static class TmpEssentials TextMesh Pro's essential resources (its default font and settings), which the save menu's text needs. They live in the game's project, not in Shipready, so the Setup Wizard checks for them and hands over to TextMesh Pro's own importer. Properties AreImported public static bool AreImported { get; } Whether the TMP Settings asset is in the project, which means the essentials were imported. EssentialsPackagePath public static string EssentialsPackagePath { get;"},{"p":"Shipready: API Reference","h":"Namespace RareGeometry.Shipready.Save.Editor.Setup","u":"save-api-reference.html#namespace-raregeometryshipreadysaveeditorsetup","x":""},{"p":"Shipready: API Reference","h":"RequiredPackages","u":"save-api-reference.html#requiredpackages","x":"static class in RareGeometry.Shipready.Save.Editor.Setup · assembly RareGeometry.Shipready.Save.Editor.Setup public static class RequiredPackages Tells the developer which Unity packages Shipready's save menu, editor tool windows and demos need, and how to install them: uGUI ( com.unity.ugui ) and TextMesh Pro, which is part of uGUI from Unity 2023.2 and the separate com.unity.textmeshpro package in Unity 2022.3. Shipready never installs, updates or removes a package itself; the developer does that in the Package Manager. This class sits in its own small editor assembly, RareGeometry.Shipready"},{"p":"Limits and FAQ","h":"Limits and FAQ","u":"save-limits-and-faq.html#limits-and-faq","x":"This page lists what Shipready does not do, where its limits are, and which platforms and Unity versions it supports. It ends with answers to the questions buyers ask most. Settings in bold, such as Save Version , are fields on the settings asset ShipreadySaveSettings . Select it in the Project window to change them."},{"p":"Limits and FAQ","h":"What Shipready does not promise","u":"save-limits-and-faq.html#what-shipready-does-not-promise","x":"It does not save any object automatically. It saves plain data and the supported Unity state listed on this page. Anything else needs a short script, and the Inspector says so clearly: the Field Picker greys out fields it cannot save and shows the reason. It is not anti-cheat. Encryption stops casual save editing, not a determined player. There is no cloud sync in 1.0. You get a step-by-step Steam Cloud guide instead ( SteamCloud.md ). Platforms are limited at launch. There is no support for macOS, iOS, Android, WebGL or consoles in 1.0. There is no mid-session co-op loading. The host loads a "},{"p":"Limits and FAQ","h":"What is saved automatically and what needs code","u":"save-limits-and-faq.html#what-is-saved-automatically-and-what-needs-code","x":"What you want saved How Effort Settings and progress values Save Data in code, or the Field Picker on a manager object One line each, or ticks Position, rotation, scale, parent Saveable → Position saver Tick Active and destroyed scene objects Saveable → Active and Destroyed savers Tick Rigidbody velocity, kinematic, sleep Saveable → Physics saver Tick Fields of your scripts (supported types) Field Picker Tick Inventory of ScriptableObject items Field Picker on lists of registered assets, or ISaveable Ticks; Diagnostics registers missing assets in one click Prefabs spawned at runtime Saveable o"},{"p":"Limits and FAQ","h":"Which values can be saved","u":"save-limits-and-faq.html#which-values-can-be-saved","x":"Built in: numbers, text, true/false, dates, durations, GUIDs and byte arrays enums, stored by name, so reordering an enum never breaks a save Vector2 , Vector3 , Vector4 , Vector2Int , Vector3Int , Quaternion , Color , Color32 , Rect , RectInt , Bounds and BoundsInt lists and arrays of all of the above Dictionary<string, T> and Dictionary<int, T> of any of the above, through SaveKit.Data.Set and Get , or SaveWriter.WriteDictionary , SaveReader.ReadDictionary and SaveReader.ReadIntDictionary . Any other dictionary is not saved, with an SK002 warning in the Console. Your own [Serializable] class"},{"p":"Limits and FAQ","h":"The limits of encryption","u":"save-limits-and-faq.html#the-limits-of-encryption","x":"Encryption is off by default. Switch it on with File Options → Encrypt . The key ships inside your game. The passphrase is stored in the settings asset, which is part of your build. A determined player can dig it out of the game files. Mono builds are especially easy to decompile, and IL2CPP only makes this harder. It stops casual editing. A player cannot change an encrypted save in a text editor. If one byte changes, the tamper check notices, and Shipready loads the newest backup instead (see SK105 ). It is not anti-cheat. In co-op, the host's save is the authority anyway. Never use it for pa"},{"p":"Limits and FAQ","h":"Platforms","u":"save-limits-and-faq.html#platforms","x":"Platform 1.0 status Windows 10 and 11, 64-bit Supported and tested: Editor tests, plus Mono and IL2CPP player builds Android Not supported in 1.0; planned for 1.1 Linux Not supported in 1.0; planned for 1.1 Steam Deck Not separately tested. Windows builds normally run there through Proton. macOS and iOS Not supported WebGL Not supported. Diagnostics warns when WebGL is the build target. Consoles Not supported. You can plug in your own storage: implement ISaveStorage and pass it to SaveKit.Initialise(settings, storage) ."},{"p":"Limits and FAQ","h":"Unity versions","u":"save-limits-and-faq.html#unity-versions","x":"Shipready is supported from Unity 2022.3 LTS, and tested on Unity 6.3 LTS and the newest Unity 6 release. Unity version Status 2022.3 LTS Supported: the minimum version. Not tested separately. 6.3 LTS Supported and tested The newest Unity 6 release Supported and tested. The co-op sample was tested here. 6.0 LTS and other 6.x versions Supported, but not tested separately The co-op sample needs Unity 6 and Netcode for GameObjects 2.x. Its scripts compile only when NGO 2.x is installed, so they stay out of the way in Unity 2022.3 and in projects without NGO. A menu command builds the sample scene"},{"p":"Limits and FAQ","h":"Known limits","u":"save-limits-and-faq.html#known-limits","x":""},{"p":"Limits and FAQ","h":"Data and files","u":"save-limits-and-faq.html#data-and-files","x":"Dates are stored as UTC. A DateTime whose Kind is Local or Unspecified is converted to UTC when it is saved. It comes back as the same moment in UTC, with Kind set to Utc , not as the same clock reading. Call .ToLocalTime() before you show it to a player. Unspecified is treated as local time. Data can be nested at most 128 levels deep. A save nested deeper is refused before anything is written (see SK002 ). Automatic Fallback off, and a crash on a slot's very first save. With File Options → Automatic Fallback off, a crash during the very first save of a slot, before the new file was swapped in"},{"p":"Limits and FAQ","h":"World persistence","u":"save-limits-and-faq.html#world-persistence","x":"Links to objects in other scenes. A link to an object in another scene comes back only when that object is awake, or when its scene is part of the same load. Otherwise the link stays empty, with a warning. Parent paths. A runtime object under a parent that is not saved remembers that parent by its path in the hierarchy. The path assumes that sibling names are unique and contain no / . If the parent cannot be found, the object returns to the scene root in the same world position (see SK130 ). Physics release timing. Restored rigidbodies are held kinematic through one physics step, then released"},{"p":"Limits and FAQ","h":"Player menu","u":"save-limits-and-faq.html#player-menu","x":"Thumbnails are not rotated with backups. After a backup is restored or loaded, the slot card still shows the picture of the newer, damaged save. The next save to that slot writes a new picture, or removes the old one. A save without a picture has no thumbnail. A slot save, autosave or quicksave made without a picture removes the thumbnail of the save it replaced, so the card shows \"No preview\" rather than an older picture. The menu takes a picture before every save it makes, while its Take a picture for each save is on and there is a camera, and every slot save, autosave and quicksave takes on"},{"p":"Limits and FAQ","h":"Co-op","u":"save-limits-and-faq.html#co-op","x":"Only the host saves and loads world data. A client that tries gets HostOnly (see SK140 ). Clients keep their own settings file. Shipready restores state on the host only. Getting that state to clients is the network library's job, so values clients must see have to be synced by your game, for example with NetworkVariables. Only Netcode for GameObjects is shown. The core works with any networking library through the co-op hooks, but the sample uses NGO 2.x only, and was tested with NGO 2.13.3. A dropped connection lingers in the sample. A player whose connection dropped stays in the game until "},{"p":"Limits and FAQ","h":"FAQ","u":"save-limits-and-faq.html#faq","x":""},{"p":"Limits and FAQ","h":"Where are my saves?","u":"save-limits-and-faq.html#where-are-my-saves","x":"On Windows, saves are kept here: %USERPROFILE%\\AppData\\LocalLow\\<Company Name>\\<Product Name>\\Saves Company Name and Product Name come from Project Settings → Player . Saves is Save Folder Name . If Profile Folder is set, the saves are in that folder inside it. {profile} in Profile Folder is replaced by SaveKit.ProfileId , such as the player's Steam ID, so each player's saves are in Saves/<their ID> . In the Editor and in a Windows build, the folder is the same. Slot 1 is slot_01.save . The global file, which holds the Settings category, is global.save , or global.local if you gave it its own "},{"p":"Limits and FAQ","h":"Do I need code to start Shipready?","u":"save-limits-and-faq.html#do-i-need-code-to-start-shipready","x":"No. With Initialise Automatically on, which is the default, Shipready starts by itself just before the first scene loads, in the Editor and in builds, using the settings asset the Setup Wizard made. Switch it off and call SaveKit.Initialise() yourself only when Shipready has to wait for something, such as the player's Steam ID for a profile folder. See \"Starting Shipready in your game\" in the Manual ."},{"p":"Limits and FAQ","h":"Can players edit saves?","u":"save-limits-and-faq.html#can-players-edit-saves","x":"By default, saves are readable JSON. They are indented in the Editor and in development builds, and compact in release builds. An edited save counts as damaged. If a player changes a value in a text editor, the file no longer matches its checksum. In a build, Shipready then loads the newest backup and keeps the edited file as .corrupt-<time> (see SK104 ). Changes to spacing alone do not count. The Editor always accepts edits. It loads edited files with a warning, so you can tweak saves while you test. To let players edit saves , for example for modding, set File Options → Checksum Policy to Ac"},{"p":"Limits and FAQ","h":"What happens if the game crashes mid-save?","u":"save-limits-and-faq.html#what-happens-if-the-game-crashes-mid-save","x":"Nothing is lost. Each save: writes a temporary file, reads it back to check it, and only then swaps it into place, using Windows' atomic replace. At every moment, at least one complete copy is on disk. The next load tries the main file first, then a complete temporary file, then each backup, newest first. Shipready's own tests cut saves short after every step, hundreds of times, and the next load always found a complete copy. The autosave on quit waits until its file is safely written."},{"p":"Limits and FAQ","h":"Does loading a slot overwrite my settings?","u":"save-limits-and-faq.html#does-loading-a-slot-overwrite-my-settings","x":"No. Categories marked Global, which by default means Settings, live in the global file, not in the slots. Loading a slot loads only Slot categories. Shipready loads the global file by itself when it starts. A slot save also writes the global file when the game holds global values, but a load never takes them back. To save settings on their own, call SaveKit.SaveGlobal() ."},{"p":"Limits and FAQ","h":"How do I start a new game?","u":"save-limits-and-faq.html#how-do-i-start-a-new-game","x":"Call SaveKit.NewGame() , then load your first scene: using RareGeometry.Shipready.Save; using UnityEngine; public class MainMenu : MonoBehaviour { public void StartNewGame() { // Every Slot category is emptied, and scene memory, players and playtime start again. // Global categories, such as the player's settings, are kept. Nothing on disk changes. SaveKit.NewGame(); SaveKit.LoadScene(\"Level1\"); } } Without NewGame , values from the last loaded game stay in memory. A later save into an old slot would also keep that slot's categories that the new game has not written yet. With NewGame , the nex"},{"p":"Limits and FAQ","h":"Can I save at any time in co-op?","u":"save-limits-and-faq.html#can-i-save-at-any-time-in-co-op","x":"The host can. The host, or a solo player, can save at any time. The one exception is while SaveKit.LoadAsync is still loading scenes (see SK154 ). Clients cannot. They get HostOnly , and Shipready's save menu hides the Save, Load, Delete and Restore backup buttons for them. Check SaveKit.CanSave before you show save buttons of your own. Loading happens before friends join. 1.0 has no mid-session co-op loading. Clients keep their own settings. They can still save their own settings file with SaveKit.SaveGlobal() ."},{"p":"Limits and FAQ","h":"Will old saves still load after I update my game?","u":"save-limits-and-faq.html#will-old-saves-still-load-after-i-update-my-game","x":"Yes, if you plan for it. Raise Save Version when your saved data changes, and write a small migration step. Most changes, such as adding values or objects, need no step at all. See GameUpdates.md ."},{"p":"Limits and FAQ","h":"Can I change compression or encryption after release?","u":"save-limits-and-faq.html#can-i-change-compression-or-encryption-after-release","x":"Yes. Each file records how it was written, so old files keep loading. Do not change Passphrase , Key Salt or Key Iterations after release. See GameUpdates.md ."},{"p":"Limits and FAQ","h":"Does it work with Steam Cloud?","u":"save-limits-and-faq.html#does-it-work-with-steam-cloud","x":"Yes, through Steam's code-free Auto-Cloud. Shipready names its files so that one pattern, *.save , uploads only the real saves. A Profile Folder of {profile} , with SaveKit.ProfileId set to the player's Steam ID, keeps two Steam users on one PC apart, and giving the global file its own extension, such as .local , keeps machine-specific settings on the PC. See SteamCloud.md ."},{"p":"Limits and FAQ","h":"Something went wrong. Where do I look?","u":"save-limits-and-faq.html#something-went-wrong-where-do-i-look","x":"Every result carries a code such as SK101 , a plain-English message and a link to its entry in Troubleshooting.md . Always check result.Success after your own save and load calls."},{"p":"Limits and FAQ","h":"What does support cover?","u":"save-limits-and-faq.html#what-does-support-cover","x":"Support covers bugs in Shipready and questions about setting it up. It does not cover reviewing or debugging your own game code. A stack trace usually shows which side a problem is on: one that ends in your script points at your code, and one inside RareGeometry.Shipready.Save points at Shipready. The end of Troubleshooting.md lists what to send so that you get a quick answer."},{"p":"Limits and FAQ","h":"Contact","u":"save-limits-and-faq.html#contact","x":"Report a bug with the bug report form , ask a question in Discussions , email the address in Tools > Shipready > Saves > Help or visit https://raregeometry.github.io/ ."},{"p":"Troubleshooting","h":"Troubleshooting","u":"save-troubleshooting.html#troubleshooting","x":"Shipready never throws an exception into your game. Every save, load and delete returns a result instead. When there is something to report, the result carries a code such as SK101 , a plain-English message and a link to this guide. This guide has one entry for each code. Each entry explains what the code means, what usually causes it and how to fix it."},{"p":"Troubleshooting","h":"If Shipready's menus are missing after import","u":"save-troubleshooting.html#if-shipreadys-menus-are-missing-after-import","x":"What it means: The save menu, the editor tools' windows (the Start window, the Setup Wizard, the Save Inspector and Diagnostics) and the demos are built on two Unity packages, Unity UI ( com.unity.ugui ) and TextMesh Pro , and one of them is missing from the project. TextMesh Pro is part of Unity UI from Unity 2023.2 and the separate com.unity.textmeshpro package in Unity 2022.3. Shipready still imports without compile errors: the runtime ( SaveKit ) and the core editor services (saved ids for Saveables, the registries and their Registries commands, and the migration link file for builds) comp"},{"p":"Troubleshooting","h":"How codes appear","u":"save-troubleshooting.html#how-codes-appear","x":"Every SaveKit call returns a result. Saves and deletes return a SaveResult and loads return a LoadResult . Both have these parts: Success : whether you got what you asked for. Only two outcomes count as success: a result with no code (SK000, which has no entry here) and SK102, a load that was rescued from a backup. Code : a SaveErrorCode , written as SK plus three digits. Code numbers never change between versions, so a code quoted in an old support email still means the same thing. Message : one plain sentence. Details such as the file name follow in brackets. DocumentationLink : the path to "},{"p":"Troubleshooting","h":"Reading the Console report","u":"save-troubleshooting.html#reading-the-console-report","x":"Every line Shipready writes to the Console starts with [Shipready] , followed by the code when there is one. A warning from a save or load looks like this: [Shipready] SK130 Chest_03 (8c1f...): it is no longer in the scene, so its saved state was skipped. Every save and load also prints a one-line report, while Report To Console is ticked (it is off by default) and Log Level is Warnings or Verbose: [Shipready] Save slot_01.save: 12.4 ms, 5 categories, 214 values, 40 objects (3 spawned), 5321 bytes, 0 warnings [Shipready] Load slot_01.save: 9.8 ms, 5 categories, 214 values, 40 objects restored,"},{"p":"Troubleshooting","h":"Quick index","u":"save-troubleshooting.html#quick-index","x":"Code Name Meaning SK001 Unknown Something failed that Shipready could not classify SK002 InvalidArgument A value handed to Shipready was missing, empty or out of range SK003 Busy Another save or load of the file did not finish in time SK004 Cancelled The call was stopped before it finished SK005 NotInitialised Shipready has no settings yet, or was stopped mid-call SK100 FileNotFound There is no file under that name SK101 FileDamaged The save and every copy of it are unreadable SK102 RecoveredFromBackup Success: a backup was loaded instead of a damaged main file SK103 UnsupportedFormat The file"},{"p":"Troubleshooting","h":"SK001: Unknown","u":"save-troubleshooting.html#sk001-unknown","x":"What it means: Something failed that Shipready could not name more precisely. The detail in brackets says what Shipready was doing. As a warning inside a report, SK001 means the rest of the save or load still went ahead. Likely cause: Usually an exception from game code Shipready calls, such as ISaveable.CaptureState , RestoreState or your ISpawnHandler . Also: a disk error Windows did not explain, a thumbnail that could not be encoded, or a saved scene that took longer than SaveKit.World.SceneLoadTimeoutSeconds (120 by default) to load. \"Stopped unexpectedly\" points to a kit bug. Fix: Read th"},{"p":"Troubleshooting","h":"SK002: InvalidArgument","u":"save-troubleshooting.html#sk002-invalidargument","x":"What it means: Shipready was given a value it cannot use, so it refused the call. Nothing was written. Likely cause: One of these: SaveKit.Quicksave() was called while Quicksave Enabled is off. Encryption is on, but File Options → Passphrase is empty. The save data is nested more than 128 levels deep. SaveKit.LoadScene was given an empty scene name, or SaveKit.UnloadScene was asked to unload the only loaded scene. SaveKit.RestoreBackup was called on a slot whose main file reads correctly. The file was left as it was. SaveKit.Load , LoadAsync , Delete , RestoreBackup or RestoreBackupAsync was g"},{"p":"Troubleshooting","h":"SK003: Busy","u":"save-troubleshooting.html#sk003-busy","x":"What it means: A save or load could not start because another one on the same file did not finish in time. Nothing was written. Likely cause: Shipready itself never returns SK003 in 1.0. Saves and loads of the same file wait in a queue and run in order, however long that takes. Only a custom ISaveStorage of your own could return this code. Fix: If your storage returns SK003, await the earlier call, then try again. If the game freezes while saving instead, look for code on the main thread that calls .Wait() or reads .Result on a SaveKit task. The task needs the main thread to finish, so the gam"},{"p":"Troubleshooting","h":"SK004: Cancelled","u":"save-troubleshooting.html#sk004-cancelled","x":"What it means: A save or load was stopped before it finished. Likely cause: The 1.0 API has no way to cancel a call, so Shipready itself never returns SK004. Only a custom ISaveStorage of your own could return it. If SaveKit.Shutdown() or a second SaveKit.Initialise() interrupts a save or load, the result is SK005, not SK004. Fix: If your storage returns SK004, run the call again once the storage is ready. If you see SK004 without custom storage, please report it as a bug in Shipready. A stopped save never replaces the file already on disk, because the new file only goes into place after it ha"},{"p":"Troubleshooting","h":"SK005: NotInitialised","u":"save-troubleshooting.html#sk005-notinitialised","x":"What it means: Shipready was used before it had settings and a save folder, or it was stopped or restarted mid-call. Nothing was read or written. Likely cause: One of these: The settings have not been created yet (the Start window's Create , or the Setup Wizard), or ShipreadySaveSettings is not inside a Resources folder. Initialise Automatically is switched off in the settings asset, and SaveKit.Initialise() was never called, or it failed and nobody checked the result. Shipready tried to start by itself and could not. The Console then shows an error that starts \"Shipready could not start by it"},{"p":"Troubleshooting","h":"SK100: FileNotFound","u":"save-troubleshooting.html#sk100-filenotfound","x":"What it means: There is no main file, temporary file or backup with that name. This is normal on a first run. Nothing was changed. Likely cause: SaveKit.LoadGlobal() , LoadNewestAutosave() or LoadQuicksave() ran before that file was ever saved. If saves that used to load have disappeared, Shipready is looking somewhere else. That happens when Save Folder Name , Profile Folder , a file name pattern or File Options → File Extension changed. It also happens when Company Name or Product Name changed in Player Settings, because that moves Application.persistentDataPath . Fix: On a first run, treat "},{"p":"Troubleshooting","h":"SK101: FileDamaged","u":"save-troubleshooting.html#sk101-filedamaged","x":"What it means: The save could not be read, and neither could the temporary file or any backup. Nothing was deleted, and the game keeps running. Likely cause: The copies are damaged in different ways: empty, cut short or not valid JSON. If they all fail the same way, you get that code instead. This usually follows a power cut or a faulty drive. SK101 also appears when: A save's new file did not read back correctly, so it was not swapped in. A save of only some categories found the old file unreadable, so it wrote nothing. SaveKit.RestoreBackup found no valid backup. Automatic Fallback is off an"},{"p":"Troubleshooting","h":"SK102: RecoveredFromBackup","u":"save-troubleshooting.html#sk102-recoveredfrombackup","x":"What it means: Success. The main file could not be used, so Shipready loaded the newest good copy. Success and RecoveredFromBackup are both true. LoadedFrom names the copy ( Temporary , Backup1 , Backup2 or OlderBackup ), and SavedUtc says when that copy was saved. Temporary is a save that was being written when the game stopped, so it is the newest. SaveKit.RestoreBackup also returns SK102 when it has put a backup back. Likely cause: The main file failed one of Shipready's checks, for example SK104 after a hand edit, or after a crash cut the file short. The report's warnings give the reason. "},{"p":"Troubleshooting","h":"SK103: UnsupportedFormat","u":"save-troubleshooting.html#sk103-unsupportedformat","x":"What it means: Shipready version cannot read the file. Either Shipready did not write it, or it uses a newer file format. Shipready treats it like damage and tries the backups. Likely cause: One of these: Another tool writes files with the same name and extension into the same folder. A file was renamed to .save by hand. A later Shipready version wrote the save in a newer format, and an older build is reading it. Fix: Give Shipready its own folder with Save Folder Name , and set it before you ship, because changing it later hides existing saves. Use the same or a newer Shipready version in eve"},{"p":"Troubleshooting","h":"SK104: ChecksumMismatch","u":"save-troubleshooting.html#sk104-checksummismatch","x":"What it means: The data no longer matches the checksum in the file's header, so the file was edited or cut short after it was written. Changes to spacing only do not count. A readable file with no checksum at all is reported the same way. Likely cause: Someone edited a readable save in a text editor, or the file was cut short. In a build, the default File Options → Checksum Policy (Treat Edits As Damaged) falls back to a backup, which gives SK102. You get SK104 when no copy can be used, or when Automatic Fallback is off. In the Editor, edited files always load, with SK104 as a warning, so you "},{"p":"Troubleshooting","h":"SK105: TamperDetected","u":"save-troubleshooting.html#sk105-tamperdetected","x":"What it means: An encrypted save was written with the current passphrase, but it failed its tamper check, so its contents changed after it was written. Shipready treats it as damaged and tries the backups. Likely cause: A player changed the encrypted file with a hex editor or a save-editing tool, or a faulty drive or another program changed bytes on disk. Even one changed byte causes this. Fix: Usually nothing is needed. The newest good backup loads (SK102), and the changed file is kept as .corrupt-<time> . If every copy fails, or Automatic Fallback is off, nothing is touched. If many players "},{"p":"Troubleshooting","h":"SK106: EncryptionKeyWrong","u":"save-troubleshooting.html#sk106-encryptionkeywrong","x":"What it means: An encrypted save was written with a different key from the one in the settings, so it cannot be opened. When every copy fails this way, nothing is moved or deleted. Likely cause: One of these: File Options → Passphrase , Key Salt or Key Iterations changed after the save was written. The settings asset was recreated. The Setup Wizard gives each new asset its own random salt. Passphrase is empty. The file came from another game or project. Fix: Put back the exact passphrase, salt and iteration count the save was written with, and the file loads again. Choose these values once, be"},{"p":"Troubleshooting","h":"SK107: CompressionFailed","u":"save-troubleshooting.html#sk107-compressionfailed","x":"What it means: The compressed (GZip) part of a save could not be unpacked. Shipready treats it as damaged and tries the backups. Likely cause: The packed bytes of a compressed save were damaged on disk, for example by a faulty drive or by another program that changed the file. Encrypted files fail their tamper check (SK105) before they reach this point, so SK107 mostly appears for files that are compressed but not encrypted. Fix: Usually nothing is needed. The newest good backup loads (SK102), and the damaged file is kept as .corrupt-<time> . If no copy can be read, or Automatic Fallback is of"},{"p":"Troubleshooting","h":"SK110: DiskFull","u":"save-troubleshooting.html#sk110-diskfull","x":"What it means: The drive ran out of free space, so the save stopped. Existing saves were not changed, because Shipready writes a temporary file first and only swaps it in once it is complete and checked. Likely cause: The player's system drive, which holds AppData\\LocalLow , is full or nearly full. Large uncompressed saves with many backups make this more likely. Fix: Tell the player plainly, and let them try again. For example: \"There isn't enough disk space to save. Free up some space and try again. Your previous save is safe.\" To make saves smaller, turn on File Options → Compress (JSON usu"},{"p":"Troubleshooting","h":"SK111: AccessDenied","u":"save-troubleshooting.html#sk111-accessdenied","x":"What it means: Windows did not let Shipready write, or sometimes read, the save file or folder. Existing saves were not changed. Likely cause: One of these: The file or folder is marked read-only. Another program has the file open, such as a text editor, a virus scan, or a backup or cloud-sync tool. A second copy of the game is running. Security software blocks the folder. If the main file is locked during a load, Shipready deliberately does not fall back to an older backup, because the newest save is probably fine. Fix: Close the other program or the second copy of the game, and try again. In"},{"p":"Troubleshooting","h":"SK112: StorageUnavailable","u":"save-troubleshooting.html#sk112-storageunavailable","x":"What it means: Shipready could not reach the save folder at all, so nothing was read or written. Existing saves are not changed. Likely cause: One of these: SaveKit.Initialise() was called from a background thread. Unity then reports no persistent data path. The folder is on a drive that was disconnected. A cloud-sync tool still holds the file. Windows reported a disk error Shipready could not classify. During a load, Shipready does not fall back to an older backup for this code, because the newest save may be perfectly good. Fix: Call SaveKit.Initialise() on the main thread, for example in Aw"},{"p":"Troubleshooting","h":"SK113: PathTooLong","u":"save-troubleshooting.html#sk113-pathtoolong","x":"What it means: The full path of a save file is longer than Windows allows (usually 260 characters), so the file could not be written or read. Existing saves were not changed. Likely cause: The parts of the path add up to too many characters. The path is C:\\Users\\<user name>\\AppData\\LocalLow\\<Company Name>\\<Product Name> , followed by Save Folder Name , Profile Folder , the file name patterns and File Options → File Extension . Long company, product or Windows user names make it worse. Fix: Shorten Save Folder Name , Profile Folder and the file name patterns in the settings asset, and keep Comp"},{"p":"Troubleshooting","h":"SK120: NewerVersion","u":"save-troubleshooting.html#sk120-newerversion","x":"What it means: A newer build of the game wrote this save: its save version is higher than Save Version in this build. Shipready refuses to load it rather than risk damaging it, and leaves the file untouched. Likely cause: The player went back to an older build, for example through a beta branch, a rollback or an old installer. Saves may also have been copied from a machine with a newer build, or Save Version was lowered. Fix: Never lower Save Version after release. Tell the player: \"This save was made with a newer version of the game. Update the game to load it.\" Use SlotInfo.IsFromNewerVersio"},{"p":"Troubleshooting","h":"SK121: MigrationMissing","u":"save-troubleshooting.html#sk121-migrationmissing","x":"What it means: The save has an older save version, and a step needed to upgrade it does not exist. The load was refused and the file was left untouched. The message names the missing version. Likely cause: Save Version was raised without a step for every version in between. A step marked [SaveMigration(fromVersion: 3)] turns a version 3 save into version 4. A step can also be missing because: It has the wrong shape. It must be static , take one SaveTree and return void . The Console warns when a step is ignored. It sits in an Editor folder, so builds do not include it. It sits in an assembly S"},{"p":"Troubleshooting","h":"SK122: MigrationFailed","u":"save-troubleshooting.html#sk122-migrationfailed","x":"What it means: A migration step ran but threw an exception. Steps work on a copy of the save, so the file was left untouched and nothing was half-changed. Likely cause: The step's own code failed. It may have expected a key or category that the old save does not have, converted a value to the wrong type, or used a Unity object or API. Steps run on a background thread together with the rest of the file work, so they may only edit the SaveTree . Fix: The message names the step ( Type.Method ) and the exception. Make the step tolerant of missing data: check that save.FindPath(...) is not null bef"},{"p":"Troubleshooting","h":"SK123: SaveVersionInvalid","u":"save-troubleshooting.html#sk123-saveversioninvalid","x":"What it means: Save Version in the settings asset is not a whole number of 1 or more, so Shipready refused to start. Nothing was read or written. Likely cause: The field was set to 0 or a negative number, usually while experimenting with migrations or after resetting values by hand. Fix: In the settings asset, under Game updates, set Save Version to 1 for a game that has never shipped. For a released game, set it back to the number your last release used. Then call SaveKit.Initialise() again. From then on, only ever raise it, by one each time your saved data changes shape, and add a [SaveMigra"},{"p":"Troubleshooting","h":"SK130: ObjectNotFound","u":"save-troubleshooting.html#sk130-objectnotfound","x":"What it means: A saved object, link or parent points at something that is no longer in the scene, so that piece was skipped. The rest of the load succeeded. Skipped objects are listed in Report.SkippedObjects . Likely cause: The object was deleted in a game update, or its Saveable was removed and added again, which gives it a new ID. A linked object or saved parent may be gone: the link becomes empty and the object stays where it is. An unsaved parent container may have been renamed: the object returns to the scene root, in the same world position. During a save, a field that links to a scene "},{"p":"Troubleshooting","h":"SK131: PrefabNotRegistered","u":"save-troubleshooting.html#sk131-prefabnotregistered","x":"What it means: A runtime object spawned from a prefab could not be re-created on load, so the object and its state were skipped. Everything else loaded. Likely cause: The prefab is not in the Prefab Registry: it was deleted, it lost the Saveable on its root, or the settings asset's Prefab Registry field is empty. SK131 also appears when your ISpawnHandler.Spawn returns null or spawns a prefab without a Saveable on its root. During a save, it means an object was copied at runtime from something that is not a prefab, so that object is not saved. Fix: Run Tools → Shipready → Saves → Registries → "},{"p":"Troubleshooting","h":"SK132: AssetNotRegistered","u":"save-troubleshooting.html#sk132-assetnotregistered","x":"What it means: A saved link to an asset, such as a ScriptableObject item definition, could not be saved or restored. The asset is in neither the Asset Registry nor the Prefab Registry. That link is empty, and everything else saved or loaded normally. Likely cause: One of these: The asset was never registered. Assets are not added automatically the way prefabs are. The settings asset's Asset Registry field is empty. The asset was deleted or recreated, which gives it a new GUID. The project was copied without its .meta files. Fix: Run Tools → Shipready → Saves → Diagnostics . It finds ticked fie"},{"p":"Troubleshooting","h":"SK133: SceneNotRegistered","u":"save-troubleshooting.html#sk133-scenenotregistered","x":"What it means: A scene that holds saved objects is not in the Scene Registry. When saving, Shipready stores that scene's objects under the scene's path instead of its permanent ID. When loading, a saved scene could not be found. Its state stays in memory and is applied if the scene loads later. Likely cause: The settings asset's Scene Registry field is empty, or the registry was not updated after the scene was added. The scene may also have been deleted and created again, which gives it a new GUID. Scenes in Build Settings are registered automatically. Fix: Run Tools → Shipready → Saves → Regi"},{"p":"Troubleshooting","h":"SK134: SceneNotInBuildSettings","u":"save-troubleshooting.html#sk134-scenenotinbuildsettings","x":"What it means: A scene that the save needs, or one passed to SaveKit.LoadScene , is not in Build Settings. In a build Unity cannot load it: the load carries on without that scene, and its saved state stays in memory and is applied if the scene loads later. In the Editor Shipready loads it straight from the project anyway and warns once per scene, because a build could not. When a saved scene cannot be loaded but is open already, its saved state is restored into the open scene, with this warning: in the Editor, an open scene with the same id, or with the same name when it has no file of its own"},{"p":"Troubleshooting","h":"SK140: HostOnly","u":"save-troubleshooting.html#sk140-hostonly","x":"What it means: This copy of the game is a co-op client, and only the host or a solo player may save, load or delete world saves. The call was refused, and no file was touched. Likely cause: SaveKit.Authority is set, and its HasSaveAuthority returned false. On this machine, Shipready then refuses slot saves, loads and deletes, autosaves, the quicksave, and SaveKit.RestoreBackup . If HasSaveAuthority throws an exception, Shipready also refuses, and says why in the Console. Fix: This behaviour is by design. Check SaveKit.CanSave and SaveKit.CanLoad before you show a save or load button of your ow"},{"p":"Troubleshooting","h":"SK141: PlayerUnknown","u":"save-troubleshooting.html#sk141-playerunknown","x":"What it means: Shipready could not match a connected player to a stable player ID, so it cannot find that player's saved data, and the player starts from defaults. Likely cause: SaveKit.Players.ForConnection(connectionId) returned null and wrote SK141 to the Console, once per connection, for one of these reasons: No IPlayerIdentity is set: SaveKit.Identity is null. Your IPlayerIdentity.TryGetPlayerId returned false, or an empty ID, for that connection. Your TryGetPlayerId threw an exception. SaveKit.Players.TryGetPlayerId reports this case too; it is otherwise the quiet form, for code that ask"},{"p":"Troubleshooting","h":"SK150: CategoryUnknown","u":"save-troubleshooting.html#sk150-categoryunknown","x":"What it means: A category name was used that the settings asset does not define. Category names must match exactly, including capital letters. Likely cause: A typo, such as \"progress\" instead of \"Progress\" . Look in SaveKit.Data.Set , in the categories passed to Save or Load , and in each Saveable's category. A category may also have been renamed or removed. Initialise also fails with SK150 in these cases: No categories exist. Default Category is not in the list. World Category is not in the list. Fix: Add the category on the settings asset's Advanced tab, under Categories , or fix the spellin"},{"p":"Troubleshooting","h":"SK151: SlotOutOfRange","u":"save-troubleshooting.html#sk151-slotoutofrange","x":"What it means: The slot number is outside the range the game offers. Slots are numbered from 1 up to Slot Count in the settings asset. Nothing was read or written. Likely cause: One of these: Slot 0 was used. Slots start at 1, not 0. A menu's list index was passed without adding 1. Slot Count was lowered in an update, so the higher slots no longer exist. Fix: Pass a number from 1 to SaveKit.Settings.SlotCount . If you lowered Slot Count (1 to 99) after release, raise it again. Files in the higher slots are still on disk. Shipready just does not touch them while they are out of range."},{"p":"Troubleshooting","h":"SK152: SlotEmpty","u":"save-troubleshooting.html#sk152-slotempty","x":"What it means: The slot has nothing saved in it yet: no main file, temporary file or backup. SlotInfo.Status also reports SK152 for empty slots shown in a menu. Nothing was changed. Likely cause: SaveKit.Load(slot) was called on a slot the player never saved to, or on one they deleted. If slots that used to hold saves are suddenly empty, Shipready is probably looking in a different folder or for different file names (see SK100). Fix: Before you offer Load, check SaveKit.HasSave(slot) , or read SaveKit.GetSlotInfo(slot).Exists when you draw the menu. For empty slots, offer New Game or Save inst"},{"p":"Troubleshooting","h":"SK153: SettingsInvalid","u":"save-troubleshooting.html#sk153-settingsinvalid","x":"What it means: A value in the settings asset cannot be used, so Shipready refused to start. Nothing was read or written. The message names the field and the problem. Likely cause: One of these: Slot File Name Format or Autosave File Name Format has no {0} in it, so every slot would write the same file; its braces are broken, as in slot_{0 or slot_{0:00 ; or it uses another placeholder such as {1} . Profile Folder holds {profile} , but SaveKit.ProfileId was empty when Shipready started, so Shipready could not tell whose saves to use. With Initialise Automatically ticked, this shows up as Shipre"},{"p":"Troubleshooting","h":"SK154: LoadInProgress","u":"save-troubleshooting.html#sk154-loadinprogress","x":"What it means: A slot save, autosave, quicksave or SaveKit.RestoreBackup was requested while SaveKit.LoadAsync was still loading the scenes its save was made in. Saving or restoring at that moment would write over the save that was just loaded while the world is only half restored, so nothing was written. Likely cause: Game code saved during a load. For example, the player pressed a save button, or a script saved straight after starting LoadAsync without awaiting it. Shipready's own timer and scene-change autosaves wait for the load to finish. The autosave on quit is skipped during a load, wit"},{"p":"Troubleshooting","h":"Still stuck?","u":"save-troubleshooting.html#still-stuck","x":"Report a bug with the bug report form , ask a question in Discussions , email the address in Tools > Shipready > Saves > Help or visit https://raregeometry.github.io/ . Get help: Discord https://discord.gg/AmpBDxRAKu · bug tracker https://github.com/raregeometry/Support · website https://raregeometry.github.io/ · e-mail the address in Tools > Shipready > Saves > Help. With a question or a bug, please paste your system info: in Unity, Tools → Shipready → Saves → Start → Help and support → Copy system info . To get an answer quickly, please send: The Console report. Include every line that start"},{"p":"Known Issues","h":"Known Issues","u":"save-known-issues.html#known-issues","x":"Problems in Shipready that are known and not yet fixed, with workarounds. Fixed issues move to the Changelog . To report something new, see Support ."},{"p":"Known Issues","h":"Saving 10,000 objects takes slightly longer than targeted with the Mono backend","u":"save-known-issues.html#saving-10000-objects-takes-slightly-longer-than-targeted-with-the-mono-backend","x":"Applies to: 1.0.0, Windows players built with the Mono scripting backend. What happens: gathering the state of 10,000 changed Saveable objects on the main thread, the part of a save the player could notice as a short pause, takes a median of 66.6 ms in a Mono release build, against Shipready's own target of 60 ms. Individual saves can take longer. With IL2CPP the same save takes 58.3 to 58.9 ms and meets the target. Writing the file happens on a background thread in both cases and is not part of this time. Who notices: only games that save around 10,000 objects whose state changed since the la"},{"p":"Changelog","h":"Changelog","u":"save-changelog.html#changelog","x":"The same list is in CHANGELOG.md in Shipready's folder, which is the original; this page is a copy for reading here and offline. This page lists every version of Shipready and what changed in it, newest first. Questions go to Discussions or the address in Tools > Shipready > Saves > Help, bugs to the bug report form . Documentation: https://raregeometry.github.io/."},{"p":"Changelog","h":"[1.0.0] - 2026-10-30","u":"save-changelog.html#100---2026-10-30","x":"First release. Everything in the two release candidates below, plus the changes here. Saves from the release candidates load as they are; there is nothing to do when you update."},{"p":"Changelog","h":"Added","u":"save-changelog.html#added","x":"Your own background pictures. The Menu Theme takes your own sprites behind the buttons, the main button, the panel and confirmation, and the slot cards (Menu Style window, Text and shape, Images, with the live preview). A sprite with borders set in the Sprite Editor is 9-sliced, and each picture is tinted by its part's colour. Keep Own Image on a Themed Element keeps a picture you set on one part by hand. Switching style keeps your pictures; a style file leaves them out, because they are your project's own assets."},{"p":"Changelog","h":"[1.0.0-rc2] - 2026-10-26","u":"save-changelog.html#100-rc2---2026-10-26","x":"Second release candidate: the same saving engine, with a much easier first ten minutes. Nothing here changes how saves are written, so saves from the first release candidate load as they are."},{"p":"Changelog","h":"Added","u":"save-changelog.html#added_1","x":"Start window (Tools → Shipready → Saves → Start). It opens once after import and offers three guided choices: make objects remember their state, add a save and load menu, or save values from your scripts. A status strip checks what a first save needs (settings, a saved scene, Build Settings, test hotkeys) and fixes each with one click. Play Mode note: in the Editor, a short note in the Game view after every save and load says what was saved, what came back, and what was skipped and why. Click it for the full report. Saveable summary: the top of each Saveable says in one sentence what it saves,"},{"p":"Changelog","h":"Changed","u":"save-changelog.html#changed","x":"Setup Wizard down to two steps: where saves go, and the menu with its hotkeys. Everything else moved to the settings asset's new Advanced tab; its Basics tab uses plain words. F5 / F9 test hotkeys now come with the menu and work in the Editor only by default, with a setting for Editor and builds, or off. Missing packages: Shipready no longer offers to install Unity UI or TextMesh Pro itself. It explains how to install them in a Console message and in Tools → Shipready → Required Packages, which opens the Package Manager on them. No dialog opens by itself. Nothing opens outside Unity: Shipready"},{"p":"Changelog","h":"Fixed","u":"save-changelog.html#fixed","x":"A Console warning on every Play about two migrations marked for the same version: automatic migration discovery found Shipready's own test fixtures. It now skips test assemblies (any that use NUnit), so a game only ever gets its own steps. Start and Save Inspector open at a comfortable size (about 640 x 720 and 1000 x 640, as much as the display holds) when the size Unity remembered is cramped or smaller than their minimum, which also stopped them being resized by their corner. Menu Style in a demo scene says it is a demo that builds its own menu on Play (T switches its styles there) instead o"},{"p":"Changelog","h":"[1.0.0-rc1] - 2026-10-16","u":"save-changelog.html#100-rc1---2026-10-16","x":"First release candidate."},{"p":"Changelog","h":"Added","u":"save-changelog.html#added_2","x":"Platforms and Unity versions. Windows 10 and 11, 64-bit, with Mono and IL2CPP. Supported from Unity 2022.3 LTS; tested on Unity 6.3 LTS and the newest Unity 6. The co-op sample needs Unity 6 with Netcode for GameObjects 2.x. Render pipelines: Built-in, URP and HDRP. The core has no rendering code. Demos are checked in Built-in and URP; HDRP gets automated tests only. Input from the Input Manager, the Input System or both. No third-party dependencies: the menu uses Unity's own uGUI and TextMeshPro. macOS, iOS, Android, Linux, WebGL and consoles are not supported in 1.0. Saving SaveKit : one cal"},{"p":"Support","h":"Support","u":"save-support.html#support","x":"Shipready is made and supported by Rare Geometry. This page says what support covers, how quickly you get an answer, how to report a problem, and how updates are released."},{"p":"Support","h":"What support covers","u":"save-support.html#what-support-covers","x":"Bugs in Shipready itself: something Shipready does differently from what its documentation says, an error from Shipready's own code, or a save that does not load the way it should. Setting up and using Shipready as documented: the Start window, the settings, Saveables, the save menu, the editor tools, game updates, co-op hooks and Steam Cloud. The tested setups: Unity 2022.3 LTS, 6.3 LTS and 6.6, with the Built-in render pipeline, URP or HDRP, on Windows 10 and 11 (64-bit). Other platforms are not tested in 1.0; questions about them are welcome, but fixes are not promised. Support does not cov"},{"p":"Support","h":"Response time","u":"save-support.html#response-time","x":"Every new bug report, question and e-mail gets a first answer within 2 business days . A first answer may be a fix, a workaround, or a request for what is needed to reproduce the problem."},{"p":"Support","h":"How to report a problem","u":"save-support.html#how-to-report-a-problem","x":"Use Report a Bug. In Unity, choose Tools → Shipready → Saves → Report a Bug , or Report a bug in the Start window. It copies a report of your setup to the clipboard (kit, Unity and render pipeline versions, platform, your last three saves and loads, and what Diagnostics finds) and shows the address of the bug report form , with what it can fill already filled, to copy into your browser. The report holds no file paths beyond your save folder's name and none of your saved values. Add what happened, what you expected, and the shortest steps that show the problem, ideally in a new empty project or"},{"p":"Support","h":"Updates","u":"save-support.html#updates","x":"Bug fixes are released as patch versions (1.0.1, 1.0.2, ...) as soon as they are ready and tested. New features come in minor versions (1.1, 1.2, ...). Your players' saves keep loading. Every version of Shipready reads saves written by earlier versions. A change that would need your save data upgraded is never made in a patch version, and the Changelog always says what changed and whether you need to do anything. Updating is done through the Package Manager (My Assets). Your settings and registries live outside Shipready's folder, so an update never overwrites them. Known Issues lists problems"},{"p":"Support","h":"Refunds","u":"save-support.html#refunds","x":"Refunds for Asset Store purchases are handled by Unity under the Asset Store's own policy: see Unity's Asset Store refund policy . If something does not work for you, please report it first: most problems have a fix or a workaround."},{"p":"Shipready","h":"Shipready","u":"tutorials-index.html#shipready","x":"In-game tutorials, onboarding and coach marks for Unity, without code. Dim the screen, spotlight a button or an object, show a short tip, and move on when the player really does the thing. Works with uGUI and UI Toolkit menus and with 2D and 3D world objects, as blocking step-by-step tours or small in-game hints that never pause play."},{"p":"Shipready","h":"What it covers","u":"tutorials-index.html#what-it-covers","x":"Page What you'll find Quick Start Your first tutorial in a few minutes Flows and Steps How a tutorial is built, starting it, progress, skipping, branching Targets Pointing at uGUI and UI Toolkit elements, 2D and 3D objects, screen areas Finishing a Step Every completion rule: clicks, keys, gestures, areas, counters, conditions, events Coach Marks and Hints Blocking tours versus in-game hints, and the input gate Make It Yours Looks, Display As, positions, your own pictures, fonts and sounds Button Pictures Keyboard, Xbox, PlayStation, Switch and touch pictures that switch live Tools Live previe"},{"p":"Quick Start","h":"Quick Start","u":"tutorials-quick-start.html#quick-start","x":""},{"p":"Quick Start","h":"See it working (1 minute)","u":"tutorials-quick-start.html#see-it-working-1-minute","x":"Choose Tools > Shipready > Tutorials > Open Demo > Menu Tour (uGUI) . Press Play . The screen dims, the Play button is spotlighted and a tip card explains it. Click Play in the game: the step finishes and the tour moves on. On the last step but one, click Make it yours to switch between the five built-in looks."},{"p":"Quick Start","h":"Your own tutorial (5 minutes)","u":"tutorials-quick-start.html#your-own-tutorial-5-minutes","x":"Open your scene. Select the buttons or objects the tutorial should point at, in the order the player meets them. Choose Tools > Shipready > Tutorials > Add Selected As Steps . You get a Tutorial Flow with one step per object. Steps that point at a button already wait for a click on it. Select a step and write its heading and text in the Inspector. With Live Preview on (the default), the Game view shows the step without pressing Play. Press Play ."},{"p":"Quick Start","h":"What next","u":"tutorials-quick-start.html#what-next","x":"Change how each step finishes: add a rule such as Key Rule or Area Rule to the step ( Finishing a Step ). For an action game, set the flow's Style to Hint : small tips that never block or pause play ( Coach Marks and Hints ). Pick a look under Theme Preset , or make your own theme ( Make It Yours ). If something doesn't happen, open Tools > Shipready > Tutorials > Why Is This Step Waiting ."},{"p":"Flows and Steps","h":"Flows and Steps","u":"tutorials-flows-and-steps.html#flows-and-steps","x":"A Tutorial Flow is one tutorial. Its steps are its child objects with a Tutorial Step component, shown in order from top to bottom. Reorder steps by reordering the children, or with the arrows in the flow's Inspector."},{"p":"Flows and Steps","h":"The flow","u":"tutorials-flows-and-steps.html#the-flow","x":"Setting What it does Theme Preset / Theme The look (see Make It Yours ). Style Coach Mark dims and waits; Hint is small and never blocks. Steps can override it. Input Gate What the player can click: only the target, only the card, or everything. Steps can override it. Start On Scene Start , or Manually (a trigger, a button, a UnityEvent or code calls Play). Run Once Per Player Once the player finishes or skips it, it never runs again for them (on this install, or in this save slot with a profile: see Progress). Resume Where Left If the player quits halfway, the flow continues from the same ste"},{"p":"Flows and Steps","h":"The step","u":"tutorials-flows-and-steps.html#the-step","x":"Setting What it does Title, Text The tip card's words. Short sentences work best. Short Text A few words for compact cards and hints. Icon A picture shown when the look's Display As uses icons. Glyph Action Shows the button picture for this action on the player's device (Jump, Interact...). Target What the step points at (see Targets ). When Target Missing Wait for it (default: nothing shows or blocks until it appears), show the card anyway, or skip the step. Finish When Any rule or all rules. A step with no rule finishes with Next. Next Button Auto shows it when the step has no rule. Optional"},{"p":"Flows and Steps","h":"Starting a flow","u":"tutorials-flows-and-steps.html#starting-a-flow","x":"On Scene Start : it plays as soon as the scene starts. Tutorial Trigger component: when the player enters an area (3D or 2D trigger), the first time an object is seen on screen, when an object turns on, or after a delay. Play from a UnityEvent (a button's On Click) or from code: flow.Play() ."},{"p":"Flows and Steps","h":"Progress","u":"tutorials-flows-and-steps.html#progress","x":"Progress is saved in PlayerPrefs under RareGeometry.Shipready.Tutorials.<flow id> . PlayerPrefs belong to this install of the game: for save slots or several players call TutorialProgress.UseProfile(\"slot-2\") , or keep progress with your game's saves by setting TutorialProgress.Store to your own ITutorialProgressStore (see Extending ). TutorialProgress.ClearAll() forgets everything (a new game, or \"Reset tutorials\" in your options). Restart starts one flow again from its first step. Each step saves a stable key next to its position, so inserting, moving or removing steps in an update never res"},{"p":"Flows and Steps","h":"Pausing","u":"tutorials-flows-and-steps.html#pausing","x":"Tutorials run on real time, so they keep working while your game is paused ( Time.timeScale = 0 ): good for menu tours. When your pause menu, a dialogue or a loading screen opens over a running tutorial, call flow.Suspend() (or TutorialFlow.SuspendAll(true) ) and Resume() when it closes: nothing shows or blocks meanwhile, and its rules and timers wait."},{"p":"Targets","h":"Targets","u":"tutorials-targets.html#targets","x":"Each step points at one target. The spotlight, outline and pointer follow it every frame, so moving and animated targets are fine. Kind Set Notes None nothing A plain message in the middle of the screen. UI Element a uGUI RectTransform (button, icon, panel) Any canvas mode; clicks on it can finish the step. UI Toolkit Element a UI Document and an element name (or a USS class) Names as set in UI Builder. World Object any object Uses its renderers (3D meshes, sprites), else its 3D or 2D colliders, else its position. Screen Area a rectangle as fractions of the screen 0,0 is bottom left, 1,1 is to"},{"p":"Targets","h":"Spawned, pooled and list items: Found By Id","u":"tutorials-targets.html#spawned-pooled-and-list-items-found-by-id","x":"Menus built from prefabs, inventory slots, pooled UI and objects in other scenes can't be dragged into a step. Instead: Add Add Component > Shipready > Tutorial Target Id to the object or prefab, and type an id ( inventory.sword ). Set the step's target to Found By Id with the same id. The step waits until an active object with that id exists, then highlights it (a uGUI element like UI Element, anything else like World Object). If the object is turned off or returned to a pool, the step hides and waits again; the newest active object with the id wins. Use a different id for each thing a step s"},{"p":"Targets","h":"Clicks","u":"tutorials-targets.html#clicks","x":"On a button (or any Selectable), only a click the button really takes counts: a click on a button that isn't interactable doesn't finish the step. Gamepad and keyboard Submit on the highlighted button counts too, and gamepad players get the highlighted button selected. On a plain panel or picture, a press and release inside it counts."},{"p":"Targets","h":"Lists: Scroll Into View","u":"tutorials-targets.html#lists-scroll-into-view","x":"A uGUI target inside a Scroll Rect (a shop list, an inventory) is scrolled into view when its step starts, so the spotlight never points at an item the list's mask hides. Turn Scroll Into View off on the target to leave the list alone."},{"p":"Targets","h":"World objects","u":"tutorials-targets.html#world-objects","x":"Bounds chooses what the highlight wraps: Auto, Renderers, Colliders, or Position (with a size you set). Camera : the camera that sees it. Empty uses the main camera, or the first camera that sees the object. For split screen, set the player's camera here. Cameras that render into a Render Texture aren't supported. When the object is behind the camera or outside the view, the pointer sits at the screen edge and points toward it."},{"p":"Targets","h":"When a target can't be found","u":"tutorials-targets.html#when-a-target-cant-be-found","x":"When Target Missing on the step decides (the menu is closed, the object isn't spawned yet, it was turned off): Choice What happens Wait (default) Nothing shows and nothing is blocked until the target appears, then the step shows. The game can never get stuck behind a dim screen. Show Card The card shows in the middle of the screen without a highlight. Skip Step The step is passed over. Why Is This Step Waiting and Scene Overview say what is wrong: not set, turned off, not under a Canvas, no element with that name, no object with that id yet, or no camera."},{"p":"Finishing a Step","h":"Finishing a Step","u":"tutorials-finishing-steps.html#finishing-a-step","x":"Add one or more rules to a step's object (Add Component > Shipready > Rules). The step finishes when any rule is done, or when all are (the step's Finish When ). A step with no rule finishes with the Next button. Every rule runs on real time and tells the debugger what it is waiting for. Rule Finishes when Notes Click Target Rule the player clicks or taps the step's target uGUI, UI Toolkit, world objects and screen areas. Set how many clicks. Key Rule one of its keys is pressed (or held for a time) Both input systems. Input Action Rule an Input System action is performed Gamepads and rebinding"},{"p":"Finishing a Step","h":"Tips","u":"tutorials-finishing-steps.html#tips","x":"\"Press Space or tap Jump\": add a Key Rule and a Click Target Rule, Finish When Any Rule. \"Collect 3 coins\": a Counter Rule, and each coin's pickup event calls the rule's Add . \"If the player already knows this, skip it\": tick the step's Skip If Already Done . A Condition Rule or a Counter Rule with Count Before Step Starts is done straight away when the player already did it. Your own rule: derive from CompletionRule (see Extending )."},{"p":"Coach Marks and Hints","h":"Coach Marks and Hints","u":"tutorials-coach-marks-and-hints.html#coach-marks-and-hints","x":"Every step shows in one of two styles. Set it on the flow ( Style ) and override it per step."},{"p":"Coach Marks and Hints","h":"Coach marks","u":"tutorials-coach-marks-and-hints.html#coach-marks","x":"For menus, mobile games and strategy games. The screen dims around a spotlight on the target, a pointer shows the way, and the tip card waits beside it. The Input Gate decides what the player can click: Only Target : the target (and the card) only. Clicks inside the spotlight reach the real button. Block All : only the card's buttons. Allow All : nothing is blocked."},{"p":"Coach Marks and Hints","h":"In-game hints","u":"tutorials-coach-marks-and-hints.html#in-game-hints","x":"For action games. A small tip that never dims, never blocks and never pauses play: \"Press Space to jump\", gone when the player jumps. Hints fit their words, sit at the bottom, top or centre of the screen (or beside their target), and can still ring their target. Set where they appear under the theme's In-game hints settings. Use Show Until Learned on the flow for hints that come back until the player has done the thing a few times, and a Tutorial Trigger to show a hint the first time the player meets something."},{"p":"Make It Yours","h":"Make It Yours","u":"tutorials-make-it-yours.html#make-it-yours","x":"Everything the player sees and hears can be changed or switched off, without code."},{"p":"Make It Yours","h":"Looks","u":"tutorials-make-it-yours.html#looks","x":"Pick a Theme Preset on the flow: Clean Dark , Minimal , Mobile Casual , Sci-Fi HUD or Fantasy Parchment . For your own, choose Assets > Create > Shipready > Theme , set it on the flow's Theme field and change anything. Presets keep your own pictures, font and sounds when you switch. Group Settings Overlay Dim on or off, colour and strength. Spotlight Shape (rounded rectangle, circle, oval, none), padding, corner radius, softness. Outline On or off, colour, width, pulse. Pointer On or off, colour, size, bob, your own picture. Message Display As, card position, custom point and offset, default i"},{"p":"Make It Yours","h":"Display As","u":"tutorials-make-it-yours.html#display-as","x":"How much each card shows: Full (heading and text), Short Text , Icon Only , Icon And Short Text , or None (only the highlight; buttons still show when the step needs Next). Set it on the theme, override it per step."},{"p":"Make It Yours","h":"Card position","u":"tutorials-make-it-yours.html#card-position","x":"Auto picks the side with room; or Below , Above , Right , Left , Center , Screen Top , Screen Bottom , or Custom (a point on screen plus an offset). The card never leaves the screen or covers its target."},{"p":"Make It Yours","h":"Per step","u":"tutorials-make-it-yours.html#per-step","x":"Any step can have its own look ( Theme Override ), Display As, position, style and input gate."},{"p":"Make It Yours","h":"Your own pictures","u":"tutorials-make-it-yours.html#your-own-pictures","x":"Card, main button and pointer each take your sprite. Sprites with borders are 9-sliced; all are tinted by the part's colour."},{"p":"Make It Yours","h":"Words","u":"tutorials-make-it-yours.html#words","x":"Every word goes through TutorialText.Translate : set it to your localisation lookup, and you can write keys instead of sentences in steps."},{"p":"Make It Yours","h":"Accessibility","u":"tutorials-make-it-yours.html#accessibility","x":"UI Scale on the presenter for larger text, Reduce Motion on the theme, high-contrast looks (Minimal), and real time timers that you can lengthen."},{"p":"Button Pictures","h":"Button Pictures","u":"tutorials-button-pictures.html#button-pictures","x":"Set a step's Glyph Action (for example Jump) and its card shows the right picture for the player's device: Space on a keyboard, A on Xbox, Cross on PlayStation, B on Nintendo Switch, a swipe on a touch screen. It switches live when the player picks up another device, with either input system. Shipready's set ( Default Input Glyphs , made from Kenney's Input Prompts, CC0) has these actions: Confirm, Back, Jump, Interact, Attack, Aim, Move, Look, Menu, Tap, Hold, Swipe, Pinch."},{"p":"Button Pictures","h":"Your own set","u":"tutorials-button-pictures.html#your-own-set","x":"Choose Assets > Create > Shipready > Input Glyph Set , add your actions and pictures, and set it on the flow's Glyphs field. A device with no picture falls back to the keyboard picture. On light cards, the theme's Icon Plate Color puts a plate behind white button pictures so they stay readable."},{"p":"Button Pictures","h":"Words that fit every device","u":"tutorials-button-pictures.html#words-that-fit-every-device","x":"The picture changes with the player's device; the words don't. Write the action, not the key (\"Jump onto the ledge\", not \"Press Space to jump onto the ledge\"), and let the button picture show which button to press. For per-device words, translate them through TutorialText.Translate and call TutorialFlow.RefreshAllViews() when the device changes."},{"p":"Tools","h":"Tools","u":"tutorials-tools.html#tools","x":"Everything is under Tools > Shipready . Tool What it does Create Tutorial Flow A new flow with one step. Create From Preset Menu Tour (coach marks), Movement Basics (in-game hints), First Time Hint (shown the first time something is seen). Add Selected As Steps Select buttons or objects, get one step per object with its target set; steps on buttons wait for a click. Picking a button's label or icon points at the button itself. Add Step Ready-made steps: Explain Something (Next button), Click A Button (select it first), Press A Key (in-game hint), Walk Somewhere (in-game hint), Wait For A Game "},{"p":"Tools","h":"In the Inspector","u":"tutorials-tools.html#in-the-inspector","x":"The Tutorial Flow Inspector lists the steps in order with Edit and move buttons, Add Step, and Restart, Next and Skip while playing. At the top of the Tutorial Step Inspector, a box says in plain words when the step finishes (\"a click on Play Button, or the Next button\") and what it points at, lists anything wrong with it (with a fix button), and while playing has Test From This Step and what the step is waiting for right now."},{"p":"Tools","h":"In the Scene view","u":"tutorials-tools.html#in-the-scene-view","x":"Select a flow or a step to see a box and the step number on each world target."},{"p":"Tools","h":"In your game","u":"tutorials-tools.html#in-your-game","x":"Add a Tutorial Debug Overlay to any object and press F8 in a test build to see what each running flow waits for, with a box around each target."},{"p":"Integrations","h":"Integrations","u":"tutorials-integrations.html#integrations","x":"Shipready needs no package. These work automatically when their package or kit is in your project. With What you get uGUI (in every Unity template) Shipready's tip card, spotlight and input gate for uGUI. UI Toolkit (built into Unity) UI Toolkit targets, and a UI Toolkit tip card. It is used when uGUI isn't in the project; add UI Toolkit Tutorial Presenter to a flow to use it anyway. Input System package Keys, pointer, touch, gamepad detection and the Input Action Rule . With Input Handling set to Both, the Input System is used. Visual Scripting package Visual Scripting Event Rule : a graph's "},{"p":"Integrations","h":"Saving progress with your save system","u":"tutorials-integrations.html#saving-progress-with-your-save-system","x":"Progress goes to PlayerPrefs by default. To keep it in your own saves, implement ITutorialProgressStore (five small methods: get and set the step, get and set done, clear) and set it once at start: TutorialProgress.Store = new MySaveSystemProgressStore();"},{"p":"Extending","h":"Extending","u":"tutorials-extending.html#extending","x":"Everything works without code. For programmers, Shipready is built to be extended."},{"p":"Extending","h":"Control a flow","u":"tutorials-extending.html#control-a-flow","x":"flow.Play(); // start (respects Run Once and Resume) flow.Next(); // finish the current step flow.Back(); flow.Skip(); // end it and remember it as done flow.Restart(); // start again from the first step flow.Stop(); // stop without saving it as done (it resumes next time) flow.Suspend(); // hold it: nothing shows or blocks, rules and timers wait flow.Resume(); // show the held step again flow.RefreshView(); // redraw after changing the look or words at runtime flow.ClearProgress(); // forget this player's progress for this flow Read IsRunning , IsSuspended , CurrentIndex , CurrentStep , StepV"},{"p":"Extending","h":"Find and start flows from anywhere","u":"tutorials-extending.html#find-and-start-flows-from-anywhere","x":"TutorialFlow.PlayById(\"shop-tour\"); // from a button in another scene, a quest, a dialogue... TutorialFlow.Find(\"shop-tour\"); // the loaded flow with that id, or null TutorialFlow.SuspendAll(true); // your pause menu or loading screen opened TutorialFlow.SuspendAll(false); // and closed TutorialFlow.RefreshAllViews(); // the player changed the language A flow lives in its scene. For a tutorial that goes from the menu into the level, make one flow in each scene and start the second with PlayById (or Start: On Scene Start ) after the level loads. Only one coach-mark flow shows at a time: a secon"},{"p":"Extending","h":"Events and analytics","u":"tutorials-extending.html#events-and-analytics","x":"Each flow has Started , StepShown , Completed , Skipped (C#) and the matching UnityEvents. For analytics, listen to every flow at once: TutorialFlow.AnyEvent += e => MyAnalytics.Log(\"tutorial\", e.Kind.ToString(), e.FlowId, e.StepKey, e.ElapsedSeconds); The kinds are FlowStarted, StepShown, StepCompleted, FlowCompleted, FlowSkipped, FlowStopped, FlowSuspended and FlowResumed. Every flow ends with exactly one of Completed, Skipped or Stopped. StepKey stays the same when you move or insert steps, so your funnels keep working after updates. Shipready itself sends nothing anywhere."},{"p":"Extending","h":"Progress, save slots and profiles","u":"tutorials-extending.html#progress-save-slots-and-profiles","x":"Progress is saved in PlayerPrefs, which belong to this install of the game. For save slots or several players: TutorialProgress.UseProfile(\"slot-2\"); // when the player picks a slot, before tutorials start TutorialProgress.ClearAll(); // \"Reset tutorials\" in your options, or a new game Or keep progress in your own save file: implement ITutorialProgressStore (five methods) and set TutorialProgress.Store . Each step saves a stable key with its position, so adding, moving or removing steps in an update never resumes a player at the wrong step (an unknown saved step starts the flow from the beginn"},{"p":"Extending","h":"Targets that appear later","u":"tutorials-extending.html#targets-that-appear-later","x":"TutorialTargets.Register(\"shop.buy\", buyButton.transform); TutorialTargets.Unregister(\"shop.buy\", buyButton.transform); Or put a Tutorial Target Id component on the prefab. See Targets."},{"p":"Extending","h":"Your own rule","u":"tutorials-extending.html#your-own-rule","x":"public sealed class OpenedMapRule : CompletionRule { protected override void OnBegin() => MapScreen.Opened += Done; protected override void OnEnd() => MapScreen.Opened -= Done; private void Done() => MarkDone(); public override void DescribeWaiting(System.Text.StringBuilder into) => into.Append(\"the map to open\"); } Add it to a step like any rule. OnTick runs every frame (real time) while the step shows; OnTargetClicked runs when the player clicks the target. Unsubscribe in OnEnd : it always runs once when the step ends, is skipped or the flow's object is destroyed."},{"p":"Extending","h":"Your own UI","u":"tutorials-extending.html#your-own-ui","x":"Implement ITutorialPresenter on a component and put it on the flow's object (or drag it into Presenter ). The flow calls ShowStep with everything already resolved (words, look, buttons, icon, position), UpdateTarget every frame with the target's screen rectangle, then HideStep and EndFlow . Call flow.Next() , Back() , Skip() and NotifyTargetClicked() from your buttons. If your UI reports clicks on uGUI targets itself, set flow.TargetReportsClicks = true ; otherwise the flow counts a press inside the target. To make your UI the default for every flow, set TutorialPresenters.CreateDefault ."},{"p":"Extending","h":"Your own input","u":"tutorials-extending.html#your-own-input","x":"Implement ITutorialInputSource and set TutorialInput.Source to drive tutorials from any input system (Rewired, InControl, your own) or a replay. If players can type somewhere Shipready doesn't know about, add a check so typed letters aren't counted as key presses: TutorialInput.AddTypingCheck(() => myChatBox.IsTyping) ."},{"p":"Extending","h":"Words","u":"tutorials-extending.html#words","x":"TutorialText.Translate = key => MyLocalisation.Get(key); and call TutorialFlow.RefreshAllViews() when the language changes."},{"p":"Extending","h":"Condition Rule and IL2CPP","u":"tutorials-extending.html#condition-rule-and-il2cpp","x":"The Condition Rule reads a field or property by name. With IL2CPP and high code stripping, a member only read this way can be removed: mark it [UnityEngine.Scripting.Preserve] , or use an Event Rule for anything the tutorial depends on."},{"p":"Samples","h":"Samples","u":"tutorials-samples.html#samples","x":"Open each from Tools > Shipready > Tutorials > Open Demo and press Play. Demo Shows Menu Tour (uGUI) A mobile-style menu, a coach-mark tour with spotlights, a click on the real Play button, and a Make it yours button that switches the five looks. First Person Basics (3D hints) In-game hints that never pause play: walk (W A S D), look at the tower, jump, reach the glowing pad, collect 3 coins. Button pictures follow your device. Platformer Hints (2D) Hints on 2D colliders with an orthographic camera: run, jump onto the ledge, coins, reach the flag. Strategy Tour (UI Toolkit) A coach-mark tour o"},{"p":"Upgrading","h":"Upgrading","u":"tutorials-upgrading.html#upgrading","x":"Updates never change your flows, steps or themes. Import the new version over the old one; your progress keys ( RareGeometry.Shipready.Tutorials.<flow id> ) stay the same. What changed in each version is in CHANGELOG.md ."},{"p":"Troubleshooting","h":"Troubleshooting","u":"tutorials-troubleshooting.html#troubleshooting","x":"First open Tools > Shipready > Tutorials > Why Is This Step Waiting while playing, and Scene Overview in the editor: they say what is wrong in plain words, and Scene Overview fixes most of it in one click. The tutorial doesn't start. It may have run before: with Run Once Per Player on, a finished or skipped flow never runs again for that player. Press Restart in the flow's Inspector while playing. Or its Start is Manually: something has to call Play (a Tutorial Trigger, a button, a UnityEvent). A step never finishes. The debugger says what it waits for (the step's Inspector says it too). A ste"},{"p":"Troubleshooting","h":"Still stuck?","u":"tutorials-troubleshooting.html#still-stuck","x":"Discord: https://discord.gg/AmpBDxRAKu (ask in #help) Report a bug: https://github.com/raregeometry/Support/issues Website: https://raregeometry.github.io/shipready/ Press Copy system info in Tools > Shipready > Tutorials > Help and Support and paste it into your message."},{"p":"Support","h":"Support","u":"tutorials-support.html#support","x":""},{"p":"Support","h":"Where to get help","u":"tutorials-support.html#where-to-get-help","x":"Documentation: https://raregeometry.github.io/shipready/docs/ Discord: https://discord.gg/AmpBDxRAKu Ask in #help , report problems in #bug-reports , suggest ideas in #feature-requests , with the Shipready tag. Report a bug: https://github.com/raregeometry/Support/issues Search the open issues first, then use the Bug report form and pick Shipready. Website: https://raregeometry.github.io/shipready/ Email: the address in Tools > Shipready > Tutorials > Help"},{"p":"Support","h":"What to include","u":"tutorials-support.html#what-to-include","x":"Open Tools > Shipready > Tutorials > Help and Support and press Copy system info : it copies Shipready version, Unity version, render pipeline, platform and input handling. Paste it into your post, then add what you did, what you expected, what happened, and any Console errors."},{"p":"Shipready","h":"Shipready","u":"playtest-index.html#shipready","x":"Testers press one key, say what went wrong, and get one file you can open: their words, a screenshot they can mark up, the last seconds as pictures, the Console log with stack traces, recent events, system info and your game's state. In the Editor, the Report Inbox opens it, links each stack line to your script, and takes you to where the tester stood. Release builds leave Shipready out completely. Reports stay on the tester's device unless you switch on Discord or your own server."},{"p":"Shipready","h":"What it covers","u":"playtest-index.html#what-it-covers","x":"Page What it explains Quick Start Your first report in five minutes Adding it to your own game Keys, pausing, scenes, cameras, UI and other consoles in an existing game The report form What testers see, what each report holds, markup and privacy Debug overlay Console, commands, stats, options and pinning Report Inbox Reading reports in the Editor Make it yours Themes, presets, Display as, your own words, images and UI Settings Every setting and its default Samples The Bug Hunt and Feature Gallery demos Builds and privacy Which builds contain Shipready, and what leaves the device Extending The "},{"p":"Quick Start","h":"Quick Start","u":"playtest-quick-start.html#quick-start","x":"Shipready works the moment it's imported. No setup is needed for the first report. Open Tools > Shipready > Playtest > Open Demo > Bug Hunt and press Play. Press Deliver the crate . The game throws an error and the crate falls through the floor. The Bug Hunt demo after triggering its bugs Press F8 . The form opens with a screenshot taken just before it, and the steps already filled in. Type what happened, draw a circle round the crate, and press Save report . The report form with a circle and an arrow drawn on the screenshot Stop playing. Open Tools > Shipready > Playtest > Report Inbox and pi"},{"p":"Quick Start","h":"In your own game","u":"playtest-quick-start.html#in-your-own-game","x":"Press F8 while playing any of your scenes. That's all. To make it yours: Tools > Shipready > Playtest > Setup Wizard creates the settings, adds your game state to reports and points you to the rest. Press the key above Tab while playing for the debug console, stats and options. In a Development Build Shipready is there; in a release build it isn't. See Builds and privacy ."},{"p":"Quick Start","h":"Where reports go","u":"playtest-quick-start.html#where-reports-go","x":"On the tester's device, in the game's persistent data folder under BugReports . Each one is a single zip with report.html (opens in any browser), report.json , the screenshot, the replay pictures and the log. Testers send you the zip; you open it in the Report Inbox, or just open the html."},{"p":"Adding it to your own game","h":"Adding it to your own game","u":"playtest-adding-to-your-game.html#adding-it-to-your-own-game","x":"Shipready starts by itself, so in most games there is nothing to do: press Play, then F8. This page is for games that already have their own input, pause menu, cameras, scenes or console, and for the questions that come up when Shipready meets them. To check that Shipready itself works, open Tools > Shipready > Playtest > Why Isn't It Working while playing and click Open the Form Now . If the button opens the form but F8 doesn't, something in your game is taking the key."},{"p":"Adding it to your own game","h":"Keys","u":"playtest-adding-to-your-game.html#keys","x":"Your game What Shipready does What you can do Already uses F8 or the key above Tab Both react Pick other keys in Settings (Open Key, Overlay Key). Set a key to None to switch it off and open the form from your own button with BugReporter.Open() Has a chat box or a name field Ignores its keys while a Unity UI, TextMeshPro or UI Toolkit text field has focus Nothing Has its own text entry, rebinding screen or console Can't see it BugReporter.KeysBlocked = () => myChat.IsOpen; Uses touch gestures A three-finger tap opens the overlay, a four-finger tap the form Switch off Touch Gestures in Settings"},{"p":"Adding it to your own game","h":"Your game's input while the form is open","u":"playtest-adding-to-your-game.html#your-games-input-while-the-form-is-open","x":"The form takes the mouse and keyboard, but your game's own input keeps running underneath: typing \"w\" in the form can also walk the player forward. Pause it with Shipready's events: void OnEnable() { BugReporter.FormOpened += PauseGameInput; BugReporter.FormClosed += ResumeGameInput; } void OnDisable() { BugReporter.FormOpened -= PauseGameInput; BugReporter.FormClosed -= ResumeGameInput; } void PauseGameInput() => playerInput.actions.FindActionMap(\"Player\").Disable(); void ResumeGameInput() => playerInput.actions.FindActionMap(\"Player\").Enable(); Shipready unlocks and shows the cursor while th"},{"p":"Adding it to your own game","h":"Pausing","u":"playtest-adding-to-your-game.html#pausing","x":"With Pause While Open on (the default), the form sets Time.timeScale to 0 and puts the old value back when it closes. If your game sets a time scale other than 0 while the form is open (slow motion, say), Shipready keeps it. If it sets 0 (its own pause menu opening behind the form), Shipready can't tell that apart from its own pause and puts the old value back; switch off Pause While Open and pause from BugReporter.FormOpened instead if your game pauses itself. The kit itself runs on unscaled time, so the form and overlay work while your game is paused. The overlay's timescale command changes "},{"p":"Adding it to your own game","h":"Scenes","u":"playtest-adding-to-your-game.html#scenes","x":"Shipready lives on one hidden object that survives scene loads. A report records the active scene and every other scene loaded next to it. Go to where the tester was opens the same set (asking first if you have unsaved changes) and tells you if one of them isn't in this project. If your game destroys every object on reset, including ones kept with DontDestroyOnLoad, it destroys Shipready's too; call BugReporter.Start() after the reset to bring it back. Game state providers on objects that are destroyed are dropped by themselves."},{"p":"Adding it to your own game","h":"Cameras","u":"playtest-adding-to-your-game.html#cameras","x":"The screenshot is always what was on screen. For Go to where the tester was , Shipready uses the camera tagged MainCamera, or else the screen camera drawn last (cameras that draw into a texture are skipped). The report names the camera it used. For split screen, a camera rig that isn't tagged, or Cinemachine, tell it which one: BugReporter.TesterCamera = () => localPlayer.Camera; With Cinemachine, give the Unity camera that has the Cinemachine Brain, not a virtual camera."},{"p":"Adding it to your own game","h":"UI","u":"playtest-adding-to-your-game.html#ui","x":"The form and overlay are drawn on their own Canvases at very high sort orders (31990 and 32000), above almost any menu. They use your game's EventSystem when there is one, and add a hidden one only while one of Shipready's screens is showing when there isn't. Your EventSystem and its input module are never changed. Without the Unity UI package Shipready uses a simpler form with no markup; Why Isn't It Working says so."},{"p":"Adding it to your own game","h":"Your own code and assembly definitions","u":"playtest-adding-to-your-game.html#your-own-code-and-assembly-definitions","x":"If your scripts are in an assembly definition, add RareGeometry.Shipready.Playtest to its references to call BugReporter , DebugOptions or [DebugCommand] . Scripts without one (Assembly-CSharp) see it already. Your calls stay in release builds and do nothing there; you don't need #if around them. Release builds leave out the Shipready's start-up code, its Unity UI screens, its Input System code and its settings, so nothing starts. [DebugCommand] and [DebugOption] keep their methods and fields when IL2CPP strips code, so commands work in IL2CPP builds too."},{"p":"Adding it to your own game","h":"Other consoles and error reporting","u":"playtest-adding-to-your-game.html#other-consoles-and-error-reporting","x":"Shipready reads the log with Application.logMessageReceivedThreaded , which works next to other consoles, Sentry, Backtrace or your own log handler. If your game sets Debug.unityLogger.logEnabled = false , reports have no log; Why Isn't It Working warns about it while playing. To use only the report form next to another console, switch off Enable Overlay in Settings."},{"p":"Adding it to your own game","h":"Big logs and attachments","u":"playtest-adding-to-your-game.html#big-logs-and-attachments","x":"Each log line and stack trace is kept up to 16,000 characters and Shipready keeps the last Log Lines of them (1,000 by default). Attachments over Max Attachment Megabytes (50) are left out with a note in the report. Read a save file into memory when your attachment callback runs, rather than while the game is writing it."},{"p":"Adding it to your own game","h":"Several game windows on one PC","u":"playtest-adding-to-your-game.html#several-game-windows-on-one-pc","x":"Every report file has its own random ID, so several copies of the game (multiplayer testing, ParrelSync) can save to the same folder. Unity sends key presses only to the focused window by default. Put \"Host\" or \"Client\" in the game state so you can tell the reports apart: BugReporter.SetContext(\"Network role\", NetworkManager.Singleton.IsHost ? \"Host\" : \"Client\");"},{"p":"Adding it to your own game","h":"Not covered","u":"playtest-adding-to-your-game.html#not-covered","x":"Native crashes and hard freezes can't be reported from inside the game. VR, consoles and phones have not been tested on real hardware; for VR, build your own form with BugReporter.UseForm ."},{"p":"The report form","h":"The report form","u":"playtest-report-form.html#the-report-form","x":"Testers open it with F8 (Settings > Open Key), the on-screen Report a bug button, a four-finger tap on a touch screen, or from your code with BugReporter.Open() . The game pauses while it's open (Settings > Pause While Open)."},{"p":"The report form","h":"What testers fill in","u":"playtest-report-form.html#what-testers-fill-in","x":"What happened? The only box they must fill. What did you expect? What did you do before? Already filled in from the recent events, so most testers only check it. Category and Severity , from the lists in the settings. An empty list hides that choice. Screenshot , taken just before the form opened, so it shows the game, not the form. Testers can draw a Circle or an Arrow , or Blur something private. Blur replaces the pixels with flat blocks, so what it hides can't be recovered. Undo takes back the last mark."},{"p":"The report form","h":"What gets saved","u":"playtest-report-form.html#what-gets-saved","x":"Every part has a tick box, and the form says how much each holds. Unticked parts are left out before anything is written, so they reach no file and no destination. Part What it is Screenshot The marked picture Replay The last few seconds as small pictures (Settings > Replay Seconds) Console log The last lines with their stack traces (Settings > Log Lines) Recent events Scene loads, warnings, errors and your breadcrumbs from the last minute System info Game version, build, Unity version, pipeline, OS, device model, CPU, memory, graphics, screen, quality, language. No user or device name Game st"},{"p":"The report form","h":"After saving","u":"playtest-report-form.html#after-saving","x":"The form says Report saved with the file's place, and lists each destination's result. Close it and a short note stays on screen for a moment. With no uGUI package, a simpler form with the same parts and no markup is used."},{"p":"Report Inbox","h":"Report Inbox","u":"playtest-report-inbox.html#report-inbox","x":"Tools > Shipready > Playtest > Report Inbox reads the zips testers send you. A report in the Inbox: status, notes, where the tester was and the marked-up screenshot Open Folder lists every report in a folder, Open File opens one, and This PC's Reports lists the ones made on this computer while playing in the Editor. Pick a report on the left. On the right: what happened, the screenshot, the steps, recent events, game state, system info and the log. Blue links under each error open your script at that line, even when the report came from another machine, as long as the script is at the same pla"},{"p":"Debug overlay","h":"Debug overlay","u":"playtest-debug-overlay.html#debug-overlay","x":"Press the key above Tab (Settings > Overlay Key), or tap with three fingers, while playing."},{"p":"Debug overlay","h":"Console","u":"playtest-debug-overlay.html#console","x":"The console with level filters and a command line, and the stats strip at the top right Search finds words in messages and stack traces. Info, Warnings, Errors show or hide each level; the numbers count everything that matches the search. Collapse groups identical lines (same message and stack) with a count. Clear hides what's there now; new lines still arrive. Tap a line for its stack trace, Copy , or Report this , which opens the form with the message filled in. Copy all copies every line shown. The command line runs commands: type help for the list, timescale 0.5 for slow motion, or your ow"},{"p":"Debug overlay","h":"Stats","u":"playtest-debug-overlay.html#stats","x":"Frame rate, average and worst frame time, memory and garbage collections, with a graph of the last 120 frames: green within 60 FPS, amber within 30, red above. Settings > Stats Strip: With Overlay, Always or Off."},{"p":"Debug overlay","h":"Options","u":"playtest-debug-overlay.html#options","x":"Switches, numbers, choices, buttons and read-only values from your Debug Options Sources and [DebugOption] fields. Pin keeps an option on screen, still working, while the overlay is closed. Keep stats on screen does the same for the stats. A pinned option at the bottom left and the stats strip at the top right, with the overlay closed"},{"p":"Debug overlay","h":"Cost","u":"playtest-debug-overlay.html#cost","x":"Closed, the overlay reads two numbers a frame and allocates nothing. Open, it refreshes four times a second."},{"p":"Settings","h":"Settings","u":"playtest-settings.html#settings","x":"Open them with Tools > Shipready > Playtest > Settings . The first time, this makes BugReportSettings.asset in Assets/RareGeometryData/Shipready . That folder is yours: it sits outside Shipready, so updating Shipready never touches it. Every setting has a tooltip."},{"p":"Settings","h":"Builds","u":"playtest-settings.html#builds","x":"Setting Default What it does Include In Development Builds Only Which builds contain Shipready. See Builds and privacy"},{"p":"Settings","h":"Look","u":"playtest-settings.html#look","x":"Setting Default What it does Theme empty Your own theme asset. Empty uses the preset below Theme Preset Studio Studio, Daylight, Terminal or High Contrast Text Table empty Your own words for everything testers read. Empty uses Shipready's English More in Make it yours ."},{"p":"Settings","h":"Opening the form","u":"playtest-settings.html#opening-the-form","x":"Setting Default What it does Open Key F8 Opens the report form Show On Screen Button off A small Report a Bug button, for touch screens and gamepads Touch Gestures on Three-finger tap opens or closes the overlay, four-finger tap opens the form Pause While Open on Stops game time while the form is open and frees the mouse cursor"},{"p":"Settings","h":"Debug overlay","u":"playtest-settings.html#debug-overlay","x":"Setting Default What it does Enable Overlay on The console, stats strip and debug options Overlay Key the key above Tab Opens and closes the overlay Stats Strip With Overlay When frame rate, frame time and memory show: never, with the overlay, or always Error Badge on While the overlay is closed, a small badge counts new warnings and errors Enable Commands on Lets testers type commands in the console More in Debug overlay ."},{"p":"Settings","h":"Choices in the form","u":"playtest-settings.html#choices-in-the-form","x":"Setting Default What it does Categories Gameplay, Visual, Audio, UI, Performance, Other What a tester can pick. An empty list hides the choice Severities Minor, Major, Blocker Same, for severity"},{"p":"Settings","h":"What is captured","u":"playtest-settings.html#what-is-captured","x":"Setting Default What it does Log Lines 1000 How many Console lines are kept in memory Breadcrumb Seconds 60 How far back the recent events go Max Attachment Megabytes 50 Attachments larger than this are left out, with a note in the report Default Parts All Parts ticked when the form opens. Testers can still untick them Replay Seconds 5 How many seconds before the report the replay covers. 0 turns it off Replay Frames Per Second 2 More is smoother and uses more memory Hide User Name on Replaces the computer's user name in logs and game state Privacy Note empty The line in the form saying where "},{"p":"Settings","h":"Optional destinations","u":"playtest-settings.html#optional-destinations","x":"The zip is always saved on the device. These are off until you switch them on. Setting Default What it does Send To Discord, Discord Webhook Url off, empty Also posts each report to a Discord channel. Use a private test channel Send To Slack, Slack Webhook Url off, empty Also posts each report's summary to a Slack channel through an incoming webhook (Slack takes text only, so the zip stays on the device). Use a private test channel Send To Http, Http Url off, empty Also sends each report to your server as a form POST: a report zip plus id , category , severity and summary Why Isn't It Working "},{"p":"Builds and privacy","h":"Builds and privacy","u":"playtest-builds-and-privacy.html#builds-and-privacy","x":""},{"p":"Builds and privacy","h":"Which builds contain Shipready","u":"playtest-builds-and-privacy.html#which-builds-contain-shipready","x":"Settings > Include In: Development Builds Only (the default): there in the Editor and in builds with Development Build ticked; gone from release builds. Always : in every build, so players can report bugs too. Never : switched off everywhere, the Editor included. A build that leaves Shipready out really leaves it out: the code that starts it, the Unity UI form and overlay and the input code are removed, and the settings (with any Discord or server address) are not in the build. A small core stays so your own calls such as BugReporter.AddBreadcrumb still compile; nothing starts it, so they simp"},{"p":"Builds and privacy","h":"What leaves the device","u":"playtest-builds-and-privacy.html#what-leaves-the-device","x":"Nothing, by default. Each report is a zip in the game's persistent data folder. Shipready has no analytics, no telemetry and no account. If you switch them on in the settings: Discord : each report is posted to a channel through its webhook. Anyone with a build can find the webhook address, so use a private test channel. Slack : each report's summary is posted to a channel through an incoming webhook (Slack takes text only, so the zip stays on the device). As with Discord, use a private test channel. Your own server : each report is sent as a form POST with the zip and a few fields. When a sen"},{"p":"Make it yours","h":"Make it yours","u":"playtest-make-it-yours.html#make-it-yours","x":"Everything testers see can be changed or switched off, with no code. Start with a preset, then change any detail."},{"p":"Make it yours","h":"Pick a look","u":"playtest-make-it-yours.html#pick-a-look","x":"Open Tools > Shipready > Playtest > Settings . Set Theme Preset to Studio (dark), Daylight (light), Terminal (green on black) or High Contrast. The report form in Studio, Daylight, Terminal and High Contrast The debug overlay in the same four presets That's it. For your own colours, make a theme asset: Assets > Create > Shipready > Theme , or one of the Theme (Daylight / Terminal / High Contrast) entries. Assign it to Theme in the settings. Its inspector starts with the preset buttons and a live preview, and warns when words would be hard to read. While playing, a theme change shows at once, e"},{"p":"Make it yours","h":"What a theme changes","u":"playtest-make-it-yours.html#what-a-theme-changes","x":"Setting What it does Colours Dim behind the form, panel, text boxes, buttons, accent (main button, chosen chips, focus ring), text, hints, text on the accent, and the info, warning, error and success colours Font Your own font, or Unity's built-in one Text Scale Every word and row from 80% to 160%. Buttons and chips grow to fit their words Your own images Panel, button and text box sprites, 9-sliced when the sprite has borders, tinted by their colour Keep Own Image Shows a sprite in its own colours instead of tinting it Form Max Width How wide the form gets at 1080p Overlay Side Console on the"},{"p":"Make it yours","h":"Display as","u":"playtest-make-it-yours.html#display-as","x":"Every status message offers the same choice: icon and short text, icon and full text, short text, full text, icon only, or nothing. Message Short Full Saved Report saved Report saved on this device Not saved Not saved Could not save Error badge (console closed) the count 2 errors 1 warnings Note after the form closes Report saved Report saved on this device Icons take the message's colour: success for saved, error for not saved, error or warning for the badge."},{"p":"Make it yours","h":"Your own words","u":"playtest-make-it-yours.html#your-own-words","x":"Every word testers read comes from one table. Make one with Assets > Create > Shipready > Text Table , press Fill With Every Word , and type yours in the right-hand column. Assign it to Text Table in the settings. Empty rows keep Shipready's words. For languages, set ReportText.Translate from your localisation code instead."},{"p":"Make it yours","h":"Switch things off","u":"playtest-make-it-yours.html#switch-things-off","x":"What Where The whole kit in a build Settings > Include In (Development Builds Only, Always, Never) Debug overlay Settings > Enable Overlay Stats strip Settings > Stats Strip: With Overlay, Always, Off Error badge Settings > Error Badge, or Display as None Commands Settings > Enable Commands On-screen Report button Settings > Show On Screen Button Touch gestures Settings > Touch Gestures Replay Settings > Replay Seconds = 0 Any part of a report Testers untick it in the form; Settings > Default Parts sets what starts ticked A status message Its Display as: None"},{"p":"Make it yours","h":"Pin things on screen","u":"playtest-make-it-yours.html#pin-things-on-screen","x":"In the console's Options tab, Pin keeps an option on screen in a small panel while the console is closed, still working. Keep stats on screen does the same for the stats strip."},{"p":"Make it yours","h":"Bring your own UI","u":"playtest-make-it-yours.html#bring-your-own-ui","x":"Replace the form with BugReporter.UseForm(yourForm) (implement IBugReportForm ) and the overlay with BugReporter.UseOverlay(yourOverlay) (implement IDebugOverlay ). Your UI gets the same report session, with its screenshot, markup, parts and Submit, so nothing else changes. BugReporter.FormOpened , FormClosed , OverlayOpened , OverlayClosed , ReportSaved and ThemeChanged let your game react."},{"p":"Make it yours","h":"Gamepads and keyboards","u":"playtest-make-it-yours.html#gamepads-and-keyboards","x":"Every button, text box and switch shows a ring in the accent colour when selected. The console opens with a tab selected, the form with the first text box, and the result with Close, so a gamepad works from the first press."},{"p":"Extending","h":"Extending","u":"playtest-extending.html#extending","x":"Everything below works with no compile switches: in builds that leave Shipready out, each call does nothing."},{"p":"Extending","h":"Add to every report","u":"playtest-extending.html#add-to-every-report","x":"BugReporter.AddBreadcrumb(\"Opened the shop\"); // a line in Recent events BugReporter.SetContext(\"Level\", \"Forest 2\"); // a line in Game state; null removes it BugReporter.AddContextProvider(myProvider); // IReportContextProvider, read when a report starts BugReporter.AddAttachment(\"save.dat\", () => File.ReadAllBytes(savePath)); ReportPrivacy.Redact = text => Regex.Replace(text, @\"\\S+@\\S+\", \"<email>\"); With no code: add a Report Context Source component and list values to read from your components."},{"p":"Extending","h":"Open, save and listen","u":"playtest-extending.html#open-save-and-listen","x":"BugReporter.Open(); // the form, with a fresh screenshot BugReporter.Open(\"Boss fight softlocked\"); // with What happened filled in BugReporter.SaveQuickReport(\"Crash in level 3\"); // straight to a zip, no form BugReporter.ReportSaved += result => Debug.Log(result.ZipPath); BugReporter.FormOpened += PauseMyInput; BugReporter.FormClosed += ResumeMyInput;"},{"p":"Extending","h":"Console commands and options","u":"playtest-extending.html#console-commands-and-options","x":"[DebugCommand(\"give_gold\", \"Adds gold.\")] static string GiveGold(int amount) { Gold += amount; return \"Gold \" + Gold; } [DebugOption(\"Cheats/God Mode\")] bool godMode; [DebugOption(\"Tuning/Speed\", Min = 0, Max = 20, Step = 0.5f)] float speed; [DebugOption] void ResetLevel() { } void OnEnable() { DebugOptions.Register(this); DebugCommands.Register(this); } void OnDisable() { DebugOptions.Unregister(this); DebugCommands.Unregister(this); } Static commands are found by themselves. With no code: a Debug Options Source component lists values and methods by name. From code: DebugOptions.AddToggle , A"},{"p":"Extending","h":"Your own UI, destinations and input","u":"playtest-extending.html#your-own-ui-destinations-and-input","x":"BugReporter.UseForm(IBugReportForm) : your form gets a ReportSession with the screenshot, markup, parts and Submit . BugReporter.UseOverlay(IDebugOverlay) : your console; read lines with BugReporter.GetLog() and watch BugReporter.LogVersion . BugReporter.AddDestination(IReportDestination) : send reports anywhere; the local zip is always saved first. BugReporter.UseKeySource(IReportKeySource) : your own input code for the keys. BugReporter.KeysBlocked : return true while Shipready's keys should do nothing, such as while your chat box is open. BugReporter.TesterCamera : return the camera the pla"},{"p":"Samples","h":"Samples","u":"playtest-samples.html#samples","x":"Two demo scenes, in Samples/Scenes . Open either with Tools > Shipready > Playtest > Open Demo . They draw correctly in Built-in, URP and HDRP with no setup, and use only Shipready's own shader and plain blocks."},{"p":"Samples","h":"Bug Hunt","u":"playtest-samples.html#bug-hunt","x":"You're testing \"Crate Delivery\", a tiny game about to ship with real bugs in it. Press Play, then trigger a bug: - Deliver the crate : the crate falls through the floor and the delivery throws an error. - Collect pay : gold goes down instead of up, and a warning is logged. - Flip the lamp switch : the lamp flickers forever. Press F8 . The form opens with the screenshot taken before it appeared and the steps filled in from what you did. Write a line, mark the screenshot, and save. Stop playing and open Tools > Shipready > Playtest > Report Inbox . Pick the report, click a stack line to go to th"},{"p":"Samples","h":"Feature Gallery","u":"playtest-samples.html#feature-gallery","x":"One button for each thing Shipready can do, with the line of code behind it shown after you press it: Open the report form, open the debug overlay, save a report with no form. Switch between the four themes live. Add a recent event, set a game state value, log a warning or an error. Open the overlay (the key above Tab) and look at the Options tab: the spinning block's switch and speed come from [DebugOption] fields in FeatureGalleryDemo.cs with no UI code. In the console, type help , hello Sam or spin off to try the [DebugCommand] methods."},{"p":"Samples","h":"Using the samples in your own project","u":"playtest-samples.html#using-the-samples-in-your-own-project","x":"The sample scripts are in their own assembly, so deleting the Samples folder removes them cleanly. Nothing else in Shipready uses them."},{"p":"Upgrading","h":"Upgrading","u":"playtest-upgrading.html#upgrading","x":""},{"p":"Upgrading","h":"Updating Shipready","u":"playtest-upgrading.html#updating-shipready","x":"Back up your project or commit it. Import the new version from the Package Manager (My Assets). Import everything it offers. If Unity reports errors after importing, delete the RareGeometry/Shipready/Playtest folder and import again: files the new version removed are then gone too. Your settings, themes, text tables, Inbox statuses and notes live in Assets/RareGeometryData/Shipready , outside the kit, so an update never changes them. You can move or rename Shipready's folder. It finds its own files wherever they are."},{"p":"Upgrading","h":"What changed in each version","u":"playtest-upgrading.html#what-changed-in-each-version","x":""},{"p":"Upgrading","h":"1.0.1","u":"playtest-upgrading.html#101","x":"Nothing to do. New: Shipready's keys are ignored while your game's text fields have focus, BugReporter.KeysBlocked , BugReporter.TesterCamera , Settings > Max Attachment Megabytes, and the \"Adding it to your own game\" page. Closing the form now keeps a slow-motion time scale your game set while it was open, and a cursor your game hid or locked."},{"p":"Upgrading","h":"1.0.0","u":"playtest-upgrading.html#100","x":"The first release. Nothing to do. The full list of changes is in CHANGELOG.md in Shipready's folder."},{"p":"Troubleshooting","h":"Troubleshooting","u":"playtest-troubleshooting.html#troubleshooting","x":"Start with Tools > Shipready > Playtest > Why Isn't It Working . It checks everything the form needs and offers a one-click fix where there is one. What you see Why, and what to do F8 does nothing in the Editor Click the Game view first so it has the keyboard. Check Settings > Include In isn't Never. In Why Isn't It Working, click Open the Form Now while playing: if that works, your game is taking the key (see Adding it to your own game ) F8 or the key above Tab does nothing while typing On purpose: Shipready ignores its keys while one of your text fields has focus My game moves or shoots whil"},{"p":"Support","h":"Support","u":"playtest-support.html#support","x":"Every message gets an answer. Documentation: https://raregeometry.github.io/shipready/docs/ Discord: https://discord.gg/AmpBDxRAKu (ask in #help) Report a bug: https://github.com/raregeometry/Support/issues Website: https://raregeometry.github.io/shipready/ Email: the address in Tools > Shipready > Playtest > Help In Unity: Tools > Shipready > Playtest > Help and Support shows these with Copy buttons, and Copy Support Info copies your Shipready version, Unity version, render pipeline and platform for your message. Shipready is free. If it helps you, a short review on the Asset Store helps a lo"},{"p":"Support","h":"More from Rare Geometry","u":"playtest-support.html#more-from-rare-geometry","x":"The same developer makes other tools for Unity, built, tested and documented to the same standard. All of them: https://assetstore.unity.com/publishers/160196"},{"p":"Changelog","h":"Changelog","u":"changelog.html#changelog","x":""},{"p":"Changelog","h":"[1.0.0]","u":"changelog.html#100","x":"First release. Launch Desk: Connect My Game (new game, existing game, keep my own menus), Player Options, and the Ship Readiness Checker with an exportable report. Menus and settings: title, main menu, pause, loading, credits and settings screens; display, graphics, audio, controls with key rebinding, accessibility with presets, languages; looks and layouts you can make your own. Saves: slots, autosaves, quicksave, backups and automatic recovery, migrations between game versions, a save menu, world objects that remember their state, a Save Inspector. Tutorials (optional): first-time hints and "},{"p":"Support","h":"Support","u":"support.html#support","x":"Stuck, or found a bug in the Shipready? Every message is read by the developer who wrote it."},{"p":"Support","h":"Where to ask","u":"support.html#where-to-ask","x":"Discord (questions, ideas, show and tell): https://discord.gg/AmpBDxRAKu Bug tracker (report a bug and follow it until it's fixed): https://github.com/raregeometry/Support Email : raregeometrysupport@gmail.com Website and documentation : https://raregeometry.github.io"},{"p":"Support","h":"Reporting a bug","u":"support.html#reporting-a-bug","x":"Send these and it can be fixed fast: The support info. Click the ? in any Shipready window, then Copy system info : it puts the kit's version, Unity's version and your render pipeline on the clipboard. Nothing is sent anywhere. What you did , step by step, and what you expected to happen. What happened instead , with a screenshot if it shows. Any red errors from the Console (click one, then copy its full text)."}];
